.SH zclone copy
Copy files from source to dest, skipping identical files.

.SH Synopsis
Copy the source to the destination.  Does not transfer files that are
identical on source and destination, testing by size and modification
time or MD5SUM.  Doesn't delete files from the destination. If you
want to also delete files from destination, to make it match source,
use the sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra] command instead.

.PP
Note that it is always the contents of the directory that is synced,
not the directory itself. So when source:path is a directory, it's the
contents of source:path that are copied, not the directory name and
contents.

.PP
To copy single files, use the copyto
\[la]//commands/zclone_copyto/\[ra]
command instead.

.PP
If dest:path doesn't exist, it is created and the source:path contents
go there.

.PP
For example

.EX
zclone copy source:sourcepath dest:destpath
.EE

.PP
Let's say there are two files in sourcepath

.EX
sourcepath/one.txt
sourcepath/two.txt
.EE

.PP
This copies them to

.EX
destpath/one.txt
destpath/two.txt
.EE

.PP
Not to

.EX
destpath/sourcepath/one.txt
destpath/sourcepath/two.txt
.EE

.PP
If you are familiar with \fBrsync\fR, zclone always works as if you had
written a trailing \fB/\fR - meaning "copy the contents of this directory".
This applies to all commands and whether you are talking about the
source or destination.

.PP
See the --no-traverse
\[la]//docs/#no\-traverse\[ra] option for controlling
whether zclone lists the destination directory or not.  Supplying this
option when copying a small number of files into a large destination
can speed transfers up greatly.

.PP
For example, if you have many files in /path/to/src but only a few of
them change every day, you can copy all the files which have changed
recently very efficiently like this:

.EX
zclone copy --max-age 24h --no-traverse /path/to/src remote:
.EE

.PP
Zclone will sync the modification times of files and directories if
the backend supports it. If metadata syncing is required then use the
\fB--metadata\fR flag.

.PP
Note that the modification time and metadata for the root directory
will \fBnot\fP be synced. See issue #7652
\[la]/\[ra]
for more info.

.PP
\fBNote\fP: Use the \fB-P\fR/\fB--progress\fR flag to view real-time transfer statistics.

.PP
\fBNote\fP: Use the \fB--dry-run\fR or the \fB--interactive\fR/\fB-i\fR flag to test without
copying anything.

.SH Logger Flags
The \fB--differ\fR, \fB--missing-on-dst\fR, \fB--missing-on-src\fR, \fB--match\fR and \fB--error\fR
flags write paths, one per line, to the file name (or stdout if it is \fB-\fR)
supplied. What they write is described in the help below. For example
\fB--differ\fR will write all paths which are present on both the source and
destination but different.

.PP
The \fB--combined\fR flag will write a file (or stdout) which contains all
file paths with a symbol and then a space and then the path to tell
you what happened to it. These are reminiscent of diff files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB= path\fR means path was found in source and destination and was identical
.IP \(bu 2
\fB- path\fR means path was missing on the source, so only in the destination
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+ path\fR means path was missing on the destination, so only in the source
.IP \(bu 2
\fB* path\fR means path was present in source and destination but different.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB! path\fR means there was an error reading or hashing the source or dest.

.PP
The \fB--dest-after\fR flag writes a list file using the same format flags
as 
\[la]//commands/zclone_lsf/#synopsis\[ra] (including customizable options
for hash, modtime, etc.
\[la]//commands/zclone_lsf/#synopsis\[ra])
Conceptually it is similar to rsync's \fB--itemize-changes\fR, but not identical
-- it should output an accurate list of what will be on the destination
after the command is finished.

.PP
When the \fB--no-traverse\fR flag is set, all logs involving files that exist only
on the destination will be incomplete or completely missing.

.PP
Note that these logger flags have a few limitations, and certain scenarios
are not currently supported:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--max-duration\fR / \fBCutoffModeHard\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--compare-dest\fR / \fB--copy-dest\fR
.IP \(bu 2
server-side moves of an entire dir at once
.IP \(bu 2
High-level retries, because there would be duplicates (use \fB--retries 1\fR to disable)
.IP \(bu 2
Possibly some unusual error scenarios

.PP
Note also that each file is logged during execution, as opposed to after, so it
is most useful as a predictor of what SHOULD happen to each file
(which may or may not match what actually DID).

.EX
zclone copy source:path dest:path [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
      --absolute                Put a leading / in front of path names
      --combined string         Make a combined report of changes to this file
      --create-empty-src-dirs   Create empty source dirs on destination after copy
      --csv                     Output in CSV format
      --dest-after string       Report all files that exist on the dest post-sync
      --differ string           Report all non-matching files to this file
  -d, --dir-slash               Append a slash to directory names (default true)
      --dirs-only               Only list directories
      --error string            Report all files with errors (hashing or reading) to this file
      --files-only              Only list files (default true)
  -F, --format string           Output format - see lsf help for details (default "p")
      --hash h                  Use this hash when h is used in the format MD5|SHA-1|DropboxHash (default "md5")
  -h, --help                    help for copy
      --match string            Report all matching files to this file
      --missing-on-dst string   Report all files missing from the destination to this file
      --missing-on-src string   Report all files missing from the source to this file
  -s, --separator string        Separator for the items in the format (default ";")
  -t, --timeformat string       Specify a custom time format - see docs for details (default: 2006-01-02 15:04:05)
.EE

.PP
Options shared with other commands are described next.
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SS Copy Options
Flags for anything which can copy a file

.EX
      --check-first                                 Do all the checks before starting transfers
