.SH zclone sync
Make source and dest identical, modifying destination only.

.SH Synopsis
Sync the source to the destination, changing the destination
only.  Doesn't transfer files that are identical on source and
destination, testing by size and modification time or MD5SUM.
Destination is updated to match source, including deleting files
if necessary (except duplicate objects, see below). If you don't
want to delete files from destination, use the
copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] command instead.

.PP
\fBImportant\fP: Since this can cause data loss, test first with the
\fB--dry-run\fR or the \fB--interactive\fR/\fBi\fR flag.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive SOURCE remote:DESTINATION
.EE

.PP
Files in the destination won't be deleted if there were any errors at any
point. Duplicate objects (files with the same name, on those providers that
support it) are not yet handled. Files that are excluded won't be deleted
unless \fB--delete-excluded\fR is used. Symlinks won't be transferred or
deleted from local file systems unless \fB--links\fR is used.

.PP
It is always the contents of the directory that is synced, not the
directory itself. So when source:path is a directory, it's the contents of
source:path that are copied, not the directory name and contents.  See
extended explanation in the copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] command if unsure.

.PP
If dest:path doesn't exist, it is created and the source:path contents
go there.

.PP
It is not possible to sync overlapping remotes. However, you may exclude
the destination from the sync with a filter rule or by putting an
exclude-if-present file inside the destination directory and sync to a
destination that is inside the source directory.

.PP
Zclone will sync the modification times of files and directories if
the backend supports it. If metadata syncing is required then use the
\fB--metadata\fR flag.

.PP
Note that the modification time and metadata for the root directory
will \fBnot\fP be synced. See </>
for more info.

.PP
\fBNote\fP: Use the \fB-P\fR/\fB--progress\fR flag to view real-time transfer statistics

.PP
\fBNote\fP: Use the \fBzclone dedupe\fR command to deal with "Duplicate
object/directory found in source/destination - ignoring" errors.
See this forum post
\[la]/\[ra]
for more info.

.SH Logger Flags
The \fB--differ\fR, \fB--missing-on-dst\fR, \fB--missing-on-src\fR, \fB--match\fR and \fB--error\fR
flags write paths, one per line, to the file name (or stdout if it is \fB-\fR)
supplied. What they write is described in the help below. For example
\fB--differ\fR will write all paths which are present on both the source and
destination but different.

.PP
The \fB--combined\fR flag will write a file (or stdout) which contains all
file paths with a symbol and then a space and then the path to tell
you what happened to it. These are reminiscent of diff files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB= path\fR means path was found in source and destination and was identical
.IP \(bu 2
\fB- path\fR means path was missing on the source, so only in the destination
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+ path\fR means path was missing on the destination, so only in the source
.IP \(bu 2
\fB* path\fR means path was present in source and destination but different.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB! path\fR means there was an error reading or hashing the source or dest.

.PP
The \fB--dest-after\fR flag writes a list file using the same format flags
as 
\[la]//commands/zclone_lsf/#synopsis\[ra] (including customizable options
for hash, modtime, etc.
\[la]//commands/zclone_lsf/#synopsis\[ra])
Conceptually it is similar to rsync's \fB--itemize-changes\fR, but not identical
-- it should output an accurate list of what will be on the destination
