.SH zclone delete
Remove the files in path.

.SH Synopsis
Remove the files in path.  Unlike purge
\[la]//commands/zclone_purge/\[ra] it
obeys include/exclude filters so can be used to selectively delete files.

.PP
\fBzclone delete\fR only deletes files but leaves the directory structure
alone. If you want to delete a directory and all of its contents use
the purge
\[la]//commands/zclone_purge/\[ra] command.

.PP
If you supply the \fB--rmdirs\fR flag, it will remove all empty directories along
with it. You can also use the separate command rmdir
\[la]//commands/zclone_rmdir/\[ra]
or rmdirs
\[la]//commands/zclone_rmdirs/\[ra] to delete empty directories only.

.PP
For example, to delete all files bigger than 100 MiB, you may first want to
check what would be deleted (use either):

.EX
zclone --min-size 100M lsl remote:path
zclone --dry-run --min-size 100M delete remote:path
.EE

.PP
Then proceed with the actual delete:

.EX
zclone --min-size 100M delete remote:path
.EE

.PP
That reads "delete everything with a minimum size of 100 MiB", hence
delete all files bigger than 100 MiB.

.PP
\fBImportant\fP: Since this can cause data loss, test first with the
\fB--dry-run\fR or the \fB--interactive\fR/\fB-i\fR flag.

.EX
zclone delete remote:path [flags]
.EE

