.SH zclone archive create
Archive source file(s) to destination.

.SH Synopsis
Creates an archive from the files in source:path and saves the archive to
dest:path. If dest:path is missing, it will write to the console.

.PP
The valid formats for the \fB--format\fR flag are listed below. If
\fB--format\fR is not set zclone will guess it from the extension of dest:path.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBFormat\fP	\fBExtensions\fP
zip	\&.zip
tar	\&.tar
tar.gz	\&.tar.gz, .tgz, .taz
tar.bz2	T{
\&.tar.bz2, .tb2, .tbz, .tbz2, .tz2
T}
tar.lz	\&.tar.lz
tar.lz4	\&.tar.lz4
tar.xz	\&.tar.xz, .txz
tar.zst	\&.tar.zst, .tzst
tar.br	\&.tar.br
tar.sz	\&.tar.sz
tar.mz	\&.tar.mz
.TE

.PP
The \fB--prefix\fR and \fB--full-path\fR flags control the prefix for the files
in the archive.

.PP
If the flag \fB--full-path\fR is set then the files will have the full source
path as the prefix.

.PP
If the flag \fB--prefix=<value>\fR is set then the files will have
\fB<value>\fR as prefix. It's possible to create invalid file names with
\fB--prefix=<value>\fR so use with caution. Flag \fB--prefix\fR has
priority over \fB--full-path\fR\&.

.PP
Given a directory \fB/sourcedir\fR with the following:

.EX
file1.txt
dir1/file2.txt
.EE

.PP
Running the command \fBzclone archive create /sourcedir /dest.tar.gz\fR
will make an archive with the contents:

.EX
file1.txt
dir1/
dir1/file2.txt
.EE

.PP
Running the command \fBzclone archive create --full-path /sourcedir /dest.tar.gz\fR
will make an archive with the contents:

.EX
sourcedir/file1.txt
sourcedir/dir1/
sourcedir/dir1/file2.txt
.EE

.PP
Running the command \fBzclone archive create --prefix=my_new_path /sourcedir /dest.tar.gz\fR
will make an archive with the contents:

.EX
my_new_path/file1.txt
my_new_path/dir1/
my_new_path/dir1/file2.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone archive create [flags] <source> [<destination>]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
      --format string   Create the archive with format or guess from extension.
      --full-path       Set prefix for files in archive to source path
  -h, --help            help for create
      --prefix string   Set prefix for files in archive to entered value or source path
.EE

.PP
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive/\[ra]	 - Perform an action on an archive.


.SH zclone archive extract
Extract archives from source to destination.

.SH Synopsis
Extract the archive contents to a destination directory auto detecting
the format. See zclone archive create
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_create/\[ra]
for the archive formats supported.

.PP
For example on this archive:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --long remote:archive.zip
        6 2025-10-30 09:46:23.000000000 file.txt
        0 2025-10-30 09:46:57.000000000 dir/
        4 2025-10-30 09:46:57.000000000 dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
You can run extract like this

.EX
$ zclone archive extract remote:archive.zip remote:extracted
.EE

.PP
Which gives this result

.EX
$ zclone tree remote:extracted
/
├── dir
│   └── bye.txt
└── file.txt
.EE

.PP
The source or destination or both can be local or remote.

.PP
Filters can be used to only extract certain files:

.EX
$ zclone archive extract archive.zip partial --include "bye.*"
$ zclone tree partial
/
└── dir
    └── bye.txt
.EE

.PP
The archive backend
\[la]//archive/\[ra] can also be used to extract files. It
can be used to read only mount archives also but it supports a
different set of archive formats to the archive commands.

.EX
zclone archive extract [flags] <source> <destination>
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -h, --help   help for extract
.EE

.PP
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive/\[ra]	 - Perform an action on an archive.


.SH zclone archive list
List archive contents from source.

.SH Synopsis
List the contents of an archive to the console, auto detecting the
format. See zclone archive create
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_create/\[ra]
for the archive formats supported.

.PP
For example:

.EX
$ zclone archive list remote:archive.zip
        6 file.txt
        0 dir/
        4 dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
Or with \fB--long\fR flag for more info:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --long remote:archive.zip
        6 2025-10-30 09:46:23.000000000 file.txt
        0 2025-10-30 09:46:57.000000000 dir/
        4 2025-10-30 09:46:57.000000000 dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
Or with \fB--plain\fR flag which is useful for scripting:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --plain /path/to/archive.zip
file.txt
dir/
dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
Or with \fB--dirs-only\fR:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --plain --dirs-only /path/to/archive.zip
dir/
.EE

.PP
Or with \fB--files-only\fR:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --plain --files-only /path/to/archive.zip
file.txt
dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
Filters may also be used:

.EX
$ zclone archive list --long archive.zip --include "bye.*"
        4 2025-10-30 09:46:57.000000000 dir/bye.txt
.EE

.PP
The archive backend
\[la]//archive/\[ra] can also be used to list files. It
can be used to read only mount archives also but it supports a
different set of archive formats to the archive commands.

.EX
zclone archive list [flags] <source>
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
      --dirs-only    Only list directories
      --files-only   Only list files
  -h, --help         help for list
      --long         List extra attributtes
      --plain        Only list file names
.EE

.PP
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive
