.SH zclone cat
Concatenates any files and sends them to stdout.

.SH Synopsis
Sends any files to standard output.

.PP
You can use it like this to output a single file

.EX
zclone cat remote:path/to/file
.EE

.PP
Or like this to output any file in dir or its subdirectories.

.EX
zclone cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
Or like this to output any .txt files in dir or its subdirectories.

.EX
zclone --include "*.txt" cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
Use the \fB--head\fR flag to print characters only at the start, \fB--tail\fR for
the end and \fB--offset\fR and \fB--count\fR to print a section in the middle.
Note that if offset is negative it will count from the end, so
\fB--offset -1 --count 1\fR is equivalent to \fB--tail 1\fR\&.

.PP
Use the \fB--separator\fR flag to print a separator value between files. Be sure to
shell-escape special characters. For example, to print a newline between
files, use:
.IP \(bu 2
bash:

.EX
  zclone --include "*.txt" --separator $'\\n' cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
powershell:

.EX
  zclone --include "*.txt" --separator "`n" cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE

.EX
zclone cat remote:path [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
      --count int          Only print N characters (default -1)
      --discard            Discard the output instead of printing
      --head int           Only print the first N characters
  -h, --help               help for cat
      --offset int         Start printing at offset N (or from end if -ve)
      --separator string   Separator to use between objects when printing multiple files
      --tail int           Only print the last N characters
