.SH zclone convmv
Convert file and directory names in place.

.SH Synopsis
convmv supports advanced path name transformations for converting and renaming
files and directories by applying prefixes, suffixes, and other alterations.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBCommand\fP	\fBDescription\fP
\fB--name-transform prefix=XXXX\fR	T{
Prepends XXXX to the file name.
T}
\fB--name-transform suffix=XXXX\fR	T{
Appends XXXX to the file name after the extension.
T}
\fB--name-transform suffix_keep_extension=XXXX\fR	T{
Appends XXXX to the file name while preserving the original file extension.
T}
\fB--name-transform trimprefix=XXXX\fR	T{
Removes XXXX if it appears at the start of the file name.
T}
\fB--name-transform trimsuffix=XXXX\fR	T{
Removes XXXX if it appears at the end of the file name.
T}
\fB--name-transform regex=pattern/replacement\fR	T{
Applies a regex-based transformation.
T}
\fB--name-transform replace=old:new\fR	T{
Replaces occurrences of old with new in the file name.
T}
\fB--name-transform date={YYYYMMDD}\fR	T{
Appends or prefixes the specified date format.
T}
\fB--name-transform truncate=N\fR	T{
Truncates the file name to a maximum of N characters.
T}
\fB--name-transform truncate_keep_extension=N\fR	T{
Truncates the file name to a maximum of N characters while preserving the original file extension.
T}
\fB--name-transform truncate_bytes=N\fR	T{
Truncates the file name to a maximum of N bytes (not characters).
T}
\fB--name-transform truncate_bytes_keep_extension=N\fR	T{
Truncates the file name to a maximum of N bytes (not characters) while preserving the original file extension.
T}
\fB--name-transform base64encode\fR	T{
Encodes the file name in Base64.
T}
\fB--name-transform base64decode\fR	T{
Decodes a Base64-encoded file name.
T}
\fB--name-transform encoder=ENCODING\fR	T{
Converts the file name to the specified encoding (e.g., ISO-8859-1, Windows-1252, Macintosh).
T}
\fB--name-transform decoder=ENCODING\fR	T{
Decodes the file name from the specified encoding.
T}
\fB--name-transform charmap=MAP\fR	T{
Applies a character mapping transformation.
T}
\fB--name-transform lowercase\fR	T{
Converts the file name to lowercase.
T}
\fB--name-transform uppercase\fR	T{
Converts the file name to UPPERCASE.
T}
\fB--name-transform titlecase\fR	T{
Converts the file name to Title Case.
T}
\fB--name-transform ascii\fR	Strips non-ASCII characters.
\fB--name-transform url\fR	URL-encodes the file name.
\fB--name-transform nfc\fR	T{
Converts the file name to NFC Unicode normalization form.
T}
\fB--name-transform nfd\fR	T{
Converts the file name to NFD Unicode normalization form.
T}
\fB--name-transform nfkc\fR	T{
Converts the file name to NFKC Unicode normalization form.
T}
\fB--name-transform nfkd\fR	T{
Converts the file name to NFKD Unicode normalization form.
T}
\fB--name-transform command=/path/to/my/programfile names.\fR	T{
Executes an external program to transform.
T}
.TE

.PP
Conversion modes:

.EX
none
nfc
nfd
nfkc
nfkd
replace
prefix
suffix
suffix_keep_extension
trimprefix
trimsuffix
index
date
truncate
truncate_keep_extension
truncate_bytes
truncate_bytes_keep_extension
base64encode
base64decode
encoder
decoder
ISO-8859-1
Windows-1252
Macintosh
charmap
lowercase
uppercase
titlecase
ascii
url
regex
command
.EE

.PP
Char maps:

.EX
IBM-Code-Page-037
IBM-Code-Page-437
IBM-Code-Page-850
IBM-Code-Page-852
IBM-Code-Page-855
Windows-Code-Page-858
IBM-Code-Page-860
IBM-Code-Page-862
IBM-Code-Page-863
IBM-Code-Page-865
IBM-Code-Page-866
IBM-Code-Page-1047
IBM-Code-Page-1140
ISO-8859-1
ISO-8859-2
ISO-8859-3
ISO-8859-4
ISO-8859-5
ISO-8859-6
ISO-8859-7
ISO-8859-8
ISO-8859-9
ISO-8859-10
ISO-8859-13
ISO-8859-14
ISO-8859-15
ISO-8859-16
KOI8-R
KOI8-U
Macintosh
Macintosh-Cyrillic
Windows-874
Windows-1250
Windows-1251
Windows-1252
Windows-1253
Windows-1254
Windows-1255
Windows-1256
Windows-1257
Windows-1258
X-User-Defined
.EE

.PP
Encoding masks:

.EX
Asterisk
BackQuote
BackSlash
Colon
CrLf
Ctl
Del
Dollar
Dot
DoubleQuote
Exclamation
Hash
InvalidUtf8
LeftCrLfHtVt
LeftPeriod
LeftSpace
LeftTilde
LtGt
None
Percent
Pipe
Question
Raw
RightCrLfHtVt
RightPeriod
RightSpace
Semicolon
SingleQuote
Slash
SquareBracket
.EE

.PP
Examples:

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,uppercase"
// Output: STORIES/THE QUICK BROWN FOX!.TXT
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,replace=Fox:Turtle" --name-transform "all,replace=Quick:Slow"
// Output: stories/The Slow Brown Turtle!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,base64encode"
// Output: c3Rvcmllcw==/VGhlIFF1aWNrIEJyb3duIEZveCEudHh0
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "c3Rvcmllcw==/VGhlIFF1aWNrIEJyb3duIEZveCEudHh0" --name-transform "all,base64decode"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt" --name-transform "all,nfc"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt" --name-transform "all,nfd"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,ascii"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown  Fox!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,trimsuffix=.txt"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox!
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,prefix=OLD_"
// Output: OLD_stories/OLD_The Quick Brown Fox!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt" --name-transform "all,charmap=ISO-8859-7"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown _ Fox Went to the Caf_!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox: A Memoir [draft].txt" --name-transform "all,encoder=Colon,SquareBracket"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox： A Memoir ［draft］.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox Went to the Café!.txt" --name-transform "all,truncate=21"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown 🦊 Fox
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,command=echo"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!" --name-transform "date=-{YYYYMMDD}"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox!-20260830
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!" --name-transform "date=-{macfriendlytime}"
// Output: stories/The Quick Brown Fox!-2026-08-30 1109PM
.EE

.EX
zclone convmv "stories/The Quick Brown Fox!.txt" --name-transform "all,regex=[\\\\.\\\\w]/ab"
// Output: ababababababab/ababab ababababab ababababab ababab!abababab
.EE

.PP
The regex command generally accepts Perl-style regular expressions, the exact
syntax is defined in the Go regular expression reference
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/\[ra]\&.
The replacement string may contain capturing group variables, referencing
capturing groups using the syntax \fB$name\fR or \fB${name}\fR, where the name can
refer to a named capturing group or it can simply be the index as a number.
To insert a literal $, use $$.

.PP
Multiple transformations can be used in sequence, applied
in the order they are specified on the command line.

.PP
The \fB--name-transform\fR flag is also available in \fBsync\fR, \fBcopy\fR, and \fBmove\fR\&.

.SH Files vs Directories
By default \fB--name-transform\fR will only apply to file names. The means only the
leaf file name will be transformed. However some of the transforms would be
better applied to the whole path or just directories. To choose which which
part of the file path is affected some tags can be added to the \fB--name-transform\fR\&.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBTag\fP	\fBEffect\fP
\fBfile\fR	T{
Only transform the leaf name of files (DEFAULT)
T}
\fBdir\fR	T{
Only transform name of directories - these may appear anywhere in the path
T}
\fBall\fR	T{
Transform the entire path for files and directories
T}
.TE

.PP
This is used by adding the tag into the transform name like this:
\fB--name-transform file,prefix=ABC\fR or \fB--name-transform dir,prefix=DEF\fR\&.

.PP
For some conversions using all is more likely to be useful, for example
\fB--name-transform all,nfc\fR\&.

.PP
Note that \fB--name-transform\fR may not add path separators \fB/\fR to the name.
This will cause an error.

.SH Ordering and Conflicts
.IP \(bu 2
Transformations will be applied in the order specified by the user.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If the \fBfile\fR tag is in use (the default) then only the leaf name of files
will be transformed.
.IP \(bu 2
If the \fBdir\fR tag is in use then directories anywhere in the path will be
transformed
.IP \(bu 2
If the \fBall\fR tag is in use then directories and files anywhere in the path
will be transformed
.IP \(bu 2
Each transformation will be run one path segment at a time.
.IP \(bu 2
If a transformation adds a \fB/\fR or ends up with an empty path segment then
that will be an error.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
It is up to the user to put the transformations in a sensible order.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Conflicting transformations, such as \fBprefix\fR followed by \fBtrimprefix\fR or
\fBnfc\fR followed by \fBnfd\fR, are possible.
.IP \(bu 2
Instead of enforcing mutual exclusivity, transformations are applied in
sequence as specified by the user, allowing for intentional use cases
(e.g., trimming one prefix before adding another).
.IP \(bu 2
Users should be aware that certain combinations may lead to unexpected
results and should verify transformations using \fB--dry-run\fR before execution.
.RE

.SH Race Conditions and Non-Deterministic Behavior
Some transformations, such as \fBreplace=old:new\fR, may introduce conflicts where
multiple source files map to the same destination name. This can lead to race
conditions when performing concurrent transfers. It is up to the user to
anticipate these.
.IP \(bu 2
If two files from the source are transformed into the same name at the
destination, the final state may be non-deterministic.
.IP \(bu 2
Running zclone check after a sync using such transformations may erroneously
report missing or differing files due to overwritten results.

.PP
To minimize risks, users should:
.IP \(bu 2
Carefully review transformations that may introduce conflicts.
.IP \(bu 2
Use \fB--dry-run\fR to inspect changes before executing a sync (but keep in mind
that it won't show the effect of non-deterministic transformations).
.IP \(bu 2
Avoid transformations that cause multiple distinct source files to map to the
same destination name.
.IP \(bu 2
Consider disabling concurrency with \fB--transfers=1\fR if necessary.
.IP \(bu 2
Certain transformations (e.g. \fBprefix\fR) will have a multiplying effect every
time they are used. Avoid these when using \fBbisync\fR\&.
\fB
zclone convmv dest:path --name-transform XXX [flags]
\fR

.SH Options
.EX
      --create-empty-src-dirs   Create empty source dirs on destination after move
