.SH zclone copyurl
Copy the contents of the URL supplied content to dest:path.

.SH Synopsis
Download a URL's content and copy it to the destination without saving
it in temporary storage.

.PP
Setting \fB--auto-filename\fR will attempt to automatically determine the
filename from the URL (after any redirections) and used in the
destination path.

.PP
With \fB--header-filename\fR in addition, if a specific filename is
set in HTTP headers, it will be used instead of the name from the URL.
With \fB--print-filename\fR in addition, the resulting file name will be
printed.

.PP
Setting \fB--no-clobber\fR will prevent overwriting file on the
destination if there is one with the same name.

.PP
Setting \fB--stdout\fR or making the output file name \fB-\fR
will cause the output to be written to standard output.

.PP
Setting \fB--urls\fR allows you to input a CSV file of URLs in format: URL,
FILENAME. If \fB--urls\fR is in use then replace the URL in the arguments with the
file containing the URLs, e.g.:

.EX
zclone copyurl --urls myurls.csv remote:dir
.EE

.PP
Missing filenames will be autogenerated equivalent to using \fB--auto-filename\fR\&.
Note that \fB--stdout\fR and \fB--print-filename\fR are incompatible with \fB--urls\fR\&.
This will do \fB--transfers\fR copies in parallel. Note that if \fB--auto-filename\fR
is desired for all URLs then a file with only URLs and no filename can be used.

.PP
Each FILENAME in the CSV file can start with a relative path which will be appended
to the destination path provided at the command line. For example, running the command
shown above with the following CSV file will write two files to the destination:
\fBremote:dir/local/path/bar.json\fR and \fBremote:dir/another/local/directory/qux.json\fR

.EX
https://example.org/foo/bar.json,local/path/bar.json
https://example.org/qux/baz.json,another/local/directory/qux.json
.EE

.SH Troubleshooting
If you can't get \fBzclone copyurl\fR to work then here are some things you can try:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--disable-http2\fR zclone will use HTTP2 if available - try disabling it
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--bind 0.0.0.0\fR zclone will use IPv6 if available - try disabling it
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--bind ::0\fR to disable IPv4
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--user agent curl\fR - some sites have whitelists for curl's user-agent - try that
.IP \(bu 2
Make sure the site works with \fBcurl\fR directly
\fB
zclone copyurl https://example.com dest:path [flags]
\fR

.SH Options
.EX
  -a, --auto-filename     Get the file name from the URL and use it for destination file path
      --header-filename   Get the file name from the Content-Disposition header
  -h, --help              help for copyurl
      --no-clobber        Prevent overwriting file with same name
  -p, --print-filename    Print the resulting name from --auto-filename
      --stdout            Write the output to stdout rather than a file
      --urls              Use a CSV file of links to process multiple URLs
.EE

