.SH zclone lsf
List directories and objects in remote:path formatted for parsing.

.SH Synopsis
List the contents of the source path (directories and objects) to
standard output in a form which is easy to parse by scripts.  By
default this will just be the names of the objects and directories,
one per line.  The directories will have a / suffix.

.PP
E.g.

.EX
$ zclone lsf swift:bucket
bevajer5jef
canole
diwogej7
ferejej3gux/
fubuwic
.EE

.PP
Use the \fB--format\fR option to control what gets listed.  By default this
is just the path, but you can use these parameters to control the
output:

.EX
p - path
s - size
t - modification time
h - hash
i - ID of object
o - Original ID of underlying object
m - MimeType of object if known
e - encrypted name
T - tier of storage if known, e.g. "Hot" or "Cool"
M - Metadata of object in JSON blob format, eg {"key":"value"}
.EE

.PP
Some backends use composite IDs. In particular, Google Drive shortcuts are
normally dereferenced, and their "i" field contains the target object's ID
followed by the shortcut's ID, separated by a tab. This tab is part of the ID
field and is not changed by \fB--separator\fR\&. Use \fB--drive-skip-shortcuts\fR to omit
shortcut entries.

.PP
So if you wanted the path, size and modification time, you would use
\fB--format "pst"\fR, or maybe \fB--format "tsp"\fR to put the path last.

.PP
E.g.

.EX
$ zclone lsf  --format "tsp" swift:bucket
2016-06-25 18:55:41;60295;bevajer5jef
2016-06-25 18:55:43;90613;canole
2016-06-25 18:55:43;94467;diwogej7
2018-04-26 08:50:45;0;ferejej3gux/
2016-06-25 18:55:40;37600;fubuwic
.EE

.PP
If you specify "h" in the format you will get the MD5 hash by default,
use the \fB--hash\fR flag to change which hash you want.  Note that this
can be returned as an empty string if it isn't available on the object
(and for directories), "ERROR" if there was an error reading it from
the object and "UNSUPPORTED" if that object does not support that hash
type.

.PP
For example, to emulate the md5sum command you can use

.EX
zclone lsf -R --hash MD5 --format hp --separator "  " --files-only .
.EE

.PP
E.g.

.EX
$ zclone lsf -R --hash MD5 --format hp --separator "  " --files-only swift:bucket
7908e352297f0f530b84a756f188baa3  bevajer5jef
cd65ac234e6fea5925974a51cdd865cc  canole
03b5341b4f234b9d984d03ad076bae91  diwogej7
8fd37c3810dd660778137ac3a66cc06d  fubuwic
99713e14a4c4ff553acaf1930fad985b  gixacuh7ku
.EE

.PP
(Though "zclone md5sum ." is an easier way of typing this.)

.PP
By default the separator is ";" this can be changed with the
\fB--separator\fR flag.  Note that separators aren't escaped in the path so
putting it last is a good strategy.

.PP
E.g.

.EX
$ zclone lsf  --separator "," --format "tshp" swift:bucket
2016-06-25 18:55:41,60295,7908e352297f0f530b84a756f188baa3,bevajer5jef
2016-06-25 18:55:43,90613,cd65ac234e6fea5925974a51cdd865cc,canole
2016-06-25 18:55:43,94467,03b5341b4f234b9d984d03ad076bae91,diwogej7
2018-04-26 08:52:53,0,,ferejej3gux/
2016-06-25 18:55:40,37600,8fd37c3810dd660778137ac3a66cc06d,fubuwic
.EE

.PP
You can output in CSV standard format.  This will escape things in "
if they contain,

.PP
E.g.

.EX
$ zclone lsf --csv --files-only --format ps remote:path
test.log,22355
test.sh,449
"this file contains a comma, in the file name.txt",6
.EE

.PP
Note that the \fB--absolute\fR parameter is useful for making lists of files
to pass to an zclone copy with the \fB--files-from-raw\fR flag.

.PP
For example, to find all the files modified within one day and copy
those only (without traversing the whole directory structure):

.EX
zclone lsf --absolute --files-only --max-age 1d /path/to/local > new_files
zclone copy --files-from-raw new_files /path/to/local remote:path
.EE

.PP
The default time format is \fB\&'2006-01-02 15:04:05'\fR\&.
Other formats
\[la]https://pkg.go.dev/time#pkg\-constants\[ra] can be specified with
the \fB--time-format\fR flag. Examples:

.EX
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format 'Jan 2, 2006 at 3:04pm (MST)'
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format '2006-01-02 15:04:05.000000000'
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format '2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00'
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format RFC3339
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format DateOnly
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format max
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format unix
zclone lsf remote:path --format pt --time-format unixnano
.EE

.PP
\fB--time-format max\fR will automatically truncate \fB2006-01-02 15:04:05.000000000\fR
to the maximum precision supported by the remote.

.PP
Any of the filtering options can be applied to this command.

.PP
There are several related list commands
.IP \(bu 2
\fBls\fR to list size and path of objects only
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsl\fR to list modification time, size and path of objects only
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsd\fR to list directories only
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsf\fR to list objects and directories in easy to parse format
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsjson\fR to list objects and directories in JSON format

.PP
\fBls\fR,\fBlsl\fR,\fBlsd\fR are designed to be human-readable.
\fBlsf\fR is designed to be human and machine-readable.
\fBlsjson\fR is designed to be machine-readable.

.PP
Note that \fBls\fR and \fBlsl\fR recurse by default - use \fB--max-depth 1\fR to stop the recursion.

.PP
The other list commands \fBlsd\fR,\fBlsf\fR,\fBlsjson\fR do not recurse by default -
use \fB-R\fR to make them recurse.

.PP
List commands prefer a recursive method that uses more memory but fewer
transactions by default. Use \fB--disable ListR\fR to suppress the behavior.
See 
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.PP
Listing a nonexistent directory will produce an error except for
remotes which can't have empty directories (e.g. s3, swift, or gcs -
the bucket-based remotes).

.EX
zclone lsf remote:path [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
      --absolute             Put a leading / in front of path names
      --csv                  Output in CSV format
  -d, --dir-slash            Append a slash to directory names (default true)
      --dirs-only            Only list directories
      --files-only           Only list files
  -F, --format string        Output format - see  help for details (default "p")
      --hash h               Use this hash when h is used in the format MD5|SHA-1|DropboxHash (default "md5")
  -h, --help                 help for lsf
  -R, --recursive            Recurse into the listing
  -s, --separator string     Separator for the items in the format (default ";")
  -t, --time-format string   Specify a custom time format - see docs for details (default: 2006-01-02 15:04:05)
.EE

.PP
Options shared with other commands are described next.
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

