.SH zclone obscure
Obscure password for use in the zclone config file.

.SH Synopsis
In the zclone config file, human-readable passwords are
obscured. Obscuring them is done by encrypting them and writing them
out in base64. This is \fBnot\fP a secure way of encrypting these
passwords as zclone can decrypt them - it is to prevent "eyedropping" -
namely someone seeing a password in the zclone config file by accident.

.PP
Many equally important things (like access tokens) are not obscured in
the config file. However it is very hard to shoulder surf a 64
character hex token.

.PP
This command can also accept a password through STDIN instead of an
argument by passing a hyphen as an argument. This will use the first
line of STDIN as the password not including the trailing newline.

.EX
echo 'secretpassword' | zclone obscure -
.EE

.PP
If there is no data on STDIN to read, zclone obscure will default to
obfuscating the hyphen itself.

.PP
If you want to encrypt the config file then please use config file
encryption - see zclone config
\[la]//commands/zclone_config/\[ra] for more
info.

.EX
zclone obscure password [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -h, --help   help for obscure
.EE

.PP
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone
\[la]//commands/zclone/\[ra]	 - Show help for zclone commands, flags and backends.


.SH zclone rc
Run a command against a running zclone.

.SH Synopsis
This runs a command against a running zclone.  Use the \fB--url\fR flag to
specify an non default URL to connect on.  This can be either a
":port" which is taken to mean 
\[la]http://localhost:port\[ra] or a
"host:port" which is taken to mean 
\[la]http://host:port\[ra]\&.

.PP
A username and password can be passed in with \fB--user\fR and \fB--pass\fR\&.

.PP
Note that \fB--rc-addr\fR, \fB--rc-user\fR, \fB--rc-pass\fR will be read also for
\fB--url\fR, \fB--user\fR, \fB--pass\fR\&.

.PP
The \fB--unix-socket\fR flag can be used to connect over a unix socket like this

.EX
# start server on /tmp/my.socket
zclone rcd --rc-addr unix:///tmp/my.socket
# Connect to it
zclone rc --unix-socket /tmp/my.socket core/stats
.EE

.PP
Arguments should be passed in as parameter=value.

.PP
The result will be returned as a JSON object by default.

.PP
The \fB--json\fR parameter can be used to pass in a JSON blob as an input
instead of key=value arguments.  This is the only way of passing in
more complicated values.

.PP
The \fB-o\fR/\fB--opt\fR option can be used to set a key "opt" with key, value
options in the form \fB-o key=value\fR or \fB-o key\fR\&. It can be repeated as
many times as required. This is useful for rc commands which take the
"opt" parameter which by convention is a dictionary of strings.

.EX
-o key=value -o key2
.EE

.PP
Will place this in the "opt" value

.EX
{"key":"value", "key2","")
.EE

.PP
The \fB-a\fR/\fB--arg\fR option can be used to set strings in the "arg" value. It
can be repeated as many times as required. This is useful for rc
commands which take the "arg" parameter which by convention is a list
of strings.

.EX
-a value -a value2
.EE

.PP
Will place this in the "arg" value

.EX
["value", "value2"]
.EE

.PP
Use \fB--loopback\fR to connect to the zclone instance running \fBzclone rc\fR\&.
This is very useful for testing commands without having to run an
zclone rc server, e.g.:

.EX
zclone rc --loopback operations/about fs=/
.EE

.PP
Use \fBzclone rc\fR to see a list of all possible commands.

.EX
zclone rc commands parameter [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -a, --arg stringArray      Argument placed in the "arg" array
  -h, --help                 help for rc
      --json string          Input JSON - use instead of key=value args
      --loopback             If set connect to this zclone instance not via HTTP
      --no-output            If set, don't output the JSON result
  -o, --opt stringArray      Option in the form name=value or name placed in the "opt" array
      --pass string          Password to use to connect to zclone remote control
      --unix-socket string   Path to a unix domain socket to dial to, instead of opening a TCP connection directly
      --url string           URL to connect to zclone remote control (default "http://localhost:5572/")
      --user string          Username to use to zclone remote control
.EE

.PP
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone
\[la]//commands/zclone/\[ra]	 - Show help for zclone commands, flags and backends.


.SH zclone rcat
Copies standard input to file on remote.

.SH Synopsis
Reads from standard input (stdin) and copies it to a single remote file.

.EX
echo "hello world" | zclone rcat remote:path/to/file
ffmpeg - | zclone rcat remote:path/to/file
.EE

.PP
If the remote file already exists, it will be overwritten.

.PP
rcat will try to upload small files in a single request, which is
usually more efficient than the streaming/chunked upload endpoints,
which use multiple requests. Exact behaviour depends on the remote.
What is considered a small file may be set through
\fB--streaming-upload-cutoff\fR\&. Uploading only starts after
the cutoff is reached or if the file ends before that. The data
must fit into RAM. The cutoff needs to be small enough to adhere
the limits of your remote, please see there. Generally speaking,
setting this cutoff too high will decrease your performance.

.PP
Use the \fB--size\fR flag to preallocate the file in advance at the remote end
and actually stream it, even if remote backend doesn't support streaming.

.PP
\fB--size\fR should be the exact size of the input stream in bytes. If the
size of the stream is different in length to the \fB--size\fR passed in
then the transfer will likely fail.

.PP
Note that the upload cannot be retried because the data is not stored.
If the backend supports multipart uploading then individual chunks can
be retried. If you need to transfer a lot of data, you may be better
off caching it locally and then \fBzclone move\fR it to the
destination which can use retries.

.EX
zclone rcat remote:path [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -h, --help       help for rcat
      --size int   File size hint to preallocate (default -1)
.EE

.PP
Options shared with other commands are described next.
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SS Important Options
Important flags useful for most commands

.EX
  -n, --dry-run         Do a trial run with no permanent changes
  -i, --interactive     Enable interactive mode
  -v, --verbose count   Print lots more stuff (repeat for more)
.EE

.SH See Also
.IP \(bu 2
zclone
\[la]//commands/zclone/\[ra]	 - Show help for zclone commands, flags and backends.


.SH zclone rcd
Run zclone listening to remote control commands only.

.SH Synopsis
This runs zclone so that it only listens to remote control commands.

.PP
This is useful if you are controlling zclone via the rc API.

.PP
If you pass in a path to a directory, zclone will serve that directory
for GET requests on the URL passed in.  It will also open the URL in
the browser when zclone is run. If authentication is configured, the URL
does not contain credentials.

.PP
See the rc documentation
\[la]//rc/\[ra] for more info on the rc flags.

.SH Server options
Use \fB--rc-addr\fR to specify which IP address and port the server should
listen on, eg \fB--rc-addr 1.2.3.4:8000\fR or \fB--rc-addr :8080\fR to listen to all
IPs.  By default it only listens on localhost.  You can use port
:0 to let the OS choose an available port.

.PP
If you set \fB--rc-addr\fR to listen on a public or LAN accessible IP address
then using Authentication is advised - see the next section for info.

.PP
You can use a unix socket by setting the url to \fBunix:///path/to/socket\fR
or just by using an absolute path name.

.PP
\fB--rc-addr\fR may be repeated to listen on multiple IPs/ports/sockets.
Socket activation, described further below, can also be used to accomplish the same.

.PP
\fB--rc-server-read-timeout\fR and \fB--rc-server-write-timeout\fR can be used to
control the timeouts on the server.  Note that this is the total time
for a transfer.

.PP
\fB--rc-max-header-bytes\fR controls the maximum number of bytes the server will
accept in the HTTP header.

.PP
\fB--rc-response-header\fR can be used to set an HTTP header for all responses,
will overriding existing values. The flag may be repeated to add multiple
headers. Use the format \fBHeader-Name: value\fR\&.

.PP
\fB--rc-baseurl\fR controls the URL prefix that zclone serves from.  By default
zclone will serve from the root.  If you used \fB--rc-baseurl "/zclone"\fR then
zclone would serve from a URL starting with "/zclone/".  This is
useful if you wish to proxy zclone serve.  Zclone automatically
inserts leading and trailing "/" on \fB--rc-baseurl\fR, so \fB--rc-baseurl "zclone"\fR,
\fB--rc-baseurl "/zclone"\fR and \fB--rc-baseurl "/zclone/"\fR are all treated
identically.

.PP
\fB--rc-disable-zip\fR may be set to disable the zipping download option.

.SS Protocol
The server supports HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2.  HTTP/2 is used automatically
for TLS connections.  For non-TLS connections, HTTP/2 cleartext (h2c)
is supported, allowing HTTP/2 without encryption.

.SS TLS (SSL)
By default this will serve over http.  If you want you can serve over
https.  You will need to supply the \fB--rc-cert\fR and \fB--rc-key\fR flags.
If you wish to do client side certificate validation then you will need to
supply \fB--rc-client-ca\fR also.

.PP
When TLS is configured every listener given with \fB--rc-addr\fR serves TLS.
An individual listener can be prefixed with \fBhttp://\fR to serve unencrypted
HTTP on that address, or with \fBtls://\fR to state explicitly that it must serve
TLS.  Using a \fBtls://\fR address without \fB--rc-cert\fR and \fB--rc-key\fR is an error.

.PP
\fB--rc-cert\fR must be set to the path of a file containing
either a PEM encoded certificate, or a concatenation of that with the CA
certificate. \fB--rc-key\fR must be set to the path of a file
with the PEM encoded private key. If setting \fB--rc-client-ca\fR,
it should be set to the path of a file with PEM encoded client certificate
authority certificates.

.PP
\fB--rc-min-tls-version\fR is minimum TLS version that is acceptable. Valid
values are "tls1.0", "tls1.1", "tls1.2" and "tls1.3" (default "tls1.2").

.SH Socket activation
Instead of the listening addresses specified above, zclone will listen to all
FDs passed by the service manager, if any (and ignore any arguments passed
by \fB--rc-addr\fR).

.PP
This allows zclone to be a socket-activated service.
It can be configured with .socket and .service unit files as described in

\[la]https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.socket.html\[ra]\&.

.PP
Socket activation can be tested ad-hoc with the \fBsystemd-socket-activate\fRcommand

.EX
systemd-socket-activate -l 8000 -- zclone serve
.EE

.PP
This will socket-activate zclone on the first connection to port 8000 over TCP.

.SS Template
\fB--rc-template\fR allows a user to specify a custom markup template for HTTP
and WebDAV serve functions.  The server exports the following markup
to be used within the template to server pages:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBParameter\fP	\fBSubparameter\fP	\fBDescription\fP
\&.Name		T{
The full path of a file/directory.
T}
\&.Title		Directory listing of '.Name'.
\&.Sort		T{
The current sort used. This is changeable via '?sort=' parameter. Possible values: namedirfirst, name, size, time (default namedirfirst).
T}
\&.Order		T{
The current ordering used. This is changeable via '?order=' parameter. Possible values: asc, desc (default asc).
T}
\&.Query		Currently unused.
\&.Breadcrumb		T{
Allows for creating a relative navigation.
T}
	\&.Link	T{
The link of the Text relative to the root.
T}
	\&.Text	The Name of the directory.
\&.Entries		T{
Information about a specific file/directory.
T}
	\&.URL	The url of an entry.
	\&.Leaf	T{
Currently same as '.URL' but intended to be just the name.
T}
	\&.IsDir	T{
Boolean for if an entry is a directory or not.
T}
	\&.Size	Size in bytes of the entry.
	\&.ModTime	The UTC timestamp of an entry.
.TE

.PP
The server also makes the following functions available so that they can be used
within the template. These functions help extend the options for dynamic
rendering of HTML. They can be used to render HTML based on specific conditions.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBFunction\fP	\fBDescription\fP
afterEpoch	T{
Returns the time since the epoch for the given time.
T}
contains	T{
Checks whether a given substring is present or not in a given string.
T}
hasPrefix	T{
Checks whether the given string begins with the specified prefix.
T}
hasSuffix	T{
Checks whether the given string end with the specified suffix.
T}
.TE

.SS Authentication
By default this will serve files without needing a login.

.PP
You can either use an htpasswd file which can take lots of users, or
set a single username and password with the \fB--rc-user\fR and \fB--rc-pass\fR flags.

.PP
Alternatively, you can have the reverse proxy manage authentication and use the
username provided in the configured header with \fB--user-from-header\fR  (e.g., \fB--rc-user-from-header=x-remote-user\fR).
Ensure the proxy is trusted and headers cannot be spoofed, as misconfiguration
may lead to unauthorized access.

.PP
If either of the above authentication methods is not configured and client
certificates are required by the \fB--client-ca\fR flag passed to the server, the
client certificate common name will be considered as the username.

.PP
Use \fB--rc-htpasswd /path/to/htpasswd\fR to provide an htpasswd file.  This is
in standard apache format and supports MD5, SHA1 and BCrypt for basic
authentication.  Bcrypt is recommended.

.PP
To create an htpasswd file:

.EX
touch htpasswd
htpasswd -B htpasswd user
htpasswd -B htpasswd anotherUser
.EE

.PP
The password file can be updated while zclone is running.

.PP
Use \fB--rc-realm\fR to set the authentication realm.

.PP
Use \fB--rc-salt\fR to change the password hashing salt from the default.

.EX
zclone rcd <path to files to serve>* [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -h, --help   help for rcd
.EE

.PP
Options shared with other commands are described next.
See the global flags page
\[la]//flags/\[ra] for global options not listed here.

.SS RC Options
Flags to control the Remote Control API

.EX
      --rc                                 Enable the remote control server
      --rc-addr stringArray                IPaddress:Port or :Port to bind server to (default localhost:5572)
      --rc-allow-origin string             Origin which cross-domain request (CORS) can be executed from
      --rc-baseurl string                  Prefix for URLs - leave blank for root
      --rc-cert string                     TLS PEM key (concatenation of certificate and CA certificate)
      --rc-client-ca string                Client certificate authority to verify clients with
      --rc-enable-metrics                  Enable the Prometheus metrics path at the remote control server
      --rc-files string                    Path to local files to serve on the HTTP server
      --rc-htpasswd string                 A htpasswd file - if not provided no authentication is done
      --rc-job-expire-duration Duration    Expire finished async jobs older than this value (default 1m0s)
      --rc-job-expire-interval Duration    Interval to check for expired async jobs (default 10s)
      --rc-key string                      TLS PEM Private key
      --rc-max-header-bytes int            Maximum size of request header (default 4096)
      --rc-min-tls-version string          Minimum TLS version that is acceptable (default "tls1.2")
      --rc-no-auth                         Don't require auth for certain methods
      --rc-pass string                     Password for authentication
      --rc-realm string                    Realm for authentication
      --rc-response-header stringArray     Set HTTP header for all responses, overriding existing values
      --rc-salt string                     Password hashing salt (default "dlPL2MqE")
      --rc-serve                           Enable the serving of remote objects
      --rc-serve-no-modtime                Don't read the modification time (can speed things up)
      --rc-server-read-timeout Duration    Timeout for server reading data (default 1h0m0s)
      --rc-server-write-timeout Duration   Timeout for server writing data (default 1h0m0s)
      --rc-template string                 User-specified template
      --rc-user string                     User name for authentication
      --rc-user-from-header string         User name from a defined HTTP header
.EE

