.SH zclone touch
Create new file or change file modification time.

.SH Synopsis
Set the modification time on file(s) as specified by remote:path to
have the current time.

.PP
If remote:path does not exist then a zero sized file will be created,
unless \fB--no-create\fR or \fB--recursive\fR is provided.

.PP
If \fB--recursive\fR is used then recursively sets the modification
time on all existing files that is found under the path. Filters are supported,
and you can test with the \fB--dry-run\fR or the \fB--interactive\fR/\fB-i\fR flag.
This will touch \fB--transfers\fR files concurrently.

.PP
If \fB--timestamp\fR is used then sets the modification time to that
time instead of the current time. Times may be specified as one of:
.IP \(bu 2
\&'YYMMDD' - e.g. 17.10.30
.IP \(bu 2
\&'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS' - e.g. 2006-01-02T15:04:05
.IP \(bu 2
\&'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.SSS' - e.g. 2006-01-02T15:04:05.123456789

.PP
Note that value of \fB--timestamp\fR is in UTC. If you want local time
then add the \fB--localtime\fR flag.

.PP
Metadata can be added when creating a new file with \fB--metadata-set\fR\&.
For example:
  zclone touch remote:path -M --metadata-set key=value

.EX
zclone touch remote:path [flags]
.EE

.SH Options
.EX
  -h, --help               help for touch
      --localtime          Use localtime for timestamp, not UTC
  -C, --no-create          Do not create the file if it does not exist (implied with --recursive)
  -R, --recursive          Recursively touch all files
  -t, --timestamp string   Use specified time instead of the current time of day
.EE

