.SH Copying single files
zclone normally syncs or copies directories.  However, if the source
remote points to a file, zclone will just copy that file.  The
destination remote must point to a directory - zclone will give the
error \fBFailed to create file system for "remote:file": is a file not a
directory\fR if it isn't.

.PP
For example, suppose you have a remote with a file in called
\fBtest.jpg\fR, then you could copy just that file like this

.EX
zclone copy remote:test.jpg /tmp/download
.EE

.PP
The file \fBtest.jpg\fR will be placed inside \fB/tmp/download\fR\&.

.PP
This is equivalent to specifying

.EX
zclone copy --files-from /tmp/files remote: /tmp/download
.EE

.PP
Where \fB/tmp/files\fR contains the single line

.EX
test.jpg
.EE

.PP
It is recommended to use \fBcopy\fR when copying individual files, not \fBsync\fR\&.
They have pretty much the same effect but \fBcopy\fR will use a lot less
memory.

.SH Syntax of remote paths
The syntax of the paths passed to the zclone command are as follows.

.SS /path/to/dir
This refers to the local file system.

.PP
On Windows \fB\\\fR may be used instead of \fB/\fR in local paths \fBonly\fP,
non local paths must use \fB/\fR\&. See local filesystem
\[la]///local/#paths\-on\-windows\[ra]
documentation for more about Windows-specific paths.

.PP
These paths needn't start with a leading \fB/\fR - if they don't then they
will be relative to the current directory.

.SS remote:path/to/dir
This refers to a directory \fBpath/to/dir\fR on \fBremote:\fR as defined in
the config file (configured with \fBzclone config\fR).

.SS remote:/path/to/dir
On most backends this is refers to the same directory as
\fBremote:path/to/dir\fR and that format should be preferred.  On a very
small number of remotes (FTP, SFTP, Dropbox for business) this will
refer to a different directory.  On these, paths without a leading \fB/\fR
will refer to your "home" directory and paths with a leading \fB/\fR will
refer to the root.

.SS :backend:path/to/dir
This is an advanced form for creating remotes on the fly.  \fBbackend\fR
should be the name or prefix of a backend (the \fBtype\fR in the config
file) and all the configuration for the backend should be provided on
the command line (or in environment variables).

.PP
Here are some examples:

.EX
zclone lsd --http-url / :http:
.EE

.PP
To list all the directories in the root of `/

.EX
zclone lsf --http-url https://example.com :http:path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
To list files and directories in \fBhttps://example.com/path/to/dir/\fR

.EX
zclone copy --http-url https://example.com :http:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE

.PP
To copy files and directories in \fBhttps://example.com/path/to/dir\fR to \fB/tmp/dir\fR\&.

.EX
zclone copy --sftp-host example.com :sftp:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE

.PP
To copy files and directories from \fBexample.com\fR in the relative
directory \fBpath/to/dir\fR to \fB/tmp/dir\fR using sftp.

.SS Connection strings {#connection-strings}
The above examples can also be written using a connection string
syntax, so instead of providing the arguments as command line
parameters \fB--http-url /\fR they are provided as
part of the remote specification as a kind of connection string.

.EX
zclone lsd ":http,url='/':"
zclone lsf ":http,url='https://example.com':path/to/dir"
zclone copy ":http,url='https://example.com':path/to/dir" /tmp/dir
zclone copy :sftp,host=example.com:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE

.PP
These can apply to modify existing remotes as well as create new
remotes with the on the fly syntax. This example is equivalent to
adding the \fB--drive-shared-with-me\fR parameter to the remote \fBgdrive:\fR\&.

.EX
zclone lsf "gdrive,shared_with_me:path/to/dir"
.EE

.PP
The major advantage to using the connection string style syntax is
that it only applies to the remote, not to all the remotes of that
type of the command line. A common confusion is this attempt to copy a
file shared on google drive to the normal drive which \fBdoes not
work\fP because the \fB--drive-shared-with-me\fR flag applies to both the
source and the destination.

.EX
zclone copy --drive-shared-with-me gdrive:shared-file.txt gdrive:
.EE

.PP
However using the connection string syntax, this does work.

.EX
zclone copy "gdrive,shared_with_me:shared-file.txt" gdrive:
.EE

.PP
Note that the connection string only affects the options of the immediate
backend. If for example gdriveCrypt is a crypt based on gdrive, then the
following command \fBwill not work\fP as intended, because
\fBshared_with_me\fR is ignored by the crypt backend:

.EX
zclone copy "gdriveCrypt,shared_with_me:shared-file.txt" gdriveCrypt:
.EE

.PP
The connection strings have the following syntax

.EX
remote,parameter=value,parameter2=value2:path/to/dir
:backend,parameter=value,parameter2=value2:path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
If the \fBparameter\fR has a \fB:\fR or \fB,\fR then it must be placed in quotes \fB"\fR or
\fB\&'\fR, so

.EX
remote,parameter="colon:value",parameter2="comma,value":path/to/dir
:backend,parameter='colon:value',parameter2='comma,value':path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
If a quoted value needs to include that quote, then it should be
doubled, so

.EX
remote,parameter="with""quote",parameter2='with''quote':path/to/dir
.EE

.PP
This will make \fBparameter\fR be \fBwith"quote\fR and \fBparameter2\fR be
\fBwith'quote\fR\&.

.PP
If you leave off the \fB=parameter\fR then zclone will substitute \fB=true\fR
which works very well with flags. For example, to use s3 configured in
the environment you could use:

.EX
zclone lsd :s3,env_auth:
.EE

.PP
Which is equivalent to

.EX
zclone lsd :s3,env_auth=true:
.EE

.PP
Note that on the command line you might need to surround these
connection strings with \fB"\fR or \fB\&'\fR to stop the shell interpreting any
special characters within them.

.PP
If you are a shell master then you'll know which strings are OK and
which aren't, but if you aren't sure then enclose them in \fB"\fR and use
\fB\&'\fR as the inside quote. This syntax works on all OSes.

.EX
zclone copy ":http,url='https://example.com':path/to/dir" /tmp/dir
.EE

.PP
On Linux/macOS some characters are still interpreted inside \fB"\fR
strings in the shell (notably \fB\\\fR and \fB$\fR and \fB"\fR) so if your strings
contain those you can swap the roles of \fB"\fR and \fB\&'\fR thus. (This syntax
does not work on Windows.)

.EX
zclone copy ':http,url="https://example.com":path/to/dir' /tmp/dir
.EE

.PP
You can use zclone config string
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_string/\[ra] to
convert a remote into a connection string.

.SS Connection strings, config and logging
If you supply extra configuration to a backend by command line flag,
environment variable or connection string then zclone will add a
suffix based on the hash of the config to the name of the remote, eg

.EX
zclone -vv lsf --s3-chunk-size 20M s3:
.EE

.PP
Has the log message

.EX
DEBUG : s3: detected overridden config - adding "{Srj1p}" suffix to name
.EE

.PP
This is so zclone can tell the modified remote apart from the
unmodified remote when caching the backends.

.PP
This should only be noticeable in the logs.

.PP
This means that on the fly backends such as

.EX
zclone -vv lsf :s3,env_auth:
.EE

.PP
Will get their own names

.EX
DEBUG : :s3: detected overridden config - adding "{YTu53}" suffix to name
.EE

.SS Valid remote names
Remote names are case sensitive, and must adhere to the following rules:
.IP \(bu 2
May contain number, letter, \fB_\fR, \fB-\fR, \fB\&.\fR, \fB+\fR, \fB@\fR and space.
.IP \(bu 2
May not start with \fB-\fR or space.
.IP \(bu 2
May not end with space.

.PP
Starting with zclone version 1.61, any Unicode numbers and letters are allowed,
while in older versions it was limited to plain ASCII (0-9, A-Z, a-z). If you use
the same zclone configuration from different shells, which may be configured with
different character encoding, you must be cautious to use characters that are
possible to write in all of them. This is mostly a problem on Windows, where
the console traditionally uses a non-Unicode character set - defined
by the so-called "code page".

.PP
Do not use single character names on Windows as it creates ambiguity with Windows
drives' names, e.g.: remote called \fBC\fR is indistinguishable from \fBC\fR drive. Zclone
will always assume that single letter name refers to a drive.

.SH Adding global configuration to a remote {#globalconfig}
It is possible to add global configuration to the remote configuration which
will be applied just before the remote is created.

.PP
This can be done in two ways. The first is to use \fBoverride.var = value\fR in the
config file or the connection string for a temporary change, and the second is
to use \fBglobal.var = value\fR in the config file or connection string for a
permanent change.

.PP
This is explained fully below.

.SS override.var
This is used to override a global variable \fBjust\fP for the duration of the
remote creation. It won't affect other remotes even if they are created at the
same time.

.PP
This is very useful for overriding networking config needed for just for that
remote. For example, say you have a remote which needs \fB--no-check-certificate\fR
as it is running on test infrastructure without a proper certificate. You could
supply the \fB--no-check-certificate\fR flag to zclone, but this will affect \fBall\fP
the remotes. To make it just affect this remote you use an override. You could
put this in the config file:

.EX
[remote]
type = XXX
\&...
override.no_check_certificate = true
.EE

.PP
or use it in the connection string \fBremote,override.no_check_certificate=true:\fR
(or just \fBremote,override.no_check_certificate:\fR).

.PP
Note how the global flag name loses its initial \fB--\fR and gets \fB-\fR replaced with
\fB_\fR and gets an \fBoverride.\fR prefix.

.PP
Not all global variables make sense to be overridden like this as the config is
only applied during the remote creation. Here is a non exhaustive list of ones
which might be useful:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbind_addr\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBca_cert\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_cert\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_key\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconnect_timeout\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdisable_http2\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdisable_http_keep_alives\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdump\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexpect_continue_timeout\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBheaders\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttp_proxy\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlow_level_retries\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax_connections\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBno_check_certificate\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBno_gzip\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtimeout\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtraffic_class\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuse_cookies\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuse_server_modtime\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser_agent\fR

.PP
An \fBoverride.var\fR will override all other config methods, but \fBjust\fP for the
duration of the creation of the remote.

.SS global.var
This is used to set a global variable \fBfor everything\fP\&. The global variable is
set just before the remote is created.

.PP
This is useful for parameters (eg sync parameters) which can't be set as an
\fBoverride\fR\&. For example, say you have a remote where you would always like to
use the \fB--checksum\fR flag. You could supply the \fB--checksum\fR flag to zclone on
every command line, but instead you could put this in the config file:

.EX
[remote]
type = XXX
\&...
global.checksum = true
.EE

.PP
or use it in the connection string \fBremote,global.checksum=true:\fR (or just
\fBremote,global.checksum:\fR). This is equivalent to using the \fB--checksum\fR flag.

.PP
Note how the global flag name loses its initial \fB--\fR and gets \fB-\fR replaced with
\fB_\fR and gets a \fBglobal.\fR prefix.

.PP
Any global variable can be set like this and it is exactly equivalent to using
the equivalent flag on the command line. This means it will affect all uses of
zclone.

.PP
If two remotes set the same global variable then the first one instantiated will
be overridden by the second one. A \fBglobal.var\fR will override all other config
methods when the remote is created.

.SH Quoting and the shell
When you are typing commands to your computer you are using something
called the command line shell.  This interprets various characters in
an OS specific way.

.PP
Here are some gotchas which may help users unfamiliar with the shell rules

.SS Linux / macOS
If your names have spaces or shell metacharacters (e.g. \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB$\fR,
\fB\&'\fR, \fB"\fR, etc.) then you must quote them.  Use single quotes \fB\&'\fR by default.

.EX
zclone copy 'Important files?' remote:backup
.EE

.PP
If you want to send a \fB\&'\fR you will need to use \fB"\fR, e.g.

.EX
zclone copy "O'Reilly Reviews" remote:backup
.EE

.PP
The rules for quoting metacharacters are complicated and if you want
the full details you'll have to consult the manual page for your
shell.

.SS Windows
If your names have spaces in you need to put them in \fB"\fR, e.g.

.EX
zclone copy "E:\\folder name\\folder name\\folder name" remote:backup
.EE

.PP
If you are using the root directory on its own then don't quote it
(see #464
\[la]/\[ra] for why), e.g.

.EX
zclone copy E:\\ remote:backup
.EE

.SH Copying files or directories with \fB:\fR in the names
zclone uses \fB:\fR to mark a remote name.  This is, however, a valid
filename component in non-Windows OSes.  The remote name parser will
only search for a \fB:\fR up to the first \fB/\fR so if you need to act on a
file or directory like this then use the full path starting with a
\fB/\fR, or use \fB\&./\fR as a current directory prefix.

.PP
So to sync a directory called \fBsync:me\fR to a remote called \fBremote:\fR use

.EX
zclone sync --interactive ./sync:me remote:path
.EE

.PP
or

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /full/path/to/sync:me remote:path
.EE

.SH Server-side copy
Most remotes (but not all - see the
overview
\[la]//overview/#optional\-features\[ra]) support server-side copy.

.PP
This means if you want to copy one folder to another then zclone won't
download all the files and re-upload them; it will instruct the server
to copy them in place.

.PP
Eg

.EX
zclone copy s3:oldbucket s3:newbucket
.EE

.PP
Will copy the contents of \fBoldbucket\fR to \fBnewbucket\fR without
downloading and re-uploading.

.PP
Remotes which don't support server-side copy \fBwill\fP download and
re-upload in this case.

.PP
Server-side copies are used with \fBsync\fR and \fBcopy\fR and will be
identified in the log when using the \fB-v\fR flag.  The \fBmove\fR command
may also use them if remote doesn't support server-side move directly.
This is done by issuing a server-side copy then a delete which is much
quicker than a download and re-upload.

.PP
Server-side copies will only be attempted if the remote names are the
same.

.PP
This can be used when scripting to make aged backups efficiently, e.g.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive remote:current-backup remote:previous-backup
zclone sync --interactive /path/to/files remote:current-backup
.EE

.SH Metadata support {#metadata}
Metadata is data about a file (or directory) which isn't the contents
of the file (or directory). Normally zclone only preserves the
modification time and the content (MIME) type where possible.

.PP
Zclone supports preserving all the available metadata on files and
directories when using the \fB--metadata\fR or \fB-M\fR flag.

.PP
Exactly what metadata is supported and what that support means depends
on the backend. Backends that support metadata have a metadata section
in their docs and are listed in the features table
\[la]//overview/#features\[ra]
(Eg local
\[la]//local/#metadata\[ra], s3
\[la]/s3/#metadata\[ra])

.PP
Some backends don't support metadata, some only support metadata on
files and some support metadata on both files and directories.

.PP
Zclone only supports a one-time sync of metadata. This means that
metadata will be synced from the source object to the destination
object only when the source object has changed and needs to be
re-uploaded. If the metadata subsequently changes on the source object
without changing the object itself then it won't be synced to the
destination object. This is in line with the way zclone syncs
\fBContent-Type\fR without the \fB--metadata\fR flag.

.PP
Using \fB--metadata\fR when syncing from local to local will preserve file
attributes such as file mode, owner, extended attributes (not
Windows).

.PP
Note that arbitrary metadata may be added to objects using the
\fB--metadata-set key=value\fR flag when the object is first uploaded.
This flag can be repeated as many times as necessary.

.PP
The --metadata-mapper
\[la]#metadata\-mapper\[ra] flag can be used to pass the
name of a program in which can transform metadata when it is being
copied from source to destination.

.PP
Zclone supports \fB--metadata-set\fR and \fB--metadata-mapper\fR when doing
server-side \fBMove\fR and server-side \fBCopy\fR, but not when doing server
side \fBDirMove\fR (renaming a directory) as this would involve recursing
into the directory. Note that you can disable \fBDirMove\fR with
\fB--disable DirMove\fR and zclone will revert back to using \fBMove\fR for
each individual object where \fB--metadata-set\fR and \fB--metadata-mapper\fR
are supported.

.SS Types of metadata
Metadata is divided into two type. System metadata and User metadata.

.PP
Metadata which the backend uses itself is called system metadata. For
example on the local backend the system metadata \fBuid\fR will store the
user ID of the file when used on a unix based platform.

.PP
Arbitrary metadata is called user metadata and this can be set however
is desired.

.PP
When objects are copied from backend to backend, they will attempt to
interpret system metadata if it is supplied. Metadata may change from
being user metadata to system metadata as objects are copied between
different backends. For example copying an object from s3 sets the
\fBcontent-type\fR metadata. In a backend which understands this (like
\fBazureblob\fR) this will become the Content-Type of the object. In a
backend which doesn't understand this (like the \fBlocal\fR backend) this
will become user metadata. However should the local object be copied
back to s3, the Content-Type will be set correctly.

.SS Metadata framework
Zclone implements a metadata framework which can read metadata from an
object and write it to the object when (and only when) it is being
uploaded.

.PP
This metadata is stored as a dictionary with string keys and string
values.

.PP
There are some limits on the names of the keys (these may be clarified
further in the future).
.IP \(bu 2
must be lower case
.IP \(bu 2
may be \fBa-z\fR \fB0-9\fR containing \fB\&.\fR \fB-\fR or \fB_\fR
.IP \(bu 2
length is backend dependent

.PP
Each backend can provide system metadata that it understands. Some
backends can also store arbitrary user metadata.

.PP
Where possible the key names are standardized, so, for example, it is
possible to copy object metadata from s3 to azureblob for example and
metadata will be translated appropriately.

.PP
Some backends have limits on the size of the metadata and zclone will
give errors on upload if they are exceeded.

.SS Metadata preservation
The goal of the implementation is to
.IP "  1." 5
Preserve metadata if at all possible
.IP "  2." 5
Interpret metadata if at all possible

.PP
The consequences of 1 is that you can copy an S3 object to a local
disk then back to S3 losslessly. Likewise you can copy a local file
with file attributes and xattrs from local disk to s3 and back again
losslessly.

.PP
The consequence of 2 is that you can copy an S3 object with metadata
to Azureblob (say) and have the metadata appear on the Azureblob
object also.

.SS Standard system metadata
Here is a table of standard system metadata which, if appropriate, a
backend may implement.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBkey\fP	\fBdescription\fP	\fBexample\fP
mode	T{
File type and mode: octal, unix style
T}	0100664
uid	T{
User ID of owner: decimal number
T}	500
gid	T{
Group ID of owner: decimal number
T}	500
rdev	T{
Device ID (if special file)  => hexadecimal
T}	0
atime	Time of last access:  RFC 3339	T{
2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00
T}
mtime	T{
Time of last modification:  RFC 3339
T}	T{
2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00
T}
btime	T{
Time of file creation (birth):  RFC 3339
T}	T{
2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00
T}
utime	Time of file upload:  RFC 3339	T{
2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00
T}
cache-control	Cache-Control header	no-cache
content-disposition	Content-Disposition header	inline
content-encoding	Content-Encoding header	gzip
content-language	Content-Language header	en-US
content-type	Content-Type header	text/plain
.TE

.PP
The metadata keys \fBmtime\fR and \fBcontent-type\fR will take precedence if
supplied in the metadata over reading the \fBContent-Type\fR or
modification time of the source object.

.PP
Hashes are not included in system metadata as there is a well defined
way of reading those already.

.SH Options
Zclone has a number of options to control its behaviour. These are
documented below, and in the flags
\[la]/flags\[ra] page.

.PP
Options that take parameters can have the values passed in two ways,
\fB--option=value\fR or \fB--option value\fR\&. However boolean (true/false)
options behave slightly differently to the other options in that
\fB--boolean\fR sets the option to \fBtrue\fR and the absence of the flag sets
it to \fBfalse\fR\&.  It is also possible to specify \fB--boolean=false\fR or
\fB--boolean=true\fR\&.  Note that \fB--boolean false\fR is not valid - this is
parsed as \fB--boolean\fR and the \fBfalse\fR is parsed as an extra command
line argument for zclone.

.PP
String values that are recognized as special identifiers, e.g. the
name of the log level to set with option \fB--log-level\fR, are case
insensitive, e.g. \fB--log-level ERROR\fR and \fB--log-level error\fR are
identical.

.PP
Options documented to take a \fBstringArray\fR parameter accept multiple
values. To pass more than one value, repeat the option; for example:
\fB--include value1 --include value2\fR\&. Other options may only accept a
single value, and should only be specified once, but where the
specified parameter may indicate a list of values separated by space
or comma. Such options are documented to take a \fBCommaSepList\fR
parameter, if comma separated, and \fBSpaceSepList\fR if space separated,
although some may also have different parameters such as \fBDumpFlags\fR
or just \fBstring\fR and the help text explains that this will be
interpreted as a list.

.PP
Floating-point values with fractional part must use period (\fB\&.\fR)
as decimal separator, common in English-speaking countries, regardless
of your configured system locale. Parameter type \fBfloat\fR accepts decimal
and hexadecimal floating-point numbers as defined by the Go syntax for
floating-point literals
\[la]https://go.dev/ref/spec#Floating\-point_literals\[ra]\&.

.SS Time and duration options {#time-options}
Options that take a \fBTime\fR or \fBDuration\fR parameter must be specified
as a formatted string describing an absolute or relative time. Note
that both \fBTime\fR and \fBDuration\fR parameter types can be expressed as
either absolute or relative time, just with different interpretations,
e.g. a relative time will be treated as an offset from the current time
when passed as a \fBTime\fR value.

.PP
An absolute time can be specified as a string in one of the following formats:
.IP \(bu 2
RFC3339 - e.g. \fB2006-01-02T15:04:05Z\fR or \fB2006-01-02T15:04:05+07:00\fR
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date and time, local timezone - \fB2006-01-02T15:04:05\fR
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date and time, local timezone - \fB2006-01-02 15:04:05\fR
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date - \fB2006-01-02\fR (YYYY-MM-DD)

.PP
A relative time is a string with a, possibly signed, sequence of decimal
numbers, each with optional fraction, and each with a unit suffix. If
the string only contains a single number, then the unit suffix is optional
and will default to seconds, i.e. a plain decimal value will be treated
as a number of seconds. The following suffixes are valid:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBms\fR - Milliseconds
.IP \(bu 2
\fBs\fR  - Seconds
.IP \(bu 2
\fBm\fR  - Minutes
.IP \(bu 2
\fBh\fR  - Hours
.IP \(bu 2
\fBd\fR  - Days
.IP \(bu 2
\fBw\fR  - Weeks
.IP \(bu 2
\fBM\fR  - Months
.IP \(bu 2
\fBy\fR  - Years

.PP
Examples: "10", "300ms", "-1.5h" or "2h45m".

.SS Size options
Options that take a SizeSuffix parameter can be specified as an
integer value, which will then be assumed to represent a KiB
value (multiples of 1024 bytes) by default. The interpretation
can be changed by appending a suffix: \fBB\fR for Byte, \fBK\fR for KiB,
\fBM\fR for MiB, \fBG\fR for GiB, \fBT\fR for TiB and \fBP\fR for PiB. These are
the binary units, e.g. 1, 2**10, 2**20, 2**30 respectively.

.PP
See also --human-readable
\[la]#human\-readable\[ra]\&.

.SH Main options
.SS --backup-dir string
When using sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or
move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra], any files which would have been overwritten
or deleted are moved in their original hierarchy into this directory.

.PP
If \fB--suffix\fR is set, then the moved files will have the suffix added
to them. If there is a file with the same path (after the suffix has
been added) in the directory, then it will be overwritten.

.PP
The remote in use must support server-side move or copy and you must
use the same remote as the destination of the sync. The backup
directory must not overlap the destination directory without it being
excluded by a filter rule.

.PP
For example

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /path/to/local remote:current --backup-dir remote:old
.EE

.PP
will sync \fB/path/to/local\fR to \fBremote:current\fR, but for any files
which would have been updated or deleted will be stored in
\fBremote:old\fR\&.

.PP
If running zclone from a script you might want to use today's date as
the directory name passed to \fB--backup-dir\fR to store the old files, or
you might want to pass \fB--suffix\fR with today's date. This can be done
with \fB--suffix $(date +%F)\fR in bash, and
\fB--suffix $(Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd')\fR in PowerShell.

.PP
See \fB--compare-dest\fR and \fB--copy-dest\fR\&.

.SS --bind string
Local address to bind to for outgoing connections.  This can be an
IPv4 address (1.2.3.4), an IPv6 address (1234::789A) or host name.  If
the host name doesn't resolve or resolves to more than one IP address
it will give an error.

.PP
You can use \fB--bind 0.0.0.0\fR to force zclone to use IPv4 addresses and
\fB--bind ::0\fR to force zclone to use IPv6 addresses.

.SS --bwlimit BwTimetable
This option controls the bandwidth limit. For example

.EX
--bwlimit 10M
.EE

.PP
would mean limit the upload and download bandwidth to 10 MiB/s.
\fBNB\fP this is \fBbytes\fP per second not \fBbits\fP per second. To use a
single limit, specify the desired bandwidth in KiB/s, or use a
suffix B|K|M|G|T|P. The default is \fB0\fR which means to not limit bandwidth.

.PP
The upload and download bandwidth can be specified separately, as
\fB--bwlimit UP:DOWN\fR, so

.EX
--bwlimit 10M:100k
.EE

.PP
would mean limit the upload bandwidth to 10 MiB/s and the download
bandwidth to 100 KiB/s. Either limit can be "off" meaning no limit, so
to just limit the upload bandwidth you would use

.EX
--bwlimit 10M:off
.EE

.PP
this would limit the upload bandwidth to 10 MiB/s but the download
bandwidth would be unlimited.

.PP
When specified as above the bandwidth limits last for the duration of
run of the zclone binary.

.PP
It is also possible to specify a "timetable" of limits, which will
cause certain limits to be applied at certain times. To specify a
timetable, format your entries as \fBWEEKDAY-HH:MM,BANDWIDTH
WEEKDAY-HH:MM,BANDWIDTH...\fR where: \fBWEEKDAY\fR is optional element.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBANDWIDTH\fR can be a single number, e.g.\fB100k\fR or a pair of numbers
for upload:download, e.g.\fB10M:1M\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWEEKDAY\fR can be written as the whole word or only using the first 3
characters. It is optional.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHH:MM\fR is an hour from 00:00 to 23:59.

.PP
Entries can be separated by spaces or semicolons.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP Semicolons can be used as separators instead of spaces to avoid
parsing issues in environments like Docker.

.PP
An example of a typical timetable to avoid link saturation during daytime
working hours could be:

.PP
Using spaces as separators:
\fB--bwlimit "08:00,512k 12:00,10M 13:00,512k 18:00,30M 23:00,off"\fR

.PP
Using semicolons as separators:
\fB--bwlimit "08:00,512k;12:00,10M;13:00,512k;18:00,30M;23:00,off"\fR

.PP
In these examples, the transfer bandwidth will be set to 512 KiB/s
at 8am every day. At noon, it will rise to 10 MiB/s, and drop back
to 512 KiB/sec at 1pm. At 6pm, the bandwidth limit will be set to
30 MiB/s, and at 11pm it will be completely disabled (full speed).
Anything between 11pm and 8am will remain unlimited.

.PP
An example of timetable with \fBWEEKDAY\fR could be:

.PP
Using spaces as separators:
\fB--bwlimit "Mon-00:00,512 Fri-23:59,10M Sat-10:00,1M Sun-20:00,off"\fR

.PP
Using semicolons as separators:
\fB--bwlimit "Mon-00:00,512;Fri-23:59,10M;Sat-10:00,1M;Sun-20:00,off"\fR

.PP
It means that, the transfer bandwidth will be set to 512 KiB/s on
Monday. It will rise to 10 MiB/s before the end of Friday. At 10:00
on Saturday it will be set to 1 MiB/s. From 20:00 on Sunday it will
be unlimited.

.PP
Timeslots without \fBWEEKDAY\fR are extended to the whole week. So this
example:

.EX
--bwlimit "Mon-00:00,512 12:00,1M Sun-20:00,off"
.EE

.PP
Is equivalent to this:

.EX
--bwlimit "Mon-00:00,512Mon-12:00,1M Tue-12:00,1M Wed-12:00,1M Thu-12:00,1M Fri-12:00,1M Sat-12:00,1M Sun-12:00,1M Sun-20:00,off"
.EE

.PP
Bandwidth limit apply to the data transfer for all backends. For most
backends the directory listing bandwidth is also included (exceptions
being the non HTTP backends, \fBftp\fR, \fBsftp\fR and \fBstorj\fR).

.PP
Note that the units are \fBByte/s\fP, not \fBbit/s\fP\&. Typically
connections are measured in bit/s - to convert divide by 8. For
example, let's say you have a 10 Mbit/s connection and you wish zclone
to use half of it - 5 Mbit/s. This is 5/8 = 0.625 MiB/s so you would
use a \fB--bwlimit 0.625M\fR parameter for zclone.

.PP
On Unix systems (Linux, macOS, …) the bandwidth limiter can be toggled by
sending a \fBSIGUSR2\fR signal to zclone. This allows to remove the limitations
of a long running zclone transfer and to restore it back to the value specified
with \fB--bwlimit\fR quickly when needed. Assuming there is only one zclone instance
running, you can toggle the limiter like this:

.EX
kill -SIGUSR2 $(pidof zclone)
.EE

.PP
If you configure zclone with a remote control
\[la]/rc\[ra] then you can use
change the bwlimit dynamically:

.EX
zclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M
.EE

.SS --bwlimit-file BwTimetable
This option controls per file bandwidth limit. For the options see the
\fB--bwlimit\fR flag.

.PP
For example use this to allow no transfers to be faster than 1 MiB/s

.EX
--bwlimit-file 1M
.EE

.PP
This can be used in conjunction with \fB--bwlimit\fR\&.

.PP
Note that if a schedule is provided the file will use the schedule in
effect at the start of the transfer.

.SS --buffer-size SizeSuffix
Use this sized buffer to speed up file transfers.  Each \fB--transfer\fR
will use this much memory for buffering.

.PP
When using \fBmount\fR or \fBcmount\fR each open file descriptor will use this much
memory for buffering.
See the mount
\[la]//commands/zclone_mount/#vfs\-file\-buffering\[ra] documentation for
more details.

.PP
Set to \fB0\fR to disable the buffering for the minimum memory usage.

.PP
Note that the memory allocation of the buffers is influenced by the
--use-mmap
\[la]#use\-mmap\[ra] flag.

.SS --cache-dir string
Specify the directory zclone will use for caching, to override
the default.

.PP
Default value is depending on operating system:
.IP \(bu 2
Windows \fB%LocalAppData%\\zclone\fR, if \fBLocalAppData\fR is defined.
.IP \(bu 2
macOS \fB$HOME/Library/Caches/zclone\fR if \fBHOME\fR is defined.
.IP \(bu 2
Unix \fB$XDG_CACHE_HOME/zclone\fR if \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is defined, else
\fB$HOME/.cache/zclone\fR if \fBHOME\fR is defined.
.IP \(bu 2
Fallback (on all OS) to \fB$TMPDIR/zclone\fR, where \fBTMPDIR\fR is the value
from --temp-dir
\[la]#temp\-dir\-string\[ra]\&.

.PP
You can use the config paths
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_paths/\[ra]
command to see the current value.

.PP
Cache directory is heavily used by the VFS File Caching
\[la]//commands/zclone_mount/#vfs\-file\-caching\[ra]
mount feature, but also by serve
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve/\[ra], GUI
\[la]/gui\[ra] and
other parts of zclone.

.SS --check-first
If this flag is set then in a sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra],
copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra], zclone
will do all the checks to see whether files need to be transferred before
doing any of the transfers. Normally zclone would start running
transfers as soon as possible.

.PP
This flag can be useful on IO limited systems where transfers
interfere with checking.

.PP
It can also be useful to ensure perfect ordering when using
\fB--order-by\fR\&.

.PP
If both \fB--check-first\fR and \fB--order-by\fR are set when doing \fBzclone move\fR
then zclone will use the transfer thread to delete source files which
don't need transferring. This will enable perfect ordering of the
transfers and deletes but will cause the transfer stats to have more
items in than expected.

.PP
Using this flag can use more memory as it effectively sets
\fB--max-backlog\fR to infinite. This means that all the info on the
objects to transfer is held in memory before the transfers start.

.SS --checkers int
Originally controlling just the number of file checkers to run in parallel,
e.g. by \fBzclone copy\fR\&. Now a fairly universal parallelism control
used by \fBzclone\fR in several places.

.PP
Note: checkers do the equality checking of files during a sync.
For some storage systems (e.g. S3, Swift, Dropbox) this can take
a significant amount of time so they are run in parallel.

.PP
The default is to run 8 checkers in parallel. However, in case
of slow-reacting backends you may need to lower (rather than increase)
this default by setting \fB--checkers\fR to 4 or less threads. This is
especially advised if you are experiencing backend server crashes
during file checking phase (e.g. on subsequent or top-up backups
where little or no file copying is done and checking takes up
most of the time). Increase this setting only with utmost care,
while monitoring your server health and file checking throughput.

.SS -c, --checksum
Normally zclone will look at modification time and size of files to
see if they are equal.  If you set this flag then zclone will check
the file hash and size to determine if files are equal.

.PP
This is useful when the remote doesn't support setting modified time
and a more accurate sync is desired than just checking the file size.

.PP
This is very useful when transferring between remotes which store the
same hash type on the object, e.g. Drive and Swift. For details of which
remotes support which hash type see the table in the overview
section
\[la]//overview/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Eg \fBzclone --checksum sync s3:/bucket swift:/bucket\fR would run much
quicker than without the \fB--checksum\fR flag.

.PP
When using this flag, zclone won't update mtimes of remote files if
they are incorrect as it would normally.

.SS --color AUTO|NEVER|ALWAYS
Specify when colors (and other ANSI codes) should be added to the output.

.PP
\fBAUTO\fR only allows ANSI codes when the output is a terminal. This is the default.

.PP
\fBNEVER\fR never allow ANSI codes.

.PP
\fBALWAYS\fR always add ANSI codes, regardless of the output format (terminal or file).

.SS --compare-dest stringArray
When using sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or
move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra], the specified paths are checked in addition
to the destination for files. If a file identical to the source is found, that
file is \fBnot\fP copied from source. This is useful to copy just files that
have changed since the last backup.

.PP
You must use the same remote as the destination of the sync. The
compare directory must not overlap the destination directory.

.PP
See \fB--copy-dest\fR and \fB--backup-dir\fR\&.

.SS --config string
Specify the location of the zclone configuration file, to override
the default. E.g. \fBzclone config --config="zclone.conf"\fR\&.

.PP
The exact default is a bit complex to describe, due to changes
introduced through different versions of zclone while preserving
backwards compatibility, but in most cases it is as simple as:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%APPDATA%/zclone/zclone.conf\fR on Windows
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~/.config/zclone/zclone.conf\fR on other

.PP
The complete logic is as follows: Zclone will look for an existing
configuration file in any of the following locations, in priority order:
.IP "  1." 5
\fBzclone.conf\fR (in program directory, where zclone executable is)
.IP "  2." 5
\fB%APPDATA%/zclone/zclone.conf\fR (only on Windows)
.IP "  3." 5
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zclone/zclone.conf\fR (on all systems, including Windows)
.IP "  4." 5
\fB~/.config/zclone/zclone.conf\fR (see below for explanation of ~ symbol)
.IP "  5." 5
\fB~/.zclone.conf\fR

.PP
If no existing configuration file is found, then a new one will be created
in the following location:
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows: Location 2 listed above, except in the unlikely event
that \fBAPPDATA\fR is not defined, then location 4 is used instead.
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix: Location 3 if \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is defined, else location 4.
.IP \(bu 2
Fallback to location 5 (on all OS), when the zclone directory cannot be
created, but if also a home directory was not found then path
\fB\&.zclone.conf\fR relative to current working directory will be used as
a final resort.

.PP
The \fB~\fR symbol in paths above represent the home directory of the current user
on any OS, and the value is defined as following:
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows: \fB%HOME%\fR if defined, else \fB%USERPROFILE%\fR, or else \fB%HOMEDRIVE%\\%HOMEPATH%\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix: \fB$HOME\fR if defined, else by looking up current user in OS-specific user
database (e.g. passwd file), or else use the result from shell command \fBcd && pwd\fR\&.

.PP
If you run \fBzclone config file\fR you will see where the default location is for
you. Running \fBzclone config touch\fR will ensure a configuration file exists,
creating an empty one in the default location if there is none.

.PP
The fact that an existing file \fBzclone.conf\fR in the same directory
as the zclone executable is always preferred, means that it is easy
to run in "portable" mode by downloading zclone executable to a
writable directory and then create an empty file \fBzclone.conf\fR in the
same directory.

.PP
If the location is set to empty string \fB""\fR or path to a file
with name \fBnotfound\fR, or the os null device represented by value \fBNUL\fR on
Windows and \fB/dev/null\fR on Unix systems, then zclone will keep the
configuration file in memory only.

.PP
You may see a log message "Config file not found - using defaults" if there is
no configuration file. This can be suppressed, e.g. if you are using zclone
entirely with on the fly remotes
\[la]//docs/#backend\-path\-to\-dir\[ra], by using
memory-only configuration file or by creating an empty configuration file, as
described above.

.PP
The file format is basic INI
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/INI_file#Format\[ra]:
Sections of text, led by a \fB[section]\fR header and followed by
\fBkey=value\fR entries on separate lines. In zclone each remote is
represented by its own section, where the section name defines the
name of the remote. Options are specified as the \fBkey=value\fR entries,
where the key is the option name without the \fB--backend-\fR prefix,
in lowercase and with \fB_\fR instead of \fB-\fR\&. E.g. option \fB--mega-hard-delete\fR
corresponds to key \fBhard_delete\fR\&. Only backend options can be specified.
A special, and required, key \fBtype\fR identifies the storage system
\[la]//overview/\[ra],
where the value is the internal lowercase name as returned by command
\fBzclone help backends\fR\&. Comments are indicated by \fB;\fR or \fB#\fR at the
beginning of a line.

.PP
Example:

.EX
[megaremote]
type = mega
user = you@example.com
pass = PDPcQVVjVtzFY-GTdDFozqBhTdsPg3qH
.EE

.PP
Note that passwords are in obscured
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]
form. Also, many storage systems uses token-based authentication instead
of passwords, and this requires additional steps. It is easier, and safer,
to use the interactive command \fBzclone config\fR instead of manually
editing the configuration file.

.PP
The configuration file will typically contain login information, and
should therefore have restricted permissions so that only the current user
can read it. Zclone tries to ensure this when it writes the file.
You may also choose to encrypt
\[la]#configuration\-encryption\[ra] the file.

.PP
When token-based authentication are used, the configuration file
must be writable, because zclone needs to update the tokens inside it.

.PP
To reduce risk of corrupting an existing configuration file, zclone
will not write directly to it when saving changes. Instead it will
first write to a new, temporary, file. If a configuration file already
existed, it will (on Unix systems) try to mirror its permissions to
the new file. Then it will rename the existing file to a temporary
name as backup. Next, zclone will rename the new file to the correct name,
before finally cleaning up by deleting the backup file.

.PP
If the configuration file path used by zclone is a symbolic link, then
this will be evaluated and zclone will write to the resolved path, instead
of overwriting the symbolic link. Temporary files used in the process
(described above) will be written to the same parent directory as that
of the resolved configuration file, but if this directory is also a
symbolic link it will not be resolved and the temporary files will be
written to the location of the directory symbolic link.

.SS --contimeout Duration
Set the connection timeout. This should be in go time format which
looks like \fB5s\fR for 5 seconds, \fB10m\fR for 10 minutes, or \fB3h30m\fR\&.

.PP
The connection timeout is the amount of time zclone will wait for a
connection to go through to a remote object storage system.  It is
\fB1m\fR by default.

.SS --copy-dest stringArray
When using sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or
move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra], the specified paths are checked in addition
to the destination for files. This part is the same as \fB--compare-dest\fR, but
the difference is that with \fB--copy-dest\fR, if a file identical to the source
is found, that file is server-side copied from the specified paths to the
destination. This is useful for incremental backup.

.PP
The remote in use must support server-side copy and you must
use the same remote as the destination of the sync.  The compare
directory must not overlap the destination directory.

.PP
See \fB--compare-dest\fR and \fB--backup-dir\fR\&.

.SS --dedupe-mode interactive|skip|first|newest|oldest|largest|smallest|rename|list
Mode to run dedupe command in.  One of \fBinteractive\fR, \fBskip\fR, \fBfirst\fR,
\fBnewest\fR, \fBoldest\fR, \fBlargest\fR, \fBsmallest\fR, \fBrename\fR \fBlist\fR\&.  The default
is \fBinteractive\fR\&.   See the dedupe
\[la]//commands/zclone_dedupe/\[ra] command
for more information as to what these options mean.

.SS --default-time Time
If a file or directory does have a modification time zclone can read
then zclone will display this fixed time instead.

.PP
The default is \fB2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC\fR\&. This can be configured in
any of the ways shown in time options
\[la]#time\-options\[ra]\&.

.PP
For example \fB--default-time 2020-06-01\fR to set the default time to the
1st of June 2020 or \fB--default-time 0s\fR to set the default time to the
time zclone started up.

.SS --disable string
This disables a comma separated list of optional features. For example
to disable server-side move and server-side copy use:

.EX
--disable move,copy
.EE

.PP
The features can be put in any case.

.PP
To see a list of which features can be disabled use:

.EX
--disable help
.EE

.PP
The features a remote has can be seen in JSON format with:

.EX
zclone backend features remote:
.EE

.PP
See the overview features
\[la]//overview/#features\[ra] and
optional features
\[la]//overview/#optional\-features\[ra] to get an idea of
which feature does what.

.PP
Note that some features can be set to \fBtrue\fR if they are \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR
feature flag features by prefixing them with \fB!\fR\&. For example the
\fBCaseInsensitive\fR feature can be forced to \fBfalse\fR with \fB--disable CaseInsensitive\fR
and forced to \fBtrue\fR with \fB--disable '!CaseInsensitive'\fR\&. In general
it isn't a good idea doing this but it may be useful in extremis.

.PP
(Note that \fB!\fR is a shell command which you will
need to escape with single quotes or a backslash on unix like
platforms.)

.PP
This flag can be useful for debugging and in exceptional circumstances
(e.g. Google Drive limiting the total volume of server-side copies to
100 GiB/day).

.SS --disable-http2
This stops zclone from trying to use HTTP/2 if available. This can
sometimes speed up transfers due to a
problem in the Go standard library
\[la]https://github.com/golang/go/issues/37373\[ra]\&.

.SS --dscp string
Specify a DSCP value or name to use in connections. This could help QoS
system to identify traffic class. BE, EF, DF, LE, CSx and AFxx are allowed.

.PP
See the description of differentiated services
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differentiated_services\[ra]
to get an idea of this field. Setting this to 1 (LE) to identify the flow to
SCAVENGER class can avoid occupying too much bandwidth in a network with DiffServ
support (RFC 8622
\[la]https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8622\[ra]).

.PP
For example, if you configured QoS on router to handle LE properly. Running:

.EX
zclone copy --dscp LE from:/from to:/to
.EE

.PP
would make the priority lower than usual internet flows.

.PP
This option has no effect on Windows (see golang/go#42728
\[la]https://github.com/golang/go/issues/42728\[ra]).

.SS -n, --dry-run
Do a trial run with no permanent changes.  Use this to see what zclone
would do without actually doing it.  Useful when setting up the
sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra] command which deletes files in the destination.

.SS --expect-continue-timeout Duration
This specifies the amount of time to wait for a server's first
response headers after fully writing the request headers if the
request has an "Expect: 100-continue" header. Not all backends support
using this.

.PP
Zero means no timeout and causes the body to be sent immediately,
without waiting for the server to approve.  This time does not include
the time to send the request header.

.PP
The default is \fB1s\fR\&.  Set to \fB0\fR to disable.

.SS --error-on-no-transfer
By default, zclone will exit with return code 0 if there were no errors.

.PP
This option allows zclone to return exit code 9 if no files were transferred
between the source and destination. This allows using zclone in scripts, and
triggering follow-on actions if data was copied, or skipping if not.

.PP
NB: Enabling this option turns a usually non-fatal error into a potentially
fatal one - please check and adjust your scripts accordingly!

.SS --fix-case
Normally, a sync to a case insensitive dest (such as macOS / Windows) will
not result in a matching filename if the source and dest filenames have
casing differences but are otherwise identical. For example, syncing \fBhello.txt\fR
to \fBHELLO.txt\fR will normally result in the dest filename remaining \fBHELLO.txt\fR\&.
If \fB--fix-case\fR is set, then \fBHELLO.txt\fR will be renamed to \fBhello.txt\fR
to match the source.

.PP
NB:
.IP \(bu 2
directory names with incorrect casing will also be fixed
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--fix-case\fR will be ignored if \fB--immutable\fR is set
.IP \(bu 2
using \fB--local-case-sensitive\fR instead is not advisable;
it will cause \fBHELLO.txt\fR to get deleted!
.IP \(bu 2
the old dest filename must not be excluded by filters.
Be especially careful with 
\[la]//filtering/#files\-from\-read\-list\-of\-source\-file\-names\[ra],
which does not respect 
\[la]//filtering/#ignore\-case\-make\-searches\-case\-insensitive\[ra]!
.IP \(bu 2
on remotes that do not support server-side move, \fB--fix-case\fR will require
downloading the file and re-uploading it. To avoid this, do not use \fB--fix-case\fR\&.

.SS --fs-cache-expire-duration Duration
When using zclone via the API zclone caches created remotes for 5
minutes by default in the "fs cache". This means that if you do
repeated actions on the same remote then zclone won't have to build it
again from scratch, which makes it more efficient.

.PP
This flag sets the time that the remotes are cached for. If you set it
to \fB0\fR (or negative) then zclone won't cache the remotes at all.

.PP
Note that if you use some flags, eg \fB--backup-dir\fR and if this is set
to \fB0\fR zclone may build two remotes (one for the source or destination
and one for the \fB--backup-dir\fR where it may have only built one
before.

.SS --fs-cache-expire-interval Duration
This controls how often zclone checks for cached remotes to expire.
See the \fB--fs-cache-expire-duration\fR documentation above for more
info. The default is 60s, set to 0 to disable expiry.

.SS --header stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all transactions. The flag can be repeated to
add multiple headers.

.PP
If you want to add headers only for uploads use \fB--header-upload\fR and
if you want to add headers only for downloads use \fB--header-download\fR\&.

.PP
This flag is supported for all HTTP based backends even those not
supported by \fB--header-upload\fR and \fB--header-download\fR so may be used
as a workaround for those with care.

.EX
zclone ls remote:test --header "X-Zclone: Foo" --header "X-LetMeIn: Yes"
.EE

.SS --header-download stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all download transactions. The flag can be repeated to
add multiple headers.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive s3:test/src ~/dst --header-download "X-Amz-Meta-Test: Foo" --header-download "X-Amz-Meta-Test2: Bar"
.EE

.PP
See GitHub issue #59
\[la]/\[ra] for
currently supported backends.

.SS --header-upload stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all upload transactions. The flag can be repeated to add
multiple headers.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive ~/src s3:test/dst --header-upload "Content-Disposition: attachment; filename='cool.html'" --header-upload "X-Amz-Meta-Test: FooBar"
.EE

.PP
See GitHub issue #59
\[la]/\[ra] for
currently supported backends.

.SS --http-proxy string
Use this option to set an HTTP proxy for all HTTP based services to
use.

.PP
Zclone also supports the standard HTTP proxy environment variables
which it will pick up automatically. The is the way the HTTP proxy
will normally be set but this flag can be used to override it.

.SS --human-readable
Zclone commands output values for sizes (e.g. number of bytes) and
counts (e.g. number of files) either as \fIraw\fP numbers, or
in \fIhuman-readable\fP format.

.PP
In human-readable format the values are scaled to larger units, indicated with
a suffix shown after the value, and rounded to three decimals. Zclone consistently
uses binary units (powers of 2) for sizes and decimal units (powers of 10) for counts.
The unit prefix for size is according to IEC standard notation, e.g. \fBKi\fR for kibi.
Used with byte unit, \fB1 KiB\fR means 1024 Byte. In list type of output, only the
unit prefix appended to the value (e.g. \fB9.762Ki\fR), while in more textual output
the full unit is shown (e.g. \fB9.762 KiB\fR). For counts the SI standard notation is
used, e.g. prefix \fBk\fR for kilo. Used with file counts, \fB1k\fR means 1000 files.

.PP
The various list
\[la]//commands/zclone_ls/\[ra] commands output raw numbers by default.
Option \fB--human-readable\fR will make them output values in human-readable format
instead (with the short unit prefix).

.PP
The about
\[la]//commands/zclone_about/\[ra] command outputs human-readable by default,
with a command-specific option \fB--full\fR to output the raw numbers instead.

.PP
Command size
\[la]//commands/zclone_size/\[ra] outputs both human-readable and raw numbers
in the same output.

.PP
The tree
\[la]//commands/zclone_tree/\[ra] command also considers \fB--human-readable\fR, but
it will not use the exact same notation as the other commands: It rounds to one
decimal, and uses single letter suffix, e.g. \fBK\fR instead of \fBKi\fR\&. The reason for
this is that it relies on an external library.

.PP
The interactive command ncdu
\[la]//commands/zclone_ncdu/\[ra] shows human-readable by
default, and responds to key \fBu\fR for toggling human-readable format.

.SS --ignore-case-sync
Using this option will cause zclone to ignore the case of the files
when synchronizing so files will not be copied/synced when the
existing filenames are the same, even if the casing is different.

.SS --ignore-checksum
Normally zclone will check that the checksums of transferred files
match, and give an error "corrupted on transfer" if they don't.

.PP
You can use this option to skip that check.  You should only use it if
you have had the "corrupted on transfer" error message and you are
sure you might want to transfer potentially corrupted data.

.SS --ignore-existing
Using this option will make zclone unconditionally skip all files
that exist on the destination, no matter the content of these files.

.PP
While this isn't a generally recommended option, it can be useful
in cases where your files change due to encryption. However, it cannot
correct partial transfers in case a transfer was interrupted.

.PP
When performing a \fBmove\fR/\fBmoveto\fR command, this flag will leave skipped
files in the source location unchanged when a file with the same name
exists on the destination.

.SS --ignore-size
Normally zclone will look at modification time and size of files to
see if they are equal.  If you set this flag then zclone will check
only the modification time.  If \fB--checksum\fR is set then it only
checks the checksum.

.PP
It will also cause zclone to skip verifying the sizes are the same
after transfer.

.PP
This can be useful for transferring files to and from OneDrive which
occasionally misreports the size of image files (see
#399
\[la]/\[ra] for more info).

.SS -I, --ignore-times
Using this option will cause zclone to unconditionally upload all
files regardless of the state of files on the destination.

.PP
Normally zclone would skip any files that have the same
modification time and are the same size (or have the same checksum if
using \fB--checksum\fR).

.SS --immutable
Treat source and destination files as immutable and disallow
modification.

.PP
With this option set, files will be created and deleted as requested,
but existing files will never be updated.  If an existing file does
not match between the source and destination, zclone will give the error
\fBSource and destination exist but do not match: immutable file modified\fR\&.

.PP
Note that only commands which transfer files (e.g. sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra],
copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra]) are affected
by this behavior, and only modification is disallowed.  Files may still be deleted
explicitly (e.g. delete
\[la]//commands/zclone_delete/\[ra], purge
\[la]//commands/zclone_purge/\[ra])
or implicitly (e.g. sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra]).
Use \fBcopy --immutable\fR if it is desired to avoid deletion as well as modification.

.PP
This can be useful as an additional layer of protection for immutable
or append-only data sets (notably backup archives), where modification
implies corruption and should not be propagated.

.SS --inplace {#inplace}
The \fB--inplace\fR flag changes the behaviour of zclone when uploading
files to some backends (backends with the \fBPartialUploads\fR feature
flag set) such as:
.IP \(bu 2
local
.IP \(bu 2
ftp
.IP \(bu 2
sftp
.IP \(bu 2
pcloud

.PP
Without \fB--inplace\fR (the default) zclone will first upload to a
temporary file with an extension like this, where \fBXXXXXX\fR represents a
hash of the source file's fingerprint and \fB\&.partial\fR is
--partial-suffix
\[la]#partial\-suffix\[ra] value (\fB\&.partial\fR by default).

.EX
original-file-name.XXXXXX.partial
.EE

.PP
(zclone will make sure the final name is no longer than 100 characters
by truncating the \fBoriginal-file-name\fR part if necessary).

.PP
When the upload is complete, zclone will rename the \fB\&.partial\fR file to
the correct name, overwriting any existing file at that point. If the
upload fails then the \fB\&.partial\fR file will be deleted.

.PP
This prevents other users of the backend from seeing partially
uploaded files in their new names and prevents overwriting the old
file until the new one is completely uploaded.

.PP
If the \fB--inplace\fR flag is supplied, zclone will upload directly to
the final name without creating a \fB\&.partial\fR file.

.PP
This means that an incomplete file will be visible in the directory
listings while the upload is in progress and any existing files will
be overwritten as soon as the upload starts. If the transfer fails
then the file will be deleted. This can cause data loss of the
existing file if the transfer fails.

.PP
Note that on the local file system if you don't use \fB--inplace\fR hard
links (Unix only) will be broken. And if you do use \fB--inplace\fR you
won't be able to update in use executables.

.PP
Note also that versions of zclone prior to v1.63.0 behave as if the
\fB--inplace\fR flag is always supplied.

.SS -i, --interactive {#interactive}
This flag can be used to tell zclone that you wish a manual
confirmation before destructive operations.

.PP
It is \fBrecommended\fP that you use this flag while learning zclone
especially with \fBzclone sync\fR\&.

.PP
For example

.EX
$ zclone delete --interactive /tmp/dir
zclone: delete "important-file.txt"?
y) Yes, this is OK (default)
n) No, skip this
s) Skip all delete operations with no more questions
!) Do all delete operations with no more questions
q) Exit zclone now.
y/n/s/!/q> n
.EE

.PP
The options mean
.IP \(bu 2
\fBy\fR: \fBYes\fP, this operation should go ahead. You can also press Return
for this to happen. You'll be asked every time unless you choose \fBs\fR
or \fB!\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBn\fR: \fBNo\fP, do not do this operation. You'll be asked every time unless
you choose \fBs\fR or \fB!\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBs\fR: \fBSkip\fP all the following operations of this type with no more
questions. This takes effect until zclone exits. If there are any
different kind of operations you'll be prompted for them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB!\fR: \fBDo all\fP the following operations with no more
questions. Useful if you've decided that you don't mind zclone doing
that kind of operation. This takes effect until zclone exits . If
there are any different kind of operations you'll be prompted for
them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBq\fR: \fBQuit\fP zclone now, just in case!

.SS --leave-root
During rmdirs it will not remove root directory, even if it's empty.

.SS -l, --links
Normally zclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows). Ignored files won't be copied, moved or
deleted in a sync.

.PP
If you supply this flag then zclone will copy symbolic links from any
supported backend backend, and store them as text files, with a
\fB\&.zclonelink\fR suffix in the destination.

.PP
The text file will contain the target of the symbolic link.

.PP
The \fB--links\fR / \fB-l\fR flag enables this feature for all supported
backends and the VFS. There are individual flags for just enabling it
for the VFS \fB--vfs-links\fR and the local backend \fB--local-links\fR if
required.

.SS --list-cutoff int {#list-cutoff}
When syncing zclone needs to sort directory entries before comparing
them. Below this threshold (1,000,000) by default, zclone will store
the directory entries in memory. 1,000,000 entries will take approx
1GB of RAM to store. Above this threshold zclone will store directory
entries on disk and sort them without using a lot of memory.

.PP
Doing this is slightly less efficient then sorting them in memory and
will only work well for the bucket based backends (eg s3, b2,
azureblob, swift) but these are the only backends likely to have
millions of entries in a directory.

.SS --log-file string
Log all of zclone's output to a file. This is not active by default.
This can be useful for tracking down problems with syncs in
combination with the \fB-v\fR flag.  See the logging
\[la]#logging\[ra] section
for more info.

.PP
If the file exists, then zclone will append to it.

.PP
Note that if you are using the \fBlogrotate\fR program to manage zclone's
logs, then you should use the \fBcopytruncate\fR option as zclone doesn't
have a signal to rotate logs.

.PP
Alternatively you can use the options below to manage zclone's built
in log rotation.

.SS --log-file-max-size SizeSuffix
Maximum size of the log file before it's rotated (eg \fB10M\fR). This SizeSuffix
is rounded to the nearest MiB or 1 MiB if lower.

.PP
If \fB--log-file\fR is not set then this option will be ignored.

.PP
If this option is not set, then the other log rotation options will be
ignored.

.PP
For example if the following flags are in use

.EX
zclone --log-file zclone.log --log-file-max-size 1M --log-file-max-backups 3
.EE

.PP
Then this will create log files which look like this

.EX
$ ls -l
-rw-------  1 user user  1048491 Apr 11 17:15 zclone-2025-04-11T17-15-29.998.log
-rw-------  1 user user  1048511 Apr 11 17:15 zclone-2025-04-11T17-15-30.467.log
-rw-------  1 user user  1048559 Apr 11 17:15 zclone-2025-04-11T17-15-30.543.log
-rw-------  1 user user   521602 Apr 11 17:15 zclone.log
.EE

.PP
The file \fBzclone.log\fR being the current one.

.SS --log-file-compress
If set, compress rotated log files using gzip. This changes the
extension of the old log files to \fB\&.log.gz\fR\&.

.PP
Defaults to false - don't compress log files.

.SS --log-file-max-age Duration
Maximum duration to retain old log files (eg \fB7d\fR). This is rounded to
the dearest day, or 1 day if lower.

.PP
The default is to retain all old log files.

.SS --log-file-max-backups int
Maximum number of old log files to retain

.PP
The default is to retain all old log files.

.SS --log-format string
Comma separated list of log format options. The accepted options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdate\fR - Add a date in the format YYYY/MM/YY to the log.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtime\fR - Add a time to the log in format HH:MM:SS.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmicroseconds\fR - Add microseconds to the time in format HH:MM:SS.SSSSSS.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUTC\fR - Make the logs in UTC not localtime.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlongfile\fR - Adds the source file and line number of the log statement.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshortfile\fR - Adds the source file and line number of the log statement.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpid\fR - Add the process ID to the log - useful with \fBzclone mount --daemon\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnolevel\fR - Don't add the level to the log.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBjson\fR - Equivalent to adding \fB--use-json-log\fR

.PP
They are added to the log line in the order above.

.PP
The default log format is \fB"date,time"\fR\&.

.SS --log-level LogLevel
This sets the log level for zclone.  The default log level is \fBNOTICE\fR\&.

.PP
\fBDEBUG\fR is equivalent to \fB-vv\fR\&. It outputs lots of debug info - useful
for bug reports and really finding out what zclone is doing.

.PP
\fBINFO\fR is equivalent to \fB-v\fR\&. It outputs information about each transfer
and prints stats once a minute by default.

.PP
\fBNOTICE\fR is the default log level if no logging flags are supplied. It
outputs very little when things are working normally. It outputs
warnings and significant events.

.PP
\fBERROR\fR is equivalent to \fB-q\fR\&. It only outputs error messages.

.PP
See also the logging
\[la]#logging\[ra] section.

.SS --windows-event-log-level LogLevel
If this is configured (the default is \fBOFF\fR) then logs of this level
and above will be logged to the Windows event log in \fBaddition\fP to
the normal logs. These will be logged in JSON format as described
below regardless of what format the main logs are configured for.

.PP
The Windows event log only has 3 levels of severity \fBInfo\fR, \fBWarning\fR
and \fBError\fR\&. If enabled we map zclone levels like this.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBError\fR ← \fBERROR\fR (and above)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWarning\fR ←  \fBWARNING\fR (note that this level is defined but not currently used).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInfo\fR ← \fBNOTICE\fR, \fBINFO\fR and \fBDEBUG\fR\&.

.PP
Zclone will declare its log source as "zclone" if it is has enough
permissions to create the registry key needed. If not then logs will
appear as "Application". You can run \fBzclone version --windows-event-log-level DEBUG\fR
once as administrator to create the registry key in advance.

.PP
\fBNote\fP that the \fB--windows-event-log-level\fR level must be greater (more
severe) than or equal to the \fB--log-level\fR\&. For example to log DEBUG
to a log file but ERRORs to the event log you would use

.EX
--log-file zclone.log --log-level DEBUG --windows-event-log-level ERROR
.EE

.PP
This option is only supported Windows platforms.

.SS --use-json-log
This switches the log format to JSON. The log messages are then
streamed as individual JSON objects, with fields: \fBlevel\fR, \fBmsg\fR, \fBsource\fR,
and \fBtime\fR\&. The resulting format is what is sometimes referred to as
newline-delimited JSON
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON_streaming#Newline\-delimited_JSON\[ra]
(NDJSON), or JSON Lines (JSONL). This is well suited for processing by
traditional line-oriented tools and shell pipelines, but a complete log
file is not strictly valid JSON and needs a parser that can handle it.

.PP
The JSON logs will be printed on a single line, but are shown expanded
here for clarity.

.EX
{
  "time": "2025-05-13T17:30:51.036237518+01:00",
  "level": "debug",
  "msg": "4 go routines active\\n",
  "source": "cmd/cmd.go:298"
}
.EE

.PP
Completed data transfer logs will have extra \fBsize\fR information. Logs
which are about a particular object will have \fBobject\fR and
\fBobjectType\fR fields also.

.EX
{
  "time": "2025-05-13T17:38:05.540846352+01:00",
  "level": "info",
  "msg": "Copied (new) to: file2.txt",
  "size": 6,
  "object": "file.txt",
  "objectType": "*local.Object",
  "source": "operations/copy.go:368"
}
.EE

.PP
Stats logs will contain a \fBstats\fR field which is the same as
returned from the rc call core/stats
\[la]//rc/#core\-stats\[ra]\&.

.EX
{
  "time": "2025-05-13T17:38:05.540912847+01:00",
  "level": "info",
  "msg": "...text version of the stats...",
  "stats": {
    "bytes": 6,
    "checks": 0,
    "deletedDirs": 0,
    "deletes": 0,
    "elapsedTime": 0.000904825,
    ...truncated for clarity...
    "totalBytes": 6,
    "totalChecks": 0,
    "totalTransfers": 1,
    "transferTime": 0.000882794,
    "transfers": 1
  },
  "source": "accounting/stats.go:569"
}
.EE

.SS --low-level-retries int
This controls the number of low level retries zclone does.

.PP
A low level retry is used to retry a failing operation - typically one
HTTP request.  This might be uploading a chunk of a big file for
example.  You will see low level retries in the log with the \fB-v\fR
flag.

.PP
This shouldn't need to be changed from the default in normal operations.
However, if you get a lot of low level retries you may wish
to reduce the value so zclone moves on to a high level retry (see the
\fB--retries\fR flag) quicker.

.PP
Disable low level retries with \fB--low-level-retries 1\fR\&.

.SS --max-backlog int
This is the maximum allowable backlog of files in a sync/copy/move
queued for being checked or transferred.

.PP
This can be set arbitrarily large.  It will only use memory when the
queue is in use.  Note that it will use in the order of N KiB of memory
when the backlog is in use.

.PP
Setting this large allows zclone to calculate how many files are
pending more accurately, give a more accurate estimated finish
time and make \fB--order-by\fR work more accurately.

.PP
Setting this small will make zclone more synchronous to the listings
of the remote which may be desirable.

.PP
Setting this to a negative number will make the backlog as large as
possible.

.SS --max-buffer-memory SizeSuffix {#max-buffer-memory}
If set, don't allocate more than given amount of memory as buffers. If
not set or set to \fB0\fR or \fBoff\fR this will not limit the amount of memory
in use.

.PP
This includes memory used by buffers created by the \fB--buffer\fR flag
and buffers used by multi-thread transfers.

.PP
Most multi-thread transfers do not take additional memory, but some do
depending on the backend (eg the s3 backend for uploads). This means
there is a tension between total setting \fB--transfers\fR as high as
possible and memory use.

.PP
Setting \fB--max-buffer-memory\fR allows the buffer memory to be
controlled so that it doesn't overwhelm the machine and allows
\fB--transfers\fR to be set large.

.SS --max-connections int
This sets the maximum number of concurrent calls to the backend API.
It may not map 1:1 to TCP or HTTP connections depending on the backend
in use and the use of HTTP1 vs HTTP2.

.PP
When downloading files, backends only limit the initial opening of the
stream. The bulk data download is not counted as a connection. This
means that the \fB--max--connections\fR flag won't limit the total number
of downloads.

.PP
Note that it is possible to cause deadlocks with this setting so it
should be used with care.

.PP
If you are doing a sync or copy then make sure \fB--max-connections\fR is
one more than the sum of \fB--transfers\fR and \fB--checkers\fR\&.

.PP
If you use \fB--check-first\fR then \fB--max-connections\fR just needs to be
one more than the maximum of \fB--checkers\fR and \fB--transfers\fR\&.

.PP
So for  \fB--max-connections 3\fR you'd use \fB--checkers 2 --transfers 2
--check-first\fR or \fB--checkers 1 --transfers 1\fR\&.

.PP
Setting this flag can be useful for backends which do multipart
uploads to limit the number of simultaneous parts being transferred.

.SS --max-delete int
This tells zclone not to delete more than N files.  If that limit is
exceeded then a fatal error will be generated and zclone will stop the
operation in progress.

.SS --max-delete-size SizeSuffix
Zclone will stop deleting files when the total size of deletions has
reached the size specified. It defaults to off.

.PP
If that limit is exceeded then a fatal error will be generated and
zclone will stop the operation in progress.

.SS --max-depth int
This modifies the recursion depth for all the commands except purge.

.PP
So if you do \fBzclone --max-depth 1 ls remote:path\fR you will see only
the files in the top level directory.  Using \fB--max-depth 2\fR means you
will see all the files in first two directory levels and so on.

.PP
For historical reasons the \fBlsd\fR command defaults to using a
\fB--max-depth\fR of 1 - you can override this with the command line flag.

.PP
You can use this command to disable recursion (with \fB--max-depth 1\fR).

.PP
Note that if you use this with \fBsync\fR and \fB--delete-excluded\fR the
files not recursed through are considered excluded and will be deleted
on the destination.  Test first with \fB--dry-run\fR if you are not sure
what will happen.

.SS --max-duration Duration
Zclone will stop transferring when it has run for the
duration specified.
Defaults to off.

.PP
When the limit is reached all transfers will stop immediately.
Use \fB--cutoff-mode\fR to modify this behaviour.

.PP
Zclone will exit with exit code 10 if the duration limit is reached.

.SS --max-transfer SizeSuffix
Zclone will stop transferring when it has reached the size specified.
Defaults to off.

.PP
When the limit is reached all transfers will stop immediately.
Use \fB--cutoff-mode\fR to modify this behaviour.

.PP
Zclone will exit with exit code 8 if the transfer limit is reached.

.SS --cutoff-mode HARD|SOFT|CAUTIOUS
Configure the behavior of \fB--max-transfer\fR and \fB--max-duration\fR\&.

.PP
\fBHARD\fR will stop transferring immediately when zclone reaches the limit.
This is the default.

.PP
\fBSOFT\fR will stop starting new transfers when zclone reaches the limit.

.PP
\fBCAUTIOUS\fR will try to prevent zclone from reaching the limit. Only applicable
for \fB--max-transfer\fR\&.

.SS -M, --metadata
Setting this flag enables zclone to copy the metadata from the source
to the destination. For local backends this is ownership, permissions,
xattr etc. See the metadata section
\[la]#metadata\[ra] for more info.

.SS --metadata-mapper SpaceSepList {#metadata-mapper}
If you supply the parameter \fB--metadata-mapper /path/to/program\fR then
zclone will use that program to map metadata from source object to
destination object.

.PP
The argument to this flag should be a command with an optional space separated
list of arguments. If one of the arguments has a space in then enclose
it in \fB"\fR, if you want a literal \fB"\fR in an argument then enclose the
argument in \fB"\fR and double the \fB"\fR\&. See CSV encoding
\[la]https://godoc.org/encoding/csv\[ra]
for more info.

.EX
--metadata-mapper "python bin/test_metadata_mapper.py"
--metadata-mapper 'python bin/test_metadata_mapper.py "argument with a space"'
--metadata-mapper 'python bin/test_metadata_mapper.py "argument with ""two"" quotes"'
.EE

.PP
This uses a simple JSON based protocol with input on STDIN and output
on STDOUT. This will be called for every file and directory copied and
may be called concurrently.

.PP
The program's job is to take a metadata blob on the input and turn it
into a metadata blob on the output suitable for the destination
backend.

.PP
Input to the program (via STDIN) might look like this. This provides
some context for the \fBMetadata\fR which may be important.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSrcFs\fR is the config string for the remote that the object is currently on.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSrcFsType\fR is the name of the source backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDstFs\fR is the config string for the remote that the object is being copied to
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDstFsType\fR is the name of the destination backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRemote\fR is the path of the object relative to the root.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSize\fR, \fBMimeType\fR, \fBModTime\fR are attributes of the object.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIsDir\fR is \fBtrue\fR if this is a directory (not yet implemented).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBID\fR is the source \fBID\fR of the object if known.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMetadata\fR is the backend specific metadata as described in the backend docs.

.EX
{
  "SrcFs": "gdrive:",
  "SrcFsType": "drive",
  "DstFs": "newdrive:user",
  "DstFsType": "onedrive",
  "Remote": "test.txt",
  "Size": 6,
  "MimeType": "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
  "ModTime": "2022-10-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00",
  "IsDir": false,
  "ID": "xyz",
  "Metadata": {
    "btime": "2022-10-11T16:53:11Z",
    "content-type": "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
    "mtime": "2022-10-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00",
    "owner": "user1@domain1.com",
    "permissions": "...",
    "description": "my nice file",
    "starred": "false"
  }
}
.EE

.PP
The program should then modify the input as desired and send it to
STDOUT. The returned \fBMetadata\fR field will be used in its entirety for
the destination object. Any other fields will be ignored. Note in this
example we translate user names and permissions and add something to
the description:

.EX
{
  "Metadata": {
    "btime": "2022-10-11T16:53:11Z",
    "content-type": "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
    "mtime": "2022-10-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00",
    "owner": "user1@domain2.com",
    "permissions": "...",
    "description": "my nice file [migrated from domain1]",
    "starred": "false"
  }
}
.EE

.PP
Metadata can be removed here too.

.PP
An example python program might look something like this to implement
the above transformations.

.EX
import sys, json

i = json.load(sys.stdin)
metadata = i["Metadata"]
# Add tag to description
if "description" in metadata:
    metadata["description"] += " [migrated from domain1]"
else:
    metadata["description"] = "[migrated from domain1]"
# Modify owner
if "owner" in metadata:
    metadata["owner"] = metadata["owner"].replace("domain1.com", "domain2.com")
o = { "Metadata": metadata }
json.dump(o, sys.stdout, indent="\\t")
.EE

.PP
You can find this example (slightly expanded) in the zclone source code at
bin/test_metadata_mapper.py
\[la]/\[ra]\&.

.PP
If you want to see the input to the metadata mapper and the output
returned from it in the log you can use \fB-vv --dump mapper\fR\&.

.PP
See the metadata section
\[la]#metadata\[ra] for more info.

.SS --metadata-set stringArray
Specify value as string in format \fBkey=value\fR to add metadata \fBkey\fR
with value \fBvalue\fR when uploading. This can be repeated as many times
as required. See the metadata section
\[la]#metadata\[ra] for more info.

.SS --modify-window Duration
When checking whether a file has been modified, this is the maximum
allowed time difference that a file can have and still be considered
equivalent.

.PP
The default is \fB1ns\fR unless this is overridden by a remote.  For
example OS X only stores modification times to the nearest second so
if you are reading and writing to an OS X filing system this will be
\fB1s\fR by default.

.PP
This command line flag allows you to override that computed default.

.SS --multi-thread-write-buffer-size SizeSuffix
When transferring with multiple threads, zclone will buffer the specified
number of bytes in memory before writing to disk for each thread.

.PP
This can improve performance if the underlying filesystem does not deal
well with a lot of small writes in different positions of the file, so
if you see transfers being limited by disk write speed, you might want
to experiment with different values. Specially for magnetic drives and
remote file systems a higher value can be useful.

.PP
Nevertheless, the default of \fB128k\fR should be fine for almost all use
cases, so before changing it ensure that network is not really your
bottleneck.

.PP
As a final hint, size is not the only factor: block size (or similar
concept) can have an impact. In one case, we observed that exact
multiples of 16k performed much better than other values.

.SS --multi-thread-chunk-size SizeSuffix
Normally the chunk size for multi thread transfers is set by the backend.
However some backends such as \fBlocal\fR and \fBsmb\fR (which implement \fBOpenWriterAt\fR
but not \fBOpenChunkWriter\fR) don't have a natural chunk size.

.PP
In this case the value of this option is used (default 64Mi).

.SS --multi-thread-cutoff SizeSuffix {#multi-thread-cutoff}
When transferring files above specified size  to capable backends,
zclone will use multiple threads to transfer the file (default 256M).

.PP
Capable backends are marked in the
overview
\[la]//overview/#optional\-features\[ra] as \fBMultithreadUpload\fR\&. (They
need to implement either the \fBOpenWriterAt\fR or \fBOpenChunkWriter\fR
internal interfaces). These include include, \fBlocal\fR, \fBs3\fR,
\fBazureblob\fR, \fBb2\fR, \fBoracleobjectstorage\fR and \fBsmb\fR at the time of
writing.

.PP
On the local disk, zclone preallocates the file (using
\fBfallocate(FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE)\fR on unix or \fBNTSetInformationFile\fR on
Windows both of which takes no time) then each thread writes directly
into the file at the correct place. This means that zclone won't
create fragmented or sparse files and there won't be any assembly time
at the end of the transfer.

.PP
The number of threads used to transfer is controlled by
\fB--multi-thread-streams\fR\&.

.PP
Use \fB-vv\fR if you wish to see info about the threads.

.PP
This will work with the sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra]
and move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra] commands, and friends
copyto
\[la]//commands/zclone_copyto/\[ra], moveto
\[la]//commands/zclone_moveto/\[ra]\&.
Multi thread transfers will be used with \fBzclone mount\fR and \fBzclone serve\fR
if \fB--vfs-cache-mode\fR is set to \fBwrites\fR or above.

.PP
Most multi-thread transfers do not take additional memory, but some do
(for example uploading to s3). In the worst case memory usage can be
at maximum \fB--transfers\fR * \fB--multi-thread-chunk-size\fR *
\fB--multi-thread-streams\fR or specifically for the s3 backend
\fB--transfers\fR * \fB--s3-chunk-size\fR * \fB--s3-concurrency\fR\&. However you
can use the --max-buffer-memory
\[la]//docs/#max\-buffer\-memory\[ra] flag
to control the maximum memory used here.

.PP
\fBNB\fP that this \fBonly\fP works with supported backends as the
destination but will work with any backend as the source.

.PP
\fBNB\fP that multi-thread copies are disabled for local to local copies
as they are faster without unless \fB--multi-thread-streams\fR is set
explicitly.

.PP
\fBNB\fP on Windows using multi-thread transfers to the local disk will
cause the resulting files to be sparse
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparse_file\[ra]\&.
Use \fB--local-no-sparse\fR to disable sparse files (which may cause long
delays at the start of transfers) or disable multi-thread transfers
with \fB--multi-thread-streams 0\fR

.SS --multi-thread-streams int
When using multi thread transfers (see above \fB--multi-thread-cutoff\fR)
this sets the number of streams to use. Set to \fB0\fR to disable multi
thread transfers (Default 4).

.PP
If the backend has a \fB--backend-upload-concurrency\fR setting (eg
\fB--s3-upload-concurrency\fR) then this setting will be used as the
number of transfers instead if it is larger than the value of
\fB--multi-thread-streams\fR or \fB--multi-thread-streams\fR isn't set.

.SS --name-transform stringArray
\fB--name-transform\fR introduces path name transformations for
\fBzclone copy\fR, \fBzclone sync\fR, and \fBzclone move\fR\&. These transformations
enable modifications to source and destination file names by applying
prefixes, suffixes, and other alterations during transfer operations.
For detailed docs and examples, see 
\[la]//commands/zclone_convmv/\[ra]\&.

.SS --no-check-dest
The \fB--no-check-dest\fR can be used with \fBmove\fR or \fBcopy\fR and it causes
zclone not to check the destination at all when copying files.

.PP
This means that:
.IP \(bu 2
the destination is not listed minimising the API calls
.IP \(bu 2
files are always transferred
.IP \(bu 2
this can cause duplicates on remotes which allow it (e.g. Google Drive)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--retries 1\fR is recommended otherwise you'll transfer everything again on a retry

.PP
This flag is useful to minimise the transactions if you know that none
of the files are on the destination.

.PP
This is a specialized flag which should be ignored by most users!

.SS --no-gzip-encoding
Don't set \fBAccept-Encoding: gzip\fR\&.  This means that zclone won't ask
the server for compressed files automatically. Useful if you've set
the server to return files with \fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR but you
uploaded compressed files.

.PP
There is no need to set this in normal operation, and doing so will
decrease the network transfer efficiency of zclone.

.SS --no-traverse
The \fB--no-traverse\fR flag controls whether the destination file system
is traversed when using the \fBcopy\fR or \fBmove\fR commands.
\fB--no-traverse\fR is not compatible with \fBsync\fR and will be ignored if
you supply it with \fBsync\fR\&.

.PP
If you are only copying a small number of files (or are filtering most
of the files) and/or have a large number of files on the destination
then \fB--no-traverse\fR will stop zclone listing the destination and save
time.

.PP
However, if you are copying a large number of files, especially if you
are doing a copy where lots of the files under consideration haven't
changed and won't need copying then you shouldn't use \fB--no-traverse\fR\&.

.PP
See zclone copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] for an example of how to use it.

.SS --no-unicode-normalization
Don't normalize unicode characters in filenames during the sync routine.

.PP
Sometimes, an operating system will store filenames containing unicode
parts in their decomposed form (particularly macOS). Some cloud storage
systems will then recompose the unicode, resulting in duplicate files if
the data is ever copied back to a local filesystem.

.PP
Using this flag will disable that functionality, treating each unicode
character as unique. For example, by default é and é will be normalized
into the same character. With \fB--no-unicode-normalization\fR they will be
treated as unique characters.

.SS --no-update-modtime
When using this flag, zclone won't update modification times of remote
files if they are incorrect as it would normally.

.PP
This can be used if the remote is being synced with another tool also
(e.g. the Google Drive client).

.SS --no-update-dir-modtime
When using this flag, zclone won't update modification times of remote
directories if they are incorrect as it would normally.

.SS --order-by string
The \fB--order-by\fR flag controls the order in which files in the backlog
are processed in \fBzclone sync\fR, \fBzclone copy\fR and \fBzclone move\fR\&.

.PP
The order by string is constructed like this.  The first part
describes what aspect is being measured:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsize\fR - order by the size of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fR - order by the full path of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmodtime\fR - order by the modification date of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpattern\fR - order by a list of path glob patterns

.PP
The \fBsize\fR, \fBname\fR and \fBmodtime\fR comparisons can have a modifier appended with
a comma:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBascending\fR or \fBasc\fR - order so that the smallest (or oldest) is processed first
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdescending\fR or \fBdesc\fR - order so that the largest (or newest) is processed first
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmixed\fR - order so that the smallest is processed first for some threads and
the largest for others

.PP
If the modifier is \fBmixed\fR then it can have an optional percentage
(which defaults to \fB50\fR), e.g. \fBsize,mixed,25\fR which means that 25% of
the threads should be taking the smallest items and 75% the
largest. The threads which take the smallest first will always take
the smallest first and likewise the largest first threads. The \fBmixed\fR
mode can be useful to minimise the transfer time when you are
transferring a mixture of large and small files - the large files are
guaranteed upload threads and bandwidth and the small files will be
processed continuously.

.PP
If no modifier is supplied then the order is \fBascending\fR\&.

.PP
For \fBpattern\fR, append one or more comma-separated patterns. Patterns use the
filter pattern syntax
\[la]//filtering/#patterns\[ra]\&. Files matching the first pattern
are processed first, followed by files matching the second pattern, and so on.
Files which do not match a pattern are processed last. Within each group, files
are ordered by full path. Patterns cannot contain a comma.

.PP
For example
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--order-by size,desc\fR - send the largest files first
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--order-by modtime,ascending\fR - send the oldest files first
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--order-by name\fR - send the files with alphabetically by path first
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--order-by 'pattern,*.tar.gz,*.md5'\fR - send archives before checksum files

.PP
If the \fB--order-by\fR flag is not supplied or it is supplied with an
empty string then the default ordering will be used which is as
scanned.  With \fB--checkers 1\fR this is mostly alphabetical, however
with the default \fB--checkers 8\fR it is somewhat random.

.SS Limitations
The \fB--order-by\fR flag does not do a separate pass over the data.  This
means that it may transfer some files out of the order specified if
.IP \(bu 2
there are no files in the backlog or the source has not been fully scanned yet
.IP \(bu 2
there are more than --max-backlog
\[la]#max\-backlog\-int\[ra] files in the backlog

.PP
Zclone will do its best to transfer the best file it has so in
practice this should not cause a problem.  Think of \fB--order-by\fR as
being more of a best efforts flag rather than a perfect ordering.

.PP
If you want perfect ordering then you will need to specify
--check-first
\[la]#check\-first\[ra] which will find all the files which need
transferring first before transferring any.

.SS --partial-suffix string {#partial-suffix}
When --inplace
\[la]#inplace\[ra] is not used, it causes zclone to use
the \fB--partial-suffix\fR as suffix for temporary files.

.PP
Suffix length limit is 16 characters.

.PP
The default is \fB\&.partial\fR\&.

.SS --password-command SpaceSepList {#password-command}
This flag supplies a program which should supply the config password
when run. This is an alternative to zclone prompting for the password
or setting the \fBZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\fR variable. It is also used when
setting the config password for the first time.

.PP
The argument to this should be a command with a space separated list
of arguments. If one of the arguments has a space in then enclose it
in \fB"\fR, if you want a literal \fB"\fR in an argument then enclose the
argument in \fB"\fR and double the \fB"\fR\&. See CSV encoding
\[la]https://godoc.org/encoding/csv\[ra]
for more info.

.PP
Eg

.EX
--password-command "echo hello"
--password-command 'echo "hello with space"'
--password-command 'echo "hello with ""quotes"" and space"'
.EE

.PP
Note that when changing the configuration password the environment
variable \fBZCLONE_PASSWORD_CHANGE=1\fR will be set. This can be used to
distinguish initial decryption of the config file from the new
password.

.PP
See the Configuration Encryption
\[la]#configuration\-encryption\[ra] for more info.

.PP
See a Windows PowerShell example on the Wiki
\[la]/\[ra]\&.

.SS -P, --progress
This flag makes zclone update the stats in a static block in the
terminal providing a realtime overview of the transfer.

.PP
Any log messages will scroll above the static block.  Log messages
will push the static block down to the bottom of the terminal where it
will stay.

.PP
Normally this is updated every 500mS but this period can be overridden
with the \fB--stats\fR flag.

.PP
This can be used with the \fB--stats-one-line\fR flag for a simpler
display.

.PP
To change the display length of filenames (for different terminal widths),
see the \fB--stats-file-name-length\fR option.  The default output is formatted
for 80 character wide terminals.

.PP
Note: On Windows until this bug
\[la]https://github.com/Azure/go\-ansiterm/issues/26\[ra]
is fixed all non-ASCII characters will be replaced with \fB\&.\fR when
\fB--progress\fR is in use.

.SS --progress-terminal-title
This flag, when used with \fB-P/--progress\fR, will print the string \fBETA: %s\fR
to the terminal title.

.SS -q, --quiet
This flag will limit zclone's output to error messages only.

.SS --refresh-times
The \fB--refresh-times\fR flag can be used to update modification times of
existing files when they are out of sync on backends which don't
support hashes.

.PP
This is useful if you uploaded files with the incorrect timestamps and
you now wish to correct them.

.PP
This flag is \fBonly\fP useful for destinations which don't support
hashes (e.g. \fBcrypt\fR).

.PP
This can be used any of the sync commands sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra],
copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra]\&.

.PP
To use this flag you will need to be doing a modification time sync
(so not using \fB--size-only\fR or \fB--checksum\fR). The flag will have no
effect when using \fB--size-only\fR or \fB--checksum\fR\&.

.PP
If this flag is used when zclone comes to upload a file it will check
to see if there is an existing file on the destination. If this file
matches the source with size (and checksum if available) but has a
differing timestamp then instead of re-uploading it, zclone will
update the timestamp on the destination file. If the checksum does not
match zclone will upload the new file. If the checksum is absent (e.g.
on a \fBcrypt\fR backend) then zclone will update the timestamp.

.PP
Note that some remotes can't set the modification time without
re-uploading the file so this flag is less useful on them.

.PP
Normally if you are doing a modification time sync zclone will update
modification times without \fB--refresh-times\fR provided that the remote
supports checksums \fBand\fP the checksums match on the file. However if the
checksums are absent then zclone will upload the file rather than
setting the timestamp as this is the safe behaviour.

.SS --retries int
Retry the entire sync if it fails this many times it fails (default 3).

.PP
Some remotes can be unreliable and a few retries help pick up the
files which didn't get transferred because of errors.

.PP
Disable retries with \fB--retries 1\fR\&.

.SS --retries-sleep Duration
This sets the interval between each retry specified by \fB--retries\fR

.PP
The default is \fB0\fR\&. Use \fB0\fR to disable.

.SS --server-side-across-configs
Allow server-side operations (e.g. copy or move) to work across
different configurations.

.PP
This can be useful if you wish to do a server-side copy or move
between two remotes which use the same backend but are configured
differently.

.PP
Note that this isn't enabled by default because it isn't easy for
zclone to tell if it will work between any two configurations.

.SS --size-only
Normally zclone will look at modification time and size of files to
see if they are equal.  If you set this flag then zclone will check
only the size.

.PP
This can be useful transferring files from Dropbox which have been
modified by the desktop sync client which doesn't set checksums of
modification times in the same way as zclone.

.SS --stats Duration
Commands which transfer data
(sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra],
copyto
\[la]//commands/zclone_copyto/\[ra], move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra],
moveto
\[la]//commands/zclone_moveto/\[ra]) will print data transfer stats at
regular intervals to show their progress.

.PP
This sets the interval.

.PP
The default is \fB1m\fR\&. Use \fB0\fR to disable.

.PP
If you set the stats interval then all commands can show stats.  This
can be useful when running other commands, \fBcheck\fR or \fBmount\fR for
example.

.PP
Stats are logged at \fBINFO\fR level by default which means they won't
show at default log level \fBNOTICE\fR\&.  Use \fB--stats-log-level NOTICE\fR or
\fB-v\fR to make them show.  See the logging
\[la]#logging\[ra] section for more
info on log levels.

.PP
Note that on macOS you can send a SIGINFO (which is normally ctrl-T in
the terminal) to make the stats print immediately.

.SS --stats-file-name-length int
By default, the \fB--stats\fR output will truncate file names and paths longer
than 40 characters.  This is equivalent to providing
\fB--stats-file-name-length 40\fR\&. Use \fB--stats-file-name-length 0\fR to disable
any truncation of file names printed by stats.

.SS --stats-log-level LogLevel
Log level to show \fB--stats\fR output at.  This can be \fBDEBUG\fR, \fBINFO\fR,
\fBNOTICE\fR, or \fBERROR\fR\&.  The default is \fBINFO\fR\&.  This means at the
default level of logging, which is \fBNOTICE\fR, the stats won't show - if
you want them to then use \fB--stats-log-level NOTICE\fR\&.  See the
logging
\[la]#logging\[ra] section for more details on log levels.

.SS --stats-one-line
When this is specified, zclone condenses the stats into a single line
showing the most important stats only.

.SS --stats-one-line-date
When this is specified, zclone enables the single-line stats and prepends

the display with a date string. The default is \fB2006/01/02 15:04:05 -\fR

.SS --stats-one-line-date-format string
When this is specified, zclone enables the single-line stats and prepends
the display with a user-supplied date string. The date string MUST be
enclosed in quotes. Follow golang specs
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/time/#Time.Format\[ra]
for date formatting syntax.

.SS --stats-unit string
By default, data transfer rates will be printed in bytes per second,
corresponding to \fB--stats-unit bytes\fR\&.

.PP
This option allows the data rate to be printed in bits per second,
by specifying \fB--stats-unit bits\fR\&. Data transfer volume will still be
reported in bytes.

.PP
The rate is reported as a binary unit, not SI unit. So 1 Mbit/s
equals 1,048,576 bit/s and not 1,000,000 bit/s.

.SS --suffix string
When using sync
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra], copy
\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra] or
move
\[la]//commands/zclone_move/\[ra] any files which would have been
overwritten or deleted will have the suffix added to them.  If there
is a file with the same path (after the suffix has been added), then
it will be overwritten.

.PP
The remote in use must support server-side move or copy and you must
use the same remote as the destination of the sync.

.PP
This is for use with files to add the suffix in the current directory
or with \fB--backup-dir\fR\&. See \fB--backup-dir\fR for more info.

.PP
For example

.EX
zclone copy --interactive /path/to/local/file remote:current --suffix .bak
.EE

.PP
will copy \fB/path/to/local\fR to \fBremote:current\fR, but for any files
which would have been updated or deleted have .bak added.

.PP
If using \fBzclone sync\fR with \fB--suffix\fR and without \fB--backup-dir\fR then
it is recommended to put a filter rule in excluding the suffix
otherwise the \fBsync\fR will delete the backup files.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /path/to/local/file remote:current --suffix .bak --exclude "*.bak"
.EE

.SS --suffix-keep-extension
When using \fB--suffix\fR, setting this causes zclone put the SUFFIX
before the extension of the files that it backs up rather than after.

.PP
So let's say we had \fB--suffix -2019-01-01\fR, without the flag \fBfile.txt\fR
would be backed up to \fBfile.txt-2019-01-01\fR and with the flag it would
be backed up to \fBfile-2019-01-01.txt\fR\&.  This can be helpful to make
sure the suffixed files can still be opened.

.PP
If a file has two (or more) extensions and the second (or subsequent)
extension is recognised as a valid mime type, then the suffix will go
before that extension. So \fBfile.tar.gz\fR would be backed up to
\fBfile-2019-01-01.tar.gz\fR whereas \fBfile.badextension.gz\fR would be
backed up to \fBfile.badextension-2019-01-01.gz\fR\&.

.SS --syslog
On capable OSes (not Windows or Plan9) send all log output to syslog.

.PP
This can be useful for running zclone in a script or \fBzclone mount\fR\&.

.SS --syslog-facility string
If using \fB--syslog\fR this sets the syslog facility (e.g. \fBKERN\fR, \fBUSER\fR).
See \fBman syslog\fR for a list of possible facilities.  The default
facility is \fBDAEMON\fR\&.

.SS --temp-dir string
Specify the directory zclone will use for temporary files, to override
the default. Make sure the directory exists and have accessible permissions.

.PP
By default the operating system's temp directory will be used:
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix systems, \fB$TMPDIR\fR if non-empty, else \fB/tmp\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows, the first non-empty value from \fB%TMP%\fR, \fB%TEMP%\fR, \fB%USERPROFILE%\fR,
or the Windows directory.

.PP
When overriding the default with this option, the specified path will be
set as value of environment variable \fBTMPDIR\fR on Unix systems
and \fBTMP\fR and \fBTEMP\fR on Windows.

.PP
You can use the config paths
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_paths/\[ra]
command to see the current value.

.SS --tpslimit float
Limit transactions per second to this number. Default is 0 which is
used to mean unlimited transactions per second.

.PP
A transaction is roughly defined as an API call; its exact meaning
will depend on the backend. For HTTP based backends it is an HTTP
PUT/GET/POST/etc and its response. For FTP/SFTP it is a round trip
transaction over TCP.

.PP
For example, to limit zclone to 10 transactions per second use
\fB--tpslimit 10\fR, or to 1 transaction every 2 seconds use \fB--tpslimit
0.5\fR\&.

.PP
Use this when the number of transactions per second from zclone is
causing a problem with the cloud storage provider (e.g. getting you
banned or rate limited).

.PP
This can be very useful for \fBzclone mount\fR to control the behaviour of
applications using it.

.PP
This limit applies to all HTTP based backends and to the FTP and SFTP
backends. It does not apply to the local backend or the Storj backend.

.PP
See also \fB--tpslimit-burst\fR\&.

.SS --tpslimit-burst int
Max burst of transactions for \fB--tpslimit\fR (default \fB1\fR).

.PP
Normally \fB--tpslimit\fR will do exactly the number of transaction per
second specified.  However if you supply \fB--tps-burst\fR then zclone can
save up some transactions from when it was idle giving a burst of up
to the parameter supplied.

.PP
For example if you provide \fB--tpslimit-burst 10\fR then if zclone has
been idle for more than 10*\fB--tpslimit\fR then it can do 10 transactions
very quickly before they are limited again.

.PP
This may be used to increase performance of \fB--tpslimit\fR without
changing the long term average number of transactions per second.

.SS --track-renames
By default, zclone doesn't keep track of renamed files, so if you
rename a file locally then sync it to a remote, zclone will delete the
old file on the remote and upload a new copy.

.PP
An zclone sync with \fB--track-renames\fR runs like a normal sync, but keeps
track of objects which exist in the destination but not in the source
(which would normally be deleted), and which objects exist in the
source but not the destination (which would normally be transferred).
These objects are then candidates for renaming.

.PP
After the sync, zclone matches up the source only and destination only
objects using the \fB--track-renames-strategy\fR specified and either
renames the destination object or transfers the source and deletes the
destination object. \fB--track-renames\fR is stateless like all of
zclone's syncs.

.PP
To use this flag the destination must support server-side copy or
server-side move, and to use a hash based \fB--track-renames-strategy\fR
(the default) the source and the destination must have a compatible
hash.

.PP
If the destination does not support server-side copy or move, zclone
will fall back to the default behaviour and log an error level message
to the console.

.PP
Encrypted destinations are not currently supported by \fB--track-renames\fR
if \fB--track-renames-strategy\fR includes \fBhash\fR\&.

.PP
Note that \fB--track-renames\fR is incompatible with \fB--no-traverse\fR and
that it uses extra memory to keep track of all the rename candidates.

.PP
Note also that \fB--track-renames\fR is incompatible with
\fB--delete-before\fR and will select \fB--delete-after\fR instead of
\fB--delete-during\fR\&.

.SS --track-renames-strategy string
This option changes the file matching criteria for \fB--track-renames\fR\&.

.PP
The matching is controlled by a comma separated selection of these tokens:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmodtime\fR - the modification time of the file - not supported on all backends
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhash\fR - the hash of the file contents - not supported on all backends
.IP \(bu 2
\fBleaf\fR - the name of the file not including its directory name
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsize\fR - the size of the file (this is always enabled)

.PP
The default option is \fBhash\fR\&.

.PP
Using \fB--track-renames-strategy modtime,leaf\fR would match files
based on modification time, the leaf of the file name and the size
only.

.PP
Using \fB--track-renames-strategy modtime\fR or \fBleaf\fR can enable
\fB--track-renames\fR support for encrypted destinations.

.PP
Note that the \fBhash\fR strategy is not supported with encrypted destinations.

.SS --delete-(before,during,after)
This option allows you to specify when files on your destination are
deleted when you sync folders.

.PP
Specifying the value \fB--delete-before\fR will delete all files present
on the destination, but not on the source \fIbefore\fP starting the
transfer of any new or updated files. This uses two passes through the
file systems, one for the deletions and one for the copies.

.PP
Specifying \fB--delete-during\fR will delete files while checking and
uploading files. This is the fastest option and uses the least memory.

.PP
Specifying \fB--delete-after\fR (the default value) will delay deletion of
files until all new/updated files have been successfully transferred.
The files to be deleted are collected in the copy pass then deleted
after the copy pass has completed successfully.  The files to be
deleted are held in memory so this mode may use more memory.  This is
the safest mode as it will only delete files if there have been no
errors subsequent to that.  If there have been errors before the
deletions start then you will get the message \fBnot deleting files as
there were IO errors\fR\&.

.SS --fast-list
When doing anything which involves a directory listing (e.g. \fBsync\fR,
\fBcopy\fR, \fBls\fR - in fact nearly every command), zclone has different
strategies to choose from.

.PP
The basic strategy is to list one directory and processes it before using
more directory lists to process any subdirectories. This is a mandatory
backend feature, called \fBList\fR, which means it is supported by all backends.
This strategy uses small amount of memory, and because it can be parallelised
it is fast for operations involving processing of the list results.

.PP
Some backends provide the support for an alternative strategy, where all
files beneath a directory can be listed in one (or a small number) of
transactions. Zclone supports this alternative strategy through an optional
backend feature called 
\[la]//overview/#listr\[ra]\&. You can see in the storage
system overview documentation's optional features
\[la]//overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
section which backends it is enabled for (these tend to be the bucket-based
ones, e.g. S3, B2, GCS, Swift). This strategy requires fewer transactions
for highly recursive operations, which is important on backends where this
is charged or heavily rate limited. It may be faster (due to fewer transactions)
or slower (because it can't be parallelized) depending on different parameters,
and may require more memory if zclone has to keep the whole listing in memory.

.PP
Which listing strategy zclone picks for a given operation is complicated, but
in general it tries to choose the best possible. It will prefer \fBListR\fR in
situations where it doesn't need to store the listed files in memory, e.g.
for unlimited recursive \fBls\fR command variants. In other situations it will
prefer \fBList\fR, e.g. for \fBsync\fR and \fBcopy\fR, where it needs to keep the listed
files in memory, and is performing operations on them where parallelization
may be a huge advantage.

.PP
Zclone is not able to take all relevant parameters into account for deciding
the best strategy, and therefore allows you to influence the choice in two ways:
You can stop zclone from using \fBListR\fR by disabling the feature, using the
--disable
\[la]#disable\-string\[ra] option (\fB--disable ListR\fR), or you can
allow zclone to use \fBListR\fR where it would normally choose not to do so due to
higher memory usage, using the \fB--fast-list\fR option. Zclone should always
produce identical results either way. Using \fB--disable ListR\fR or \fB--fast-list\fR
on a remote which doesn't support \fBListR\fR does nothing, zclone will just ignore
it.

.PP
A rule of thumb is that if you pay for transactions and can fit your entire
sync listing into memory, then \fB--fast-list\fR is recommended. If you have a
very big sync to do, then don't use \fB--fast-list\fR, otherwise you will run out
of memory. Run some tests and compare before you decide, and if in doubt then
just leave the default, let zclone decide, i.e. not use \fB--fast-list\fR\&.

.SS --timeout Duration
This sets the IO idle timeout.  If a transfer has started but then
becomes idle for this long it is considered broken and disconnected.

.PP
The default is \fB5m\fR\&.  Set to \fB0\fR to disable.

.SS --transfers int
The number of file transfers to run in parallel.  It can sometimes be
useful to set this to a smaller number if the remote is giving a lot
of timeouts or bigger if you have lots of bandwidth and a fast remote.

.PP
The default is to run 4 file transfers in parallel.

.PP
Look at --multi-thread-streams if you would like to control single file transfers.

.SS -u, --update
This forces zclone to skip any files which exist on the destination
and have a modified time that is newer than the source file.

.PP
This can be useful in avoiding needless transfers when transferring to
a remote which doesn't support modification times directly (or when
using \fB--use-server-modtime\fR to avoid extra API calls) as it is more
accurate than a \fB--size-only\fR check and faster than using
\fB--checksum\fR\&. On such remotes (or when using \fB--use-server-modtime\fR)
the time checked will be the uploaded time.

.PP
If an existing destination file has a modification time older than the
source file's, it will be updated if the sizes are different. If the
sizes are the same, it will be updated if the checksum is different or
not available.

.PP
If an existing destination file has a modification time equal (within
the computed modify window) to the source file's, it will be updated
if the sizes are different. The checksum will not be checked in this
case unless the \fB--checksum\fR flag is provided.

.PP
In all other cases the file will not be updated.

.PP
Consider using the \fB--modify-window\fR flag to compensate for time skews
between the source and the backend, for backends that do not support
mod times, and instead use uploaded times. However, if the backend
does not support checksums, note that syncing or copying within the
time skew window may still result in additional transfers for safety.

.SS --use-mmap
If this flag is set then zclone will use anonymous memory allocated by
mmap on Unix based platforms and VirtualAlloc on Windows for its
transfer buffers (size controlled by \fB--buffer-size\fR).  Memory
allocated like this does not go on the Go heap and can be returned to
the OS immediately when it is finished with.

.PP
If this flag is not set then zclone will allocate and free the buffers
using the Go memory allocator which may use more memory as memory
pages are returned less aggressively to the OS.

.PP
It is possible this does not work well on all platforms so it is
disabled by default; in the future it may be enabled by default.

.SS --use-server-modtime
Some object-store backends (e.g, Swift, S3) do not preserve file modification
times (modtime). On these backends, zclone stores the original modtime as
additional metadata on the object. By default it will make an API call to
retrieve the metadata when the modtime is needed by an operation.

.PP
Use this flag to disable the extra API call and rely instead on the server's
modified time. In cases such as a local to remote sync using \fB--update\fR,
knowing the local file is newer than the time it was last uploaded to the
remote is sufficient. In those cases, this flag can speed up the process and
reduce the number of API calls necessary.

.PP
This flag is only supported on certain backends and will be silently
ignored on unsupported backends. Supported backends include
\fBazureblob\fR, \fBoracleobjectstorage\fR, \fBs3\fR, \fBswift\fR\&.

.PP
Using this flag on a sync operation without also using \fB--update\fR would cause
all files modified at any time other than the last upload time to be uploaded
again, which is probably not what you want.

.SS -v, -vv, --verbose
With \fB-v\fR zclone will tell you about each file that is transferred and
a small number of significant events.

.PP
With \fB-vv\fR zclone will become very verbose telling you about every
file it considers and transfers.  Please send bug reports with a log
with this setting.

.PP
When setting verbosity as an environment variable, use
\fBZCLONE_VERBOSE=1\fR or \fBZCLONE_VERBOSE=2\fR for \fB-v\fR and \fB-vv\fR respectively.

.SS -V, --version
Prints the version number

.SH SSL/TLS options
The outgoing SSL/TLS connections zclone makes can be controlled with
these options.  For example this can be very useful with the HTTP or
WebDAV backends. Zclone HTTP servers have their own set of
configuration for SSL/TLS which you can find in their documentation.

.SS --ca-cert stringArray
This loads the PEM encoded certificate authority certificates and uses
it to verify the certificates of the servers zclone connects to.

.PP
If you have generated certificates signed with a local CA then you
will need this flag to connect to servers using those certificates.

.SS --client-cert string
This loads the PEM encoded client side certificate.

.PP
This is used for mutual TLS authentication
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutual_authentication\[ra]\&.

.PP
The \fB--client-key\fR flag is required too when using this.

.SS --client-key string
This loads the PEM encoded client side private key used for mutual TLS
authentication.  Used in conjunction with \fB--client-cert\fR\&.

.PP
Supported types are:
.IP \(bu 2
Unencrypted PKCS#1 ("BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY")
.IP \(bu 2
Unencrypted PKCS#8 ("BEGIN PRIVATE KEY")
.IP \(bu 2
Encrypted PKCS#8 ("BEGIN ENCRYPTED PRIVATE KEY")
.IP \(bu 2
Legacy PEM encryption (e.g., DEK-Info headers), which are automatically detected.

.SS --client-pass string
This can be used to supply an optional password to decrypt the client key file.

.PP
\fBNB\fP the password should be obscured so it should be the output of
\fBzclone obscure YOURPASSWORD\fR\&.

.SS --no-check-certificate
\fB--no-check-certificate\fR controls whether a client verifies the
server's certificate chain and host name.
If \fB--no-check-certificate\fR is true, TLS accepts any certificate
presented by the server and any host name in that certificate.
In this mode, TLS is susceptible to man-in-the-middle attacks.

.PP
This option defaults to \fBfalse\fR\&.

.PP
\fBThis should be used only for testing.\fP

.SH Configuration encryption
Your configuration file contains information for logging in to
your cloud services. This means that you should keep your
\fBzclone.conf\fR file in a secure location.

.PP
If you are in an environment where that isn't possible, you can
add a password to your configuration. This means that you will
have to supply the password every time you start zclone.

.PP
To add a password to your zclone configuration, execute \fBzclone config\fR\&.

.EX
$ zclone config
Current remotes:

e) Edit existing remote
n) New remote
d) Delete remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
e/n/d/s/q>
.EE

.PP
Go into \fBs\fR, Set configuration password:

.EX
e/n/d/s/q> s
Your configuration is not encrypted.
If you add a password, you will protect your login information to cloud services.
a) Add Password
q) Quit to main menu
a/q> a
Enter NEW configuration password:
password:
Confirm NEW password:
password:
Password set
Your configuration is encrypted.
c) Change Password
u) Unencrypt configuration
q) Quit to main menu
c/u/q>
.EE

.PP
Your configuration is now encrypted, and every time you start zclone
you will have to supply the password. See below for details.
In the same menu, you can change the password or completely remove
encryption from your configuration.

.PP
There is no way to recover the configuration if you lose your password.

.PP
You can also use
.IP \(bu 2
zclone config encryption set
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_encryption_set/\[ra]
to set the config encryption directly
.IP \(bu 2
zclone config encryption remove
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_encryption_remove/\[ra]
to remove it
.IP \(bu 2
zclone config encryption check
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_encryption_check/\[ra]
to check that it is encrypted properly.

.PP
zclone uses nacl secretbox
\[la]https://godoc.org/golang.org/x/crypto/nacl/secretbox\[ra]
which in turn uses XSalsa20 and Poly1305 to encrypt and authenticate
your configuration with secret-key cryptography.
The password is SHA-256 hashed, which produces the key for secretbox.
The hashed password is not stored.

.PP
While this provides very good security, we do not recommend storing
your encrypted zclone configuration in public if it contains sensitive
information, maybe except if you use a very strong password.

.PP
If it is safe in your environment, you can set the \fBZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\fR
environment variable to contain your password, in which case it will be
used for decrypting the configuration.

.PP
You can set this for a session from a script.  For unix like systems
save this to a file called \fBset-zclone-password\fR:

.EX
#!/bin/echo Source this file don't run it

read -s ZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS
export ZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS
.EE

.PP
Then source the file when you want to use it.  From the shell you
would do \fBsource set-zclone-password\fR\&.  It will then ask you for the
password and set it in the environment variable.

.PP
An alternate means of supplying the password is to provide a script
which will retrieve the password and print on standard output.  This
script should have a fully specified path name and not rely on any
environment variables.  The script is supplied either via

\[la]#password\-command\[ra] command line argument or via the
\fBZCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND\fR environment variable.

.PP
One useful example of this is using the \fBpasswordstore\fR application
to retrieve the password:

.EX
export ZCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND="pass zclone/config"
.EE

.PP
If the \fBpasswordstore\fR password manager holds the password for the
zclone configuration, using the script method means the password
is primarily protected by the \fBpasswordstore\fR system, and is never
embedded in the clear in scripts, nor available for examination
using the standard commands available.  It is quite possible with
long running zclone sessions for copies of passwords to be innocently
captured in log files or terminal scroll buffers, etc.  Using the
script method of supplying the password enhances the security of
the config password considerably.

.PP
If you are running zclone inside a script, unless you are using the
\fB--password-command\fR method, you might want to disable
password prompts. To do that, pass the parameter
\fB--ask-password=false\fR to zclone. This will make zclone fail instead
of asking for a password if \fBZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\fR doesn't contain
a valid password, and \fB--password-command\fR has not been supplied.

.PP
Whenever running commands that may be affected by options in a
configuration file, zclone will look for an existing file according
to the rules described above
\[la]#config\-string\[ra], and load any it
finds. If an encrypted file is found, this includes decrypting it,
with the possible consequence of a password prompt. When executing
a command line that you know are not actually using anything from such
a configuration file, you can avoid it being loaded by overriding the
location, e.g. with one of the documented special values for
memory-only configuration. Since only backend options can be stored
in configuration files, this is normally unnecessary for commands
that do not operate on backends, e.g. \fBcompletion\fR\&. However,
it will be relevant for commands that do operate on backends in
general, but are used without referencing a stored remote, e.g.
listing local filesystem paths, or
connection strings
\[la]#connection\-strings\[ra]: \fBzclone --config="" ls .\fR

.SS Configuration encryption cheatsheet
You can quickly apply a configuration encryption without plain-text
at rest or transfer. Detailed instructions for popular OSes:

.SS Mac
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password

.EX
  security add-generic-password -a zclone -s config -w $(openssl rand -base64 40)
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
Add the retrieval instruction to your \fB\&.zprofile\fR / \fB\&.profile\fR

.EX
  export ZCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND="/usr/bin/security find-generic-password -a zclone -s config -w"
.EE

.SS Linux
.IP \(bu 2
Prerequisite: Linux doesn't come with a default password manager. Let's install
the "pass" utility using a package manager, e.g. \fBapt install pass\fR,
\fByum install pass\fR, etc.
\[la]https://www.passwordstore.org/#download\[ra];
then initialize a password store: \fBpass init zclone\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password

.EX
  echo $(openssl rand -base64 40) | pass insert -m zclone/config
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
Add the retrieval instruction

.EX
  export ZCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND="/usr/bin/pass zclone/config"
.EE

.SS Windows
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password

.EX
  New-Object -TypeName PSCredential -ArgumentList "zclone", (ConvertTo-SecureString -String ([System.Web.Security.Membership]::GeneratePassword(40, 10)) -AsPlainText -Force) | Export-Clixml -Path "zclone-credential.xml"
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
Add the password retrieval instruction

.EX
  [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("ZCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND", "[System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::PtrToStringAuto([System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::SecureStringToBSTR((Import-Clixml -Path "zclone-credential.xml").Password))")
.EE

.SS Encrypt the config file (all systems)
.IP \(bu 2
Execute \fBzclone config\fR, and select option \fBs) Set configuration password\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Add/update the password from previous steps

.SH Developer options
These options are useful when developing or debugging zclone.  There
are also some more remote specific options which aren't documented
here which are used for testing.  These start with remote name e.g.
\fB--drive-test-option\fR - see the docs for the remote in question.

.SS --cpuprofile string
Write CPU profile to a file. This can be analysed with \fBgo tool pprof\fR\&.

.SS --memprofile string
Write memory profile to a file. This can be analysed with \fBgo tool pprof\fR\&.

.SS --dump DumpFlags
The \fB--dump\fR flag takes a comma separated list of flags to dump info
about.

.PP
Note that some headers, such as \fBAccept-Encoding\fR, may not be correct
as shown in the request, and the response may not show \fBContent-Encoding\fR
if the go standard libraries auto gzip encoding was in effect. In this case
the body of the request will be gunzipped before showing it.

.PP
The available flags are:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBheaders\fR dumps HTTP headers. Any \fBAuthorization:\fR headers will be excluded,
but output may still contain sensitive information.  Can be very verbose.
Useful for debugging only. Use \fBauth\fR if you do want the \fBAuthorization:\fR headers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauth\fR dumps HTTP headers like \fBheaders\fR, but also includes any \fBAuthorization:\fR
headers. This means the output will probably contain sensitive information.
Use \fBheaders\fR to dump without \fBAuthorization:\fR headers. Can be very verbose.
Useful for debugging only. This flag also makes the debug log of the config
process (e.g. \fBzclone config -vv\fR) show answers to questions, passwords and
tokens which are otherwise redacted.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbodies\fR dumps HTTP headers and bodies. May contain sensitive info.
Can be very verbose.  Useful for debugging only. Note that the bodies
are buffered in memory so don't use this for enormous files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequests\fR is similar to \fBbodies\fR, but dumps the request bodies and the
response headers.  Useful for debugging download problems.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBresponses\fR is similar to \fBbodies\fR, but dumps the response bodies and the
request headers. Useful for debugging upload problems.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfilters\fR dumps the filters.  Useful to see exactly what include
and exclude options are filtering on.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgoroutines\fR dumps a list of the running go-routines at the end of
the command.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBopenfiles\fR dumps a list of the open files at the end of the command.
It uses the \fBlsof\fR Unix command to do that, so you'll need that installed
to use it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmapper\fR dumps the JSON blobs being sent to the program supplied with
\fB--metadata-mapper\fR and received from it. It can be useful for debugging
the metadata mapper interface.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcurl\fR dumps the HTTP request as a \fBcurl\fR command. Can be used with
the other HTTP debugging flags (e.g. \fBrequests\fR, \fBbodies\fR). By
default the auth will be masked - use with \fBauth\fR to have the curl
commands with authentication too.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBerrors\fR only dumps the HTTP transactions which fail with a retryable
error - that is a transport error, an HTTP 429 (too many requests) or an
HTTP 5xx server error. The other dump flags control what is dumped, so
for example use \fB--dump errors,bodies\fR to dump the headers and bodies of
failed transactions only. On its own \fB--dump errors\fR dumps the headers.
This lets you capture first-failure diagnostics without the noise of
dumping every transaction. May contain sensitive info.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrace\fR logs connection level events for each HTTP transaction using Go's
\fBnet/http/httptrace\fR - DNS resolution, TCP connect, TLS handshake (including
the negotiated TLS version, cipher, ALPN protocol and server certificate),
connection reuse, request write and time to first response byte. Each line is
tagged with the time elapsed since the start of the transaction so you can see
where the time goes. This is complementary to the other dump flags: it shows
how the connection behaved rather than what was sent, so it is useful for
debugging connectivity, DNS, TLS, proxy and keep-alive problems. It does not
dump headers or bodies - combine it with \fBheaders\fR or \fBbodies\fR for that.

