.SH Logging
zclone has 4 levels of logging, \fBERROR\fR, \fBNOTICE\fR, \fBINFO\fR and \fBDEBUG\fR\&.

.PP
By default, zclone logs to standard error.  This means you can redirect
standard error and still see the normal output of zclone commands (e.g.
\fBzclone ls\fR).

.PP
By default, zclone will produce \fBError\fR and \fBNotice\fR level messages.

.PP
If you use the \fB-q\fR flag, zclone will only produce \fBError\fR messages.

.PP
If you use the \fB-v\fR flag, zclone will produce \fBError\fR, \fBNotice\fR and
\fBInfo\fR messages.

.PP
If you use the \fB-vv\fR flag, zclone will produce \fBError\fR, \fBNotice\fR,
\fBInfo\fR and \fBDebug\fR messages.

.PP
You can also control the log levels with the \fB--log-level\fR flag.

.PP
If you use the \fB--log-file\fR option, zclone will redirect \fBError\fR,
\fBInfo\fR and \fBDebug\fR messages along with standard error to a file.

.PP
If you use the \fB--syslog\fR flag then zclone will log to syslog and the
\fB--syslog-facility\fR control which facility it uses.

.PP
Zclone prefixes all log messages with their level in capitals, e.g. INFO
which makes it easy to grep the log file for different kinds of
information.

.SH Metrics
Zclone can publish metrics in the OpenMetrics/Prometheus format.

.PP
To enable the metrics endpoint, use the \fB--metrics-addr\fR flag. Metrics can
also be published on the \fB--rc-addr\fR port if the \fB--rc\fR flag and
\fB--rc-enable-metrics\fR flags are supplied or if using zclone rcd
\fB--rc-enable-metrics\fR

.PP
Zclone provides extensive configuration options for the metrics HTTP endpoint.
These settings are grouped under the Metrics section and have a prefix
\fB--metrics-*\fR\&.

.PP
When metrics are enabled with \fB--rc-enable-metrics\fR, they will be published on
the same port as the rc API. In this case, the \fB--metrics-*\fR flags will be
ignored, and the HTTP endpoint configuration will be managed by the \fB--rc-*\fR
parameters.

.SH Exit code
If any errors occur during the command execution, zclone will exit with a
non-zero exit code.  This allows scripts to detect when zclone
operations have failed.

.PP
During the startup phase, zclone will exit immediately if an error is
detected in the configuration.  There will always be a log message
immediately before exiting.

.PP
When zclone is running it will accumulate errors as it goes along, and
only exit with a non-zero exit code if (after retries) there were
still failed transfers.  For every error counted there will be a high
priority log message (visible with \fB-q\fR) showing the message and
which file caused the problem. A high priority message is also shown
when starting a retry so the user can see that any previous error
messages may not be valid after the retry. If zclone has done a retry
it will log a high priority message if the retry was successful.

.SS List of exit codes
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR - Success
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fR - Error not otherwise categorised
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fR - Syntax or usage error
.IP \(bu 2
\fB3\fR - Directory not found
.IP \(bu 2
\fB4\fR - File not found
.IP \(bu 2
\fB5\fR - Temporary error (one that more retries might fix) (Retry errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB6\fR - Less serious errors (like 461 errors from dropbox) (NoRetry errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB7\fR - Fatal error (one that more retries won't fix, like account suspended)
(Fatal errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB8\fR - Transfer exceeded - limit set by --max-transfer reached
.IP \(bu 2
\fB9\fR - Operation successful, but no files transferred (Requires

\[la]#error\-on\-no\-transfer\[ra])
.IP \(bu 2
\fB10\fR - Duration exceeded - limit set by --max-duration reached

.SH Environment variables
Zclone can be configured entirely using environment variables.  These
can be used to set defaults for options or config file entries.

.SS Options
Every option in zclone can have its default set by environment
variable.

.PP
To find the name of the environment variable, first, take the long
option name, strip the leading \fB--\fR, change \fB-\fR to \fB_\fR, make
upper case and prepend \fBZCLONE_\fR\&.

.PP
For example, to always set \fB--stats 5s\fR, set the environment variable
\fBZCLONE_STATS=5s\fR\&.  If you set stats on the command line this will
override the environment variable setting.

.PP
Or to always use the trash in drive \fB--drive-use-trash\fR, set
\fBZCLONE_DRIVE_USE_TRASH=true\fR\&.

.PP
Verbosity is slightly different, the environment variable
equivalent of \fB--verbose\fR or \fB-v\fR is \fBZCLONE_VERBOSE=1\fR,
or for \fB-vv\fR, \fBZCLONE_VERBOSE=2\fR\&.

.PP
The same parser is used for the options and the environment variables
so they take exactly the same form.

.PP
The options set by environment variables can be seen with the \fB-vv\fR flag,
e.g. \fBzclone version -vv\fR\&.

.PP
Options that can appear multiple times (type \fBstringArray\fR) are
treated slightly differently as environment variables can only be
defined once. In order to allow a simple mechanism for adding one or
many items, the input is treated as a CSV encoded
\[la]https://godoc.org/encoding/csv\[ra]
string. For example

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBEnvironment variable\fP	\fBEquivalent options\fP
\fBZCLONE_EXCLUDE="*.jpg"\fR	\fB--exclude "*.jpg"\fR
\fBZCLONE_EXCLUDE="*.jpg,*.png"\fR	\fB--exclude "*.jpg"\fR \fB--exclude "*.png"\fR
\fBZCLONE_EXCLUDE='"*.jpg","*.png"'\fR	\fB--exclude "*.jpg"\fR \fB--exclude "*.png"\fR
\fBZCLONE_EXCLUDE='"/directory with comma , in it /**"'\fR	T{
`--exclude "/directory with comma , in it /**"
T}
.TE

.PP
If \fBstringArray\fR options are defined as environment variables \fBand\fP
options on the command line then all the values will be used.

.SS Config file
You can set defaults for values in the config file on an individual
remote basis. The names of the config items are documented in the page
for each backend.

.PP
To find the name of the environment variable, you need to set, take
\fBZCLONE_CONFIG_\fR + name of remote + \fB_\fR + name of config file option
and make it all uppercase.
Note one implication here is the remote's name must be
convertible into a valid environment variable name,
so it can only contain letters, digits, or the \fB_\fR (underscore) character.

.PP
For example, to configure an S3 remote named \fBmys3:\fR without a config
file (using unix ways of setting environment variables):

.EX
$ export ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_TYPE=s3
$ export ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=XXX
$ export ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=XXX
$ zclone lsd mys3:
          -1 2016-09-21 12:54:21        -1 my-bucket
$ zclone listremotes | grep mys3
mys3:
.EE

.PP
Note that if you want to create a remote using environment variables
you must create the \fB\&..._TYPE\fR variable as above.

.PP
Note that the name of a remote created using environment variable is
case insensitive, in contrast to regular remotes stored in config
file as documented above
\[la]#valid\-remote\-names\[ra]\&.
You must write the name in uppercase in the environment variable, but
as seen from example above it will be listed and can be accessed in
lowercase, while you can also refer to the same remote in uppercase:

.EX
$ zclone lsd mys3:
          -1 2016-09-21 12:54:21        -1 my-bucket
$ zclone lsd MYS3:
          -1 2016-09-21 12:54:21        -1 my-bucket
.EE

.PP
Note that you can only set the options of the immediate backend,
so ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3CRYPT_ACCESS_KEY_ID has no effect, if myS3Crypt is
a crypt remote based on an S3 remote. However ZCLONE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID will
set the access key of all remotes using S3, including myS3Crypt.

.PP
Note also that now zclone has connection strings
\[la]#connection\-strings\[ra],
it is probably easier to use those instead which makes the above example

.EX
zclone lsd :s3,access_key_id=XXX,secret_access_key=XXX:
.EE

.SS Precedence
The various different methods of backend configuration are read in
this order and the first one with a value is used.
.IP \(bu 2
Parameters in connection strings, e.g. \fBmyRemote,skip_links:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Flag values as supplied on the command line, e.g. \fB--skip-links\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Remote specific environment vars, e.g. \fBZCLONE_CONFIG_MYREMOTE_SKIP_LINKS\fR
(see above).
.IP \(bu 2
Backend-specific environment vars, e.g. \fBZCLONE_LOCAL_SKIP_LINKS\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Backend generic environment vars, e.g. \fBZCLONE_SKIP_LINKS\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Config file, e.g. \fBskip_links = true\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Default values, e.g. \fBfalse\fR - these can't be changed.

.PP
So if both \fB--skip-links\fR is supplied on the command line and an
environment variable \fBZCLONE_LOCAL_SKIP_LINKS\fR is set, the command line
flag will take preference.

.PP
The backend configurations set by environment variables can be seen with the
\fB-vv\fR flag, e.g. \fBzclone about myRemote: -vv\fR\&.

.PP
For non backend configuration the order is as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
Flag values as supplied on the command line, e.g. \fB--stats 5s\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Environment vars, e.g. \fBZCLONE_STATS=5s\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Default values, e.g. \fB1m\fR - these can't be changed.

.SS Other environment variables
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\fR set to contain your config file password (see
Configuration Encryption
\[la]#configuration\-encryption\[ra] section)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHTTP_PROXY\fR, \fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR and \fBNO_PROXY\fR (or the lowercase versions thereof).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR takes precedence over \fBHTTP_PROXY\fR for https requests.
.IP \(bu 2
The environment values may be either a complete URL or a "host[:port]"
for, in which case the "http" scheme is assumed.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUSER\fR and \fBLOGNAME\fR values are used as fallbacks for current username.
The primary method for looking up username is OS-specific: Windows API on
Windows, real user ID in /etc/passwd on Unix systems. In the documentation
the current username is simply referred to as \fB$USER\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZCLONE_CONFIG_DIR\fR - zclone \fBsets\fP this variable for use in config files
and sub processes to point to the directory holding the config file.

.PP
The options set by environment variables can be seen with the \fB-vv\fR and
\fB--log-level=DEBUG\fR flags, e.g. \fBzclone version -vv\fR\&.


.SH Configuring zclone on a remote / headless machine
Some of the configurations (those involving oauth2) require an
internet-connected web browser.

.PP
If you are trying to set zclone up on a remote or headless machine with no
browser available on it (e.g. a NAS or a server in a datacenter), then
you will need to use an alternative means of configuration. There are
three ways of doing it, described below.

.SH Configuring using zclone authorize
On the headless machine run zclone config
\[la]/commands/zclone_config\[ra], but
answer \fBN\fR to the question \fBUse web browser to automatically authenticate
zclone with remote?\fR\&.

.EX
Use web browser to automatically authenticate zclone with remote?
 * Say Y if the machine running zclone has a web browser you can use
 * Say N if running zclone on a (remote) machine without web browser access
If not sure try Y. If Y failed, try N.

y) Yes (default)
n) No
y/n> n

Option config_token.
For this to work, you will need zclone available on a machine that has
a web browser available.
For more help and alternate methods see: //remote_setup/
Execute the following on the machine with the web browser (same zclone
version recommended):
        zclone authorize "onedrive"
Then paste the result.
Enter a value.
config_token>
.EE

.PP
Then on your main desktop machine, run zclone authorize
\[la]//commands/zclone_authorize/\[ra]\&.

.EX
zclone authorize "onedrive"
NOTICE: Make sure your Redirect URL is set to "http://localhost:53682/" in your custom config.
NOTICE: If your browser doesn't open automatically go to the following link: http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
NOTICE: Log in and authorize zclone for access
NOTICE: Waiting for code...

Got code
Paste the following into your remote machine --->
SECRET_TOKEN
<---End paste
.EE

.PP
Then back to the headless machine, paste in the code.

.EX
config_token> SECRET_TOKEN
--------------------
[acd12]
client_id =
client_secret =
token = SECRET_TOKEN
--------------------
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d>
.EE

.SH Configuring by copying the config file
Zclone stores all of its configuration in a single file. This can easily be
copied to configure a remote zclone (although some backends do not support
reusing the same configuration, consult your backend documentation to be
sure).

.PP
Start by running zclone config
\[la]/commands/zclone_config\[ra] to create the
configuration file on your desktop machine.

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
Then locate the file by running zclone config file
\[la]/commands/zclone_config_file\[ra]\&.

.EX
$ zclone config file
Configuration file is stored at:
/home/user/.zclone.conf
.EE

.PP
Finally, transfer the file to the remote machine (scp, cut paste, ftp, sftp, etc.)
and place it in the correct location (use zclone config file
\[la]/commands/zclone_config_file\[ra]
on the remote machine to find out where).

.SH Configuring using SSH Tunnel
If you have an SSH client installed on your local machine, you can set up an
SSH tunnel to redirect the port 53682 into the headless machine by using the
following command:

.EX
ssh -L localhost:53682:localhost:53682 username@remote_server
.EE

.PP
Then on the headless machine run zclone config
\[la]/commands/zclone_config\[ra] and
answer \fBY\fR to the question \fBUse web browser to automatically authenticate zclone
with remote?\fR\&.

.EX
Use web browser to automatically authenticate zclone with remote?
 * Say Y if the machine running zclone has a web browser you can use
 * Say N if running zclone on a (remote) machine without web browser access
If not sure try Y. If Y failed, try N.

y) Yes (default)
n) No
y/n> y
NOTICE: Make sure your Redirect URL is set to "http://localhost:53682/" in your custom config.
NOTICE: If your browser doesn't open automatically go to the following link: http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
NOTICE: Log in and authorize zclone for access
NOTICE: Waiting for code...
.EE

.PP
Finally, copy and paste the presented URL \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\fR
to the browser on your local machine, complete the auth and you are done.


.SH Filtering, includes and excludes
Filter flags determine which files zclone \fBsync\fR, \fBmove\fR, \fBls\fR, \fBlsl\fR,
\fBmd5sum\fR, \fBsha1sum\fR, \fBsize\fR, \fBdelete\fR, \fBcheck\fR and similar commands
apply to.

.PP
They are specified in terms of path/file name patterns; path/file
lists; file age and size, or presence of a file in a directory. Bucket
based remotes without the concept of directory apply filters to object
key, age and size in an analogous way.

.PP
Zclone \fBpurge\fR does not obey filters.

.PP
To test filters without risk of damage to data, apply them to \fBzclone
ls\fR, or with the \fB--dry-run\fR and \fB-vv\fR flags.

.PP
Zclone filter patterns can only be used in filter command line options, not
in the specification of a remote.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone copy "remote:dir*.jpg" /path/to/dir\fR does not have a filter effect.
\fBzclone copy remote:dir /path/to/dir --include "*.jpg"\fR does.

.PP
\fBImportant\fP Avoid mixing any two of \fB--include...\fR, \fB--exclude...\fR or
\fB--filter...\fR flags in an zclone command. The results might not be what
you expect. Instead use a \fB--filter...\fR flag.

.SH Patterns for matching path/file names
.SS Pattern syntax {#patterns}
Here is a formal definition of the pattern syntax,
examples
\[la]#examples\[ra] are below.

.PP
Zclone matching rules follow a glob style:

.EX
*         matches any sequence of non-separator (/) characters
**        matches any sequence of characters including / separators
?         matches any single non-separator (/) character
[ [ ! ] { character-range } ]
          character class (must be non-empty)
{ pattern-list }
          pattern alternatives
{{ regexp }}
          regular expression to match
c         matches character c (c != *, **, ?, \\, [, {, })
\\c        matches reserved character c (c = *, **, ?, \\, [, {, }) or character class
.EE

.PP
character-range:

.EX
c         matches character c (c != \\, -, ])
\\c        matches reserved character c (c = \\, -, ])
lo - hi   matches character c for lo <= c <= hi
.EE

.PP
pattern-list:

.EX
pattern { , pattern }
          comma-separated (without spaces) patterns
.EE

.PP
character classes (see Go regular expression reference
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/\[ra])
include:

.EX
Named character classes (e.g. [\\d], [^\\d], [\\D], [^\\D])
Perl character classes (e.g. \\s, \\S, \\w, \\W)
ASCII character classes (e.g. [[:alnum:]], [[:alpha:]], [[:punct:]], [[:xdigit:]])
.EE

.PP
regexp for advanced users to insert a regular expression - see below
\[la]#regexp\[ra]
for more info:

.EX
Any re2 regular expression not containing `}}`
.EE

.PP
If the filter pattern starts with a \fB/\fR then it only matches
at the top level of the directory tree,
\fBrelative to the root of the remote\fP (not necessarily the root
of the drive). If it does not start with \fB/\fR then it is matched
starting at the \fBend of the path/file name\fP but it only matches
a complete path element - it must match from a \fB/\fR
separator or the beginning of the path/file.

.EX
file.jpg   - matches "file.jpg"
           - matches "directory/file.jpg"
           - doesn't match "afile.jpg"
           - doesn't match "directory/afile.jpg"
/file.jpg  - matches "file.jpg" in the root directory of the remote
           - doesn't match "afile.jpg"
           - doesn't match "directory/file.jpg"
.EE

.PP
The top level of the remote might not be the top level of the drive.

.PP
E.g. for a Microsoft Windows local directory structure

.EX
F:
├── bkp
├── data
│   ├── excl
│   │   ├── 123.jpg
│   │   └── 456.jpg
│   ├── incl
│   │   └── document.pdf
.EE

.PP
To copy the contents of folder \fBdata\fR into folder \fBbkp\fR excluding the contents
of subfolder \fBexcl\fRthe following command treats \fBF:\\data\fR and \fBF:\\bkp\fR as top
level for filtering.

.PP
\fBzclone copy F:\\data\\ F:\\bkp\\ --exclude=/excl/**\fR

.PP
\fBImportant\fP Use \fB/\fR in path/file name patterns and not \fB\\\fR even if
running on Microsoft Windows.

.PP
Simple patterns are case sensitive unless the \fB--ignore-case\fR flag is used.

.PP
Without \fB--ignore-case\fR (default)

.EX
potato - matches "potato"
       - doesn't match "POTATO"
.EE

.PP
With \fB--ignore-case\fR

.EX
potato - matches "potato"
       - matches "POTATO"
.EE

.SH Using regular expressions in filter patterns {#regexp}
The syntax of filter patterns is glob style matching (like \fBbash\fR
uses) to make things easy for users. However this does not provide
absolute control over the matching, so for advanced users zclone also
provides a regular expression syntax.

.PP
Zclone generally accepts Perl-style regular expressions, the exact syntax
is defined in the Go regular expression reference
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/\[ra]\&.
Regular expressions should be enclosed in \fB{{\fR \fB}}\fR\&. They will match only the
last path segment if the glob doesn't start with \fB/\fR or the whole path
name if it does. Note that zclone does not attempt to parse the
supplied regular expression, meaning that using any regular expression
filter will prevent zclone from using directory filter rules
\[la]#directory_filter\[ra],
as it will instead check every path against
the supplied regular expression(s).

.PP
Here is how the \fB{{regexp}}\fR is transformed into an full regular
expression to match the entire path:

.EX
{{regexp}}  becomes (^|/)(regexp)$
/{{regexp}} becomes ^(regexp)$
.EE

.PP
Regexp syntax can be mixed with glob syntax, for example

.EX
*.{{jpe?g}} to match file.jpg, file.jpeg but not file.png
.EE

.PP
You can also use regexp flags - to set case insensitive, for example

.EX
*.{{(?i)jpg}} to match file.jpg, file.JPG but not file.png
.EE

.PP
Be careful with wildcards in regular expressions - you don't want them
to match path separators normally. To match any file name starting
with \fBstart\fR and ending with \fBend\fR write

.EX
{{start[^/]*end\\.jpg}}
.EE

.PP
Not

.EX
{{start.*end\\.jpg}}
.EE

.PP
Which will match a directory called \fBstart\fR with a file called
\fBend.jpg\fR in it as the \fB\&.*\fR will match \fB/\fR characters.

.PP
Note that you can use \fB-vv --dump filters\fR to show the filter patterns
in regexp format - zclone implements the glob patterns by transforming
them into regular expressions.

.SH Filter pattern examples {#examples}
.TS
allbox;
l l l l 
l l l l .
\fBDescription\fP	\fBPattern\fP	\fBMatches\fP	\fBDoes not match\fP
Wildcard	\fB*.jpg\fR	\fB/file.jpg\fR	\fB/file.png\fR
		\fB/dir/file.jpg\fR	\fB/dir/file.png\fR
Rooted	\fB/*.jpg\fR	\fB/file.jpg\fR	\fB/file.png\fR
		\fB/file2.jpg\fR	\fB/dir/file.jpg\fR
Alternates	\fB*.{jpg,png}\fR	\fB/file.jpg\fR	\fB/file.gif\fR
		\fB/dir/file.png\fR	\fB/dir/file.gif\fR
Path Wildcard	\fBdir/**\fR	\fB/dir/anyfile\fR	\fBfile.png\fR
		\fB/subdir/dir/subsubdir/anyfile\fR	\fB/subdir/file.png\fR
Any Char	\fB*.t?t\fR	\fB/file.txt\fR	\fB/file.qxt\fR
		\fB/dir/file.tzt\fR	\fB/dir/file.png\fR
Range	\fB*.[a-z]\fR	\fB/file.a\fR	\fB/file.0\fR
		\fB/dir/file.b\fR	\fB/dir/file.1\fR
Escape	\fB*.\\?\\?\\?\fR	\fB/file.???\fR	\fB/file.abc\fR
		\fB/dir/file.???\fR	\fB/dir/file.def\fR
Class	\fB*.\\d\\d\\d\fR	\fB/file.012\fR	\fB/file.abc\fR
		\fB/dir/file.345\fR	\fB/dir/file.def\fR
Regexp	\fB*.{{jpe?g}}\fR	\fB/file.jpeg\fR	\fB/file.png\fR
		\fB/dir/file.jpg\fR	\fB/dir/file.jpeeg\fR
Rooted Regexp	\fB/{{.*\\.jpe?g}}\fR	\fB/file.jpeg\fR	\fB/file.png\fR
		\fB/file.jpg\fR	\fB/dir/file.jpg\fR
.TE

.SH How filter rules are applied to files {#how-filter-rules-work}
Zclone path/file name filters are made up of one or more of the following flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--include\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--include-from\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--exclude\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--exclude-from\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--filter\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--filter-from\fR

.PP
There can be more than one instance of individual flags.

.PP
Zclone internally uses a combined list of all the include and exclude
rules. The order in which rules are processed can influence the result
of the filter.

.PP
All flags of the same type are processed together in the order
above, regardless of what order the different types of flags are
included on the command line.

.PP
Multiple instances of the same flag are processed from left
to right according to their position in the command line.

.PP
To mix up the order of processing includes and excludes use \fB--filter...\fR
flags.

.PP
Within \fB--include-from\fR, \fB--exclude-from\fR and \fB--filter-from\fR flags
rules are processed from top to bottom of the referenced file.

.PP
If there is an \fB--include\fR or \fB--include-from\fR flag specified, zclone
implies a \fB- **\fR rule which it adds to the bottom of the internal rule
list. Specifying a \fB+\fR rule with a \fB--filter...\fR flag does not imply
that rule.

.PP
Each path/file name passed through zclone is matched against the
combined filter list. At first match to a rule the path/file name
is included or excluded and no further filter rules are processed for
that path/file.

.PP
If zclone does not find a match, after testing against all rules
(including the implied rule if appropriate), the path/file name
is included.

.PP
Any path/file included at that stage is processed by the zclone
command.

.PP
\fB--files-from\fR, \fB--files-from-raw\fR and \fB--files-from0\fR flags
over-ride and cannot be combined with other filter options.

.PP
To see the internal combined rule list, in regular expression form,
for a command add the \fB--dump filters\fR flag. Running an zclone command
with \fB--dump filters\fR and \fB-vv\fR flags lists the internal filter elements
and shows how they are applied to each source path/file. There is not
currently a means provided to pass regular expression filter options into
zclone directly though character class filter rules contain character
classes. Go regular expression reference
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/\[ra]

.SS How filter rules are applied to directories {#directory_filter}
Zclone commands are applied to path/file names not
directories. The entire contents of a directory can be matched
to a filter by the pattern \fBdirectory/*\fR or recursively by
\fBdirectory/**\fR\&.

.PP
Directory filter rules are defined with a closing \fB/\fR separator.

.PP
E.g. \fB/directory/subdirectory/\fR is an zclone directory filter rule.

.PP
Zclone commands can use directory filter rules to determine whether they
recurse into subdirectories. This potentially optimises access to a remote
by avoiding listing unnecessary directories. Whether optimisation is
desirable depends on the specific filter rules and source remote content.

.PP
If any regular expression filters
\[la]#regexp\[ra] are in use, then no
directory recursion optimisation is possible, as zclone must check
every path against the supplied regular expression(s).

.PP
Directory recursion optimisation occurs if either:
.IP \(bu 2
A source remote does not support the zclone \fBListR\fR primitive. local,
sftp, Microsoft OneDrive and WebDAV do not support \fBListR\fR\&. Google
Drive and most bucket type storage do. Full list
\[la]///overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
On other remotes (those that support \fBListR\fR), if the zclone command is not
naturally recursive, and provided it is not run with the \fB--fast-list\fR flag.
\fBls\fR, \fBlsf -R\fR and \fBsize\fR are naturally recursive but \fBsync\fR, \fBcopy\fR and \fBmove\fR
are not.
.IP \(bu 2
Whenever the \fB--disable ListR\fR flag is applied to an zclone command.

.PP
Zclone commands imply directory filter rules from path/file filter
rules. To view the directory filter rules zclone has implied for a
command specify the \fB--dump filters\fR flag.

.PP
E.g. for an include rule

.EX
/a/*.jpg
.EE

.PP
Zclone implies the directory include rule

.EX
/a/
.EE

.PP
Directory filter rules specified in an zclone command can limit
the scope of an zclone command but path/file filters still have
to be specified.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --include /directory/\fR will not match any
files. Because it is an \fB--include\fR option the \fB--exclude **\fR rule
is implied, and the \fB/directory/\fR pattern serves only to optimise
access to the remote by ignoring everything outside of that directory.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --filter-from filter-list.txt\fR with a file
\fBfilter-list.txt\fR:

.EX
- /dir1/
- /dir2/
+ *.pdf
- **
.EE

.PP
All files in directories \fBdir1\fR or \fBdir2\fR or their subdirectories
are completely excluded from the listing. Only files of suffix
\fBpdf\fR in the root of \fBremote:\fR or its subdirectories are listed.
The \fB- **\fR rule prevents listing of any path/files not previously
matched by the rules above.

.PP
Option \fBexclude-if-present\fR creates a directory exclude rule based
on the presence of a file in a directory and takes precedence over
other zclone directory filter rules.

.PP
When using pattern list syntax, if a pattern item contains either
\fB/\fR or \fB**\fR, then zclone will not able to imply a directory filter rule
from this pattern list.

.PP
E.g. for an include rule

.EX
{dir1/**,dir2/**}
.EE

.PP
Zclone will match files below directories \fBdir1\fR or \fBdir2\fR only,
but will not be able to use this filter to exclude a directory \fBdir3\fR
from being traversed.

.PP
Directory recursion optimisation may affect performance, but normally
not the result. One exception to this is sync operations with option
\fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR, where any traversed empty directories will
be created. With the pattern list example \fB{dir1/**,dir2/**}\fR above,
this would create an empty directory \fBdir3\fR on destination (when it exists
on source). Changing the filter to \fB{dir1,dir2}/**\fR, or splitting it into
two include rules \fB--include dir1/** --include dir2/**\fR, will match the
same files while also filtering directories, with the result that an empty
directory \fBdir3\fR will no longer be created.

.SS \fB--exclude\fR - Exclude files matching pattern
Excludes path/file names from an zclone command based on a single exclude
rule.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
\fB--exclude\fR should not be used with \fB--include\fR, \fB--include-from\fR,
\fB--filter\fR or \fB--filter-from\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--exclude\fR has no effect when combined with \fB--files-from\fR,
\fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR flags.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --exclude *.bak\fR excludes all .bak files
from listing.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone size remote: --exclude "/dir/**"\fR returns the total size of
all files on \fBremote:\fR excluding those in root directory \fBdir\fR and sub
directories.

.PP
E.g. on Microsoft Windows \fBzclone ls remote: --exclude "*\\[{JP,KR,HK}\\]*"\fR
lists the files in \fBremote:\fR without \fB[JP]\fR or \fB[KR]\fR or \fB[HK]\fR in
their name. Quotes prevent the shell from interpreting the \fB\\\fR
characters.\fB\\\fR characters escape the \fB[\fR and \fB]\fR so an zclone filter
treats them literally rather than as a character-range. The \fB{\fR and \fB}\fR
define an zclone pattern list. For other operating systems single quotes are
required ie \fBzclone ls remote: --exclude '*\\[{JP,KR,HK}\\]*'\fR

.SS \fB--exclude-from\fR - Read exclude patterns from file
Excludes path/file names from an zclone command based on rules in a
named file. The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules.

.PP
For an example \fBexclude-file.txt\fR:

.EX
# a sample exclude rule file
*.bak
file2.jpg
.EE

.PP
\fBzclone ls remote: --exclude-from exclude-file.txt\fR lists the files on
\fBremote:\fR except those named \fBfile2.jpg\fR or with a suffix \fB\&.bak\fR\&. That is
equivalent to \fBzclone ls remote: --exclude file2.jpg --exclude "*.bak"\fR\&.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
The \fB--exclude-from\fR flag is useful where multiple exclude filter rules
are applied to an zclone command.

.PP
\fB--exclude-from\fR should not be used with \fB--include\fR, \fB--include-from\fR,
\fB--filter\fR or \fB--filter-from\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--exclude-from\fR has no effect when combined with \fB--files-from\fR,
\fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--exclude-from\fR followed by \fB-\fR reads filter rules from standard input.

.SS \fB--include\fR - Include files matching pattern
Adds a single include rule based on path/file names to an zclone
command.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
\fB--include\fR has no effect when combined with \fB--files-from\fR,
\fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--include\fR implies \fB--exclude **\fR at the end of an zclone internal
filter list. Therefore if you mix \fB--include\fR and \fB--include-from\fR
flags with \fB--exclude\fR, \fB--exclude-from\fR, \fB--filter\fR or \fB--filter-from\fR,
you must use include rules for all the files you want in the include
statement. For more flexibility use the \fB--filter-from\fR flag.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --include "*.{png,jpg}"\fR lists the files on
\fBremote:\fR with suffix \fB\&.png\fR and \fB\&.jpg\fR\&. All other files are excluded.

.PP
E.g. multiple zclone copy commands can be combined with \fB--include\fR and a
pattern-list.

.EX
zclone copy /vol1/A remote:A
zclone copy /vol1/B remote:B
.EE

.PP
is equivalent to:

.EX
zclone copy /vol1 remote: --include "{A,B}/**"
.EE

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote:/wheat --include "??[^[:punct:]]*"\fR lists the
files \fBremote:\fR directory \fBwheat\fR (and subdirectories) whose third
character is not punctuation. This example uses
an ASCII character class
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/\[ra]\&.

.SS \fB--include-from\fR - Read include patterns from file
Adds path/file names to an zclone command based on rules in a
named file. The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules.

.PP
For an example \fBinclude-file.txt\fR:

.EX
# a sample include rule file
*.jpg
file2.avi
.EE

.PP
\fBzclone ls remote: --include-from include-file.txt\fR lists the files on
\fBremote:\fR with name \fBfile2.avi\fR or suffix \fB\&.jpg\fR\&. That is equivalent to
\fBzclone ls remote: --include file2.avi --include "*.jpg"\fR\&.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
The \fB--include-from\fR flag is useful where multiple include filter rules
are applied to an zclone command.

.PP
\fB--include-from\fR implies \fB--exclude **\fR at the end of an zclone internal
filter list. Therefore if you mix \fB--include\fR and \fB--include-from\fR
flags with \fB--exclude\fR, \fB--exclude-from\fR, \fB--filter\fR or \fB--filter-from\fR,
you must use include rules for all the files you want in the include
statement. For more flexibility use the \fB--filter-from\fR flag.

.PP
\fB--include-from\fR has no effect when combined with \fB--files-from\fR,
\fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--include-from\fR followed by \fB-\fR reads filter rules from standard input.

.SS \fB--filter\fR - Add a file-filtering rule
Specifies path/file names to an zclone command, based on a single
include or exclude rule, in \fB+\fR or \fB-\fR format.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
\fB--filter +\fR differs from \fB--include\fR\&. In the case of \fB--include\fR zclone
implies an \fB--exclude *\fR rule which it adds to the bottom of the internal rule
list. \fB--filter...+\fR does not imply
that rule.

.PP
\fB--filter\fR has no effect when combined with \fB--files-from\fR,
\fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR flags.

.PP
\fB--filter\fR should not be used with \fB--include\fR, \fB--include-from\fR,
\fB--exclude\fR or \fB--exclude-from\fR flags.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --filter "- *.bak"\fR excludes all \fB\&.bak\fR files
from a list of \fBremote:\fR\&.

.SS \fB--filter-from\fR - Read filtering patterns from a file
Adds path/file names to an zclone command based on rules in a
named file. The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules. Include

rules start with \fB+\fR and exclude rules with \fB-\fR\&. \fB!\fR clears existing
rules. Rules are processed in the order they are defined.

.PP
This flag can be repeated. See above for the order filter flags are
processed in.

.PP
Arrange the order of filter rules with the most restrictive first and
work down.

.PP
Lines starting with # or ; are ignored, and can be used to write comments.
Inline comments are not supported. \fIUse \fB-vv --dump filters\fR to see how they
appear in the final regexp.\fP

.PP
E.g. for \fBfilter-file.txt\fR:

.EX
# a sample filter rule file
- secret*.jpg
+ *.jpg
+ *.png
+ file2.avi
- /dir/tmp/** # WARNING! This text will be treated as part of the path.
- /dir/Trash/**
+ /dir/**
# exclude everything else
- *
.EE

.PP
\fBzclone ls remote: --filter-from filter-file.txt\fR lists the path/files on
\fBremote:\fR including all \fBjpg\fR and \fBpng\fR files, excluding any
matching \fBsecret*.jpg\fR and including \fBfile2.avi\fR\&.  It also includes
everything in the directory \fBdir\fR at the root of \fBremote\fR, except
\fBremote:dir/Trash\fR which it excludes.  Everything else is excluded.

.PP
E.g. for an alternative \fBfilter-file.txt\fR:

.EX
- secret*.jpg
+ *.jpg
+ *.png
+ file2.avi
- *
.EE

.PP
Files \fBfile1.jpg\fR, \fBfile3.png\fR and \fBfile2.avi\fR are listed whilst
\fBsecret17.jpg\fR and files without the suffix \fB\&.jpg\fR or \fB\&.png\fR are excluded.

.PP
E.g. for an alternative \fBfilter-file.txt\fR:

.EX
+ *.jpg
+ *.gif
!
+ 42.doc
- *
.EE

.PP
Only file 42.doc is listed. Prior rules are cleared by the \fB!\fR\&.

.SS \fB--files-from\fR - Read list of source-file names
Adds path/files to an zclone command from a list in a named file.
Zclone processes the path/file names in the order of the list, and
no others.

.PP
Other filter flags (\fB--include\fR, \fB--include-from\fR, \fB--exclude\fR,
\fB--exclude-from\fR, \fB--filter\fR and \fB--filter-from\fR) are ignored when
\fB--files-from\fR, \fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR is used.

.PP
\fB--files-from\fR expects a list of files as its input. Leading or
trailing whitespace is stripped from the input lines. Lines starting
with \fB#\fR or \fB;\fR are ignored.

.PP
\fB--files-from\fR followed by \fB-\fR reads the list of files from standard input.

.PP
Zclone commands with a \fB--files-from\fR flag traverse the remote,
treating the names in \fB--files-from\fR as a set of filters.

.PP
If the \fB--no-traverse\fR and \fB--files-from\fR flags are used together
an zclone command does not traverse the remote. Instead it addresses
each path/file named in the file individually. For each path/file name, that
requires typically 1 API call. This can be efficient for a short \fB--files-from\fR
list and a remote containing many files.

.PP
Zclone commands do not error if any names in the \fB--files-from\fR file are
missing from the source remote.

.PP
The \fB--files-from\fR flag can be repeated in a single zclone command to
read path/file names from more than one file. The files are read from left
to right along the command line.

.PP
Paths within the \fB--files-from\fR file are interpreted as starting
with the root specified in the zclone command.  Leading \fB/\fR separators are
ignored. See --files-from-raw
\[la]#files\-from\-raw\-read\-list\-of\-source\-file\-names\-without\-any\-processing\[ra]
if you need the input to be processed in a raw manner.

.PP
E.g. for a file \fBfiles-from.txt\fR:

.EX
# comment
file1.jpg
subdir/file2.jpg
.EE

.PP
\fBzclone copy --files-from files-from.txt /home/me/pics remote:pics\fR
copies the following, if they exist, and only those files.

.EX
/home/me/pics/file1.jpg        → remote:pics/file1.jpg
/home/me/pics/subdir/file2.jpg → remote:pics/subdir/file2.jpg
.EE

.PP
E.g. to copy the following files referenced by their absolute paths:

.EX
/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect
.EE

.PP
First find a common subdirectory - in this case \fB/home\fR
and put the remaining files in \fBfiles-from.txt\fR with or without
leading \fB/\fR, e.g.

.EX
user1/42
user1/dir/ford
user2/prefect
.EE

.PP
Then copy these to a remote:

.EX
zclone copy --files-from files-from.txt /home remote:backup
.EE

.PP
The three files are transferred as follows:

.EX
/home/user1/42       → remote:backup/user1/important
/home/user1/dir/ford → remote:backup/user1/dir/file
/home/user2/prefect  → remote:backup/user2/stuff
.EE

.PP
Alternatively if \fB/\fR is chosen as root \fBfiles-from.txt\fR will be:

.EX
/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect
.EE

.PP
The copy command will be:

.EX
zclone copy --files-from files-from.txt / remote:backup
.EE

.PP
Then there will be an extra \fBhome\fR directory on the remote:

.EX
/home/user1/42       → remote:backup/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford → remote:backup/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect  → remote:backup/home/user2/prefect
.EE

.SS \fB--files-from-raw\fR - Read list of source-file names without any processing
This flag is the same as \fB--files-from\fR except that input is read in a
raw manner. Lines with leading / trailing whitespace, and lines starting
with \fB;\fR or \fB#\fR are read without any processing. zclone lsf
\[la]//commands/zclone_lsf/\[ra]
has a compatible format that can be used to export file lists from remotes for
input to \fB--files-from-raw\fR\&.

.SS \fB--files-from0\fR - Read NUL separated list of source-file names
This flag is the same as \fB--files-from-raw\fR except that input is
split on NUL (\fB\\0\fR) characters instead of newlines. This allows
handling filenames that contain embedded newline characters.

.PP
It is similar to the \fB-print0\fR option of \fBfind\fR and the \fB-0\fR
option of \fBxargs\fR\&.

.PP
E.g. to copy files listed by \fBfind\fR:

.EX
find /path -print0 | zclone copy --files-from0 - / remote:path
.EE

.SS \fB--ignore-case\fR - make searches case insensitive
By default, zclone filter patterns are case sensitive. The \fB--ignore-case\fR
flag makes all of the filters patterns on the command line case
insensitive.

.PP
E.g. \fB--include "zaphod.txt"\fR does not match a file \fBZaphod.txt\fR\&. With
\fB--ignore-case\fR a match is made.

.SH Quoting shell metacharacters
Zclone commands with filter patterns containing shell metacharacters may
not as work as expected in your shell and may require quoting.

.PP
E.g. linux, OSX (\fB*\fR metacharacter)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--include \\*.jpg\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--include '*.jpg'\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--include='*.jpg'\fR

.PP
Microsoft Windows expansion is done by the command, not shell, so
\fB--include *.jpg\fR does not require quoting.

.PP
If the zclone error
\fBCommand .... needs .... arguments maximum: you provided .... non flag arguments:\fR
is encountered, the cause is commonly spaces within the name of a
remote or flag value. The fix then is to quote values containing spaces.

.SH Other filters
.SS \fB--min-size\fR - Don't transfer any file smaller than this
Controls the minimum size file within the scope of an zclone command.
Default units are \fBKiB\fR but abbreviations \fBB\fR, \fBK\fR, \fBM\fR, \fBG\fR, \fBT\fR or \fBP\fR are valid.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --min-size 50k\fR lists files on \fBremote:\fR of 50 KiB
size or larger.

.PP
See the size option docs
\[la]//docs/#size\-options\[ra] for more info.

.SS \fB--max-size\fR - Don't transfer any file larger than this
Controls the maximum size file within the scope of an zclone command.
Default units are \fBKiB\fR but abbreviations \fBB\fR, \fBK\fR, \fBM\fR, \fBG\fR, \fBT\fR or \fBP\fR are valid.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --max-size 1G\fR lists files on \fBremote:\fR of 1 GiB
size or smaller.

.PP
See the size option docs
\[la]//docs/#size\-options\[ra] for more info.

.SS \fB--max-age\fR - Don't transfer any file older than this
Controls the maximum age of files within the scope of an zclone command.

.PP
\fB--max-age\fR applies only to files and not to directories.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --max-age 2d\fR lists files on \fBremote:\fR of 2 days
old or less.

.PP
See the time option docs
\[la]//docs/#time\-options\[ra] for valid formats.

.SS \fB--min-age\fR - Don't transfer any file younger than this
Controls the minimum age of files within the scope of an zclone command.
(see \fB--max-age\fR for valid formats)

.PP
\fB--min-age\fR applies only to files and not to directories.

.PP
E.g. \fBzclone ls remote: --min-age 2d\fR lists files on \fBremote:\fR of 2 days
old or more.

.PP
See the time option docs
\[la]//docs/#time\-options\[ra] for valid formats.

.SS \fB--hash-filter\fR - Deterministically select a subset of files {#hash-filter}
The \fB--hash-filter\fR flag enables selecting a deterministic subset of files,
useful for:
.IP "  1." 5
Running large sync operations across multiple machines.
.IP "  2." 5
Checking a subset of files for bitrot.
.IP "  3." 5
Any other operations where a sample of files is required.

.SS Syntax
The flag takes two parameters expressed as a fraction:

.EX
--hash-filter K/N
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
\fBN\fR: The total number of partitions (must be a positive integer).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBK\fR: The specific partition to select (an integer from \fB0\fR to \fBN\fR).

.PP
For example:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--hash-filter 1/3\fR: Selects the first third of the files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--hash-filter 2/3\fR and \fB--hash-filter 3/3\fR: Select the second and third
partitions, respectively.

.PP
Each partition is non-overlapping, ensuring all files are covered without duplication.

.SS Random Partition Selection
Use \fB@\fR as \fBK\fR to randomly select a partition:

.EX
--hash-filter @/M
.EE

.PP
For example, \fB--hash-filter @/3\fR will randomly select a number between 0 and 2.
This will stay constant across retries.

.SS How It Works
.IP \(bu 2
Zclone takes each file's full path, normalizes it to lowercase, and applies
Unicode normalization.
.IP \(bu 2
It then hashes the normalized path into a 64 bit number.
.IP \(bu 2
The hash result is reduced modulo \fBN\fR to assign the file to a partition.
.IP \(bu 2
If the calculated partition does not match \fBK\fR the file is excluded.
.IP \(bu 2
Other filters may apply if the file is not excluded.

.PP
\fBImportant:\fP Zclone will traverse all directories to apply the filter.

.SS Usage Notes
.IP \(bu 2
Safe to use with \fBzclone sync\fR; source and destination selections will match.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDo not\fP use with \fB--delete-excluded\fR, as this could delete unselected files.
.IP \(bu 2
Ignored if \fB--files-from\fR, \fB--files-from-raw\fR or \fB--files-from0\fR is used.

.SS Examples
.SS Dividing files into 4 partitions
Assuming the current directory contains \fBfile1.jpg\fR through \fBfile9.jpg\fR:

.EX
$ zclone lsf --hash-filter 0/4 .
file1.jpg
file5.jpg

$ zclone lsf --hash-filter 1/4 .
file3.jpg
file6.jpg
file9.jpg

$ zclone lsf --hash-filter 2/4 .
file2.jpg
file4.jpg

$ zclone lsf --hash-filter 3/4 .
file7.jpg
file8.jpg

$ zclone lsf --hash-filter 4/4 . # the same as --hash-filter 0/4
file1.jpg
file5.jpg
.EE

.SS Syncing the first quarter of files
.EX
zclone sync --hash-filter 1/4 source:path destination:path
.EE

.SS Checking a random 1% of files for integrity
.EX
zclone check --download --hash-filter @/100 source:path destination:path
.EE

.SH Other flags
.SS \fB--delete-excluded\fR - Delete files on dest excluded from sync
\fBImportant\fP this flag is dangerous to your data - use with \fB--dry-run\fR
and \fB-v\fR first.

.PP
In conjunction with \fBzclone sync\fR, \fB--delete-excluded\fR deletes any files
on the destination which are excluded from the command.

.PP
E.g. the scope of \fBzclone sync --interactive A: B:\fR can be restricted:

.EX
zclone --min-size 50k --delete-excluded sync A: B:
.EE

.PP
All files on \fBB:\fR which are less than 50 KiB are deleted
because they are excluded from the zclone sync command.

.SS \fB--dump filters\fR - dump the filters to the output
Dumps the defined filters to standard output in regular expression
format.

.PP
Useful for debugging.

.SH Exclude directory based on a file
The \fB--exclude-if-present\fR flag controls whether a directory is
within the scope of an zclone command based on the presence of a
named file within it. The flag can be repeated to check for
multiple file names, presence of any of them will exclude the
directory.

.PP
This flag has a priority over other filter flags.

.PP
E.g. for the following directory structure:

.EX
dir1/file1
dir1/dir2/file2
dir1/dir2/dir3/file3
dir1/dir2/dir3/.ignore
.EE

.PP
The command \fBzclone ls --exclude-if-present .ignore dir1\fR does
not list \fBdir3\fR, \fBfile3\fR or \fB\&.ignore\fR\&.

.SH Metadata filters {#metadata}
The metadata filters work in a very similar way to the normal file
name filters, except they match metadata
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] on the
object.

.PP
The metadata should be specified as \fBkey=value\fR patterns. This may be
wildcarded using the normal filter patterns
\[la]#patterns\[ra] or regular
expressions
\[la]#regexp\[ra]\&.

.PP
For example if you wished to list only local files with a mode of
\fB100664\fR you could do that with:

.EX
zclone lsf -M --files-only --metadata-include "mode=100664" .
.EE

.PP
Or if you wished to show files with an \fBatime\fR, \fBmtime\fR or \fBbtime\fR at a given date:

.EX
zclone lsf -M --files-only --metadata-include "[abm]time=2022-12-16*" .
.EE

.PP
Like file filtering, metadata filtering only applies to files not to
directories.

.PP
The filters can be applied using these flags.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-include\fR      - Include metadatas matching pattern
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-include-from\fR - Read metadata include patterns from file
(use - to read from stdin)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-exclude\fR      - Exclude metadatas matching pattern
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-exclude-from\fR - Read metadata exclude patterns from file
(use - to read from stdin)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-filter\fR       - Add a metadata filtering rule
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--metadata-filter-from\fR  - Read metadata filtering patterns from a file
(use - to read from stdin)

.PP
Each flag can be repeated. See the section on how filter rules are
applied
\[la]#how\-filter\-rules\-work\[ra] for more details - these flags work
in an identical way to the file name filtering flags, but instead of
file name patterns have metadata patterns.

.SH Common pitfalls
The most frequent filter support issues on
the zclone forum
\[la]/\[ra] are:
.IP \(bu 2
Not using paths relative to the root of the remote
.IP \(bu 2
Not using \fB/\fR to match from the root of a remote
.IP \(bu 2
Not using \fB**\fR to match the contents of a directory


.SH zclone gui
The \fBzclone gui\fR command starts the official Web GUI that comes
bundled with zclone.

.PP
With this command, zclone can serve a web-based GUI (graphical user
interface) that is accessible from a normal web browser.

.PP
Run it in a terminal and zclone will initialize and then start the
GUI.

.EX
zclone gui
.EE

.PP
The terminal window needs to stay open to continue to run the GUI. It will log
separate loopback URLs for the RC API and local control page:

.EX
2026/04/14 11:36:04 NOTICE: Serving remote control on http://127.0.0.1:50803/
2026/04/14 11:36:04 NOTICE: Serving GUI on http://127.0.0.1:50802/
.EE

.PP
You can also add debugging flags when running the GUI, such as \fB-v\fR,
which will show more logging output from the rc server.

.SH Using the GUI
The bundled local control page submits RC API commands. Enter the RC username
and password locally in the page, choose an RC command such as \fBcore/version\fR,
and run it. Credentials are used only for that browser request: they are not
written to the URL, persistent browser storage, or Zclone logs.

.PP
The bundled page is intentionally local and minimal. It does not check for
updates, collect telemetry, fetch external assets, or contact a central web
service.

.SH How it works
When you run \fBzclone gui\fR this is what happens
.IP \(bu 2
Zclone starts the remote control API ("rc").
.IP \(bu 2
Zclone starts a second server to serve the Web GUI.
.IP \(bu 2
Authentication requires \fB--pass\fR unless \fB--no-auth\fR is explicitly set.
.IP \(bu 2
If a username is not specified, Zclone uses \fBgui\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Unless \fB--no-open-browser\fR is passed, a browser window will open.
.IP \(bu 2
The URL contains no credentials.

.SH Security
It's important to think first about what zclone has access to and what
you might be sharing.

.PP
A few good measures:
.IP \(bu 2
Don't use \fB--no-auth\fR except on a trusted local network.
.IP \(bu 2
Do not expose to the local network (eg with \fB--api-addr :5572 --addr
:8080\fR) unless you trust all devices on your local network. Prefer
\fB127.0.0.1\fR or \fBlocalhost\fR (the default).
.IP \(bu 2
Use a strong password and non-obvious usernames like "admin" or
"zclone" if you are using \fB--user\fR and \fB--pass\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
If you expose the GUI beyond loopback, configure a trusted reverse proxy,
TLS, and an explicit \fB--rc-allow-origin\fR\&. Do not expose the RC API directly.

.SH Options
.EX
      --addr stringArray       IPaddress:Port for the GUI server (default auto-chosen localhost port)
      --api-addr stringArray   IPaddress:Port for the RC API server (default auto-chosen localhost port)
      --enable-metrics         Enable OpenMetrics/Prometheus compatible endpoint at /metrics
  -h, --help                   help for gui
      --no-auth                Don't require auth for the RC API
      --no-open-browser        Skip opening the browser automatically
      --pass string            Password for RC authentication
      --user string            User name for RC authentication
.EE

.PP
The GUI bundle ships as a compressed zip embedded in the Zclone binary and is
served from the zip at runtime.


.SH Remote controlling zclone with its API
If zclone is run with the \fB--rc\fR flag then it starts an HTTP server
which can be used to remote control zclone using its API.

.PP
You can either use the rc
\[la]#api\-rc\[ra] command to access the API
or use HTTP directly
\[la]#api\-http\[ra]\&.

.PP
If you just want to run a remote control then see the rcd
\[la]//commands/zclone_rcd/\[ra]
command.

.SH Security {#security}
\fBAccess to the rc API is equivalent to shell access as the user running
zclone.\fP Treat the rc port as you would an interactive login on the host.

.PP
Any caller who can reach the API (and pass authentication, if it is enabled)
can, among other things:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRun OS commands\fP as the zclone user. \fBcore/command\fR re-executes the zclone
binary with arbitrary arguments, and several backend options shell out to
programs, so even creating a remote with \fBconfig/create\fR can lead to command
execution.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRead and write any file\fP reachable by the zclone process, by pointing
\fBoperations/*\fR or \fBsync/*\fR at a \fBlocal\fR remote (or via \fB--rc-files\fR /
\fB--rc-serve\fR). Writing arbitrary files as the zclone user is itself a route to
code execution.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRead back stored credentials.\fP zclone configs routinely hold cloud-provider
secrets. \fBconfig/dump\fR and friends expose them, so a compromise of the rc
reaches every configured backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBChange zclone's runtime behaviour\fP with \fBoptions/set\fR, \fBmanage remotes\fP
with \fBconfig/*\fR, and \fBstop the process\fP with \fBcore/quit\fR\&.

.PP
There is currently no per-endpoint capability or scope system: authentication is
all-or-nothing. Granting any access grants all of the above.

.PP
Consequently:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDo not bind the rc to a network address you do not control.\fP The default
bind is loopback (\fBlocalhost:5572\fR); keep it there unless you have a specific
reason to change it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDo not use \fB--rc-no-auth\fR on a non-loopback bind.\fP It disables
authentication on the endpoints that access remotes — see

\[la]#\-\-rc\-no\-auth\[ra]\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUse authentication and TLS\fP (\fB--rc-user\fR/\fB--rc-pass\fR or \fB--rc-htpasswd\fR,
plus \fB--rc-cert\fR/\fB--rc-key\fR) whenever the port is reachable by anyone you do
not fully trust. Zclone defaults to TLS 1.2 when TLS is enabled.

.SH Supported parameters
.SS --rc
Flag to start the http server listen on remote requests.

.SS --rc-addr=IP
IPaddress:Port or :Port to bind server to. (default "localhost:5572").

.SS --rc-cert=KEY
SSL PEM key (concatenation of certificate and CA certificate).

.SS --rc-client-ca=PATH
Client certificate authority to verify clients with.

.SS --rc-htpasswd=PATH
htpasswd file - if not provided no authentication is done.

.SS --rc-key=PATH
TLS PEM private key file.

.SS --rc-max-header-bytes=VALUE
Maximum size of request header (default 4096).

.SS --rc-min-tls-version=VALUE
The minimum TLS version that is acceptable. Valid values are "tls1.0",
"tls1.1", "tls1.2" and "tls1.3" (default "tls1.2").

.SS --rc-user=VALUE
User name for authentication.

.SS --rc-pass=VALUE
Password for authentication.

.SS --rc-realm=VALUE
Realm for authentication (default "zclone").

.SS --rc-server-read-timeout=DURATION
Timeout for server reading data (default 1h0m0s).

.SS --rc-server-write-timeout=DURATION
Timeout for server writing data (default 1h0m0s).

.SS --rc-serve
Enable the serving of remote objects via the HTTP interface. This
means objects will be accessible at \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:5572/\fR by default,
so you can browse to \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:5572/\fR or \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:5572/*\fR
to see a listing of the remotes.  Objects may be requested from
remotes using this syntax \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:5572/[remote:path]/path/to/object\fR

.PP
Unless the rc server has authentication configured (\fB--rc-user\fR/\fB--rc-pass\fR
or \fB--rc-htpasswd\fR) or the \fB--rc-no-auth\fR flag is set, only remotes already
present in the config file may be served this way. Inline remotes (e.g.
\fB[:webdav,url=...:]\fR), connection string parameters and bare local paths are
rejected, since instantiating them from an unauthenticated request could run
commands or read arbitrary local files.

.PP
Default Off.

.SS global.* connection string options and the rc
Remotes instantiated by the rc do not let connection
string
\[la]//docs/#connection\-strings\[ra] \fBglobal.*\fR options change zclone's
process-wide configuration. Remotes created directly on the command
line or defined in the config file are unaffected.

.PP
A \fBglobal.*\fR option still takes effect for the individual backend it
is set on (exactly like an \fBoverride.*\fR option), it just does not leak
into the global config for the rest of the process.

.SS --rc-serve-no-modtime
Set this flag to skip reading the modification time (can speed things up).

.PP
Default Off.

.SS --rc-files /path/to/directory
Path to local files to serve on the HTTP server.

.PP
If this is set then zclone will serve the files in that directory.  It
will also open the root in the web browser if specified.  This is for
implementing browser based GUIs for zclone functions.

.PP
If authentication is configured, the browser URL never contains credentials.
Use the browser's normal authentication prompt or a reverse proxy that supplies
credentials securely.

.PP
Default Off.

.SS --rc-enable-metrics
Enable OpenMetrics/Prometheus compatible endpoint at \fB/metrics\fR\&.
If more control over the metrics is desired (for example running it on a
different port or with different auth) then endpoint can be enabled with
the \fB--metrics-*\fR flags instead.

.PP
Default Off.

.SS --rc-allow-origin
Set the allowed Access-Control-Allow-Origin for rc requests.

.PP
Use a specific trusted origin when the RC API is accessed by a browser. The
\fBzclone gui\fR command configures its own local origin; its legacy
\fB--rc-web-gui*\fR flags are disabled and have been superseded by that command.

.SS --rc-job-expire-duration=DURATION
Expire finished async jobs older than DURATION (default 60s).

.SS --rc-job-expire-interval=DURATION
Interval duration to check for expired async jobs (default 10s).

.SS --rc-no-auth
By default zclone will require authorisation to have been set up on
the rc interface in order to use any methods which access any zclone
remotes.  Eg \fBoperations/list\fR is denied as it involved creating a
remote as is \fBsync/copy\fR\&.

.PP
If this is set then no authorisation will be required on the server to
use these methods.  The alternative is to use \fB--rc-user\fR and
\fB--rc-pass\fR and use these credentials in the request.

.PP
Default Off.

.SS --rc-baseurl
Prefix for URLs.

.PP
Default is root

.SS --rc-template
User-specified template.

.SH Accessing the remote control via the zclone rc command {#api-rc}
Zclone itself implements the remote control protocol in its \fBzclone
rc\fR command.

.PP
You can use it like this:

.EX
$ zclone rc rc/noop param1=one param2=two
{
    "param1": "one",
    "param2": "two"
}
.EE

.PP
If the remote is running on a different URL than the default
\fBhttp://localhost:5572/\fR, use the \fB--url\fR option to specify it:

.EX
zclone rc --url http://some.remote:1234/ rc/noop
.EE

.PP
Or, if the remote is listening on a Unix socket, use the \fB--unix-socket\fR option
instead:

.EX
zclone rc --unix-socket /tmp/zclone.sock rc/noop
.EE

.PP
Run \fBzclone rc\fR on its own, without any commands, to see the help for the
installed remote control commands. Note that this also needs to connect to the
remote server.

.SH JSON input
\fBzclone rc\fR also supports a \fB--json\fR flag which can be used to send
more complicated input parameters.

.EX
$ zclone rc --json '{ "p1": [1,"2",null,4], "p2": { "a":1, "b":2 } }' rc/noop
{
    "p1": [
        1,
        "2",
        null,
        4
    ],
    "p2": {
        "a": 1,
        "b": 2
    }
}
.EE

.PP
If the parameter being passed is an object then it can be passed as a
JSON string rather than using the \fB--json\fR flag which simplifies the
command line.

.EX
zclone rc operations/list fs=/tmp remote=test opt='{"showHash": true}'
.EE

.PP
Rather than

.EX
zclone rc operations/list --json '{"fs": "/tmp", "remote": "test", "opt": {"showHash": true}}'
.EE

.SH Special parameters
The rc interface supports some special parameters which apply to
\fBall\fP commands.  These start with \fB_\fR to show they are different.

.SS Running asynchronous jobs with _async = true
Each rc call is classified as a job and it is assigned its own id. By default
jobs are executed immediately as they are created or synchronously.

.PP
If \fB_async\fR has a true value when supplied to an rc call then it will
return immediately with a job id and execute id, and the task will be run in the
background. The \fBjob/status\fR call can be used to get information of
the background job. The job can be queried for up to 1 minute after
it has finished.

.PP
It is recommended that potentially long running jobs, e.g. \fBsync/sync\fR,
\fBsync/copy\fR, \fBsync/move\fR, \fBoperations/purge\fR are run with the \fB_async\fR
flag to avoid any potential problems with the HTTP request and
response timing out.

.PP
As an alternative to \fB_async\fR, you can send the HTTP header
\fBPrefer: respond-async\fR (RFC 7240). This has the same effect as
\fB_async = true\fR but additionally returns HTTP status 202 (Accepted)
instead of 200, and includes a \fBPreference-Applied: respond-async\fR
response header. The 202 status code makes it easy for clients to distinguish
an async response from a completed one without inspecting the body.

.PP
Starting a job with the \fB_async\fR flag:

.EX
$ zclone rc --json '{ "p1": [1,"2",null,4], "p2": { "a":1, "b":2 }, "_async": true }' rc/noop
{
    "jobid": 2,
    "executeId": "d794c33c-463e-4acf-b911-f4b23e4f40b7"
}
.EE

.PP
The \fBjobid\fR is a unique identifier for the job within this zclone instance.
The \fBexecuteId\fR identifies the zclone process instance and changes after
zclone restart. Together, the pair (\fBexecuteId\fR, \fBjobid\fR) uniquely identifies
a job across zclone restarts.

.PP
Query the status to see if the job has finished.  For more information
on the meaning of these return parameters see the \fBjob/status\fR call.

.EX
$ zclone rc --json '{ "jobid":2 }' job/status
{
    "duration": 0.000124163,
    "endTime": "2018-10-27T11:38:07.911245881+01:00",
    "error": "",
    "executeId": "d794c33c-463e-4acf-b911-f4b23e4f40b7",
    "finished": true,
    "id": 2,
    "output": {
        "_async": true,
        "p1": [
            1,
            "2",
            null,
            4
        ],
        "p2": {
            "a": 1,
            "b": 2
        }
    },
    "startTime": "2018-10-27T11:38:07.911121728+01:00",
    "success": true
}
.EE

.PP
\fBjob/list\fR can be used to show running or recently completed jobs along with their status

.EX
$ zclone rc job/list
{
    "executeId": "d794c33c-463e-4acf-b911-f4b23e4f40b7",
    "finished_ids": [
        1
    ],
    "jobids": [
        1,
        2
    ],
    "running_ids": [
        2
    ]
}
.EE

.PP
This shows:
- \fBexecuteId\fR - the current zclone instance ID (same for all jobs, changes after restart)
- \fBjobids\fR - array of all job IDs (both running and finished)
- \fBrunning_ids\fR - array of currently running job IDs
- \fBfinished_ids\fR - array of finished job IDs

.SS Setting config flags with _config
If you wish to set config (the equivalent of the global flags) for the
duration of an rc call only then pass in the \fB_config\fR parameter.

.PP
Alternatively, you can pass config options flat at the top level of the parameter map. The option names are the same as their CLI flags without \fB--\fR and with \fB-\fR replaced by \fB_\fR (e.g. \fBtransfers\fR instead of \fBTransfers\fR inside \fB_config\fR).

.PP
This should be in the same format as the \fBmain\fR key returned by
options/get
\[la]#options\-get\[ra]\&.

.EX
zclone rc --loopback options/get blocks=main
.EE

.PP
You can see more help on these options with this command (see the
options blocks section
\[la]#option\-blocks\[ra] for more info).

.EX
zclone rc --loopback options/info blocks=main
.EE

.PP
For example, if you wished to run a sync with the \fB--checksum\fR
parameter, you would pass this parameter in your JSON blob.

.EX
"_config":{"CheckSum": true}
.EE

.PP
Or pass it flat at the top level:

.EX
"checksum": true
.EE

.PP
If using \fBzclone rc\fR this could be passed as

.EX
zclone rc sync/sync ... _config='{"CheckSum": true}'
.EE

.PP
Or simply flat:

.EX
zclone rc sync/sync ... checksum=true
.EE

.PP
If both flat parameters and \fB_config\fR are supplied, the parameters in the legacy \fB_config\fR block will take precedence.

.PP
Any config parameters you don't set will inherit the global defaults
which were set with command line flags or environment variables.

.PP
Note that it is possible to set some values as strings or integers -
see data types
\[la]#data\-types\[ra] for more info. Here is an example
setting the equivalent of \fB--buffer-size\fR in string or integer format.

.EX
"_config":{"BufferSize": "42M"}
"_config":{"BufferSize": 44040192}
.EE

.PP
Or flat:

.EX
"buffer_size": "42M"
"buffer_size": 44040192
.EE

.PP
If you wish to check the \fB_config\fR assignment has worked properly then
calling \fBoptions/local\fR will show what the value got set to.

.SS Setting filter flags with _filter
If you wish to set filters for the duration of an rc call only then
pass in the \fB_filter\fR parameter.

.PP
Alternatively, you can pass filter options flat at the top level of the parameter map. The option names are the same as their CLI flags without \fB--\fR and with \fB-\fR replaced by \fB_\fR (e.g. \fBexclude\fR instead of \fBExclude\fR inside \fB_filter\fR).

.PP
This should be in the same format as the \fBfilter\fR key returned by
options/get
\[la]#options\-get\[ra]\&.

.EX
zclone rc --loopback options/get blocks=filter
.EE

.PP
You can see more help on these options with this command (see the
options blocks section
\[la]#option\-blocks\[ra] for more info).

.EX
zclone rc --loopback options/info blocks=filter
.EE

.PP
For example, if you wished to run a sync with these flags

.EX
--max-size 1M --max-age 42s --include "a" --include "b"
.EE

.PP
you would pass this parameter in your JSON blob.

.EX
"_filter":{"MaxSize":"1M", "IncludeRule":["a","b"], "MaxAge":"42s"}
.EE

.PP
Or pass them flat at the top level:

.EX
"max_size":"1M", "include":["a","b"], "max_age":"42s"
.EE

.PP
If using \fBzclone rc\fR this could be passed as

.EX
zclone rc ... _filter='{"MaxSize":"1M", "IncludeRule":["a","b"], "MaxAge":"42s"}'
.EE

.PP
Or simply flat:

.EX
zclone rc ... max_size=1M include="a,b" max_age=42s
.EE

.PP
If both flat parameters and \fB_filter\fR are supplied, the parameters in the legacy \fB_filter\fR block will take precedence.

.PP
Any filter parameters you don't set will inherit the global defaults
which were set with command line flags or environment variables.

.PP
Note that it is possible to set some values as strings or integers -
see data types
\[la]#data\-types\[ra] for more info. Here is an example
setting the equivalent of \fB--buffer-size\fR in string or integer format.

.EX
"_filter":{"MinSize": "42M"}
"_filter":{"MinSize": 44040192}
.EE

.PP
Or flat:

.EX
"min_size": "42M"
.EE

.PP
If you wish to check the \fB_filter\fR assignment has worked properly then
calling \fBoptions/local\fR will show what the value got set to.

.SS Assigning operations to groups with _group = value
Each rc call has its own stats group for tracking its metrics. By default
grouping is done by the composite group name from prefix \fBjob/\fR and  id of the
job like so \fBjob/1\fR\&.

.PP
If \fB_group\fR has a value then stats for that request will be grouped under that
value. This allows caller to group stats under their own name.

.PP
Stats for specific group can be accessed by passing \fBgroup\fR to \fBcore/stats\fR:

.EX
$ zclone rc --json '{ "group": "job/1" }' core/stats
{
    "speed": 12345
    ...
}
.EE

.SH Data types {#data-types}
When the API returns types, these will mostly be straight forward
integer, string or boolean types.

.PP
However some of the types returned by the options/get
\[la]#options\-get\[ra]
call and taken by the options/set
\[la]#options\-set\[ra] calls as well as the
\fBvfsOpt\fR, \fBmountOpt\fR and the \fB_config\fR parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDuration\fR - these are returned as an integer duration in
nanoseconds. They may be set as an integer, or they may be set with
time string, eg "5s". See the options section
\[la]//docs/#options\[ra] for
more info.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSize\fR - these are returned as an integer number of bytes. They may
be set as an integer or they may be set with a size suffix string,
eg "10M". See the options section
\[la]//docs/#options\[ra] for more info.
.IP \(bu 2
Enumerated type (such as \fBCutoffMode\fR, \fBDumpFlags\fR, \fBLogLevel\fR,
\fBVfsCacheMode\fR - these will be returned as an integer and may be set
as an integer but more conveniently they can be set as a string, eg
"HARD" for \fBCutoffMode\fR or \fBDEBUG\fR for \fBLogLevel\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBandwidthSpec\fR - this will be set and returned as a string, eg
"1M".

.SS Option blocks {#option-blocks}
The calls options/info
\[la]#options\-info\[ra] (for the main config) and
config/providers
\[la]#config\-providers\[ra] (for the backend config) may be
used to get information on the zclone configuration options. This can
be used to build user interfaces for displaying and setting any zclone
option.

.PP
These consist of arrays of \fBOption\fR blocks. These have the following
format. Each block describes a single option.

.TS
allbox;
l l l l 
l l l l .
\fBField\fP	\fBType\fP	\fBOptional\fP	\fBDescription\fP
Name	string	N	T{
name of the option in snake_case
T}
FieldName	string	N	T{
name of the field used in the rc - if blank use Name. May contain "." for nested fields.
T}
Help	string	N	T{
help, started with a single sentence on a single line
T}
Groups	string	Y	T{
groups this option belongs to - comma separated string for options classification
T}
Provider	string	Y	set to filter on provider
Default	any	N	T{
default value, if set (and not to nil or "") then Required does nothing
T}
Value	any	N	value to be set by flags
Examples	Examples	Y	T{
predefined values that can be selected from list (multiple-choice option)
T}
ShortOpt	string	Y	T{
the short command line option for this
T}
Hide	Visibility	N	T{
if non zero, this option is hidden from the configurator or the command line
T}
Required	bool	N	T{
this option is required, meaning value cannot be empty unless there is a default
T}
IsPassword	bool	N	T{
set if the option is a password
T}
NoPrefix	bool	N	T{
set if the option for this should not use the backend prefix
T}
Advanced	bool	N	T{
set if this is an advanced config option
T}
Exclusive	bool	N	T{
set if the answer can only be one of the examples (empty string allowed unless Required or Default is set)
T}
Sensitive	bool	N	T{
set if this option should be redacted when using \fBzclone config redacted\fR
T}
.TE

.PP
An example of this might be the \fB--log-level\fR flag. Note that the
\fBName\fR of the option becomes the command line flag with \fB_\fR replaced
with \fB-\fR\&.

.EX
{
    "Advanced": false,
    "Default": 5,
    "DefaultStr": "NOTICE",
    "Examples": [
        {
            "Help": "",
            "Value": "EMERGENCY"
        },
        {
            "Help": "",
            "Value": "ALERT"
        },
        ...
    ],
    "Exclusive": true,
    "FieldName": "LogLevel",
    "Groups": "Logging",
    "Help": "Log level DEBUG|INFO|NOTICE|ERROR",
    "Hide": 0,
    "IsPassword": false,
    "Name": "log_level",
    "NoPrefix": true,
    "Required": true,
    "Sensitive": false,
    "Type": "LogLevel",
    "Value": null,
    "ValueStr": "NOTICE"
},
.EE

.PP
Note that the \fBHelp\fR may be multiple lines separated by \fB\\n\fR\&. The
first line will always be a short sentence and this is the sentence
shown when running \fBzclone help flags\fR\&.

.SH Specifying remotes to work on
Remotes are specified with the \fBfs=\fR, \fBsrcFs=\fR, \fBdstFs=\fR
parameters depending on the command being used.

.PP
The parameters can be a string as per the rest of zclone, eg
\fBs3:bucket/path\fR or \fB:sftp:/my/dir\fR\&. They can also be specified as
JSON blobs.

.PP
If specifying a JSON blob it should be a object mapping strings to
strings. These values will be used to configure the remote. There are
3 special values which may be set:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype\fR -  set to \fBtype\fR to specify a remote called \fB:type:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB_name\fR - set to \fBname\fR to specify a remote called \fBname:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB_root\fR - sets the root of the remote - may be empty

.PP
One of \fB_name\fR or \fBtype\fR should normally be set. If the \fBlocal\fR
backend is desired then \fBtype\fR should be set to \fBlocal\fR\&. If \fB_root\fR
isn't specified then it defaults to the root of the remote.

.PP
For example this JSON is equivalent to \fBremote:/tmp\fR

.EX
{
    "_name": "remote",
    "_root": "/tmp"
}
.EE

.PP
And this is equivalent to \fB:sftp,host='example.com':/tmp\fR

.EX
{
    "type": "sftp",
    "host": "example.com",
    "_root": "/tmp"
}
.EE

.PP
And this is equivalent to \fB/tmp/dir\fR

.EX
{
    "type": "local",
    "_root": "/tmp/dir"
}
.EE

.SH Supported commands
.SS backend/command: Runs a backend command. {#backend-command}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
command - a string with the command name
.IP \(bu 2
fs - a remote name string e.g. "drive:"
.IP \(bu 2
arg - a list of arguments for the backend command
.IP \(bu 2
opt - a map of string to string of options

.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
result - result from the backend command

.PP
Example:

.EX
zclone rc backend/command command=noop fs=. -o echo=yes -o blue -a path1 -a path2
.EE

.PP
Returns

.EX
{
	"result": {
		"arg": [
			"path1",
			"path2"
		],
		"name": "noop",
		"opt": {
			"blue": "",
			"echo": "yes"
		}
	}
}
.EE

.PP
Note that this is the direct equivalent of using this "backend"
command:

.EX
zclone backend noop . -o echo=yes -o blue path1 path2
.EE

.PP
Note that arguments must be preceded by the "-a" flag

.PP
See the backend
\[la]//commands/zclone_backend/\[ra] command for more information.

.SS cache/expire: Purge a remote from cache {#cache-expire}
Purge a remote from the cache backend. Supports either a directory or a file.
Params:
  - remote = path to remote (required)
  - withData = true/false to delete cached data (chunks) as well (optional)

.PP
Eg

.EX
zclone rc cache/expire remote=path/to/sub/folder/
zclone rc cache/expire remote=/ withData=true
.EE

.SS cache/fetch: Fetch file chunks {#cache-fetch}
Ensure the specified file chunks are cached on disk.

.PP
The chunks= parameter specifies the file chunks to check.
It takes a comma separated list of array slice indices.
The slice indices are similar to Python slices: start[:end]

.PP
start is the 0 based chunk number from the beginning of the file
to fetch inclusive. end is 0 based chunk number from the beginning
of the file to fetch exclusive.
Both values can be negative, in which case they count from the back
of the file. The value "-5:" represents the last 5 chunks of a file.

.PP
Some valid examples are:
":5,-5:" -> the first and last five chunks
"0,-2" -> the first and the second last chunk
"0:10" -> the first ten chunks

.PP
Any parameter with a key that starts with "file" can be used to
specify files to fetch, e.g.

.EX
zclone rc cache/fetch chunks=0 file=hello file2=home/goodbye
.EE

.PP
File names will automatically be encrypted when the a crypt remote
is used on top of the cache.

.SS cache/stats: Get cache stats {#cache-stats}
Show statistics for the cache remote.

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS config/create: create the config for a remote. {#config-create}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters - a map of { "key": "value" } pairs
.IP \(bu 2
type - type of the new remote
.IP \(bu 2
opt - a dictionary of options to control the configuration
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
obscure - declare passwords are plain and need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noObscure - declare passwords are already obscured and don't need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noOutput - don't print anything to stdout
.IP \(bu 2
nonInteractive - don't interact with a user, return questions
.IP \(bu 2
continue - continue the config process with an answer
.IP \(bu 2
all - ask all the config questions not just the post config ones
.IP \(bu 2
state - state to restart with - used with continue
.IP \(bu 2
result - result to restart with - used with continue
.RE

.PP
See the config create
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_create/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/delete: Delete a remote in the config file. {#config-delete}
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote to delete

.PP
See the config delete
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_delete/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/dump: Dumps the config file. {#config-dump}
Returns a JSON object:
- key: value

.PP
Where keys are remote names and values are the config parameters.

.PP
See the config dump
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_dump/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/get: Get a remote in the config file. {#config-get}
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote to get

.PP
See the config dump
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_dump/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/listremotes: Lists the remotes in the config file and defined in environment variables. {#config-listremotes}
Returns
- remotes - array of remote names

.PP
See the listremotes
\[la]//commands/zclone_listremotes/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/oauthstatus: Get the status of the OAuth authentication server. {#config-oauthstatus}
Returns the current status of the OAuth authentication server.

.PP
Returns a JSON object:
- status - "running" or "stopped"
- authUrl - URL for the authorization (only if status is "running")

.PP
Eg

.EX
{
    "status": "running",
    "authUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=..."
}
.EE

.SS config/oauthstop: Stop any running OAuth authentication server. {#config-oauthstop}
Stops the OAuth authentication server if one is running.

.PP
This can be used to recover from an interrupted OAuth flow without
restarting zclone. If no OAuth authentication is in progress, an error
is returned.

.SS config/password: password the config for a remote. {#config-password}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters - a map of { "key": "value" } pairs

.PP
See the config password
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_password/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/paths: Reads the config file path and other important paths. {#config-paths}
Returns a JSON object with the following keys:
.IP \(bu 2
config: path to config file
.IP \(bu 2
cache: path to root of cache directory
.IP \(bu 2
temp: path to root of temporary directory

.PP
Eg

.EX
{
    "cache": "/home/USER/.cache/zclone",
    "config": "/home/USER/.zclone.conf",
    "temp": "/tmp"
}
.EE

.PP
See the config paths
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_paths/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/providers: Shows how providers are configured in the config file. {#config-providers}
Returns a JSON object:
- providers - array of objects

.PP
See the config providers
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_providers/\[ra] command
for more information on the above.

.PP
Note that the Options blocks are in the same format as returned by
"options/info". They are described in the
option blocks
\[la]#option\-blocks\[ra] section.

.SS config/setpath: Set the path of the config file {#config-setpath}
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
path - path to the config file to use

.SS config/unlock: Unlock the config file. {#config-unlock}
Unlocks the config file if it is locked.

.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\&'configPassword' - password to unlock the config file

.PP
A good idea is to disable AskPassword before making this call

.SS config/unset: Unset keys in a remote in the config file. {#config-unset}
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
keys - a list of key names to remove

.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
removed - a list of the keys that were actually removed

.PP
See the config unset
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_unset/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS config/update: update the config for a remote. {#config-update}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name - name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters - a map of { "key": "value" } pairs
.IP \(bu 2
opt - a dictionary of options to control the configuration
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
obscure - declare passwords are plain and need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noObscure - declare passwords are already obscured and don't need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noOutput - don't print anything to stdout
.IP \(bu 2
nonInteractive - don't interact with a user, return questions
.IP \(bu 2
continue - continue the config process with an answer
.IP \(bu 2
all - ask all the config questions not just the post config ones
.IP \(bu 2
state - state to restart with - used with continue
.IP \(bu 2
result - result to restart with - used with continue
.RE

.PP
See the config update
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_update/\[ra] command for more information on the above.

.SS core/bwlimit: Set the bandwidth limit. {#core-bwlimit}
This sets the bandwidth limit to the string passed in. This should be
a single bandwidth limit entry or a pair of upload:download bandwidth.

.PP
Eg

.EX
zclone rc core/bwlimit rate=off
{
    "bytesPerSecond": -1,
    "bytesPerSecondTx": -1,
    "bytesPerSecondRx": -1,
    "rate": "off"
}
zclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M
{
    "bytesPerSecond": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondTx": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondRx": 1048576,
    "rate": "1M"
}
zclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M:100k
{
    "bytesPerSecond": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondTx": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondRx": 131072,
    "rate": "1M"
}
.EE

.PP
If the rate parameter is not supplied then the bandwidth is queried

.EX
zclone rc core/bwlimit
{
    "bytesPerSecond": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondTx": 1048576,
    "bytesPerSecondRx": 1048576,
    "rate": "1M"
}
.EE

.PP
The format of the parameter is exactly the same as passed to --bwlimit
except only one bandwidth may be specified.

.PP
In either case "rate" is returned as a human-readable string, and
"bytesPerSecond" is returned as a number.

.SS core/command: Run a zclone terminal command over rc. {#core-command}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
command - a string with the command name.
.IP \(bu 2
arg - a list of arguments for the backend command.
.IP \(bu 2
opt - a map of string to string of options.
.IP \(bu 2
returnType - one of ("COMBINED_OUTPUT", "STREAM", "STREAM_ONLY_STDOUT", "STREAM_ONLY_STDERR").
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Defaults to "COMBINED_OUTPUT" if not set.
.IP \(bu 2
The STREAM returnTypes will write the output to the body of the HTTP message.
.IP \(bu 2
The COMBINED_OUTPUT will write the output to the "result" parameter.
.RE

.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
result - result from the backend command.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Only set when using returnType "COMBINED_OUTPUT".
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
error	 - set if zclone exits with an error code.
.IP \(bu 2
returnType - one of ("COMBINED_OUTPUT", "STREAM", "STREAM_ONLY_STDOUT", "STREAM_ONLY_STDERR").

.PP
Example:

.EX
zclone rc core/command command=ls -a mydrive:/ -o max-depth=1
zclone rc core/command -a ls -a mydrive:/ -o max-depth=1
.EE

.PP
Returns:

.EX
{
	"error": false,
	"result": "<Raw command line output>"
}

OR
{
	"error": true,
	"result": "<Raw command line output>"
}

.EE

.SS core/disks: List the local disks {#core-disks}
This does not take any parameters

.PP
This call is for zclone GUI programs to enumerate local disks and
important directories for doing transfers to and from. The list
returned will include the root directory and the user's home directory
and any mounted disks. The returned items should be usable directly as
remotes.

.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
disks
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
This is an array of strings of local disk names
.RE

.SS core/du: Returns disk usage of a locally attached disk. {#core-du}
This returns the disk usage for the local directory passed in as dir.

.PP
If the directory is not passed in, it defaults to the directory
pointed to by --cache-dir.
.IP \(bu 2
dir - string (optional)

.PP
Returns:

.EX
{
	"dir": "/",
	"info": {
		"Available": 361769115648,
		"Free": 361785892864,
		"Total": 982141468672
	}
}
.EE

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS core/gc: Runs a garbage collection. {#core-gc}
This tells the go runtime to do a garbage collection run.  It isn't
necessary to call this normally, but it can be useful for debugging
memory problems.

.SS core/group-list: Returns list of stats. {#core-group-list}
This returns list of stats groups currently in memory.

.PP
Returns the following values:

.EX
{
	"groups":  an array of group names:
		[
			"group1",
			"group2",
			...
		]
}
.EE

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS core/memstats: Returns the memory statistics {#core-memstats}
This returns the memory statistics of the running program.  What the values mean
are explained in the go docs: https://golang.org/pkg/runtime/#MemStats

.PP
The most interesting values for most people are:
.IP \(bu 2
HeapAlloc - this is the amount of memory zclone is actually using
.IP \(bu 2
HeapSys - this is the amount of memory zclone has obtained from the OS
.IP \(bu 2
Sys - this is the total amount of memory requested from the OS
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
It is virtual memory so may include unused memory
.RE

.SS core/obscure: Obscures a string passed in. {#core-obscure}
Pass a clear string and zclone will obscure it for the config file:
- clear - string

.PP
Returns:
- obscured - string

.SS core/pid: Return PID of current process {#core-pid}
This returns PID of current process.
Useful for stopping zclone process.

.SS core/quit: Terminates the app. {#core-quit}
(Optional) Pass an exit code to be used for terminating the app:
- exitCode - int

.SS core/stats: Returns stats about current transfers. {#core-stats}
This returns all available stats:

.EX
zclone rc core/stats
.EE

.PP
If group is not provided then summed up stats for all groups will be
returned.

.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group - name of the stats group (string, optional)
.IP \(bu 2
short - if true will not return the transferring and checking arrays (boolean, optional)

.PP
Returns the following values:

.EX
{
	"bytes": total transferred bytes since the start of the group,
	"checks": number of files checked,
	"deletedDirs": number of directories deleted,
	"deletes" : number of files deleted,
	"elapsedTime": time in floating point seconds since zclone was started,
	"errors": number of errors,
	"eta": estimated time in seconds until the group completes,
	"fatalError": boolean whether there has been at least one fatal error,
	"lastError": last error string,
	"renames" : number of files renamed,
	"listed" : number of directory entries listed,
	"retryError": boolean showing whether there has been at least one non-NoRetryError,
        "serverSideCopies": number of server side copies done,
        "serverSideCopyBytes": number bytes server side copied,
        "serverSideMoves": number of server side moves done,
        "serverSideMoveBytes": number bytes server side moved,
	"speed": average speed in bytes per second since start of the group,
	"totalBytes": total number of bytes in the group,
	"totalChecks": total number of checks in the group,
	"totalTransfers": total number of transfers in the group,
	"transferTime" : total time spent on running jobs,
	"transfers": number of transferred files,
	"transferring": an array of currently active file transfers:
		[
			{
				"bytes": total transferred bytes for this file,
				"eta": estimated time in seconds until file transfer completion
				"name": name of the file,
				"percentage": progress of the file transfer in percent,
				"speed": average speed over the whole transfer in bytes per second,
				"speedAvg": current speed in bytes per second as an exponentially weighted moving average,
				"size": size of the file in bytes
			}
		],
	"checking": an array of names of currently active file checks
		[]
}
.EE

.PP
Values for "transferring", "checking" and "lastError" are only assigned if data is available.
The value for "eta" is null if an eta cannot be determined.

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS core/stats-delete: Delete stats group. {#core-stats-delete}
This deletes entire stats group.

.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group - name of the stats group (string)

.SS core/stats-reset: Reset stats. {#core-stats-reset}
This clears counters, errors and finished transfers for all stats or specific
stats group if group is provided.

.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group - name of the stats group (string)

.SS core/transferred: Returns stats about completed transfers. {#core-transferred}
This returns stats about completed transfers:

.EX
zclone rc core/transferred
.EE

.PP
If group is not provided then completed transfers for all groups will be
returned.

.PP
Note only the last 100 completed transfers are returned.

.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group - name of the stats group (string)

.PP
Returns the following values:

.EX
{
	"transferred":  an array of completed transfers (including failed ones):
		[
			{
				"name": name of the file,
				"size": size of the file in bytes,
				"bytes": total transferred bytes for this file,
				"checked": if the transfer is only checked (skipped, deleted),
				"what": the purpose of the transfer (transferring, deleting, checking, importing, hashing, merging, listing, moving, renaming),
				"timestamp": integer representing millisecond unix epoch,
				"error": string description of the error (empty if successful),
				"jobid": id of the job that this transfer belongs to
			}
		]
}
.EE

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS core/version: Shows the current version of zclone, Go and the OS. {#core-version}
This shows the current versions of zclone, Go and the OS:
.IP \(bu 2
version - zclone version, e.g. "v1.71.2"
.IP \(bu 2
decomposed - version number as [major, minor, patch]
.IP \(bu 2
isGit - boolean - true if this was compiled from the git version
.IP \(bu 2
isBeta - boolean - true if this is a beta version
.IP \(bu 2
os - OS in use as according to Go GOOS (e.g. "linux")
.IP \(bu 2
osKernel - OS Kernel version (e.g. "6.8.0-86-generic (x86_64)")
.IP \(bu 2
osVersion -  OS Version (e.g. "ubuntu 24.04 (64 bit)")
.IP \(bu 2
osArch - cpu architecture in use (e.g. "arm64 (ARMv8 compatible)")
.IP \(bu 2
arch - cpu architecture in use according to Go GOARCH (e.g. "arm64")
.IP \(bu 2
goVersion - version of Go runtime in use (e.g. "go1.25.0")
.IP \(bu 2
linking - type of zclone executable (static or dynamic)
.IP \(bu 2
goTags - space separated build tags or "none"

.PP
\fBAuthentication is not required for this call.\fP

.SS debug/set-block-profile-rate: Set runtime.SetBlockProfileRate for blocking profiling. {#debug-set-block-profile-rate}
SetBlockProfileRate controls the fraction of goroutine blocking events
that are reported in the blocking profile. The profiler aims to sample
an average of one blocking event per rate nanoseconds spent blocked.

.PP
To include every blocking event in the profile, pass rate = 1. To turn
off profiling entirely, pass rate <= 0.

.PP
After calling this you can use this to see the blocking profile:

.EX
go tool pprof http://localhost:5572/debug/pprof/block
.EE

.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
rate - int

.SS debug/set-gc-percent: Call runtime/debug.SetGCPercent for setting the garbage collection target percentage. {#debug-set-gc-percent}
SetGCPercent sets the garbage collection target percentage: a collection is triggered
when the ratio of freshly allocated data to live data remaining after the previous collection
reaches this percentage. SetGCPercent returns the previous setting. The initial setting is the
value of the GOGC environment variable at startup, or 100 if the variable is not set.

.PP
This setting may be effectively reduced in order to maintain a memory limit.
A negative percentage effectively disables garbage collection, unless the memory limit is reached.

.PP
See https://pkg.go.dev/runtime/debug#SetMemoryLimit for more details.

.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
gc-percent - int

.SS debug/set-mutex-profile-fraction: Set runtime.SetMutexProfileFraction for mutex profiling. {#debug-set-mutex-profile-fraction}
SetMutexProfileFraction controls the fraction of mutex contention
events that are reported in the mutex profile. On average 1/rate
events are reported. The previous rate is returned.

.PP
To turn off profiling entirely, pass rate 0. To just read the current
rate, pass rate < 0. (For n>1 the details of sampling may change.)

.PP
Once this is set you can look use this to profile the mutex contention:

.EX
go tool pprof http://localhost:5572/debug/pprof/mutex
.EE

.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
rate - int

.PP
Results:
.IP \(bu 2
previousRate - int

.SS debug/set-soft-memory-limit: Call runtime/debug.SetMemoryLimit for setting a soft memory limit for the runtime. {#debug-set-soft-memory-limit}
SetMemoryLimit provides the runtime with a soft memory limit.

.PP
The runtime undertakes several processes to try to respect this memory limit, including
adjustments to the frequency of garbage collections and returning memory to the underlying
system more aggressively. This limit will be respected even if GOGC=off (or, if SetGCPercent(-1) is executed).

.PP
The input limit is provided as bytes, and includes all memory mapped, managed, and not
released by the Go runtime. Notably, it does not account for space used by the Go binary
and memory external to Go, such as memory managed by the underlying system on behalf of
the process, or memory managed by non-Go code inside the same process.
Examples of excluded memory sources include: OS kernel memory held on behalf of the process,
memory allocated by C code, and memory mapped by syscall.Mmap (because it is not managed by the Go runtime).

.PP
A zero limit or a limit that's lower than the amount of memory used by the Go runtime may cause
the garbage collector to run nearly continuously. However, the application may still make progress.

.PP
The memory limit is always respected by the Go runtime, so to effectively disable this behavior,
set the limit very high. math.MaxInt64 is the canonical value for disabling the limit, but values
much greater than the available memory on the underlying system work just as well.

.PP
See https://go.dev/doc/gc-guide for a detailed guide explaining the soft memory limit in more detail,
as well as a variety of common use-cases and scenarios.

.PP
SetMemoryLimit returns the previously set memory limit. A negative input does not adjust the limit,
and allows for retrieval of the currently set memory limit.

.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
mem-limit - int

.SS fscache/clear: Clear the Fs cache. {#fscache-clear}
This clears the fs cache. This is where remotes created from backends
are cached for a short while to make repeated rc calls more efficient.

.PP
If you change the parameters of a backend then you may want to call
this to clear an existing remote out of the cache before re-creating
it.

.SS fscache/entries: Returns the number of entries in the fs cache. {#fscache-entries}
This returns the number of entries in the fs cache.

.PP
Returns
- entries - number of items in the cache

.SS job/batch: Run a batch of zclone rc commands concurrently. {#job-batch}
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
concurrency - int - do this many commands concurrently. Defaults to \fB--transfers\fR if not set.
.IP \(bu 2
inputs - an list of inputs to the commands with an extra \fB_path\fR parameter

.EX
{
    "_path": "rc/path",
    "param1": "parameter for the path as documented",
    "param2": "parameter for the path as documented, etc",
}
.EE

.PP
The inputs may use \fB_async\fR, \fB_group\fR, \fB_config\fR and \fB_filter\fR as normal when using the rc.

.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
results - a list of results from the commands with one entry for each in inputs.

.PP
For example:

.EX
zclone rc job/batch --json '{
  "inputs": [
    {
      "_path": "rc/noop",
      "parameter": "OK"
    },
    {
      "_path": "rc/error",
      "parameter": "BAD"
    }
  ]
}
\&'
.EE

.PP
Gives the result:

.EX
{
  "results": [
    {
      "parameter": "OK"
    },
    {
      "error": "arbitrary error on input map[parameter:BAD]",
      "input": {
        "parameter": "BAD"
      },
      "path": "rc/error",
      "status": 500
    }
  ]
}
.EE

