.SS Invalid UTF-8 bytes {#invalid-utf8}
Some backends only support a sequence of well formed UTF-8 bytes
as file or directory names.

.PP
In this case all invalid UTF-8 bytes will be replaced with a quoted
representation of the byte value to allow uploading a file to such a
backend. For example, the invalid byte \fB0xFE\fR will be encoded as \fB‛FE\fR\&.

.PP
A common source of invalid UTF-8 bytes are local filesystems, that store
names in a different encoding than UTF-8 or UTF-16, like latin1. See the
local filenames
\[la]//local/#filenames\[ra] section for details.

.SS Encoding option {#encoding}
Most backends have an encoding option, specified as a flag
\fB--backend-encoding\fR where \fBbackend\fR is the name of the backend, or as
a config parameter \fBencoding\fR (you'll need to select the Advanced
config in \fBzclone config\fR to see it).

.PP
This will have default value which encodes and decodes characters in
such a way as to preserve the maximum number of characters (see
above).

.PP
However this can be incorrect in some scenarios, for example if you
have a Windows file system with Unicode fullwidth characters
\fB＊\fR, \fB？\fR or \fB：\fR, that you want to remain as those characters on the
remote rather than being translated to regular (halfwidth) \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR and \fB:\fR\&.

.PP
The \fB--backend-encoding\fR flags allow you to change that. You can
disable the encoding completely with \fB--backend-encoding Raw\fR or set
\fBencoding = Raw\fR in the config file.

.PP
Encoding takes a comma separated list of encodings. You can see the
list of all possible values by passing an invalid value to this
flag, e.g. \fB--local-encoding "help"\fR\&. The command \fBzclone help flags encoding\fR
will show you the defaults for the backends.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBEncoding\fP	\fBCharacters\fP	\fBEncoded as\fP
Asterisk	\fB*\fR	\fB＊\fR
BackQuote	\fB`\fR	\fB｀\fR
BackSlash	\fB\\\fR	\fB＼\fR
Colon	\fB:\fR	\fB：\fR
CrLf	CR 0x0D, LF 0x0A	\fB␍\fR, \fB␊\fR
Ctl	T{
All control characters 0x00-0x1F
T}	\fB␀␁␂␃␄␅␆␇␈␉␊␋␌␍␎␏␐␑␒␓␔␕␖␗␘␙␚␛␜␝␞␟\fR
Del	DEL 0x7F	\fB␡\fR
Dollar	\fB$\fR	\fB＄\fR
Dot	\fB\&.\fR or \fB\&..\fR as entire string	\fB．\fR, \fB．．\fR
DoubleQuote	\fB"\fR	\fB＂\fR
Exclamation	\fB!\fR	\fB！\fR
Hash	\fB#\fR	\fB＃\fR
InvalidUtf8	T{
An invalid UTF-8 character (e.g. latin1)
T}	\fB�\fR
LeftCrLfHtVt	T{
CR 0x0D, LF 0x0A, HT 0x09, VT 0x0B on the left of a string
T}	\fB␍\fR, \fB␊\fR, \fB␉\fR, \fB␋\fR
LeftPeriod	\fB\&.\fR on the left of a string	\fB\&.\fR
LeftSpace	SPACE on the left of a string	\fB␠\fR
LeftTilde	\fB~\fR on the left of a string	\fB～\fR
LtGt	\fB<\fR, \fB>\fR	\fB＜\fR, \fB＞\fR
None ¹	NUL 0x00	␀
Percent	\fB%\fR	\fB％\fR
Pipe	|	\fB｜\fR
Question	\fB?\fR	\fB？\fR
RightCrLfHtVt	T{
CR 0x0D, LF 0x0A, HT 0x09, VT 0x0B on the right of a string
T}	\fB␍\fR, \fB␊\fR, \fB␉\fR, \fB␋\fR
RightPeriod	\fB\&.\fR on the right of a string	\fB\&.\fR
RightSpace	SPACE on the right of a string	\fB␠\fR
Semicolon	\fB;\fR	\fB；\fR
SingleQuote	\fB\&'\fR	\fB＇\fR
Slash	\fB/\fR	\fB／\fR
SquareBracket	\fB[\fR, \fB]\fR	\fB［\fR, \fB］\fR
.TE

.PP
¹ Encoding from NUL 0x00 to ␀ is always implicit except when using Raw.
It was previously incorrectly documented as disabling encoding,
and to maintain backward compatibility, its behavior has not been changed.

.SS Encoding example: FTP
To take a specific example, the FTP backend's default encoding is

.EX
--ftp-encoding "Slash,Del,Ctl,RightSpace,Dot"
.EE

.PP
However, let's say the FTP server is running on Windows and can't have
any of the invalid Windows characters in file names. You are backing
up Linux servers to this FTP server which do have those characters in
file names. So you would add the Windows set which are

.EX
Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot
.EE

.PP
to the existing ones, giving:

.EX
Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot,Del,RightSpace
.EE

.PP
This can be specified using the \fB--ftp-encoding\fR flag or using an \fBencoding\fR
parameter in the config file.

.SS Encoding example: Windows
As another example, take a Windows system where there is a file with
name \fBTest：1.jpg\fR, where \fB：\fR is the Unicode fullwidth colon symbol.
When using zclone to copy this to a remote which supports \fB:\fR,
the regular (halfwidth) colon (such as Google Drive), you will notice
that the file gets renamed to \fBTest:1.jpg\fR\&.

.PP
To avoid this you can change the set of characters zclone should convert
for the local filesystem, using command-line argument \fB--local-encoding\fR\&.
Zclone's default behavior on Windows corresponds to

.EX
--local-encoding "Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot"
.EE

.PP
If you want to use fullwidth characters \fB：\fR, \fB＊\fR and \fB？\fR in your filenames
without zclone changing them when uploading to a remote, then set the same as
the default value but without \fBColon,Question,Asterisk\fR:

.EX
--local-encoding "Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot"
.EE

.PP
Alternatively, you can disable the conversion of any characters with
\fB--local-encoding Raw\fR\&.

.PP
Instead of using command-line argument \fB--local-encoding\fR, you may also set it
as environment variable
\[la]//docs/#environment\-variables\[ra] \fBZCLONE_LOCAL_ENCODING\fR,
or configure
\[la]//docs/#configure\[ra] a remote of type \fBlocal\fR in your config,
and set the \fBencoding\fR option there.

.PP
The risk by doing this is that if you have a filename with the regular (halfwidth)
\fB:\fR, \fB*\fR and \fB?\fR in your cloud storage, and you try to download
it to your Windows filesystem, this will fail. These characters are not
valid in filenames on Windows, and you have told zclone not to work around
this by converting them to valid fullwidth variants.

.SS MIME Type
MIME types (also known as media types) classify types of documents
using a simple text classification, e.g. \fBtext/html\fR or
\fBapplication/pdf\fR\&.

.PP
Some cloud storage systems support reading (\fBR\fR) the MIME type of
objects and some support writing (\fBW\fR) the MIME type of objects.

.PP
The MIME type can be important if you are serving files directly to
HTTP from the storage system.

.PP
If you are copying from a remote which supports reading (\fBR\fR) to a
remote which supports writing (\fBW\fR) then zclone will preserve the MIME
types.  Otherwise they will be guessed from the extension, or the
remote itself may assign the MIME type.

.SS Metadata
Backends may or may support reading or writing metadata. They may
support reading and writing system metadata (metadata intrinsic to
that backend) and/or user metadata (general purpose metadata).

.PP
The levels of metadata support are

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBKey\fP	\fBExplanation\fP
\fBR\fR	T{
Read only System Metadata on files only
T}
\fBRW\fR	T{
Read and write System Metadata on files only
T}
\fBRWU\fR	T{
Read and write System Metadata and read and write User Metadata on files only
T}
\fBDR\fR	T{
Read only System Metadata on files and directories
T}
\fBDRW\fR	T{
Read and write System Metadata on files and directories
T}
\fBDRWU\fR	T{
Read and write System Metadata and read and write User Metadata on files and directories
T}
.TE

.PP
See the metadata docs
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] for more info.

.SH Optional Features
All zclone remotes support a base command set. Other features depend
upon backend-specific capabilities.

.SS Purge
This deletes a directory quicker than just deleting all the files in
the directory.

.SS Copy
Used when copying an object to and from the same remote.  This known
as a server-side copy so you can copy a file without downloading it
and uploading it again.  It is used if you use \fBzclone copy\fR or
\fBzclone move\fR if the remote doesn't support \fBMove\fR directly.

.PP
If the server doesn't support \fBCopy\fR directly then for copy operations
the file is downloaded then re-uploaded.

.SS Move
Used when moving/renaming an object on the same remote.  This is known
as a server-side move of a file.  This is used in \fBzclone move\fR if the
server doesn't support \fBDirMove\fR\&.

.PP
If the server isn't capable of \fBMove\fR then zclone simulates it with
\fBCopy\fR then delete.  If the server doesn't support \fBCopy\fR then zclone
will download the file and re-upload it.

.SS DirMove
This is used to implement \fBzclone move\fR to move a directory if
possible.  If it isn't then it will use \fBMove\fR on each file (which
falls back to \fBCopy\fR then download and upload - see \fBMove\fR section).

.SS CleanUp
This is used for emptying the trash for a remote by \fBzclone cleanup\fR\&.

.PP
If the server can't do \fBCleanUp\fR then \fBzclone cleanup\fR will return an
error.

.PP
‡‡ Note that while Box implements this it has to delete every file
individually so it will be slower than emptying the trash via the WebUI

.SS ListR
The remote supports a recursive list to list all the contents beneath
a directory quickly.  This enables the \fB--fast-list\fR flag to work.
See the zclone docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.SS StreamUpload
Some remotes allow files to be uploaded without knowing the file size
in advance. This allows certain operations to work without spooling the
file to local disk first, e.g. \fBzclone rcat\fR\&.

.SS MultithreadUpload
Some remotes allow transfers to the remote to be sent as chunks in
parallel. If this is supported then zclone will use multi-thread
copying to transfer files much faster.

.SS LinkSharing
Sets the necessary permissions on a file or folder and prints a link
that allows others to access them, even if they don't have an account
on the particular cloud provider.

.SS About
Zclone \fBabout\fR prints quota information for a remote. Typical output
includes bytes used, free, quota and in trash.

.PP
If a remote lacks about capability \fBzclone about remote:\fRreturns
an error.

.PP
Backends without about capability cannot determine free space for an
zclone mount, or use policy \fBmfs\fR (most free space) as a member of an
zclone union remote.

.PP
See zclone about command
\[la]///commands/zclone_about/\[ra]

.SS EmptyDir
The remote supports empty directories. See Limitations
\[la]//bugs/#limitations\[ra]
 for details. Most Object/Bucket-based remotes do not support this.


.SH Tiers
Zclone backends are divided into tiers to give users an idea of the stability of each backend.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBTier\fP	\fBLabel\fP	\fBIntended meaning\fP
	Core	Production-grade, first-class
	Stable	Well-supported, minor gaps
	Supported	T{
Works for many uses; known caveats
T}
	Experimental	T{
Use with care; expect gaps/changes
T}
	Deprecated	T{
No longer maintained or supported
T}
.TE

