.SH Docker Volume Plugin
.SH Introduction
Docker 1.9 has added support for creating
named volumes
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/storage/volumes/\[ra] via
command-line interface
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/volume_create/\[ra]
and mounting them in containers as a way to share data between them.
Since Docker 1.10 you can create named volumes with
Docker Compose
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/\[ra] by descriptions in
docker-compose.yml
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose\-file/compose\-file\-v2/#volume\-configuration\-reference\[ra]
files for use by container groups on a single host.
As of Docker 1.12 volumes are supported by
Docker Swarm
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/key\-concepts/\[ra]
included with Docker Engine and created from descriptions in
swarm compose v3
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose\-file/compose\-file\-v3/#volume\-configuration\-reference\[ra]
files for use with \fIswarm stacks\fP across multiple cluster nodes.

.PP
Docker Volume Plugins
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/plugins_volume/\[ra]
augment the default \fBlocal\fR volume driver included in Docker with stateful
volumes shared across containers and hosts. Unlike local volumes, your
data will \fInot\fP be deleted when such volume is removed. Plugins can run
managed by the docker daemon, as a native system service
(under systemd, \fIsysv\fP or \fIupstart\fP) or as a standalone executable.
Zclone can run as docker volume plugin in all these modes.
It interacts with the local docker daemon
via plugin API
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/plugin_api/\[ra] and
handles mounting of remote file systems into docker containers so it must
run on the same host as the docker daemon or on every Swarm node.

.SH Getting started
In the first example we will use the SFTP
\[la]//sftp/\[ra]
zclone volume with Docker engine on a standalone Ubuntu machine.

.PP
Start from installing Docker
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/\[ra]
on the host.

.PP
The \fIFUSE\fP driver is a prerequisite for zclone mounting and should be
installed on host:

.EX
sudo apt-get -y install fuse3
.EE

.PP
Create two directories required by zclone docker plugin:

.EX
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/config
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/cache
.EE

.PP
Install the managed zclone docker plugin for your architecture (here \fBamd64\fR):

.EX
docker plugin install zclone/docker-volume-zclone:amd64 args="-v" --alias zclone --grant-all-permissions
docker plugin list
.EE

.PP
Create your SFTP volume
\[la]//sftp/#standard\-options\[ra]:

.EX
docker volume create firstvolume -d zclone -o type=sftp -o sftp-host=_hostname_ -o sftp-user=_username_ -o sftp-pass=_password_ -o allow-other=true
.EE

.PP
Note that since all options are static, you don't even have to run
\fBzclone config\fR or create the \fBzclone.conf\fR file (but the \fBconfig\fR directory
should still be present). In the simplest case you can use \fBlocalhost\fR
as \fIhostname\fP and your SSH credentials as \fIusername\fP and \fIpassword\fP\&.
You can also change the remote path to your home directory on the host,
for example \fB-o path=/home/username\fR\&.

.PP
Time to create a test container and mount the volume into it:

.EX
docker run --rm -it -v firstvolume:/mnt --workdir /mnt ubuntu:latest bash
.EE

.PP
If all goes well, you will enter the new container and change right to
the mounted SFTP remote. You can type \fBls\fR to list the mounted directory
or otherwise play with it. Type \fBexit\fR when you are done.
The container will stop but the volume will stay, ready to be reused.
When it's not needed anymore, remove it:

.EX
docker volume list
docker volume remove firstvolume
.EE

.PP
Now let us try \fBsomething more elaborate\fP:
Google Drive
\[la]//drive/\[ra] volume on multi-node Docker Swarm.

.PP
You should start from installing Docker and FUSE, creating plugin
directories and installing zclone plugin on \fIevery\fP swarm node.
Then setup the Swarm
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/swarm\-mode/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Google Drive volumes need an access token which can be setup via web
browser and will be periodically renewed by zclone. The managed
plugin cannot run a browser so we will use a technique similar to the
zclone setup on a headless box
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Run zclone config
\[la]//commands/zclone_config_create/\[ra]
on \fIanother\fP machine equipped with \fIweb browser\fP and graphical user interface.
Create the Google Drive remote
\[la]//drive/#standard\-options\[ra]\&.
When done, transfer the resulting \fBzclone.conf\fR to the Swarm cluster
and save as \fB/var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/config/zclone.conf\fR
on \fIevery\fP node. By default this location is accessible only to the
root user so you will need appropriate privileges. The resulting config
will look like this:

.EX
[gdrive]
type = drive
scope = drive
drive_id = 1234567...
root_folder_id = 0Abcd...
token = {"access_token":...}
.EE

.PP
Now create the file named \fBexample.yml\fR with a swarm stack description
like this:

.EX
version: '3'
services:
  heimdall:
    image: linuxserver/heimdall:latest
    ports: [8080:80]
    volumes: [configdata:/config]
volumes:
  configdata:
    driver: zclone
    driver_opts:
      remote: 'gdrive:heimdall'
      allow_other: 'true'
      vfs_cache_mode: full
      poll_interval: 0
.EE

.PP
and run the stack:

.EX
docker stack deploy example -c ./example.yml
.EE

.PP
After a few seconds docker will spread the parsed stack description
over cluster, create the \fBexample_heimdall\fR service on port \fI8080\fP,
run service containers on one or more cluster nodes and request
the \fBexample_configdata\fR volume from zclone plugins on the node hosts.
You can use the following commands to confirm results:

.EX
docker service ls
docker service ps example_heimdall
docker volume ls
.EE

.PP
Point your browser to \fBhttp://cluster.host.address:8080\fR and play with
the service. Stop it with \fBdocker stack remove example\fR when you are done.
Note that the \fBexample_configdata\fR volume(s) created on demand at the
cluster nodes will not be automatically removed together with the stack
but stay for future reuse. You can remove them manually by invoking
the \fBdocker volume remove example_configdata\fR command on every node.

.SH Creating Volumes via CLI
Volumes can be created with docker volume create
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/volume_create/\[ra]\&.
Here are a few examples:

.EX
docker volume create vol1 -d zclone -o remote=storj: -o vfs-cache-mode=full
docker volume create vol2 -d zclone -o remote=:storj,access_grant=xxx:heimdall
docker volume create vol3 -d zclone -o type=storj -o path=heimdall -o storj-access-grant=xxx -o poll-interval=0
.EE

.PP
Note the \fB-d zclone\fR flag that tells docker to request volume from the
zclone driver. This works even if you installed managed driver by its full
name \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone\fR because you provided the \fB--alias zclone\fR
option.

.PP
Volumes can be inspected as follows:

.EX
docker volume list
docker volume inspect vol1
.EE

.SH Volume Configuration
Zclone flags and volume options are set via the \fB-o\fR flag to the
\fBdocker volume create\fR command. They include backend-specific parameters
as well as mount and \fIVFS\fP options. Also there are a few
special \fB-o\fR options:
\fBremote\fR, \fBfs\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBpath\fR, \fBmount-type\fR and \fBpersist\fR\&.

.PP
\fBremote\fR determines an existing remote name from the config file, with
trailing colon and optionally with a remote path. See the full syntax in
the zclone documentation
\[la]//docs/#syntax\-of\-remote\-paths\[ra]\&.
This option can be aliased as \fBfs\fR to prevent confusion with the
\fIremote\fP parameter of such backends as \fIcrypt\fP or \fIalias\fP\&.

.PP
The \fBremote=:backend:dir/subdir\fR syntax can be used to create
on-the-fly (config-less) remotes
\[la]//docs/#backend\-path\-to\-dir\[ra],
while the \fBtype\fR and \fBpath\fR options provide a simpler alternative for this.
Using two split options

.EX
-o type=backend -o path=dir/subdir
.EE

.PP
is equivalent to the combined syntax

.EX
-o remote=:backend:dir/subdir
.EE

.PP
but is arguably easier to parameterize in scripts.
The \fBpath\fR part is optional.

.PP
Mount and VFS options
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve_docker/#options\[ra]
as well as backend parameters
\[la]//flags/#backend\[ra] are named
like their twin command-line flags without the \fB--\fR CLI prefix.
Optionally you can use underscores instead of dashes in option names.
For example, \fB--vfs-cache-mode full\fR becomes
\fB-o vfs-cache-mode=full\fR or \fB-o vfs_cache_mode=full\fR\&.
Boolean CLI flags without value will gain the \fBtrue\fR value, e.g.
\fB--allow-other\fR becomes \fB-o allow-other=true\fR or \fB-o allow_other=true\fR\&.

.PP
Please note that you can provide parameters only for the backend immediately
referenced by the backend type of mounted \fBremote\fR\&.
If this is a wrapping backend like \fIalias, chunker or crypt\fP, you cannot
provide options for the referred to remote or backend. This limitation is
imposed by the zclone connection string parser. The only workaround is to
feed plugin with \fBzclone.conf\fR or configure plugin arguments (see below).

.SH Special Volume Options
\fBmount-type\fR determines the mount method and in general can be one of:
\fBmount\fR, \fBcmount\fR, or \fBmount2\fR\&. This can be aliased as \fBmount_type\fR\&.
It should be noted that the managed zclone docker plugin currently does
not support the \fBcmount\fR method and \fBmount2\fR is rarely needed.
This option defaults to the first found method, which is usually \fBmount\fR
so you generally won't need it.

.PP
\fBpersist\fR is a reserved boolean (true/false) option.
In future it will allow to persist on-the-fly remotes in the plugin
\fBzclone.conf\fR file.

.SH Connection Strings
The \fBremote\fR value can be extended
with connection strings
\[la]//docs/#connection\-strings\[ra]
as an alternative way to supply backend parameters. This is equivalent
to the \fB-o\fR backend options with one \fIsyntactic difference\fP\&.
Inside connection string the backend prefix must be dropped from parameter
names but in the \fB-o param=value\fR array it must be present.
For instance, compare the following option array

.EX
-o remote=:sftp:/home -o sftp-host=localhost
.EE

.PP
with equivalent connection string:

.EX
-o remote=:sftp,host=localhost:/home
.EE

.PP
This difference exists because flag options \fB-o key=val\fR include not only
backend parameters but also mount/VFS flags and possibly other settings.
Also it allows to discriminate the \fBremote\fR option from the \fBcrypt-remote\fR
(or similarly named backend parameters) and arguably simplifies scripting
due to clearer value substitution.

.SH Using with Swarm or Compose
Both \fIDocker Swarm\fP and \fIDocker Compose\fP use
YAML
\[la]http://yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html\[ra]-formatted text files to describe
groups (stacks) of containers, their properties, networks and volumes.
\fICompose\fP uses the compose v2
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose\-file/compose\-file\-v2/#volume\-configuration\-reference\[ra]
format,
\fISwarm\fP uses the compose v3
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose\-file/compose\-file\-v3/#volume\-configuration\-reference\[ra]
format.
They are mostly similar, differences are explained in the
docker documentation
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose\-file/compose\-versioning/#upgrading\[ra]\&.

.PP
Volumes are described by the children of the top-level \fBvolumes:\fR node.
Each of them should be named after its volume and have at least two
elements, the self-explanatory \fBdriver: zclone\fR value and the
\fBdriver_opts:\fR structure playing the same role as \fB-o key=val\fR CLI flags:

.EX
volumes:
  volume_name_1:
    driver: zclone
    driver_opts:
      remote: 'gdrive:'
      allow_other: 'true'
      vfs_cache_mode: full
      token: '{"type": "borrower", "expires": "2021-12-31"}'
      poll_interval: 0
.EE

.PP
Notice a few important details:
.IP \(bu 2
YAML prefers \fB_\fR in option names instead of \fB-\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
YAML treats single and double quotes interchangeably.
Simple strings and integers can be left unquoted.
.IP \(bu 2
Boolean values must be quoted like \fB\&'true'\fR or \fB"false"\fR because
these two words are reserved by YAML.
.IP \(bu 2
The filesystem string is keyed with \fBremote\fR (or with \fBfs\fR).
Normally you can omit quotes here, but if the string ends with colon,
you \fBmust\fP quote it like \fBremote: "storage_box:"\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
YAML is picky about surrounding braces in values as this is in fact
another syntax for key/value mappings
\[la]http://yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html#id2790832\[ra]\&.
For example, JSON access tokens usually contain double quotes and
surrounding braces, so you must put them in single quotes.

.SH Installing as Managed Plugin
Docker daemon can install plugins from an image registry and run them managed.
We maintain the
docker-volume-zclone
\[la]https://hub.docker.com/p/zclone/docker\-volume\-zclone/\[ra]
plugin image on Docker Hub
\[la]https://hub.docker.com\[ra]\&.

.PP
Zclone volume plugin requires \fBDocker Engine >= 19.03.15\fP

.PP
The plugin requires presence of two directories on the host before it can
be installed. Note that plugin will \fBnot\fP create them automatically.
By default they must exist on host at the following locations
(though you can tweak the paths):
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/config\fR
is reserved for the \fBzclone.conf\fR config file and \fBmust\fP exist
even if it's empty and the config file is not present.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/cache\fR
holds the plugin state file as well as optional VFS caches.

.PP
You can install managed plugin
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/plugin_install/\[ra]
with default settings as follows:

.EX
docker plugin install zclone/docker-volume-zclone:amd64 --grant-all-permissions --alias zclone
.EE

.PP
The \fB:amd64\fR part of the image specification after colon is called a \fItag\fP\&.
Usually you will want to install the latest plugin for your architecture. In
this case the tag will just name it, like \fBamd64\fR above. The following plugin
architectures are currently available:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBamd64\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarm64\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarm-v7\fR

.PP
Sometimes you might want a concrete plugin version, not the latest one.
Then you should use image tag in the form \fB:ARCHITECTURE-VERSION\fR\&.
For example, to install plugin version \fBv1.56.2\fR on architecture \fBarm64\fR
you will use tag \fBarm64-1.56.2\fR (note the removed \fBv\fR) so the full image
specification becomes \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone:arm64-1.56.2\fR\&.

.PP
We also provide the \fBlatest\fR plugin tag, but since docker does not support
multi-architecture plugins as of the time of this writing, this tag is
currently an \fBalias for \fBamd64\fR\fP\&.
By convention the \fBlatest\fR tag is the default one and can be omitted, thus
both \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone:latest\fR and just \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone\fR
will refer to the latest plugin release for the \fBamd64\fR platform.

.PP
Also the \fBamd64\fR part can be omitted from the versioned zclone plugin tags.
For example, zclone image reference \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone:amd64-1.56.2\fR
can be abbreviated as \fBzclone/docker-volume-zclone:1.56.2\fR for convenience.
However, for non-intel architectures you still have to use the full tag as
\fBamd64\fR or \fBlatest\fR will fail to start.

.PP
Managed plugin is in fact a special container running in a namespace separate
from normal docker containers. Inside it runs the \fBzclone serve docker\fR
command. The config and cache directories are bind-mounted into the
container at start. The docker daemon connects to a unix socket created
by the command inside the container. The command creates on-demand remote
mounts right inside, then docker machinery propagates them through kernel
mount namespaces and bind-mounts into requesting user containers.

.PP
You can tweak a few plugin settings after installation when it's disabled
(not in use), for instance:

.EX
docker plugin disable zclone
docker plugin set zclone ZCLONE_VERBOSE=2 config=/etc/zclone args="--vfs-cache-mode=writes --allow-other"
docker plugin enable zclone
docker plugin inspect zclone
.EE

.PP
Note that if docker refuses to disable the plugin, you should find and
remove all active volumes connected with it as well as containers and
swarm services that use them. This is rather tedious so please carefully
plan in advance.

.PP
You can tweak the following settings:
\fBargs\fR, \fBconfig\fR, \fBcache\fR, \fBHTTP_PROXY\fR, \fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR, \fBNO_PROXY\fR
and \fBZCLONE_VERBOSE\fR\&.
It's \fIyour\fP task to keep plugin settings in sync across swarm cluster nodes.

.PP
\fBargs\fR sets command-line arguments for the \fBzclone serve docker\fR command
(\fInone\fP by default). Arguments should be separated by space so you will
normally want to put them in quotes on the
docker plugin set
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/plugin_set/\[ra]
command line. Both serve docker flags
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve_docker/#options\[ra]
and generic zclone flags
\[la]//flags/\[ra] are supported, including backend
parameters that will be used as defaults for volume creation.
Note that plugin will fail (due to this docker bug
\[la]https://github.com/moby/moby/blob/v20.10.7/plugin/v2/plugin.go#L195\[ra])
if the \fBargs\fR value is empty. Use e.g. \fBargs="-v"\fR as a workaround.

.PP
\fBconfig=/host/dir\fR sets alternative host location for the config directory.
Plugin will look for \fBzclone.conf\fR here. It's not an error if the config
file is not present but the directory must exist. Please note that plugin
can periodically rewrite the config file, for example when it renews
storage access tokens. Keep this in mind and try to avoid races between
the plugin and other instances of zclone on the host that might try to
change the config simultaneously resulting in corrupted \fBzclone.conf\fR\&.
You can also put stuff like private key files for SFTP remotes in this
directory. Just note that it's bind-mounted inside the plugin container
at the predefined path \fB/data/config\fR\&. For example, if your key file is
named \fBsftp-box1.key\fR on the host, the corresponding volume config option
should read \fB-o sftp-key-file=/data/config/sftp-box1.key\fR\&.

.PP
\fBcache=/host/dir\fR sets alternative host location for the \fIcache\fP directory.
The plugin will keep VFS caches here. Also it will create and maintain
the \fBdocker-plugin.state\fR file in this directory. When the plugin is
restarted or reinstalled, it will look in this file to recreate any volumes
that existed previously. However, they will not be re-mounted into
consuming containers after restart. Usually this is not a problem as
the docker daemon normally will restart affected user containers after
failures, daemon restarts or host reboots.

.PP
\fBZCLONE_VERBOSE\fR sets plugin verbosity from \fB0\fR (errors only, by default)
to \fB2\fR (debugging). Verbosity can be also tweaked via \fBargs="-v [-v] ..."\fR\&.
Since arguments are more generic, you will rarely need this setting.
The plugin output by default feeds the docker daemon log on local host.
Log entries are reflected as \fIerrors\fP in the docker log but retain their
actual level assigned by zclone in the encapsulated message string.

.PP
\fBHTTP_PROXY\fR, \fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR, \fBNO_PROXY\fR customize the plugin proxy settings.

.PP
You can set custom plugin options right when you install it, \fIin one go\fP:

.EX
docker plugin remove zclone
docker plugin install zclone/docker-volume-zclone:amd64 \\
       --alias zclone --grant-all-permissions \\
       args="-v --allow-other" config=/etc/zclone
docker plugin inspect zclone
.EE

.SH Healthchecks
The docker plugin volume protocol doesn't provide a way for plugins
to inform the docker daemon that a volume is (un-)available.
As a workaround you can setup a healthcheck to verify that the mount
is responding, for example:

.EX
services:
  my_service:
    image: my_image
    healthcheck:
      test: ls /path/to/zclone/mount || exit 1
      interval: 1m
      timeout: 15s
      retries: 3
      start_period: 15s
.EE

.SH Running Plugin under Systemd
In most cases you should prefer managed mode. Moreover, MacOS and Windows
do not support native Docker plugins. Please use managed mode on these
systems. Proceed further only if you are on Linux.

.PP
First, install zclone
\[la]//install/\[ra]\&.
You can just run it (type \fBzclone serve docker\fR and hit enter) for the test.

.PP
Install FUSE from an approved local package source. The two required systemd
configuration files are included in this checkout under
\fBcontrib/docker-plugin/systemd/\fR\&. Copy them to \fB/etc/systemd/system/\fR:

.EX
cp contrib/docker-plugin/systemd/docker-volume-zclone.service /etc/systemd/system/
cp contrib/docker-plugin/systemd/docker-volume-zclone.socket /etc/systemd/system/
.EE

.PP
Please note that all commands in this section must be run as \fIroot\fP but
we omit \fBsudo\fR prefix for brevity.
Now create directories required by the service:

.EX
mkdir -p /var/lib/docker-volumes/zclone
mkdir -p /var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/config
mkdir -p /var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/cache
.EE

.PP
Run the docker plugin service in the socket activated mode:

.EX
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl start docker-volume-zclone.service
systemctl enable docker-volume-zclone.socket
systemctl start docker-volume-zclone.socket
systemctl restart docker
.EE

.PP
Or run the service directly:
.IP \(bu 2
run \fBsystemctl daemon-reload\fR to let systemd pick up new config
.IP \(bu 2
run \fBsystemctl enable docker-volume-zclone.service\fR to make the new
service start automatically when you power on your machine.
.IP \(bu 2
run \fBsystemctl start docker-volume-zclone.service\fR
to start the service now.
.IP \(bu 2
run \fBsystemctl restart docker\fR to restart docker daemon and let it
detect the new plugin socket. Note that this step is not needed in
managed mode where docker knows about plugin state changes.

.PP
The two methods are equivalent from the user perspective, but I personally
prefer socket activation.

.SH Troubleshooting
You can see managed plugin settings
\[la]https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/#debugging\-plugins\[ra]
with

.EX
docker plugin list
docker plugin inspect zclone
.EE

.PP
Note that docker (including latest 20.10.7) will not show actual values
of \fBargs\fR, just the defaults.

.PP
Use \fBjournalctl --unit docker\fR to see managed plugin output as part of
the docker daemon log. Note that docker reflects plugin lines as \fIerrors\fP
but their actual level can be seen from encapsulated message string.

.PP
You will usually install the latest version of managed plugin for your platform.
Use the following commands to print the actual installed version:

.EX
PLUGID=$(docker plugin list --no-trunc | awk '/zclone/{print$1}')
sudo runc --root /run/docker/runtime-runc/plugins.moby exec $PLUGID zclone version
.EE

.PP
You can even use \fBrunc\fR to run shell inside the plugin container:

.EX
sudo runc --root /run/docker/runtime-runc/plugins.moby exec --tty $PLUGID bash
.EE

.PP
Also you can use curl to check the plugin socket connectivity:

.EX
docker plugin list --no-trunc
PLUGID=123abc...
sudo curl -H Content-Type:application/json -XPOST -d {} --unix-socket /run/docker/plugins/$PLUGID/zclone.sock http://localhost/Plugin.Activate
.EE

.PP
though this is rarely needed.

.PP
If the plugin fails to work properly, and only as a last resort after you tried
diagnosing with the above methods, you can try clearing the state of the plugin.
\fBNote that all existing zclone docker volumes will probably have to be recreated.\fP
This might be needed because a reinstall don't cleanup existing state files to
allow for easy restoration, as stated above.

.EX
docker plugin disable zclone # disable the plugin to ensure no interference
sudo rm /var/lib/docker-plugins/zclone/cache/docker-plugin.state # removing the plugin state
docker plugin enable zclone # re-enable the plugin afterward
.EE

.SH Caveats
Finally I'd like to mention a \fIcaveat with updating volume settings\fP\&.
Docker CLI does not have a dedicated command like \fBdocker volume update\fR\&.
It may be tempting to invoke \fBdocker volume create\fR with updated options
on existing volume, but there is a gotcha. The command will do nothing,
it won't even return an error. I hope that docker maintainers will fix
this some day. In the meantime be aware that you must remove your volume
before recreating it with new settings:

.EX
docker volume remove my_vol
docker volume create my_vol -d zclone -o opt1=new_val1 ...
.EE

.PP
and verify that settings did update:

.EX
docker volume list
docker volume inspect my_vol
.EE

.PP
If docker refuses to remove the volume, you should find containers
or swarm services that use it and stop them first.

.SH Bisync
\fBbisync\fR is considered an \fBadvanced command\fP, so use with care.
Make sure you have read and understood the entire
manual
\[la]//bisync\[ra] (especially the Limitations
\[la]#limitations\[ra]
section) before using, or data loss can result. Questions can be asked in the
Zclone Forum
\[la]/\[ra]\&.

.SH Getting started {#getting-started}
.IP \(bu 2
Install zclone
\[la]//install/\[ra] and setup your remotes.
.IP \(bu 2
Bisync will create its working directory   at \fB~/.cache/zclone/bisync\fR on
Linux, \fB/Users/yourusername/Library/Caches/zclone/bisync\fR on Mac,   or
\fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\AppData\\Local\\zclone\\bisync\fR on Windows.   Make sure that this
location is writable.
.IP \(bu 2
Run bisync with the \fB--resync\fR flag, specifying the paths
to the local and remote sync directory roots.
.IP \(bu 2
For successive sync runs, leave off the \fB--resync\fR flag. (\fBImportant!\fP)
.IP \(bu 2
Consider using a filters file
\[la]#filtering\[ra] for excluding
unnecessary files and directories from the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
Consider setting up the --check-access
\[la]#check\-access\[ra] feature
for safety.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux or Mac, consider setting up a crontab entry
\[la]#cron\[ra]\&. bisync can
safely run in concurrent cron jobs thanks to lock files it maintains.

.PP
For example, your first command might look like this:

.EX
zclone bisync remote1:path1 remote2:path2 --create-empty-src-dirs --compare size,modtime,checksum --slow-hash-sync-only --resilient -MvP --drive-skip-gdocs --fix-case --resync --dry-run
.EE

.PP
If all looks good, run it again without \fB--dry-run\fR\&.
After that, remove \fB--resync\fR as well.

.PP
Here is a typical run log (with timestamps removed for clarity):

.EX
