.SH Command line syntax
.EX
$ zclone bisync --help
Usage:
  zclone bisync remote1:path1 remote2:path2 [flags]

Positional arguments:
  Path1, Path2  Local path, or remote storage with ':' plus optional path.
                Type 'zclone listremotes' for list of configured remotes.

Optional Flags:
      --backup-dir1 string                   --backup-dir for Path1. Must be a non-overlapping path on the same remote.
      --backup-dir2 string                   --backup-dir for Path2. Must be a non-overlapping path on the same remote.
      --check-access                         Ensure expected ZCLONE_TEST files are found on both Path1 and Path2 filesystems, else abort.
      --check-filename string                Filename for --check-access (default: ZCLONE_TEST)
      --check-sync string                    Controls comparison of final listings: true|false|only (default: true) (default "true")
      --compare string                       Comma-separated list of bisync-specific compare options ex. 'size,modtime,checksum' (default: 'size,modtime')
      --conflict-loser ConflictLoserAction   Action to take on the loser of a sync conflict (when there is a winner) or on both files (when there is no winner): , num, pathname, delete (default: num)
      --conflict-resolve string              Automatically resolve conflicts by preferring the version that is: none, path1, path2, newer, older, larger, smaller (default: none) (default "none")
      --conflict-suffix string               Suffix to use when renaming a --conflict-loser. Can be either one string or two comma-separated strings to assign different suffixes to Path1/Path2. (default: 'conflict')
      --create-empty-src-dirs                Sync creation and deletion of empty directories. (Not compatible with --remove-empty-dirs)
      --download-hash                        Compute hash by downloading when otherwise unavailable. (warning: may be slow and use lots of data!)
      --filters-file string                  Read filtering patterns from a file
      --force                                Bypass --max-delete safety check and run the sync. Consider using with --verbose
  -h, --help                                 help for bisync
      --ignore-listing-checksum              Do not use checksums for listings (add --ignore-checksum to additionally skip post-copy checksum checks)
      --max-lock Duration                    Consider lock files older than this to be expired (default: 0 (never expire)) (minimum: 2m) (default 0s)
      --no-cleanup                           Retain working files (useful for troubleshooting and testing).
      --no-slow-hash                         Ignore listing checksums only on backends where they are slow
      --recover                              Automatically recover from interruptions without requiring --resync.
      --remove-empty-dirs                    Remove ALL empty directories at the final cleanup step.
      --resilient                            Allow future runs to retry after certain less-serious errors, instead of requiring --resync.
  -1, --resync                               Performs the resync run. Equivalent to --resync-mode path1. Consider using --verbose or --dry-run first.
      --resync-mode string                   During resync, prefer the version that is: path1, path2, newer, older, larger, smaller (default: path1 if --resync, otherwise none for no resync.) (default "none")
      --retries int                          Retry operations this many times if they fail (requires --resilient). (default 3)
      --retries-sleep Duration               Interval between retrying operations if they fail, e.g. 500ms, 60s, 5m (0 to disable) (default 0s)
      --slow-hash-sync-only                  Ignore slow checksums for listings and deltas, but still consider them during sync calls.
      --workdir string                       Use custom working dir - useful for testing. (default: {WORKDIR})
      --max-delete PERCENT                   Safety check on maximum percentage of deleted files allowed. If exceeded, the bisync run will abort. (default: 50%)
  -n, --dry-run                              Go through the motions - No files are copied/deleted.
  -v, --verbose                              Increases logging verbosity. May be specified more than once for more details.
.EE

.PP
Arbitrary zclone flags may be specified on the
bisync command line
\[la]//commands/zclone_bisync/\[ra], for example
\fBzclone bisync ./testdir/path1/ gdrive:testdir/path2/ --drive-skip-gdocs -v -v
--timeout 10s\fR
Note that interactions of various zclone flags with bisync process flow
has not been fully tested yet.

.SS Paths
Path1 and Path2 arguments may be references to any mix of local directory
paths (absolute or relative), UNC paths (\fB//server/share/path\fR),
Windows drive paths (with a drive letter and \fB:\fR) or configured
remotes
\[la]//docs/#syntax\-of\-remote\-paths\[ra] with optional subdirectory paths.
Cloud references are distinguished by having a \fB:\fR in the argument
(see Windows support
\[la]#windows\[ra] below).

.PP
Path1 and Path2 are treated equally, in that neither has priority for file
changes (except during 
\[la]#resync\[ra]), and access efficiency does not
change whether a remote is on Path1 or Path2.

.PP
The listings in bisync working directory (default: \fB~/.cache/zclone/bisync\fR)
are named based on the Path1 and Path2 arguments so that separate syncs
to individual directories within the tree may be set up, e.g.:
\fBpath_to_local_tree..dropbox_subdir.lst\fR\&.

.PP
Any empty directories after the sync on both the Path1 and Path2 filesystems are
not deleted by default, unless \fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR is specified. If the
\fB--remove-empty-dirs\fR flag is specified, then both paths will have ALL empty
directories purged as the last step in the process.

.SH Command-line flags
.SS --resync
This will effectively make both Path1 and Path2 filesystems contain a matching
superset of all files. By default, Path2 files that do not exist in Path1 will
be copied to Path1, and the process will then copy the Path1 tree to Path2.

.PP
The \fB--resync\fR sequence is roughly equivalent to the following
(but see 
\[la]#resync\-mode\[ra] for other options):

.EX
zclone copy Path2 Path1 --ignore-existing [--create-empty-src-dirs]
zclone copy Path1 Path2 [--create-empty-src-dirs]
.EE

.PP
The base directories on both Path1 and Path2 filesystems must exist
or bisync will fail. This is required for safety - that bisync can verify
that both paths are valid.

.PP
When using \fB--resync\fR, a newer version of a file on the Path2 filesystem
will (by default) be overwritten by the Path1 filesystem version.
(Note that this is NOT entirely symmetrical
\[la]/\[ra],
and more symmetrical options can be specified with the

\[la]#resync\-mode\[ra] flag.)
Carefully evaluate deltas using --dry-run
\[la]//flags/#non\-backend\-flags\[ra]\&.

.PP
For a resync run, one of the paths may be empty (no files in the path tree).
The resync run should result in files on both paths, else a normal non-resync
run will fail.

.PP
For a non-resync run, either path being empty (no files in the tree) fails with
\fBEmpty current PathN listing. Cannot sync to an empty directory: X.pathN.lst\fR
This is a safety check that an unexpected empty path does not result in
deleting \fBeverything\fP in the other path.

.PP
Note that \fB--resync\fR implies \fB--resync-mode path1\fR unless a different

\[la]#resync\-mode\[ra]  is explicitly specified.
It is not necessary to use both the \fB--resync\fR and \fB--resync-mode\fR flags --
either one is sufficient without the other.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP \fB--resync\fR (including \fB--resync-mode\fR) should only be used under three
specific (rare) circumstances:
.IP "  1." 5
It is your \fIfirst\fP bisync run (between these two paths)
.IP "  2." 5
You've just made changes to your bisync settings (such as editing the
contents of your \fB--filters-file\fR)
.IP "  3." 5
There was an error on the prior run, and as a result, bisync now requires
\fB--resync\fR to recover

.PP
The rest of the time, you should \fIomit\fP \fB--resync\fR\&. The reason is because \fB--resync\fR
will only \fIcopy\fP (not \fIsync\fP) each side to the other. Therefore, if you included
\fB--resync\fR for every bisync run, it would never be possible to delete a file -- the
deleted file would always keep reappearing at the end of every run (because it's
being copied from the other side where it still exists). Similarly, renaming a
file would always result in a duplicate copy (both old and new name) on both
sides.

.PP
If you find that frequent interruptions from #3 are an issue, rather than
automatically running \fB--resync\fR, the recommended alternative is to use the

\[la]#resilient\[ra], 
\[la]#recover\[ra], and

\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra] flags, (along with Graceful
Shutdown
\[la]#graceful\-shutdown\[ra] mode, when needed) for a very robust
"set-it-and-forget-it" bisync setup that can automatically bounce back from
almost any interruption it might encounter. Consider adding something like the
following:

.EX
--resilient --recover --max-lock 2m --conflict-resolve newer
.EE

.SS --resync-mode CHOICE {#resync-mode}
In the event that a file differs on both sides during a \fB--resync\fR,
\fB--resync-mode\fR controls which version will overwrite the other. The supported
options are similar to 
\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra]\&. For all of
the following options, the version that is kept is referred to as the "winner",
and the version that is overwritten (deleted) is referred to as the "loser".
The options are named after the "winner":
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath1\fR - (the default) - the version from Path1 is unconditionally
considered the winner (regardless of \fBmodtime\fR and \fBsize\fR, if any). This can be
useful if one side is more trusted or up-to-date than the other, at the time of
the \fB--resync\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath2\fR - same as \fBpath1\fR, except the path2 version is considered the winner.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnewer\fR - the newer file (by \fBmodtime\fR) is considered the winner, regardless
of which side it came from. This may result in having a mix of some winners
from Path1, and some winners from Path2. (The implementation is analogous to
running \fBzclone copy --update\fR in both directions.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBolder\fR - same as \fBnewer\fR, except the older file is considered the winner,
and the newer file is considered the loser.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlarger\fR - the larger file (by \fBsize\fR) is considered the winner (regardless
of \fBmodtime\fR, if any). This can be a useful option for remotes without
\fBmodtime\fR support, or with the kinds of files (such as logs) that tend to grow
but not shrink, over time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsmaller\fR - the smaller file (by \fBsize\fR) is considered the winner (regardless
of \fBmodtime\fR, if any).

.PP
For all of the above options, note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
If either of the underlying remotes lacks support for the chosen method, it
will be ignored and will fall back to the default of \fBpath1\fR\&. (For example, if
\fB--resync-mode newer\fR is set, but one of the paths uses a remote that doesn't
support \fBmodtime\fR\&.)
.IP \(bu 2
If a winner can't be determined because the chosen method's attribute is
missing or equal, it will be ignored, and bisync will instead try to determine
whether the files differ by looking at the other \fB--compare\fR methods in effect.
(For example, if \fB--resync-mode newer\fR is set, but the Path1 and Path2 modtimes
are identical, bisync will compare the sizes.) If bisync concludes that they
differ, preference is given to whichever is the "source" at that moment. (In
practice, this gives a slight advantage to Path2, as the 2to1 copy comes before
the 1to2 copy.) If the files \fIdo not\fP differ, nothing is copied (as both sides
are already correct).
.IP \(bu 2
These options apply only to files that exist on both sides (with the same
name and relative path). Files that exist \fIonly\fP on one side and not the other
are \fIalways\fP copied to the other, during \fB--resync\fR (this is one of the main
differences between resync and non-resync runs.).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--conflict-resolve\fR, \fB--conflict-loser\fR, and \fB--conflict-suffix\fR do not
apply during \fB--resync\fR, and unlike these flags, nothing is renamed during
\fB--resync\fR\&. When a file differs on both sides during \fB--resync\fR, one version
always overwrites the other (much like in \fBzclone copy\fR\&.) (Consider using

\[la]#backup\-dir1\-and\-backup\-dir2\[ra] to retain a backup of the losing
version.)
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike for \fB--conflict-resolve\fR, \fB--resync-mode none\fR is not a valid option
(or rather, it will be interpreted as "no resync", unless \fB--resync\fR has also
been specified, in which case it will be ignored.)
.IP \(bu 2
Winners and losers are decided at the individual file-level only (there is
not currently an option to pick an entire winning directory atomically,
although the \fBpath1\fR and \fBpath2\fR options typically produce a similar result.)
.IP \(bu 2
To maintain backward-compatibility, the \fB--resync\fR flag implies
\fB--resync-mode path1\fR unless a different \fB--resync-mode\fR is explicitly
specified. Similarly, all \fB--resync-mode\fR options (except \fBnone\fR) imply
\fB--resync\fR, so it is not necessary to use both the \fB--resync\fR and
\fB--resync-mode\fR flags simultaneously -- either one is sufficient without the
other.

.SS --check-access
Access check files are an additional safety measure against data loss.
bisync will ensure it can find matching \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files in the same places
in the Path1 and Path2 filesystems.
\fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files are not generated automatically.
For \fB--check-access\fR to succeed, you must first either:
\fBA)\fP Place one or more \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files in both systems, or
\fBB)\fP Set \fB--check-filename\fR to a filename already in use in various locations
throughout your sync'd fileset. Recommended methods for \fBA)\fP include:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone touch Path1/ZCLONE_TEST\fR (create a new file)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone copyto Path1/ZCLONE_TEST Path2/ZCLONE_TEST\fR (copy an existing file)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone copy Path1/ZCLONE_TEST Path2/ZCLONE_TEST  --include "ZCLONE_TEST"\fR
(copy multiple files at once, recursively)
.IP \(bu 2
create the files manually (outside of zclone)
.IP \(bu 2
run \fBbisync\fR once \fIwithout\fP \fB--check-access\fR to set matching files on both filesystems
will also work, but is not preferred, due to potential for user error
(you are temporarily disabling the safety feature).

.PP
Note that \fB--check-access\fR is still enforced on \fB--resync\fR, so \fBbisync --resync --check-access\fR
will not work as a method of initially setting the files
(this is to ensure that bisync can't
inadvertently circumvent its own safety switch
\[la]/\[ra]\&.)

.PP
Time stamps and file contents for \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files are not important, just
the names and locations. If you have symbolic links in your sync tree it is
recommended to place \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files in the linked-to directory tree to
protect against bisync assuming a bunch of deleted files if the linked-to tree
should not be accessible. See also the 
\[la]\-\-check\-filename\[ra] flag.

.SS --check-filename
Name of the file(s) used in access health validation.
The default \fB--check-filename\fR is \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR\&.
One or more files having this filename must exist, synchronized between your
source and destination filesets, in order for \fB--check-access\fR to succeed.
See --check-access
\[la]#check\-access\[ra] for additional details.

.SS --compare
As of \fBv1.66\fR, bisync fully supports comparing based on any combination of
size, modtime, and checksum (lifting the prior restriction on backends without
modtime support.)

.PP
By default (without the \fB--compare\fR flag), bisync inherits the same comparison
options as \fBsync\fR
(that is: \fBsize\fR and \fBmodtime\fR by default, unless modified with flags such as

\[la]//docs/#c\-checksum\[ra] or 
\[la]/docs/#size\-only\[ra]\&.)

.PP
If the \fB--compare\fR flag is set, it will override these defaults. This can be
useful if you wish to compare based on combinations not currently supported in
\fBsync\fR, such as comparing all three of \fBsize\fR AND \fBmodtime\fR AND \fBchecksum\fR
simultaneously (or just \fBmodtime\fR AND \fBchecksum\fR).

.PP
\fB--compare\fR takes a comma-separated list, with the currently supported values
being \fBsize\fR, \fBmodtime\fR, and \fBchecksum\fR\&. For example, if you want to compare
size and checksum, but not modtime, you would do:

.EX
--compare size,checksum
.EE

.PP
Or if you want to compare all three:

.EX
--compare size,modtime,checksum
.EE

.PP
\fB--compare\fR overrides any conflicting flags. For example, if you set the
conflicting flags \fB--compare checksum --size-only\fR, \fB--size-only\fR will be
ignored, and bisync will compare checksum and not size. To avoid confusion, it
is recommended to use \fIeither\fP \fB--compare\fR or the normal \fBsync\fR flags, but not
both.

.PP
If \fB--compare\fR includes \fBchecksum\fR and both remotes support checksums but have
no hash types in common with each other, checksums will be considered \fIonly\fP
for comparisons within the same side (to determine what has changed since the
prior sync), but not for comparisons against the opposite side. If one side
supports checksums and the other does not, checksums will only be considered on
the side that supports them.

.PP
When comparing with \fBchecksum\fR and/or \fBsize\fR without \fBmodtime\fR, bisync cannot
determine whether a file is \fBnewer\fR or \fBolder\fR -- only whether it is \fBchanged\fR
or \fBunchanged\fR\&. (If it is \fBchanged\fR on both sides, bisync still does the
standard equality-check to avoid declaring a sync conflict unless it absolutely
has to.)

.PP
It is recommended to do a \fB--resync\fR when changing \fB--compare\fR settings, as
otherwise your prior listing files may not contain the attributes you wish to
compare (for example, they will not have stored checksums if you were not
previously comparing checksums.)

.SS --ignore-listing-checksum
When \fB--checksum\fR or \fB--compare checksum\fR is set, bisync will retrieve (or
generate) checksums (for backends that support them) when creating the listings
for both paths, and store the checksums in the listing files.
\fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR will disable this behavior, which may speed things
up considerably, especially on backends (such as local
\[la]//local/\[ra]) where hashes
must be computed on the fly instead of retrieved. Please note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
As of \fBv1.66\fR, \fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR is now automatically set when
neither \fB--checksum\fR nor \fB--compare checksum\fR are in use (as the checksums
would not be used for anything.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR is NOT the same as

\[la]//docs/#ignore\-checksum\[ra],
and you may wish to use one or the other, or both. In a nutshell:
\fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR controls whether checksums are considered when
scanning for diffs,
while \fB--ignore-checksum\fR controls whether checksums are considered during the
copy/sync operations that follow,
if there ARE diffs.
.IP \(bu 2
Unless \fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR is passed, bisync currently computes
hashes for one path
\fIeven when there's no common hash with the other path\fP
(for example, a crypt
\[la]//crypt/#modification\-times\-and\-hashes\[ra] remote.)
This can still be beneficial, as the hashes will still be used to detect
changes within the same side
(if \fB--checksum\fR or \fB--compare checksum\fR is set), even if they can't be used to
compare against the opposite side.
.IP \(bu 2
If you wish to ignore listing checksums \fIonly\fP on remotes where they are slow
to compute, consider using

\[la]#no\-slow\-hash\[ra] (or

\[la]#slow\-hash\-sync\-only\[ra]) instead of
\fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
If \fB--ignore-listing-checksum\fR is used simultaneously with \fB--compare
checksum\fR (or \fB--checksum\fR), checksums will be ignored for bisync deltas,
but still considered during the sync operations that follow (if deltas are
detected based on modtime and/or size.)

.SS --no-slow-hash
On some remotes (notably \fBlocal\fR), checksums can dramatically slow down a bisync
run, because hashes cannot be stored and need to be computed in real-time when
they are requested. On other remotes (such as \fBdrive\fR), they add practically no
time at all. The \fB--no-slow-hash\fR flag will automatically skip checksums on remotes
where they are slow, while still comparing them on others (assuming

\[la]#compare\[ra] includes \fBchecksum\fR\&.) This can be useful when one of your
bisync paths is slow but you still want to check checksums on the other, for a
more robust sync.

.SS --slow-hash-sync-only
Same as 
\[la]#no\-slow\-hash\[ra], except slow hashes are still
considered during sync calls. They are still NOT considered for determining
deltas, nor or they included in listings. They are also skipped during
\fB--resync\fR\&. The main use case for this flag is when you have a large number of
files, but relatively few of them change from run to run -- so you don't want
to check your entire tree every time (it would take too long), but you still
want to consider checksums for the smaller group of files for which a \fBmodtime\fR
or \fBsize\fR change was detected. Keep in mind that this speed savings comes with
a safety trade-off: if a file's content were to change without a change to its
\fBmodtime\fR or \fBsize\fR, bisync would not detect it, and it would not be synced.

.PP
\fB--slow-hash-sync-only\fR is only useful if both remotes share a common hash
type (if they don't, bisync will automatically fall back to \fB--no-slow-hash\fR\&.)
Both \fB--no-slow-hash\fR and \fB--slow-hash-sync-only\fR have no effect without
\fB--compare checksum\fR (or \fB--checksum\fR).

.SS --download-hash
If \fB--download-hash\fR is set, bisync will use best efforts to obtain an MD5
checksum by downloading and computing on-the-fly, when checksums are not
otherwise available (for example, a remote that doesn't support them.) Note
that since zclone has to download the entire file, this may dramatically slow
down your bisync runs, and is also likely to use a lot of data, so it is
probably not practical for bisync paths with a large total file size. However,
it can be a good option for syncing small-but-important files with maximum
accuracy (for example, a source code repo on a \fBcrypt\fR remote.) An additional
advantage over methods like 
\[la]//commands/zclone_cryptcheck/\[ra] is
that the original file is not required for comparison (for example,
\fB--download-hash\fR can be used to bisync two different crypt remotes with
different passwords.)

.PP
When \fB--download-hash\fR is set, bisync still looks for more efficient checksums
first, and falls back to downloading only when none are found. It takes
priority over conflicting flags such as \fB--no-slow-hash\fR\&. \fB--download-hash\fR is
not suitable for Google Docs
\[la]#gdocs\[ra] and other files of unknown size, as
their checksums would change from run to run (due to small variances in the
internals of the generated export file.) Therefore, bisync automatically skips
\fB--download-hash\fR for files with a size less than 0.

.PP
See also: 
\[la]//hasher/\[ra] backend,

\[la]//commands/zclone_cryptcheck/\[ra] command,

\[la]//commands/zclone_check/\[ra] option,

\[la]//commands/zclone_md5sum/\[ra] command

.SS --max-delete
As a safety check, if greater than the \fB--max-delete\fR percent of files were
deleted on either the Path1 or Path2 filesystem, then bisync will abort with
a warning message, without making any changes.
The default \fB--max-delete\fR is \fB50%\fR\&.
One way to trigger this limit is to rename a directory that contains more
than half of your files. This will appear to bisync as a bunch of deleted
files and a bunch of new files.
This safety check is intended to block bisync from deleting all of the
files on both filesystems due to a temporary network access issue, or if
the user had inadvertently deleted the files on one side or the other.
To force the sync, either set a different delete percentage limit,
e.g. \fB--max-delete 75\fR (allows up to 75% deletion), or use \fB--force\fR
to bypass the check.

.PP
Also see the all files changed
\[la]#all\-files\-changed\[ra] check.

.SS --filters-file {#filters-file}
By using zclone filter features you can exclude file types or directory
sub-trees from the sync.
See the bisync filters
\[la]#filtering\[ra] section and generic
--filter-from
\[la]//filtering/#filter\-from\-read\-filtering\-patterns\-from\-a\-file\[ra]
documentation.
An example filters file
\[la]#example\-filters\-file\[ra] contains filters for
non-allowed files for syncing with Dropbox.

.PP
If you make changes to your filters file then bisync requires a run
with \fB--resync\fR\&. This is a safety feature, which prevents existing files
on the Path1 and/or Path2 side from seeming to disappear from view
(since they are excluded in the new listings), which would fool bisync
into seeing them as deleted (as compared to the prior run listings),
and then bisync would proceed to delete them for real.

.PP
To block this from happening, bisync calculates an MD5 hash of the filters file
and stores the hash in a \fB\&.md5\fR file in the same place as your filters file.
On the next run with \fB--filters-file\fR set, bisync re-calculates the MD5 hash
of the current filters file and compares it to the hash stored in the \fB\&.md5\fR file.
If they don't match, the run aborts with a critical error and thus forces you
to do a \fB--resync\fR, likely avoiding a disaster.

.SS --conflict-resolve CHOICE {#conflict-resolve}
In bisync, a "conflict" is a file that is \fInew\fP or \fIchanged\fP on \fIboth sides\fP
(relative to the prior run) AND is \fInot currently identical\fP on both sides.
\fB--conflict-resolve\fR controls how bisync handles such a scenario. The currently
supported options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnone\fR - (the default) - do not attempt to pick a winner, keep and rename
both files according to 
\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra] and

\[la]#conflict\-suffix\[ra] settings. For example, with the default
settings, \fBfile.txt\fR on Path1 is renamed \fBfile.txt.conflict1\fR and \fBfile.txt\fR on
Path2 is renamed \fBfile.txt.conflict2\fR\&. Both are copied to the opposite path
during the run, so both sides end up with a copy of both files. (As \fBnone\fR is
the default, it is not necessary to specify \fB--conflict-resolve none\fR -- you
can just omit the flag.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnewer\fR - the newer file (by \fBmodtime\fR) is considered the winner and is
copied without renaming. The older file (the "loser") is handled according to
\fB--conflict-loser\fR and \fB--conflict-suffix\fR settings (either renamed or
deleted.) For example, if \fBfile.txt\fR on Path1 is newer than \fBfile.txt\fR on
Path2, the result on both sides (with other default settings) will be \fBfile.txt\fR
(winner from Path1) and \fBfile.txt.conflict1\fR (loser from Path2).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBolder\fR - same as \fBnewer\fR, except the older file is considered the winner,
and the newer file is considered the loser.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlarger\fR - the larger file (by \fBsize\fR) is considered the winner (regardless
of \fBmodtime\fR, if any).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsmaller\fR - the smaller file (by \fBsize\fR) is considered the winner (regardless
of \fBmodtime\fR, if any).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath1\fR - the version from Path1 is unconditionally considered the winner
(regardless of \fBmodtime\fR and \fBsize\fR, if any). This can be useful if one side is
usually more trusted or up-to-date than the other.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath2\fR - same as \fBpath1\fR, except the path2 version is considered the
winner.

.PP
For all of the above options, note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
If either of the underlying remotes lacks support for the chosen method, it
will be ignored and fall back to \fBnone\fR\&. (For example, if \fB--conflict-resolve
newer\fR is set, but one of the paths uses a remote that doesn't support
\fBmodtime\fR\&.)
.IP \(bu 2
If a winner can't be determined because the chosen method's attribute is
missing or equal, it will be ignored and fall back to \fBnone\fR\&. (For example, if
\fB--conflict-resolve newer\fR is set, but the Path1 and Path2 modtimes are
identical, even if the sizes may differ.)
.IP \(bu 2
If the file's content is currently identical on both sides, it is not
considered a "conflict", even if new or changed on both sides since the prior
sync. (For example, if you made a change on one side and then synced it to the
other side by other means.) Therefore, none of the conflict resolution flags
apply in this scenario.
.IP \(bu 2
The conflict resolution flags do not apply during a \fB--resync\fR, as there is
no "prior run" to speak of (but see 
\[la]#resync\-mode\[ra] for similar
options.)

.SS --conflict-loser CHOICE {#conflict-loser}
\fB--conflict-loser\fR determines what happens to the "loser" of a sync conflict
(when 
\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra] determines a winner) or to both
files (when there is no winner.) The currently supported options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnum\fR - (the default) - auto-number the conflicts by automatically appending
the next available number to the \fB--conflict-suffix\fR, in chronological order.
For example, with the default settings, the first conflict for \fBfile.txt\fR will
be renamed \fBfile.txt.conflict1\fR\&. If \fBfile.txt.conflict1\fR already exists,
\fBfile.txt.conflict2\fR will be used instead (etc., up to a maximum of
9223372036854775807 conflicts.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpathname\fR - rename the conflicts according to which side they came from,
which was the default behavior prior to \fBv1.66\fR\&. For example, with
\fB--conflict-suffix path\fR, \fBfile.txt\fR from Path1 will be renamed
\fBfile.txt.path1\fR, and \fBfile.txt\fR from Path2 will be renamed \fBfile.txt.path2\fR\&.
If two non-identical suffixes are provided (ex. \fB--conflict-suffix
cloud,local\fR), the trailing digit is omitted. Importantly, note that with
\fBpathname\fR, there is no auto-numbering beyond \fB2\fR, so if \fBfile.txt.path2\fR
somehow already exists, it will be overwritten. Using a dynamic date variable
in your \fB--conflict-suffix\fR (see below) is one possible way to avoid this. Note
also that conflicts-of-conflicts are possible, if the original conflict is not
manually resolved -- for example, if for some reason you edited
\fBfile.txt.path1\fR on both sides, and those edits were different, the result
would be \fBfile.txt.path1.path1\fR and \fBfile.txt.path1.path2\fR (in addition to
\fBfile.txt.path2\fR\&.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelete\fR - keep the winner only and delete the loser, instead of renaming it.
If a winner cannot be determined (see \fB--conflict-resolve\fR for details on how
this could happen), \fBdelete\fR is ignored and the default \fBnum\fR is used instead
(i.e. both versions are kept and renamed, and neither is deleted.) \fBdelete\fR is
inherently the most destructive option, so use it only with care.

.PP
For all of the above options, note that if a winner cannot be determined (see
\fB--conflict-resolve\fR for details on how this could happen), or if
\fB--conflict-resolve\fR is not in use, \fIboth\fP files will be renamed.

.SS --conflict-suffix STRING[,STRING] {#conflict-suffix}
\fB--conflict-suffix\fR controls the suffix that is appended when bisync renames a

\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra] (default: \fBconflict\fR).
\fB--conflict-suffix\fR will accept either one string or two comma-separated
strings to assign different suffixes to Path1 vs. Path2. This may be helpful
later in identifying the source of the conflict. (For example,
\fB--conflict-suffix dropboxconflict,laptopconflict\fR)

.PP
With \fB--conflict-loser num\fR, a number is always appended to the suffix. With
\fB--conflict-loser pathname\fR, a number is appended only when one suffix is
specified (or when two identical suffixes are specified.) i.e. with
\fB--conflict-loser pathname\fR, all of the following would produce exactly the
same result:

.EX
--conflict-suffix path
--conflict-suffix path,path
--conflict-suffix path1,path2
.EE

.PP
Suffixes may be as short as 1 character. By default, the suffix is appended
after any other extensions (ex. \fBfile.jpg.conflict1\fR), however, this can be
changed with the 
\[la]//docs/#suffix\-keep\-extension\[ra] flag
(i.e. to instead result in \fBfile.conflict1.jpg\fR).

.PP
\fB--conflict-suffix\fR supports several \fIdynamic date variables\fP when enclosed in
curly braces as globs. This can be helpful to track the date and/or time that
each conflict was handled by bisync. For example:

.EX
--conflict-suffix {DateOnly}-conflict
// result: myfile.txt.2006-01-02-conflict1
.EE

.PP
All of the formats described here (go Time.Layout
constants)
\[la]https://pkg.go.dev/time#pkg\-constants\[ra] and here (go Time.Format
examples)
\[la]https://pkg.go.dev/time#example\-Time.Format\[ra] are supported, but take
care to ensure that your chosen format does not use any characters that are
illegal on your remotes (for example, macOS does not allow colons in filenames,
and slashes are also best avoided as they are often interpreted as directory
separators.) To address this particular issue, an additional \fB{MacFriendlyTime}\fR
(or just \fB{mac}\fR) option is supported, which results in \fB2006-01-02 0304PM\fR\&.

.PP
Note that \fB--conflict-suffix\fR is entirely separate from zclone's main

\[la]//docs/#suffix\-string\[ra] flag. This is intentional, as users may wish
to use both flags simultaneously, if also using

\[la]#backup\-dir1\-and\-backup\-dir2\[ra]\&.

.PP
Finally, note that the default in bisync prior to \fBv1.66\fR was to rename
conflicts with \fB\&..path1\fR and \fB\&..path2\fR (with two periods, and \fBpath\fR instead of
\fBconflict\fR\&.) Bisync now defaults to a single dot instead of a double dot, but
additional dots can be added by including them in the specified suffix string.
For example, for behavior equivalent to the previous default, use:

.EX
[--conflict-resolve none] --conflict-loser pathname --conflict-suffix .path
.EE

.SS --check-sync
Enabled by default, the check-sync function checks that all of the same
files exist in both the Path1 and Path2 history listings. This \fIcheck-sync\fP
integrity check is performed at the end of the sync run by default.
Any untrapped failing copy/deletes between the two paths might result
in differences between the two listings and in the untracked file content
differences between the two paths. A resync run would correct the error.

.PP
Note that the default-enabled integrity check locally executes a load of both
the final Path1 and Path2 listings, and thus adds to the run time of a sync.
Using \fB--check-sync=false\fR will disable it and may significantly reduce the
sync run times for very large numbers of files.

.PP
The check may be run manually with \fB--check-sync=only\fR\&. It runs only the
integrity check and terminates without actually syncing.

.PP
Note that currently, \fB--check-sync\fR \fBonly checks listing snapshots and NOT the
actual files on the remotes.\fP Note also that the listing snapshots will not
know about any changes that happened during or after the latest bisync run, as
those will be discovered on the next run. Therefore, while listings should
always match \fIeach other\fP at the end of a bisync run, it is \fIexpected\fP that
they will not match the underlying remotes, nor will the remotes match each
other, if there were changes during or after the run. This is normal, and any
differences will be detected and synced on the next run.

.PP
For a robust integrity check of the current state of the remotes (as opposed to
just their listing snapshots), consider using 
\[la]commands/zclone_check/\[ra]
(or 
\[la]//commands/zclone_cryptcheck/\[ra], if at least one path is a
\fBcrypt\fR remote) instead of \fB--check-sync\fR, keeping in mind that differences are
expected if files changed during or after your last bisync run.

.PP
For example, a possible sequence could look like this:
.IP "  1." 5
Normally scheduled bisync run:

.EX
zclone bisync Path1 Path2 -MPc --check-access --max-delete 10 --filters-file /path/to/filters.txt -v --no-cleanup --ignore-listing-checksum --disable ListR --checkers=16 --drive-pacer-min-sleep=10ms --create-empty-src-dirs --resilient
.EE

.RS
.IP "  1." 5
Periodic independent integrity check (perhaps scheduled nightly or weekly):
\fBconsole
zclone check -MvPc Path1 Path2 --filter-from /path/to/filters.txt
\fR
.RE
.IP "  2." 5
If diffs are found, you have some choices to correct them.
If one side is more up-to-date and you want to make the other side match it,
you could run:

.EX
zclone sync Path1 Path2 --filter-from /path/to/filters.txt --create-empty-src-dirs -MPc -v
.EE

(or switch Path1 and Path2 to make Path2 the source-of-truth)
Or, if neither side is totally up-to-date, you could run a \fB--resync\fR to bring them
back into agreement (but remember that this could cause deleted files to
re-appear.)
*Note also that \fBzclone check\fR does not currently include empty directories,
so if you want to know if any empty directories are out of sync,
consider alternatively running the above \fBzclone sync\fR command with \fB--dry-run\fR added.
See also: Concurrent modifications
\[la]#concurrent\-modifications\[ra], 
\[la]#resilient\[ra]

.SS --resilient
By default, most errors or interruptions will cause bisync to abort and
require 
\[la]#resync\[ra] to recover. This is a safety feature,  to prevent
bisync from running again until a user checks things out.  However, in some
cases, bisync can go too far and enforce a lockout when one isn't actually
necessary,  like for certain less-serious errors that might resolve themselves
on the next run.  When \fB--resilient\fR is specified, bisync tries its best to
recover and self-correct,  and only requires \fB--resync\fR as a last resort when a
human's involvement is absolutely necessary.  The intended use case is for
running bisync as a background process (such as via scheduled cron
\[la]#cron\[ra]).
When using \fB--resilient\fR mode, bisync will still report the error and abort,
however it will not lock out future runs -- allowing the possibility of
retrying at the next normally scheduled time,  without requiring a \fB--resync\fR
first. Examples of such retryable errors include  access test failures, missing
listing files, and filter change detections.  These safety features will still
prevent the \fIcurrent\fP run from proceeding --  the difference is that if
conditions have improved by the time of the \fInext\fP run,  that next run will be
allowed to proceed.  Certain more serious errors will still enforce a
\fB--resync\fR lockout, even in \fB--resilient\fR mode, to prevent data loss.
Behavior of \fB--resilient\fR may change in a future version. (See also:

\[la]#recover\[ra], 
\[la]#max\-lock\[ra], Graceful
Shutdown
\[la]#graceful\-shutdown\[ra])

.SS --recover
If \fB--recover\fR is set, in the event of a sudden interruption or other
un-graceful shutdown, bisync will attempt to automatically recover on the next
run, instead of requiring \fB--resync\fR\&. Bisync is able to recover robustly by
keeping one "backup" listing at all times, representing the state of both paths
after the last known successful sync. Bisync can then compare the current state
with this snapshot to determine which changes it needs to retry. Changes that
were synced after this snapshot (during the run that was later interrupted)
will appear to bisync as if they are "new or changed on both sides", but in
most cases this is not a problem, as bisync will simply do its usual "equality
check" and learn that no action needs to be taken on these files, since they
are already identical on both sides.
In the rare event that a file is synced successfully during a run that later
aborts, and then that same file changes AGAIN before the next run, bisync will
think it is a sync conflict, and handle it accordingly. (From bisync's
perspective, the file has changed on both sides since the last trusted sync,
and the files on either side are not currently identical.) Therefore,
\fB--recover\fR carries with it a slightly increased chance of having conflicts --
though in practice this is pretty rare, as the conditions required to cause it
are quite specific. This risk can be reduced by using bisync's "Graceful
Shutdown"
\[la]#graceful\-shutdown\[ra] mode (triggered by sending \fBSIGINT\fR or
\fBCtrl+C\fR), when you have the choice, instead of forcing a sudden termination.
\fB--recover\fR and \fB--resilient\fR are similar, but distinct -- the main difference
is that \fB--resilient\fR is about \fIretrying\fP, while \fB--recover\fR is about
\fIrecovering\fP\&. Most users will probably want both. \fB--resilient\fR allows retrying
when bisync has chosen to abort itself due to safety features such as failing
\fB--check-access\fR or detecting a filter change. \fB--resilient\fR does not cover
external interruptions such as a user shutting down their computer in the
middle of a sync -- that is what \fB--recover\fR is for.

.SS --max-lock
Bisync uses lock files
\[la]#lock\-file\[ra] as a safety feature to prevent
interference from other bisync runs while it is running. Bisync normally
removes these lock files at the end of a run, but if bisync is abruptly
interrupted, these files will be left behind. By default, they will lock out
all future runs, until the user has a chance to manually check things out and
remove the lock. As an alternative, \fB--max-lock\fR can be used to make them
automatically expire after a certain period of time, so that future runs are
not locked out forever, and auto-recovery is possible. \fB--max-lock\fR can be any
duration \fB2m\fR or greater (or \fB0\fR to disable). If set, lock files older than
this will be considered "expired", and future runs will be allowed to disregard
them and proceed. (Note that the \fB--max-lock\fR duration must be set by the
process that left the lock file -- not the later one interpreting it.)
If set, bisync will also "renew" these lock files every \fB--max-lock minus one
minute\fR throughout a run, for extra safety. (For example, with \fB--max-lock 5m\fR,
bisync would renew the lock file (for another 5 minutes) every 4 minutes until
the run has completed.) In other words, it should not be possible for a lock
file to pass its expiration time while the process that created it is still
running -- and you can therefore be reasonably sure that any \fIexpired\fP lock
file you may find was left there by an interrupted run, not one that is still
running and just taking awhile.
If a lock file exists but its contents are unreadable (for example, due to an
incomplete write or disk error), it is treated as expired when \fB--max-lock\fR is
set to a value greater than \fB0\fR\&. If \fB--max-lock\fR is \fB0\fR or not set, an
unreadable lock file will produce an error and block future runs until removed
manually.
If \fB--max-lock\fR is \fB0\fR or not set, the default is that lock files will never
expire, and will block future runs (of these same two bisync paths)
indefinitely.
For maximum resilience from disruptions, consider setting a relatively short
duration like \fB--max-lock 2m\fR along with 
\[la]#resilient\[ra] and

\[la]#recover\[ra], and a relatively frequent cron schedule
\[la]#cron\[ra]\&. The
result will be a very robust "set-it-and-forget-it" bisync run that can
automatically bounce back from almost any interruption it might encounter,
without requiring the user to get involved and run a \fB--resync\fR\&. (See also:
Graceful Shutdown
\[la]#graceful\-shutdown\[ra] mode)

.SS --backup-dir1 and --backup-dir2
As of \fBv1.66\fR, 
\[la]//docs/#backup\-dir\-string\[ra] is supported in bisync.
Because \fB--backup-dir\fR must be a non-overlapping path on the same remote,
Bisync has introduced new \fB--backup-dir1\fR and \fB--backup-dir2\fR flags to support
separate backup-dirs for \fBPath1\fR and \fBPath2\fR (bisyncing between different
remotes with \fB--backup-dir\fR would not otherwise be possible.) \fB--backup-dir1\fR
and \fB--backup-dir2\fR can use different remotes from each other, but
\fB--backup-dir1\fR must use the same remote as \fBPath1\fR, and \fB--backup-dir2\fR must
use the same remote as \fBPath2\fR\&. Each backup directory must not overlap its
respective bisync Path without being excluded by a filter rule.
The standard \fB--backup-dir\fR will also work, if both paths use the same remote
(but note that deleted files from both paths would be mixed together in the
same dir). If either \fB--backup-dir1\fR and \fB--backup-dir2\fR are set, they will
override \fB--backup-dir\fR\&.
Example:

.EX
zclone bisync /Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync gdrive:Bisync --backup-dir1 /Users/someuser/some/local/path/BackupDir --backup-dir2 gdrive:BackupDir --suffix -2023-08-26 --suffix-keep-extension --check-access --max-delete 10 --filters-file /Users/someuser/some/local/path/bisync_filters.txt --no-cleanup --ignore-listing-checksum --checkers=16 --drive-pacer-min-sleep=10ms --create-empty-src-dirs --resilient -MvP --drive-skip-gdocs --fix-case
.EE

.PP
In this example, if the user deletes a file in
\fB/Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync\fR, bisync will propagate the delete to
the other side by moving the corresponding file from \fBgdrive:Bisync\fR to
\fBgdrive:BackupDir\fR\&. If the user deletes a file from \fBgdrive:Bisync\fR, bisync
moves it from \fB/Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync\fR to
\fB/Users/someuser/some/local/path/BackupDir\fR\&.

.PP
In the event of a rename due to a sync conflict
\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra], the
rename is not considered a delete, unless a previous conflict with the same
name already exists and would get overwritten.

.PP
See also: 
\[la]//docs/#suffix\-string\[ra],

\[la]//docs/#suffix\-keep\-extension\[ra]

.SH Operation
.SS Runtime flow details
bisync retains the listings of the \fBPath1\fR and \fBPath2\fR filesystems
from the prior run.
On each successive run it will:
.IP \(bu 2
list files on \fBpath1\fR and \fBpath2\fR, and check for changes on each side.
Changes include \fBNew\fR, \fBNewer\fR, \fBOlder\fR, and \fBDeleted\fR files.
.IP \(bu 2
Propagate changes on \fBpath1\fR to \fBpath2\fR, and vice-versa.

.SS Safety measures
.IP \(bu 2
Lock file prevents multiple simultaneous runs when taking a while.
This can be particularly useful if bisync is run by cron scheduler.
.IP \(bu 2
Handle change conflicts non-destructively by creating
\fB\&.conflict1\fR, \fB\&.conflict2\fR, etc. file versions, according to

\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra], 
\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra],
and 
\[la]#conflict\-suffix\[ra] settings.
.IP \(bu 2
File system access health check using \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files
(see the \fB--check-access\fR flag).
.IP \(bu 2
Abort on excessive deletes - protects against a failed listing
being interpreted as all the files were deleted.
See the \fB--max-delete\fR and \fB--force\fR flags.
.IP \(bu 2
If something evil happens, bisync goes into a safe state to block
damage by later runs. (See Error Handling
\[la]#error\-handling\[ra])

.SS Normal sync checks
.TS
allbox;
l l l l 
l l l l .
\fBType\fP	\fBDescription\fP	\fBResult\fP	\fBImplementation\fP
Path2 new	T{
File is new on Path2, does not exist on Path1
T}	Path2 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path2 to Path1
Path2 newer	T{
File is newer on Path2, unchanged on Path1
T}	Path2 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path2 to Path1
Path2 deleted	T{
File is deleted on Path2, unchanged on Path1
T}	File is deleted	\fBzclone delete\fR Path1
Path1 new	T{
File is new on Path1, does not exist on Path2
T}	Path1 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path1 to Path2
Path1 newer	T{
File is newer on Path1, unchanged on Path2
T}	Path1 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path1 to Path2
Path1 older	T{
File is older on Path1, unchanged on Path2
T}	\fIPath1 version survives\fP	\fBzclone copy\fR Path1 to Path2
Path2 older	T{
File is older on Path2, unchanged on Path1
T}	\fIPath2 version survives\fP	\fBzclone copy\fR Path2 to Path1
Path1 deleted	File no longer exists on Path1	File is deleted	\fBzclone delete\fR Path2
.TE

.SS Unusual sync checks
.TS
allbox;
l l l l 
l l l l .
\fBType\fP	\fBDescription\fP	\fBResult\fP	\fBImplementation\fP
T{
Path1 new/changed AND Path2 new/changed AND Path1 == Path2
T}	T{
File is new/changed on Path1 AND new/changed on Path2 AND Path1 version is currently identical to Path2
T}	No change	None
Path1 new AND Path2 new	T{
File is new on Path1 AND new on Path2 (and Path1 version is NOT identical to Path2)
T}	T{
Conflicts handled according to 
\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra] & 
\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra] settings
T}	default: \fBzclone copy\fR renamed \fBPath2.conflict2\fR file to Path1, \fBzclone copy\fR renamed \fBPath1.conflict1\fR file to Path2
Path2 newer AND Path1 changed	T{
File is newer on Path2 AND also changed (newer/older/size) on Path1 (and Path1 version is NOT identical to Path2)
T}	T{
Conflicts handled according to 
\[la]#conflict\-resolve\[ra] & 
\[la]#conflict\-loser\[ra] settings
T}	default: \fBzclone copy\fR renamed \fBPath2.conflict2\fR file to Path1, \fBzclone copy\fR renamed \fBPath1.conflict1\fR file to Path2
Path2 newer AND Path1 deleted	T{
File is newer on Path2 AND also deleted on Path1
T}	Path2 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path2 to Path1
T{
Path2 deleted AND Path1 changed
T}	T{
File is deleted on Path2 AND changed (newer/older/size) on Path1
T}	Path1 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path1 to Path2
T{
Path1 deleted AND Path2 changed
T}	T{
File is deleted on Path1 AND changed (newer/older/size) on Path2
T}	Path2 version survives	\fBzclone copy\fR Path2 to Path1
.TE

.PP
As of \fBzclone v1.64\fR, bisync is now better at detecting \fIfalse positive\fP sync
conflicts, which would previously have resulted in unnecessary renames and
duplicates. Now, when bisync comes to a file that it wants to rename (because it
is new/changed on both sides), it first checks whether the Path1 and Path2
versions are currently \fIidentical\fP (using the same underlying function as

\[la]commands/zclone_check/\[ra]\&.) If bisync concludes that the files are
identical, it will skip them and move on. Otherwise, it will create renamed
duplicates, as before. This behavior also improves the experience of renaming
directories
\[la]/\[ra],
as a \fB--resync\fR is no longer required, so long as the same change has been made
on both sides.

.SS All files changed check {#all-files-changed}
If \fIall\fP prior existing files on either of the filesystems have changed
(e.g. timestamps have changed due to changing the system's timezone)
then bisync will abort without making any changes.
Any new files are not considered for this check. You could use \fB--force\fR
to force the sync (whichever side has the changed timestamp files wins).
Alternately, a \fB--resync\fR may be used (Path1 versions will be pushed
to Path2). Consider the situation carefully and perhaps use \fB--dry-run\fR
before you commit to the changes.

.SS Modification times
By default, bisync compares files by modification time and size.
If you or your application should change the content of a file
without changing the modification time and size, then bisync will \fInot\fP
notice the change, and thus will not copy it to the other side.
As an alternative, consider comparing by checksum (if your remotes support it).
See 
\[la]#compare\[ra] for details.

.SS Error handling {#error-handling}
Certain bisync critical errors, such as file copy/move failing, will result in
a bisync lockout of following runs. The lockout is asserted because the sync
status and history of the Path1 and Path2 filesystems cannot be trusted,
so it is safer to block any further changes until someone checks things out.
The recovery is to do a \fB--resync\fR again.

.PP
It is recommended to use \fB--resync --dry-run --verbose\fR initially and
\fIcarefully\fP review what changes will be made before running the \fB--resync\fR
without \fB--dry-run\fR\&.

.PP
Most of these events come up due to an error status from an internal call.
On such a critical error the \fB{...}.path1.lst\fR and \fB{...}.path2.lst\fR
listing files are renamed to extension \fB\&.lst-err\fR, which blocks any future
bisync runs (since the normal \fB\&.lst\fR files are not found).
Bisync keeps them under \fBbisync\fR subdirectory of the zclone cache directory,
typically at \fB${HOME}/.cache/zclone/bisync/\fR on Linux.

.PP
Some errors are considered temporary and re-running the bisync is not blocked.
The \fIcritical return\fP blocks further bisync runs.

.PP
See also: 
\[la]#resilient\[ra], 
\[la]#recover\[ra],

\[la]#max\-lock\[ra], Graceful Shutdown
\[la]#graceful\-shutdown\[ra]

.SS Lock file
When bisync is running, a lock file is created in the bisync working directory,
typically at \fB~/.cache/zclone/bisync/PATH1..PATH2.lck\fR on Linux.
If bisync should crash or hang, the lock file will remain in place and block
any further runs of bisync \fIfor the same paths\fP\&.
Delete the lock file as part of debugging the situation.
The lock file effectively blocks follow-on (e.g., scheduled by \fIcron\fP) runs
when the prior invocation is taking a long time.
The lock file contains \fIPID\fP of the blocking process, which may help in debug.
Lock files can be set to automatically expire after a certain amount of time,
using the 
\[la]#max\-lock\[ra] flag.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
that while concurrent bisync runs are allowed, \fIbe very cautious\fP
that there is no overlap in the trees being synched between concurrent runs,
lest there be replicated files, deleted files and general mayhem.

.SS Exit codes
\fBzclone bisync\fR returns the following codes to calling program:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR on a successful run,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fR for a non-critical failing run (a rerun may be successful),
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fR on syntax or usage error,
.IP \(bu 2
\fB7\fR for a critically aborted run (requires a \fB--resync\fR to recover).

.PP
See also the section about exit codes
\[la]//docs/#exit\-code\[ra] in main docs.

.SS Graceful Shutdown
Bisync has a "Graceful Shutdown" mode which is activated by sending \fBSIGINT\fR or
pressing \fBCtrl+C\fR during a run. Once triggered, bisync will use best efforts to
exit cleanly before the timer runs out. If bisync is in the middle of
transferring files, it will attempt to cleanly empty its queue by finishing
what it has started but not taking more. If it cannot do so within 30 seconds,
it will cancel the in-progress transfers at that point and then give itself a
maximum of 60 seconds to wrap up, save its state for next time, and exit. With
the \fB-vP\fR flags you will see constant status updates and a final confirmation
of whether or not the graceful shutdown was successful.

.PP
At any point during the "Graceful Shutdown" sequence, a second \fBSIGINT\fR or
\fBCtrl+C\fR will trigger an immediate, un-graceful exit, which will leave things
in a messier state. Usually a robust recovery will still be possible if using

\[la]#recover\[ra] mode, otherwise you will need to do a \fB--resync\fR\&.

.PP
If you plan to use Graceful Shutdown mode, it is recommended to use

\[la]#resilient\[ra] and 
\[la]#recover\[ra], and it is important to
NOT use 
\[la]//docs/#inplace\[ra], otherwise you risk leaving
partially-written files on one side, which may be confused for real files on
the next run. Note also that in the event of an abrupt interruption, a lock
file
\[la]#lock\-file\[ra] will be left behind to block concurrent runs. You will need
to delete it before you can proceed with the next run (or wait for it to
expire on its own, if using \fB--max-lock\fR\&.)

