\[la]/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Early beta versions of \fBzclone bisync\fR required both underlying backends to support
modification times, and refused to run otherwise.
This limitation has been lifted as of \fBv1.66\fR, as bisync now supports comparing
checksum and/or size instead of (or in addition to) modtime.
See 
\[la]#compare\[ra] for details.

.SS Concurrent modifications
When using \fBLocal, FTP or SFTP\fP remotes with 
\[la]//docs/#inplace\[ra],
zclone does not create \fItemporary\fP files at the destination when copying, and
thus if the connection is lost the created file may be corrupt, which will
likely propagate back to the original path on the next sync, resulting in data
loss. It is therefore recommended to \fIomit\fP \fB--inplace\fR\&.

.PP
Files that \fBchange during\fP a bisync run may result in data loss. Prior to
\fBzclone v1.66\fR, this was commonly seen in highly dynamic environments, where the
filesystem was getting hammered by running processes during the sync. As of
\fBzclone v1.66\fR, bisync was redesigned to use a "snapshot" model, greatly
reducing the risks from changes during a sync. Changes that are not detected
during the current sync will now be detected during the following sync, and will
no longer cause the entire run to throw a critical error. There is additionally
a mechanism to mark files as needing to be internally rechecked next time, for
added safety. It should therefore no longer be necessary to sync only at quiet
times -- however, note that an error can still occur if a file happens to change
at the exact moment it's being read/written by bisync (same as would happen in
\fBzclone sync\fR\&.) (See also: 
\[la]//docs/#ignore\-checksum\[ra],

\[la]//local/#local\-no\-check\-updated\[ra])

.SS Empty directories
By default, new/deleted empty directories on one path are \fInot\fP propagated to
the other side. This is because bisync (and zclone) natively works on files, not
directories. However, this can be changed with the \fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR flag,
which works in much the same way as in 
\[la]//commands/zclone_sync/\[ra] and

\[la]//commands/zclone_copy/\[ra]\&. When used, empty directories created or
deleted on one side will also be created or deleted on the other side. The
following should be noted:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR is not compatible with \fB--remove-empty-dirs\fR\&.
Use only one or the other (or neither).
.IP \(bu 2
It is not recommended to switch back and forth between \fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR
and the default (no \fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR) without running \fB-- resync\fR\&. This
is because it may appear as though all directories (not just the empty ones) were
created/deleted, when actually you've just toggled between making them
visible/invisible to bisync. It looks scarier than it is, but it's still
probably best to stick to one or the other, and use \fB--resync\fR when you need to
switch.

.SS Renamed directories
By default, renaming a folder on the Path1 side results in deleting all files on
the Path2 side and then copying all files again from Path1 to Path2.
Bisync sees this as all files in the old directory name as deleted and all
files in the new directory name as new.

.PP
A recommended solution is to use 
\[la]//docs/#track\-renames\[ra],
which is now supported in bisync as of \fBzclone v1.66\fR\&.
Note that \fB--track-renames\fR is not available during \fB--resync\fR,
as \fB--resync\fR does not delete anything (\fB--track-renames\fR only supports \fBsync\fR,
not \fBcopy\fR\&.)

.PP
Otherwise, the most effective and efficient method of renaming a directory
is to rename it to the same name on both sides. (As of \fBzclone v1.64\fR,
a \fB--resync\fR is no longer required after doing so, as bisync will automatically
detect that Path1 and Path2 are in agreement.)

.PP
Note that although the flag --track-renames ensures that renamed/moved files won't
be deleted and uploaded again, they are still counted as deleted files for purposes
of the --max-delete flag (as this check happens before the rename detection
operation). See this issue
\[la]/\[ra]\&.

.SS \fB--fast-list\fR used by default
Unlike most other zclone commands, bisync uses 
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra]
by default, for backends that support it. In many cases this is desirable, however,
there are some scenarios in which bisync could be faster \fIwithout\fP \fB--fast-list\fR,
and there is also a known issue concerning Google Drive users with many empty directories
\[la]/\[ra]\&.
For now, the recommended way to avoid using \fB--fast-list\fR is to add \fB--disable ListR\fR
to all bisync commands. The default behavior may change in a future version.

.SS Case (and unicode) sensitivity {#case-sensitivity}
As of \fBv1.66\fR, case and unicode form differences no longer cause critical errors,
and normalization (when comparing between filesystems) is handled according to
the same flags and defaults as \fBzclone sync\fR\&. See the following options (all of
which are supported by bisync) to control this behavior more granularly:
.IP \(bu 2

\[la]//docs/#fix\-case\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2

\[la]//docs/#ignore\-case\-sync\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2

\[la]//docs/#no\-unicode\-normalization\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2

\[la]//local/#local\-unicode\-normalization\[ra] and

\[la]//local/#local\-case\-sensitive\[ra] (caution: these are
normally not what you want.)

.PP
Note that in the (probably rare) event that \fB--fix-case\fR is used AND a file is
new/changed on both sides AND the checksums match AND the filename case does not
match, the Path1 filename is considered the winner, for the purposes of
\fB--fix-case\fR (Path2 will be renamed to match it).

.SH Windows support {#windows}
Bisync has been tested on Windows 8.1, Windows 10 Pro 64-bit and on Windows
GitHub runners.

.PP
Drive letters are allowed, including drive letters mapped to network drives
(\fBzclone bisync J:\\localsync GDrive:\fR).
If a drive letter is omitted, the shell current drive is the default.
Drive letters are a single character follows by \fB:\fR, so cloud names
must be more than one character long.

.PP
Absolute paths (with or without a drive letter), and relative paths
(with or without a drive letter) are supported.

.PP
Working directory is created at \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\AppData\\Local\\zclone\\bisync\fR\&.

.PP
Note that bisync output may show a mix of forward \fB/\fR and back \fB\\\fR slashes.

.PP
Be careful of case independent directory and file naming on Windows
vs. case dependent Linux

.SH Filtering {#filtering}
See filtering documentation
\[la]//filtering/\[ra]
for how filter rules are written and interpreted.

.PP
Bisync's 
\[la]#filters\-file\[ra] flag slightly extends the zclone's
--filter-from
\[la]//filtering/#filter\-from\-read\-filtering\-patterns\-from\-a\-file\[ra]
filtering mechanism.
For a given bisync run you may provide \fIonly one\fP \fB--filters-file\fR\&.
The \fB--include*\fR, \fB--exclude*\fR, and \fB--filter\fR flags are also supported.

.SS How to filter directories
Filtering portions of the directory tree is a critical feature for syncing.

.PP
Examples of directory trees (always beneath the Path1/Path2 root level)
you may want to exclude from your sync:
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing only software build intermediate files.
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing application temporary files and data
such as the Windows \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\AppData\\\fR tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing files that are large, less important,
or are getting thrashed continuously by ongoing processes.

.PP
On the other hand, there may be only select directories that you
actually want to sync, and exclude all others. See the
Example include-style filters for Windows user directories
\[la]#include\-filters\[ra]
below.

.SS Filters file writing guidelines
.IP "  1." 5
Begin with excluding directory trees:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
e.g. \fB- /AppData/\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB**\fR on the end is not necessary. Once a given directory level
is excluded then everything beneath it won't be looked at by zclone.
.IP \(bu 2
Exclude such directories that are unneeded, are big, dynamically thrashed,
or where there may be access permission issues.
.IP \(bu 2
Excluding such dirs first will make zclone operations (much) faster.
.IP \(bu 2
Specific files may also be excluded, as with the Dropbox exclusions
example below.
.RE
.IP "  2." 5
Decide if it's easier (or cleaner) to:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Include select directories and therefore \fIexclude everything else\fP -- or --
.IP \(bu 2
Exclude select directories and therefore \fIinclude everything else\fP
.RE
.IP "  3." 5
Include select directories:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Add lines like: \fB+ /Documents/PersonalFiles/**\fR to select which
directories to include in the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB**\fR on the end specifies to include the full depth of the specified tree.
.IP \(bu 2
With Include-style filters, files at the Path1/Path2 root are not included.
They may be included with \fB+ /*\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Place ZCLONE_TEST files within these included directory trees.
They will only be looked for in these directory trees.
.IP \(bu 2
Finish by excluding everything else by adding \fB- **\fR at the end
of the filters file.
.IP \(bu 2
Disregard step 4.
.RE
.IP "  4." 5
Exclude select directories:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Add more lines like in step 1.
For example: \fB-/Desktop/tempfiles/\fR, or \fB- /testdir/\fR\&.
Again, a \fB**\fR on the end is not necessary.
.IP \(bu 2
Do \fInot\fP add a \fB- **\fR in the file. Without this line, everything
will be included that has not been explicitly excluded.
.IP \(bu 2
Disregard step 3.
.RE

.PP
A few rules for the syntax of a filter file expanding on
filtering documentation
\[la]//filtering/\[ra]:
.IP \(bu 2
Lines may start with spaces and tabs - zclone strips leading whitespace.
.IP \(bu 2
If the first non-whitespace character is a \fB#\fR then the line is a comment
and will be ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
The first non-whitespace character on a filter line must be a \fB+\fR or \fB-\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Exactly 1 space is allowed between the \fB+/-\fR and the path term.
.IP \(bu 2
Only forward slashes (\fB/\fR) are used in path terms, even on Windows.
.IP \(bu 2
The rest of the line is taken as the path term.
Trailing whitespace is taken literally, and probably is an error.

.SS Example include-style filters for Windows user directories {#include-filters}
This Windows \fIinclude-style\fP example is based on the sync root (Path1)
set to \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\fR\&. The strategy is to select specific directories
to be synched with a network drive (Path2).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB- /AppData/\fR excludes an entire tree of Windows stored stuff
that need not be synched.
In my case, AppData has >11 GB of stuff I don't care about, and there are
some subdirectories beneath AppData that are not accessible to my
user login, resulting in bisync critical aborts.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows creates cache files starting with both upper and
lowercase \fBNTUSER\fR at \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\fR\&. These files may be dynamic,
locked, and are generally \fIdon't care\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
There are just a few directories with \fImy\fP data that I do want synched,
in the form of \fB+ /<path>\fR\&. By selecting only the directory trees I
want to avoid the dozen plus directories that various apps make
at \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Documents\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Include files in the root of the sync point, \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\fR,
by adding the \fB+ /*\fR line.
.IP \(bu 2
This is an Include-style filters file, therefore it ends with \fB- **\fR
which excludes everything not explicitly included.

.EX
- /AppData/
- NTUSER*
- ntuser*
+ /Documents/Family/**
+ /Documents/Sketchup/**
+ /Documents/Microcapture_Photo/**
+ /Documents/Microcapture_Video/**
+ /Desktop/**
+ /Pictures/**
+ /*
- **
.EE

.PP
Note also that Windows implements several "library" links such as
\fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\My Documents\\My Music\fR pointing to \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Music\fR\&.
zclone sees these as links, so you must add \fB--links\fR to the
bisync command line if you which to follow these links. I find that I get
permission errors in trying to follow the links, so I don't include the
zclone \fB--links\fR flag, but then you get lots of \fBCan't follow symlink…\fR
noise from zclone about not following the links. This noise can be
quashed by adding \fB--quiet\fR to the bisync command line.

.SH Example exclude-style filters files for use with Dropbox {#exclude-filters}
.IP \(bu 2
Dropbox disallows syncing the listed temporary and configuration/data files.
The \fB- <filename>\fR filters exclude these files where ever they may occur
in the sync tree. Consider adding similar exclusions for file types
you don't need to sync, such as core dump and software build files.
.IP \(bu 2
bisync testing creates \fB/testdir/\fR at the top level of the sync tree,
and usually deletes the tree after the test. If a normal sync should run
while the \fB/testdir/\fR tree exists the \fB--check-access\fR phase may fail
due to unbalanced ZCLONE_TEST files.
The \fB- /testdir/\fR filter blocks this tree from being synched.
You don't need this exclusion if you are not doing bisync development testing.
.IP \(bu 2
Everything else beneath the Path1/Path2 root will be synched.
.IP \(bu 2
ZCLONE_TEST files may be placed anywhere within the tree, including the root.

.SS Example filters file for Dropbox {#example-filters-file}
.EX
# Filter file for use with bisync
# See //filtering/ for filtering rules
# NOTICE: If you make changes to this file you MUST do a --resync run.
#         Run with --dry-run to see what changes will be made.

# Dropbox won't sync some files so filter them away here.
# See https://help.dropbox.com/installs-integrations/sync-uploads/files-not-syncing
- .dropbox.attr
- ~*.tmp
- ~$*
- .~*
- desktop.ini
- .dropbox

# Used for bisync testing, so excluded from normal runs
- /testdir/

# Other example filters
#- /TiBU/
#- /Photos/
.EE

.SS How --check-access handles filters
At the start of a bisync run, listings are gathered for Path1 and Path2
while using the user's \fB--filters-file\fR\&. During the check access phase,
bisync scans these listings for \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files.
Any \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR files hidden by the \fB--filters-file\fR are \fInot\fP in the
listings and thus not checked during the check access phase.

.SH Troubleshooting {#troubleshooting}
.SS Reading bisync logs
Here are two normal runs. The first one has a newer file on the remote.
The second has no deltas between local and remote.

.EX
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Synching Path1 "/path/to/local/tree/" with Path2 "dropbox:/"
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Path1 checking for diffs
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : - Path1    File is new                         - file.txt
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Path1:    1 changes:    1 new,    0 newer,    0 older,    0 deleted
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Path2 checking for diffs
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Applying changes
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : - Path1    Queue copy to Path2                 - dropbox:/file.txt
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : - Path1    Do queued copies to                 - Path2
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Updating listings
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Validating listings for Path1 "/path/to/local/tree/" vs Path2 "dropbox:/"
2021/05/16 00:24:38 INFO  : Bisync successful

2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Synching Path1 "/path/to/local/tree/" with Path2 "dropbox:/"
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Path1 checking for diffs
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Path2 checking for diffs
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : No changes found
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Updating listings
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Validating listings for Path1 "/path/to/local/tree/" vs Path2 "dropbox:/"
2021/05/16 00:36:52 INFO  : Bisync successful
.EE

.SS Dry run oddity
The \fB--dry-run\fR messages may indicate that it would try to delete some files.
For example, if a file is new on Path2 and does not exist on Path1 then
it would normally be copied to Path1, but with \fB--dry-run\fR enabled those
copies don't happen, which leads to the attempted delete on Path2,
blocked again by --dry-run: \fB\&... Not deleting as --dry-run\fR\&.

.PP
This whole confusing situation is an artifact of the \fB--dry-run\fR flag.
Scrutinize the proposed deletes carefully, and if the files would have been
copied to Path1 then the threatened deletes on Path2 may be disregarded.

.SS Retries
Zclone has built-in retries. If you run with \fB--verbose\fR you'll see
error and retry messages such as shown below. This is usually not a bug.
If at the end of the run, you see \fBBisync successful\fR and not
\fBBisync critical error\fR or \fBBisync aborted\fR then the run was successful,
and you can ignore the error messages.

.PP
The following run shows an intermittent fail. Lines \fI5\fP and \fI6\fP are
low-level messages. Line \fI6\fP is a bubbled-up \fIwarning\fP message, conveying
the error. Zclone normally retries failing commands, so there may be
numerous such messages in the log.

.PP
Since there are no final error/warning messages on line \fI7\fP, zclone has
recovered from failure after a retry, and the overall sync was successful.

.EX
1: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 INFO  : Synching Path1 "/path/to/local/tree" with Path2 "dropbox:"
2: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 INFO  : Path1 checking for diffs
3: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 INFO  : Path2 checking for diffs
4: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 INFO  : Path2:  113 changes:   22 new,    0 newer,    0 older,   91 deleted
5: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 ERROR : /path/to/local/tree/objects/af: error listing: unexpected end of JSON input
6: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 NOTICE: WARNING  listing try 1 failed.                 - dropbox:
7: 2021/05/14 00:44:12 INFO  : Bisync successful
.EE

.PP
This log shows a \fICritical failure\fP which requires a \fB--resync\fR to recover from.
See the Runtime Error Handling
\[la]#error\-handling\[ra] section.

.EX
2021/05/12 00:49:40 INFO  : Google drive root '': Waiting for checks to finish
2021/05/12 00:49:40 INFO  : Google drive root '': Waiting for transfers to finish
2021/05/12 00:49:40 INFO  : Google drive root '': not deleting files as there were IO errors
2021/05/12 00:49:40 ERROR : Attempt 3/3 failed with 3 errors and: not deleting files as there were IO errors
2021/05/12 00:49:40 ERROR : Failed to sync: not deleting files as there were IO errors
2021/05/12 00:49:40 NOTICE: WARNING  zclone sync try 3 failed.           - /path/to/local/tree/
2021/05/12 00:49:40 ERROR : Bisync aborted. Must run --resync to recover.
.EE

.SS Denied downloads of "infected" or "abusive" files
Google Drive has a filter for certain file types (\fB\&.exe\fR, \fB\&.apk\fR, et cetera)
that by default cannot be copied from Google Drive to the local filesystem.
If you are having problems, run with \fB--verbose\fR to see specifically which
files are generating complaints. If the error is
\fBThis file has been identified as malware or spam and cannot be downloaded\fR,
consider using the flag
--drive-acknowledge-abuse
\[la]//drive/#drive\-acknowledge\-abuse\[ra]\&.

.SS Google Docs (and other files of unknown size) {#gdocs}
As of \fBv1.66\fR, Google Docs
\[la]//drive/#import\-export\-of\-google\-documents\[ra]
(including Google Sheets, Slides, etc.) are now supported in bisync, subject to
the same options, defaults, and limitations as in \fBzclone sync\fR\&. When bisyncing
drive with non-drive backends, the drive -> non-drive direction is controlled
by 
\[la]//drive/#drive\-export\-formats\[ra] (default
\fB"docx,xlsx,pptx,svg"\fR) and the non-drive -> drive direction is controlled by

\[la]//drive/#drive\-import\-formats\[ra] (default none.)

.PP
For example, with the default export/import formats, a Google Sheet on the
drive side will be synced to an \fB\&.xlsx\fR file on the non-drive side. In the
reverse direction, \fB\&.xlsx\fR files with filenames that match an existing Google
Sheet will be synced to that Google Sheet, while \fB\&.xlsx\fR files that do NOT
match an existing Google Sheet will be copied to drive as normal \fB\&.xlsx\fR files
(without conversion to Sheets, although the Google Drive web browser UI may
still give you the option to open it as one.)

.PP
If \fB--drive-import-formats\fR is set (it's not, by default), then all of the
specified formats will be converted to Google Docs, if there is no existing
Google Doc with a matching name. Caution: such conversion can be quite lossy,
and in most cases it's probably not what you want!

.PP
To bisync Google Docs as URL shortcut links (in a manner similar to "Drive for
Desktop"), use: \fB--drive-export-formats url\fR (or
alternatives
\[la]//drive/#exportformats:~:text=available%20Google%20Documents.\-,Extension,macOS,\-Standard%20options\[ra]\&.)

.PP
Note that these link files cannot be edited on the non-drive side -- you will
get errors if you try to sync an edited link file back to drive. They CAN be
deleted (it will result in deleting the corresponding Google Doc.) If you
create a \fB\&.url\fR file on the non-drive side that does not match an existing
Google Doc, bisyncing it will just result in copying the literal \fB\&.url\fR file
over to drive (no Google Doc will be created.) So, as a general rule of thumb,
think of them as read-only placeholders on the non-drive side, and make all
your changes on the drive side.

.PP
Likewise, even with other export-formats, it is best to only move/rename Google
Docs on the drive side. This is because otherwise, bisync will interpret this
as a file deleted and another created, and accordingly, it will delete the
Google Doc and create a new file at the new path. (Whether or not that new file
is a Google Doc depends on \fB--drive-import-formats\fR\&.)

.PP
Lastly, take note that all Google Docs on the drive side have a size of \fB-1\fR
and no checksum. Therefore, they cannot be reliably synced with the
\fB--checksum\fR or \fB--size-only\fR flags. (To be exact: they will still get
created/deleted, and bisync's delta engine will notice changes and queue them
for syncing, but the underlying sync function will consider them identical and
skip them.) To work around this, use the default (modtime and size) instead of
\fB--checksum\fR or \fB--size-only\fR\&.

.PP
To ignore Google Docs entirely, use

\[la]//drive/#drive\-skip\-gdocs\[ra]\&.

.SH Usage examples
.SS Cron {#cron}
Zclone does not yet have a built-in capability to monitor the local file
system for changes and must be blindly run periodically.
On Windows this can be done using a \fITask Scheduler\fP,
on Linux you can use \fICron\fP which is described below.

.PP
The 1st example runs a sync every 5 minutes between a local directory
and an OwnCloud server, with output logged to a runlog file:

.EX
# Minute (0-59)
#      Hour (0-23)
#           Day of Month (1-31)
#                Month (1-12 or Jan-Dec)
#                     Day of Week (0-6 or Sun-Sat)
#                         Command
  */5  *    *    *    *   /path/to/zclone bisync /local/files MyCloud: --check-access --filters-file /path/to/bysync-filters.txt --log-file /path/to//bisync.log
.EE

.PP
See crontab syntax
\[la]https://www.man7.org/linux/man\-pages/man1/crontab.1p.html#INPUT_FILES\[ra]
for the details of crontab time interval expressions.

.PP
If you run \fBzclone bisync\fR as a cron job, redirect stdout/stderr to a file.
The 2nd example runs a sync to Dropbox every hour and logs all stdout (via the \fB>>\fR)
and stderr (via \fB2>&1\fR) to a log file.

.EX
0 * * * * /path/to/zclone bisync /path/to/local/dropbox Dropbox: --check-access --filters-file /home/user/filters.txt >> /path/to/logs/dropbox-run.log 2>&1
.EE

.SS Sharing an encrypted folder tree between hosts
bisync can keep a local folder in sync with a cloud service,
but what if you have some highly sensitive files to be synched?

.PP
Usage of a cloud service is for exchanging both routine and sensitive
personal files between one's home network, one's personal notebook when on the
road, and with one's work computer. The routine data is not sensitive.
For the sensitive data, configure an zclone crypt remote
\[la]//crypt/\[ra] to point to
a subdirectory within the local disk tree that is bisync'd to Dropbox,
and then set up an bisync for this local crypt directory to a directory
outside of the main sync tree.

.SS Linux server setup
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/path/to/DBoxroot\fR is the root of my local sync tree.
There are numerous subdirectories.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/path/to/DBoxroot/crypt\fR is the root subdirectory for files
that are encrypted. This local directory target is setup as an
zclone crypt remote named \fBDropcrypt:\fR\&.
See zclone.conf
\[la]#zclone\-conf\-snippet\[ra] snippet below.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/path/to/my/unencrypted/files\fR is the root of my sensitive
files - not encrypted, not within the tree synched to Dropbox.
.IP \(bu 2
To sync my local unencrypted files with the encrypted Dropbox versions
I manually run \fBbisync /path/to/my/unencrypted/files DropCrypt:\fR\&.
This step could be bundled into a script to run before and after
the full Dropbox tree sync in the last step,
thus actively keeping the sensitive files in sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbisync /path/to/DBoxroot Dropbox:\fR runs periodically via cron,
keeping my full local sync tree in sync with Dropbox.

.SS Windows notebook setup
.IP \(bu 2
The Dropbox client runs keeping the local tree \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Dropbox\fR
always in sync with Dropbox. I could have used \fBzclone bisync\fR instead.
.IP \(bu 2
A separate directory tree at \fBC:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Documents\\DropLocal\fR
hosts the tree of unencrypted files/folders.
.IP \(bu 2
To sync my local unencrypted files with the encrypted
Dropbox versions I manually run the following command:
\fBzclone bisync C:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Documents\\DropLocal Dropcrypt:\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The Dropbox client then syncs the changes with Dropbox.

.SS zclone.conf snippet {#zclone-conf-snippet}
.EX
[Dropbox]
type = dropbox
\&...

[Dropcrypt]
type = crypt
remote = /path/to/DBoxroot/crypt          # on the Linux server
remote = C:\\Users\\MyLogin\\Dropbox\\crypt   # on the Windows notebook
filename_encryption = standard
directory_name_encryption = true
password = ...
\&...
.EE

.SH Testing {#testing}
You should read this section only if you are developing for zclone.
You need to have zclone source code locally to work with bisync tests.

.PP
Bisync has a dedicated test framework implemented in the \fBbisync_test.go\fR
file located in the zclone source tree. The test suite is based on the
\fBgo test\fR command. Series of tests are stored in subdirectories below the
\fBcmd/bisync/testdata\fR directory. Individual tests can be invoked by their
directory name, e.g.
\fBgo test . -case basic -remote local -remote2 gdrive: -v\fR

.PP
Tests will make a temporary folder on remote and purge it afterwards.
If during test run there are intermittent errors and zclone retries,
these errors will be captured and flagged as invalid MISCOMPAREs.
Rerunning the test will let it pass. Consider such failures as noise.

.SS Test command syntax
.EX
usage: go test ./cmd/bisync [options...]

Options:
  -case NAME        Name(s) of the test case(s) to run. Multiple names should
                    be separated by commas. You can remove the `test_` prefix
                    and replace `_` by `-` in test name for convenience.
                    If not `all`, the name(s) should map to a directory under
                    `./cmd/bisync/testdata`.
                    Use `all` to run all tests (default: all)
  -remote PATH1     `local` or name of cloud service with `:` (default: local)
  -remote2 PATH2    `local` or name of cloud service with `:` (default: local)
  -no-compare       Disable comparing test results with the golden directory
                    (default: compare)
  -no-cleanup       Disable cleanup of Path1 and Path2 testdirs.
                    Useful for troubleshooting. (default: cleanup)
  -golden           Store results in the golden directory (default: false)
                    This flag can be used with multiple tests.
  -debug            Print debug messages
  -stop-at NUM      Stop test after given step number. (default: run to the end)
                    Implies `-no-compare` and `-no-cleanup`, if the test really
                    ends prematurely. Only meaningful for a single test case.
  -refresh-times    Force refreshing the target modtime, useful for Dropbox
                    (default: false)
  -verbose          Run tests verbosely
.EE

.PP
Note: unlike zclone flags which must be prefixed by double dash (\fB--\fR), the
test command flags can be equally prefixed by a single \fB-\fR or double dash.

.SS Running tests
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgo test . -case basic -remote local -remote2 local\fR
runs the \fBtest_basic\fR test case using only the local filesystem,
syncing one local directory with another local directory.
Test script output is to the console, while commands within scenario.txt
have their output sent to the \fB\&.../workdir/test.log\fR file,
which is finally compared to the golden copy.
.IP \(bu 2
The first argument after \fBgo test\fR should be a relative name of the
directory containing bisync source code. If you run tests right from there,
the argument will be \fB\&.\fR (current directory) as in most examples below.
If you run bisync tests from the zclone source directory, the command
should be \fBgo test ./cmd/bisync ...\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The test engine will mangle zclone output to ensure comparability
with golden listings and logs.
.IP \(bu 2
Test scenarios are located in \fB\&./cmd/bisync/testdata\fR\&. The test \fB-case\fR
argument should match the full name of a subdirectory under that
directory. Every test subdirectory name on disk must start with \fBtest_\fR,
this prefix can be omitted on command line for brevity. Also, underscores
in the name can be replaced by dashes for convenience.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgo test . -remote local -remote2 local -case all\fR runs all tests.
.IP \(bu 2
Path1 and Path2 may either be the keyword \fBlocal\fR
or may be names of configured cloud services.
\fBgo test . -remote gdrive: -remote2 dropbox: -case basic\fR
will run the test between these two services, without transferring
any files to the local filesystem.
.IP \(bu 2
Test run stdout and stderr console output may be directed to a file, e.g.
\fBgo test . -remote gdrive: -remote2 local -case all > runlog.txt 2>&1\fR

.SS Test execution flow
.IP "  1." 5
The base setup in the \fBinitial\fR directory of the testcase is applied
on the Path1 and Path2 filesystems (via zclone copy the initial directory
to Path1, then zclone sync Path1 to Path2).
.IP "  2." 5
The commands in the scenario.txt file are applied, with output directed
to the \fBtest.log\fR file in the test working directory.
Typically, the first actual command in the \fBscenario.txt\fR file is
to do a \fB--resync\fR, which establishes the baseline
\fB{...}.path1.lst\fR and \fB{...}.path2.lst\fR files in the test working
directory (\fB\&.../workdir/\fR relative to the temporary test directory).
Various commands and listing snapshots are done within the test.
.IP "  3." 5
Finally, the contents of the test working directory are compared
to the contents of the testcase's golden directory.

.SS Notes about testing
.IP \(bu 2
Test cases are in individual directories beneath \fB\&./cmd/bisync/testdata\fR\&.
A command line reference to a test is understood to reference a directory
beneath \fBtestdata\fR\&. For example,
\fBgo test ./cmd/bisync -case dry-run -remote gdrive: -remote2 local\fR
refers to the test case in \fB\&./cmd/bisync/testdata/test_dry_run\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The test working directory is located at \fB\&.../workdir\fR relative to a
temporary test directory, usually under \fB/tmp\fR on Linux.
.IP \(bu 2
The local test sync tree is created at a temporary directory named
like \fBbisync.XXX\fR under system temporary directory.
.IP \(bu 2
The remote test sync tree is located at a temporary directory
under \fB<remote:>/bisync.XXX/\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath1\fR and/or \fBpath2\fR subdirectories are created in a temporary
directory under the respective local or cloud test remote.
.IP \(bu 2
By default, the Path1 and Path2 test dirs and workdir will be deleted
after each test run. The \fB-no-cleanup\fR flag disables purging these
directories when validating and debugging a given test.
These directories will be flushed before running another test,
independent of the \fB-no-cleanup\fR usage.
.IP \(bu 2
You will likely want to add \fB- /testdir/\fR to your normal
bisync \fB--filters-file\fR so that normal syncs do not attempt to sync
the test temporary directories, which may have \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR miscompares
in some testcases which would otherwise trip the \fB--check-access\fR system.
The \fB--check-access\fR mechanism is hard-coded to ignore \fBZCLONE_TEST\fR
files beneath \fBbisync/testdata\fR, so the test cases may reside on the
synched tree even if there are check file mismatches in the test tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Some Dropbox tests can fail, notably printing the following message:
\fBsrc and dst identical but can't set mod time without deleting and re-uploading\fR
This is expected and happens due to the way Dropbox handles modification times.
You should use the \fB-refresh-times\fR test flag to make up for this.
.IP \(bu 2
If Dropbox tests hit request limit for you and print error message
\fBtoo_many_requests/...: Too many requests or write operations.\fR
then follow the
Dropbox App ID instructions
\[la]//dropbox/#get\-your\-own\-dropbox\-app\-id\[ra]\&.

.SS Updating golden results
Sometimes even a slight change in the bisync source can cause little changes
spread around many log files. Updating them manually would be a nightmare.

.PP
The \fB-golden\fR flag will store the \fBtest.log\fR and \fB*.lst\fR listings from each
test case into respective golden directories. Golden results will
automatically contain generic strings instead of local or cloud paths which
means that they should match when run with a different cloud service.

.PP
Your normal workflow might be as follows:
.IP "  1." 5
Git-clone the zclone sources locally
.IP "  2." 5
Modify bisync source and check that it builds
.IP "  3." 5
Run the whole test suite \fBgo test ./cmd/bisync -remote local\fR
.IP "  4." 5
If some tests show log difference, recheck them individually, e.g.:
\fBgo test ./cmd/bisync -remote local -case basic\fR
.IP "  5." 5
If you are convinced with the difference, goldenize all tests at once:
\fBgo test ./cmd/bisync -remote local -golden\fR
.IP "  6." 5
Use word diff: \fBgit diff --word-diff ./cmd/bisync/testdata/\fR\&.
Please note that normal line-level diff is generally useless here.
.IP "  7." 5
Check the difference \fIcarefully\fP!
.IP "  8." 5
Commit the change (\fBgit commit\fR) \fIonly\fP if you are sure.
If unsure, save your code changes then wipe the log diffs from git:
\fBgit reset [--hard]\fR\&.

.SS Structure of test scenarios
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<testname>/initial/\fR contains a tree of files that will be set
as the initial condition on both Path1 and Path2 testdirs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<testname>/modfiles/\fR contains files that will be used to
modify the Path1 and/or Path2 filesystems.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<testname>/golden/\fR contains the expected content of the test
working directory (\fBworkdir\fR) at the completion of the testcase.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<testname>/scenario.txt\fR contains the body of the test, in the form of
various commands to modify files, run bisync, and snapshot listings.
Output from these commands is captured to \fB\&.../workdir/test.log\fR
for comparison to the golden files.

.SS Supported test commands
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtest <some message>\fR
Print the line to the console and to the \fBtest.log\fR:
\fBtest sync is working correctly with options x, y, z\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcopy-listings <prefix>\fR
Save a copy of all \fB\&.lst\fR listings in the test working directory
with the specified prefix:
\fBsave-listings exclude-pass-run\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmove-listings <prefix>\fR
Similar to \fBcopy-listings\fR but removes the source
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpurge-children <dir>\fR
This will delete all child files and purge all child subdirs under given
directory but keep the parent intact. This behavior is important for tests
with Google Drive because removing and re-creating the parent would change
its ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelete-file <file>\fR
Delete a single file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelete-glob <dir> <pattern>\fR
Delete a group of files located one level deep in the given directory
with names matching a given glob pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtouch-glob YYYY-MM-DD <dir> <pattern>\fR
Change modification time on a group of files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtouch-copy YYYY-MM-DD <source-file> <dest-dir>\fR
Change file modification time then copy it to destination.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcopy-file <source-file> <dest-dir>\fR
Copy a single file to given directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcopy-as <source-file> <dest-file>\fR
Similar to above but destination must include both directory
and the new file name at destination.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcopy-dir <src> <dst>\fR and \fBsync-dir <src> <dst>\fR
Copy/sync a directory. Equivalent of \fBzclone copy\fR and \fBzclone sync\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist-dirs <dir>\fR
Equivalent to \fBzclone lsf -R --dirs-only <dir>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbisync [options]\fR
Runs bisync against \fB-remote\fR and \fB-remote2\fR\&.

.SS Supported substitution terms
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{testdir/}\fR - the root dir of the testcase
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{datadir/}\fR - the \fBmodfiles\fR dir under the testcase root
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{workdir/}\fR - the temporary test working directory
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{path1/}\fR - the root of the Path1 test directory tree
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{path2/}\fR - the root of the Path2 test directory tree
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{session}\fR - base name of the test listings
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{/}\fR - OS-specific path separator
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{spc}\fR, \fB{tab}\fR, \fB{eol}\fR - whitespace
.IP \(bu 2
\fB{chr:HH}\fR - raw byte with given hexadecimal code

.PP
Substitution results of the terms named like \fB{dir/}\fR will end with
\fB/\fR (or backslash on Windows), so it is not necessary to include
slash in the usage, for example \fBdelete-file {path1/}file1.txt\fR\&.

.SH Benchmarks
\fIThis section is work in progress.\fP

.PP
Here are a few data points for scale, execution times, and memory usage.

.PP
The first set of data was taken between a local disk to Dropbox.
The speedtest.net
\[la]https://speedtest.net\[ra] download speed was ~170 Mbps,
and upload speed was ~10 Mbps. 500 files (~9.5 MB each) had been already
synched. 50 files were added in a new directory, each ~9.5 MB, ~475 MB total.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBChange\fP	\fBOperations and times\fP	\fBOverall run time\fP
T{
500 files synched (nothing to move)
T}	1x listings for Path1 & Path2	1.5 sec
T{
500 files synched with --check-access
T}	1x listings for Path1 & Path2	1.5 sec
50 new files on remote	Queued 50 copies down: 27 sec	29 sec
Moved local dir	T{
Queued 50 copies up: 410 sec, 50 deletes up: 9 sec
T}	421 sec
Moved remote dir	T{
Queued 50 copies down: 31 sec, 50 deletes down:  sec
T}	33 sec
Delete local dir	Queued 50 deletes up: 9 sec	13 sec
.TE

.PP
This next data is from a user's application. They had ~400GB of data
over 1.96 million files being sync'ed between a Windows local disk and some
remote cloud. The file full path length was on average 35 characters
(which factors into load time and RAM required).
.IP \(bu 2
Loading the prior listing into memory (1.96 million files, listing file
size 140 MB) took ~30 sec and occupied about 1 GB of RAM.
.IP \(bu 2
Getting a fresh listing of the local file system (producing the
140 MB output file) took about XXX sec.
.IP \(bu 2
Getting a fresh listing of the remote file system (producing the 140 MB
output file) took about XXX sec. The network download speed was measured
at XXX Mb/s.
.IP \(bu 2
Once the prior and current Path1 and Path2 listings were loaded (a total
of four to be loaded, two at a time), determining the deltas was pretty
quick (a few seconds for this test case), and the transfer time for any
files to be copied was dominated by the network bandwidth.

.SH References
zclone's bisync implementation was derived from
the zclonesync-V2
\[la]https://github.com/cjnaz/zclonesync\-V2\[ra] project,
including documentation and test mechanisms,
with @cjnaz
\[la]https://github.com/cjnaz\[ra]\&'s full support and encouragement.

.PP
\fBzclone bisync\fR is similar in nature to a range of other projects:
.IP \(bu 2
unison
\[la]https://github.com/bcpierce00/unison\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
syncthing
\[la]https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
cjnaz/zclonesync
\[la]https://github.com/cjnaz/zclonesync\-V2\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
ConorWilliams/rsinc
\[la]https://github.com/ConorWilliams/rsinc\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
jwink3101/synczclone
\[la]https://github.com/Jwink3101/synczclone\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
DavideRossi/upback
\[la]https://github.com/DavideRossi/upback\[ra]

.PP
Bisync adopts the differential synchronization technique, which is
based on keeping history of changes performed by both synchronizing sides.
See the \fIDual Shadow Method\fP section in
Neil Fraser's article
\[la]https://neil.fraser.name/writing/sync/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Also note a number of academic publications by
Benjamin Pierce
\[la]http://www.cis.upenn.edu/%7Ebcpierce/papers/index.shtml#File%20Synchronization\[ra]
about \fIUnison\fP and synchronization in general.

