.SS Modification times and hashes
.SS Modification times
The modified time is stored as metadata on the object as
\fBX-Amz-Meta-Mtime\fR as floating point since the epoch, accurate to 1 ns.

.PP
If the modification time needs to be updated zclone will attempt to perform a server
side copy to update the modification if the object can be copied in a single part.
In the case the object is larger than 5Gb or is in Glacier or Glacier Deep Archive
storage the object will be uploaded rather than copied.

.PP
Note that reading this from the object takes an additional \fBHEAD\fR
request as the metadata isn't returned in object listings.

.SS Hashes
For small objects which weren't uploaded as multipart uploads (objects
sized below \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR if uploaded with zclone) zclone uses
the \fBETag:\fR header as an MD5 checksum.

.PP
However for objects which were uploaded as multipart uploads or with
server side encryption (SSE-AWS or SSE-C) the \fBETag\fR header is no
longer the MD5 sum of the data, so zclone adds an additional piece of
metadata \fBX-Amz-Meta-Md5chksum\fR which is a base64 encoded MD5 hash (in
the same format as is required for \fBContent-MD5\fR).  You can use base64 -d and
hexdump to check this value manually:

.EX
echo 'VWTGdNx3LyXQDfA0e2Edxw==' | base64 -d | hexdump
.EE

.PP
or you can use \fBzclone check\fR to verify the hashes are OK.

.PP
For large objects, calculating this hash can take some time so the
addition of this hash can be disabled with \fB--s3-disable-checksum\fR\&.
This will mean that these objects do not have an MD5 checksum.

.PP
Note that reading this from the object takes an additional \fBHEAD\fR
request as the metadata isn't returned in object listings.

.SS Reducing costs
.SS Avoiding HEAD requests to read the modification time
By default, zclone will use the modification time of objects stored in
S3 for syncing.  This is stored in object metadata which unfortunately
takes an extra HEAD request to read which can be expensive (in time
and money).

.PP
The modification time is used by default for all operations that
require checking the time a file was last updated. It allows zclone to
treat the remote more like a true filesystem, but it is inefficient on
S3 because it requires an extra API call to retrieve the metadata.

.PP
The extra API calls can be avoided when syncing (using \fBzclone sync\fR
or \fBzclone copy\fR) in a few different ways, each with its own
tradeoffs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--size-only\fR
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Only checks the size of files.
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
If the file doesn't change size then zclone won't detect it has
changed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone sync --size-only /path/to/source s3:bucket\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--checksum\fR
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Checks the size and MD5 checksum of files.
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
The most accurate detection of changes possible.
.IP \(bu 2
Will cause the source to read an MD5 checksum which, if it is a
local disk, will cause lots of disk activity.
.IP \(bu 2
If the source and destination are both S3 this is the
\fBrecommended\fP flag to use for maximum efficiency.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone sync --checksum /path/to/source s3:bucket\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--update --use-server-modtime\fR
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
Modification time becomes the time the object was uploaded.
.IP \(bu 2
For many operations this is sufficient to determine if it needs
uploading.
.IP \(bu 2
Using \fB--update\fR along with \fB--use-server-modtime\fR, avoids the
extra API call and uploads files whose local modification time
is newer than the time it was last uploaded.
.IP \(bu 2
Files created with timestamps in the past will be missed by the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone sync --update --use-server-modtime /path/to/source s3:bucket\fR
.RE

.PP
These flags can and should be used in combination with \fB--fast-list\fR -
see below.

.PP
If using \fBzclone mount\fR or any command using the VFS (eg \fBzclone
serve\fR) commands then you might want to consider using the VFS flag
\fB--no-modtime\fR which will stop zclone reading the modification time
for every object. You could also use \fB--use-server-modtime\fR if you are
happy with the modification times of the objects being the time of
upload.

.SS Avoiding GET requests to read directory listings
Zclone's default directory traversal is to process each directory
individually.  This takes one API call per directory.  Using the
\fB--fast-list\fR flag will read all info about the objects into
memory first using a smaller number of API calls (one per 1000
objects). See the zclone docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.EX
zclone sync --fast-list --checksum /path/to/source s3:bucket
.EE

.PP
\fB--fast-list\fR trades off API transactions for memory use. As a rough
guide zclone uses 1k of memory per object stored, so using
\fB--fast-list\fR on a sync of a million objects will use roughly 1 GiB of
RAM.

.PP
If you are only copying a small number of files into a big repository
then using \fB--no-traverse\fR is a good idea. This finds objects directly
instead of through directory listings. You can do a "top-up" sync very
cheaply by using \fB--max-age\fR and \fB--no-traverse\fR to copy only recent
files, eg

.EX
zclone copy --max-age 24h --no-traverse /path/to/source s3:bucket
.EE

.PP
You'd then do a full \fBzclone sync\fR less often.

.PP
Note that \fB--fast-list\fR isn't required in the top-up sync.

.SS Avoiding HEAD requests after PUT
By default, zclone will HEAD every object it uploads. It does this to
check the object got uploaded correctly.

.PP
You can disable this with the --s3-no-head
\[la]#s3\-no\-head\[ra] option - see
there for more details.

.PP
Setting this flag increases the chance for undetected upload failures.

.SS Increasing performance
.SS Using server-side copy
If you are copying objects between S3 buckets in the same region, you should
use server-side copy. This is much faster than downloading and re-uploading
the objects, as no data is transferred.

.PP
For zclone to use server-side copy, you must use the same remote for the
source and destination.

.EX
zclone copy s3:source-bucket s3:destination-bucket
.EE

.PP
When using server-side copy, the performance is limited by the rate at which
zclone issues API requests to S3. See below for how to increase the number of
API requests zclone makes.

.SS Increasing the rate of API requests
You can increase the rate of API requests to S3 by increasing the parallelism
using \fB--transfers\fR and \fB--checkers\fR options.

.PP
Zclone uses a very conservative defaults for these settings, as not all
providers support high rates of requests. Depending on your provider, you can
increase significantly the number of transfers and checkers.

.PP
For example, with AWS S3, if you can increase the number of checkers to values
like 200. If you are doing a server-side copy, you can also increase the number
of transfers to 200.

.EX
zclone sync --transfers 200 --checkers 200 --checksum s3:source-bucket s3:destination-bucket
.EE

.PP
You will need to experiment with these values to find the optimal settings for
your setup.

.SS Data integrity
Zclone does its best to verify every part of an upload or download to
the s3 provider using various hashes.

.PP
Every HTTP transaction to/from the provider has a
\fBX-Amz-Content-Sha256\fR or a \fBContent-Md5\fR header to guard against
corruption of the HTTP body. The HTTP Header is protected by the
signature passed in the \fBAuthorization\fR header.

.PP
All communications with the provider is done over https for encryption
and additional error protection.

.SS Single part uploads
.IP \(bu 2
Zclone uploads single part uploads with a \fBContent-Md5\fR using the
MD5 hash read from the source. The provider checks this is correct
on receipt of the data.
.IP \(bu 2
Zclone then does a HEAD request (disable with \fB--s3-no-head\fR) to
read the \fBETag\fR back which is the MD5 of the file and checks that with
what it sent.

.PP
Note that if the source does not have an MD5 then the single part
uploads will not have hash protection. In this case it is recommended
to use \fB--s3-upload-cutoff 0\fR so all files are uploaded as multipart
uploads.

.SS Multipart uploads
For files above \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR zclone splits the file into
multiple parts for upload.
.IP \(bu 2
Each part is protected with both an \fBX-Amz-Content-Sha256\fR and a
\fBContent-Md5\fR

.PP
When zclone has finished the upload of all the parts it then completes
the upload by sending:
.IP \(bu 2
The MD5 hash of each part
.IP \(bu 2
The number of parts
.IP \(bu 2
This info is all protected with a \fBX-Amz-Content-Sha256\fR

.PP
The provider checks the MD5 for all the parts it has received against
what zclone sends and if it is good it returns OK.

.PP
Zclone then does a HEAD request (disable with \fB--s3-no-head\fR) and
checks the ETag is what it expects (in this case it should be the MD5
sum of all the MD5 sums of all the parts with the number of parts on
the end).

.PP
If the source has an MD5 sum then zclone will attach the
\fBX-Amz-Meta-Md5chksum\fR with it as the \fBETag\fR for a multipart upload
can't easily be checked against the file as the chunk size must be
known in order to calculate it.

.SS Downloads
Zclone checks the MD5 hash of the data downloaded against either the
ETag or the \fBX-Amz-Meta-Md5chksum\fR metadata (if present) which zclone
uploads with multipart uploads.

.SS Further checking
At each stage zclone and the provider are sending and checking hashes of
\fBeverything\fP\&. Zclone deliberately HEADs each object after upload to
check it arrived safely for extra security. (You can disable this with
\fB--s3-no-head\fR).

.PP
If you require further assurance that your data is intact you can use
\fBzclone check\fR to check the hashes locally vs the remote.

.PP
And if you are feeling ultimately paranoid use \fBzclone check --download\fR
which will download the files and check them against the local copies.
(Note that this doesn't use disk to do this - it streams them in
memory).

.SS Versions
When bucket versioning is enabled (this can be done with zclone with
the 
\[la]#versioning\[ra] command) when zclone
uploads a new version of a file it creates a
new version of it
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html\[ra]
Likewise when you delete a file, the old version will be marked hidden
and still be available.

.PP
Old versions of files, where available, are visible using the

\[la]#s3\-versions\[ra] flag.

.PP
It is also possible to view a bucket as it was at a certain point in
time, using the 
\[la]#s3\-version\-at\[ra] flag. This will
show the file versions as they were at that time, showing files that
have been deleted afterwards, and hiding files that were created
since.

.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions then you can use the

\[la]#cleanup\-hidden\[ra]
command which will delete all the old hidden versions of files,
leaving the current ones intact. You can also supply a path and only
old versions under that path will be deleted, e.g.
\fBzclone backend cleanup-hidden remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\fR\&.

.PP
When you \fBpurge\fR a bucket, the current and the old versions will be
deleted then the bucket will be deleted.

.PP
However \fBdelete\fR will cause the current versions of the files to
become hidden old versions.

.PP
Here is a session showing the listing and retrieval of an old
version followed by a \fBcleanup\fR of the old versions.

.PP
Show current version and all the versions with \fB--s3-versions\fR flag.

.EX
$ zclone -q ls s3:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt

$ zclone -q --s3-versions ls s3:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one-v2016-07-04-141032-000.txt
       16 one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
       15 one-v2016-07-02-155621-000.txt
.EE

.PP
Retrieve an old version

.EX
$ zclone -q --s3-versions copy s3:cleanup-test/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt /tmp

$ ls -l /tmp/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
-rw-rw-r-- 1 ncw ncw 16 Jul  2 17:46 /tmp/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
.EE

.PP
Clean up all the old versions and show that they've gone.

.EX
$ zclone -q backend cleanup-hidden s3:cleanup-test

$ zclone -q ls s3:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt

$ zclone -q --s3-versions ls s3:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
.EE

.SS Versions naming caveat
When using \fB--s3-versions\fR flag zclone is relying on the file name
to work out whether the objects are versions or not. Versions' names
are created by inserting timestamp between file name and its extension.

.EX
        9 file.txt
        8 file-v2023-07-17-161032-000.txt
       16 file-v2023-06-15-141003-000.txt
.EE

.PP
If there are real files present with the same names as versions, then
behaviour of \fB--s3-versions\fR can be unpredictable.

.SS Cleanup
If you run \fBzclone cleanup s3:bucket\fR then it will remove all pending
multipart uploads older than 24 hours. You can use the \fB--interactive\fR/\fBi\fR
or \fB--dry-run\fR flag to see exactly what it will do. If you want more control
over the expiry date then run \fBzclone backend cleanup s3:bucket -o max-age=1h\fR
to expire all uploads older than one hour. You can use \fBzclone backend
list-multipart-uploads s3:bucket\fR to see the pending multipart
uploads.

.SS Restricted filename characters
S3 allows any valid UTF-8 string as a key.

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra], as
they can't be used in XML.

.PP
The following characters are replaced since these are problematic when
dealing with the REST API:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
/	0x2F	／
.TE

.PP
The encoding will also encode these file names as they don't seem to
work with the SDK properly:

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBFile name\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
\&.	．
\&..	．．
.TE

.SS Important note about double slashes (\fB//\fR)
Object keys containing consecutive forward slashes (\fB//\fR) are \fBnot supported\fP by zclone.

.PP
When zclone encounters an object key with \fB//\fR (e.g., \fBa//b\fR), it will normalize the path to use a single slash (e.g., \fBa/b\fR). This normalization can cause "object not found" errors when trying to access the original object, as zclone will look for the normalized path instead of the actual object key.

.PP
\fBExample:\fP
- Original S3 object key: \fBfolder//file.txt\fR
- zclone interprets it as: \fBfolder/file.txt\fR
- Result: "object not found" error when trying to access \fBfolder//file.txt\fR

.PP
\fBWorkaround:\fP
Avoid using consecutive forward slashes (\fB//\fR) in S3 object keys when using zclone. If you have existing objects with \fB//\fR in their keys, you will need to rename them to use single slashes or access them through other means.

.SS Multipart uploads
zclone supports multipart uploads with S3 which means that it can
upload files bigger than 5 GiB.

.PP
Note that files uploaded \fIboth\fP with multipart upload \fIand\fP through
crypt remotes do not have MD5 sums.

.PP
zclone switches from single part uploads to multipart uploads at the
point specified by \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR\&.  This can be a maximum of 5 GiB
and a minimum of 0 (ie always upload multipart files).

.PP
The chunk sizes used in the multipart upload are specified by
\fB--s3-chunk-size\fR and the number of chunks uploaded concurrently is
specified by \fB--s3-upload-concurrency\fR\&.

.PP
Multipart uploads will use extra memory equal to: \fB--transfers\fR ×
\fB--s3-upload-concurrency\fR × \fB--s3-chunk-size\fR\&. Single part uploads do not
use extra memory.

.PP
Single part transfers can be faster than multipart transfers or slower
depending on your latency from S3 - the more latency, the more likely
single part transfers will be faster.

.PP
Increasing \fB--s3-upload-concurrency\fR will increase throughput (8 would
be a sensible value) and increasing \fB--s3-chunk-size\fR also increases
throughput (16M would be sensible).  Increasing either of these will
use more memory.  The default values are high enough to gain most of
the possible performance without using too much memory.

.SS Buckets and Regions
With Amazon S3 you can list buckets (\fBzclone lsd\fR) using any region,
but you can only access the content of a bucket from the region it was
created in.  If you attempt to access a bucket from the wrong region,
you will get an error, \fBincorrect region, the bucket is not in 'XXX'
region\fR\&.

.SS Authentication
There are a number of ways to supply \fBzclone\fR with a set of AWS
credentials, with and without using the environment.

.PP
The different authentication methods are tried in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
Directly in the zclone configuration file (\fBenv_auth = false\fR in the config file):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaccess_key_id\fR and \fBsecret_access_key\fR are required.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsession_token\fR can be optionally set when using AWS STS.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime configuration (\fBenv_auth = true\fR in the config file):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Export the following environment variables before running \fBzclone\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
Access Key ID: \fBAWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fR or \fBAWS_ACCESS_KEY\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Secret Access Key: \fBAWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fR or \fBAWS_SECRET_KEY\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Session Token: \fBAWS_SESSION_TOKEN\fR (optional)
.IP \(bu 2
Or, use a named profile
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli\-configure\-files.html\[ra]:
.IP \(bu 2
Profile files are standard files used by AWS CLI tools
.IP \(bu 2
By default it will use the profile in your home directory (e.g. \fB~/.aws/credentials\fR
on unix based systems) file and the "default" profile, to change set these
environment variables or config keys:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE\fR to control which file or the \fBshared_credentials_file\fR
config key.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAWS_PROFILE\fR to control which profile to use or the \fBprofile\fR config key.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \fBzclone\fR in an ECS task with an IAM role (AWS only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \fBzclone\fR on an EC2 instance with an IAM role (AWS only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \fBzclone\fR in an EKS pod with an IAM role that is associated with a
service account (AWS only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, use process credentials
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature\-process\-credentials.html\[ra]
to read config from an external program.
.RE

.PP
With \fBenv_auth = true\fR zclone (which uses the SDK for Go v2) should support
all authentication methods
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/standardized\-credentials.html\[ra]
that the \fBaws\fR CLI tool does and the other AWS SDKs.

.PP
If none of these option actually end up providing \fBzclone\fR with AWS
credentials then S3 interaction will be non-authenticated (see the
anonymous access
\[la]#anonymous\-access\[ra] section for more info).

.SS Assume Role (Cross-Account Access)
If you need to access S3 resources in a different AWS account, you can use IAM role assumption.
This is useful for cross-account access scenarios where you have credentials in one account
but need to access resources in another account.

.PP
To use assume role, configure the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrole_arn\fR - The ARN (Amazon Resource Name) of the IAM role to assume in the target account.
Format: \fBarn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT-ID:role/ROLE-NAME\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrole_session_name\fR (optional) - A name for the assumed role session. If not specified,
zclone will generate one automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrole_session_duration\fR (optional) - Duration for which the assumed role credentials are valid.
If not specified, AWS default duration will be used (typically 1 hour).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrole_external_id\fR (optional) - An external ID required by the role's trust policy for additional security.
This is typically used when the role is accessed by a third party.

.PP
The assume role feature works with both direct credentials (\fBenv_auth = false\fR) and environment-based
authentication (\fBenv_auth = true\fR). Zclone will first authenticate using the base credentials, then
use those credentials to assume the specified role.

.PP
Example configuration for cross-account access:

.EX
[s3-cross-account]
type = s3
provider = AWS
env_auth = true
region = us-east-1
role_arn = arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/CrossAccountS3Role
role_session_name = zclone-session
role_external_id = unique-role-external-id-12345
.EE

.PP
In this example:
- Base credentials are obtained from the environment (IAM role, credentials file, or environment variables)
- These credentials are then used to assume the role \fBCrossAccountS3Role\fR in account \fB123456789012\fR
- An external ID is provided for additional security as required by the role's trust policy

.PP
The target role's trust policy in the destination account must allow the source account or user to assume it.
Example trust policy:

.EX
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::SOURCE-ACCOUNT-ID:root"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "sts:ExternalID": "unique-role-external-id-12345"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
.EE

.SS S3 Permissions
When using the \fBsync\fR subcommand of \fBzclone\fR the following minimum
permissions are required to be available on the bucket being written to:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBListBucket\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDeleteObject\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGetObject\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPutObject\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPutObjectACL\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCreateBucket\fR (unless using s3-no-check-bucket
\[la]#s3\-no\-check\-bucket\[ra])

.PP
When using the \fBlsd\fR subcommand, the \fBListAllMyBuckets\fR permission is required.

.PP
Example policy:

.EX
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::USER_SID:user/USER_NAME"
      },
      "Action": [
        "s3:ListBucket",
        "s3:DeleteObject",
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:PutObjectAcl"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:ListAllMyBuckets",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::*"
    }
  ]
}
.EE

.PP
Notes on above:
.IP "  1." 5
This is a policy that can be used when creating bucket. It assumes
that \fBUSER_NAME\fR has been created.
.IP "  2." 5
The Resource entry must include both resource ARNs, as one implies
the bucket and the other implies the bucket's objects.
.IP "  3." 5
When using s3-no-check-bucket
\[la]#s3\-no\-check\-bucket\[ra] and the bucket already
exists, the \fB"arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME"\fR doesn't have to be included.

.PP
For reference, here's an Ansible script
\[la]https://gist.github.com/ebridges/ebfc9042dd7c756cd101cfa807b7ae2b\[ra]
that will generate one or more buckets that will work with \fBzclone sync\fR\&.

.SS Key Management System (KMS)
If you are using server-side encryption with KMS then you must make
sure zclone is configured with \fBserver_side_encryption = aws:kms\fR
otherwise you will find you can't transfer small objects - these will
create checksum errors.

.SS Glacier and Glacier Deep Archive
You can upload objects using the glacier storage class or transition them to
glacier using a lifecycle policy
\[la]http://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/user\-guide/create\-lifecycle.html\[ra]\&.
The bucket can still be synced or copied into normally, but if zclone
tries to access data from the glacier storage class you will see an error like below.

.EX
2017/09/11 19:07:43 Failed to sync: failed to open source object: Object in GLACIER, restore first: path/to/file
.EE

.PP
In this case you need to restore
\[la]http://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/user\-guide/restore\-archived\-objects.html\[ra]
the object(s) in question before accessing object contents.
The restore
\[la]#restore\[ra] section below shows how to do this with zclone.

.PP
Note that zclone only speaks the S3 API it does not speak the Glacier
Vault API, so zclone cannot directly access Glacier Vaults.

.SS Object-lock enabled S3 bucket
According to AWS's documentation on S3 Object Lock
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object\-lock\-overview.html#object\-lock\-permission\[ra]:

.PP
.RS

.PP
If you configure a default retention period on a bucket, requests to upload
objects in such a bucket must include the Content-MD5 header.

.RE

.PP
Zclone automatically computes and includes the Content-MD5 header when
uploading objects with Object Lock parameters set. No special
configuration is needed.

.SS Setting Object Lock retention
Zclone supports setting Object Lock retention on uploaded objects with
the following options:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-object-lock-mode\fR - Set the Object Lock mode (GOVERNANCE or COMPLIANCE)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-object-lock-retain-until-date\fR - Set the retention date (RFC3339 format or duration like \fB365d\fR, \fB24h\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-object-lock-legal-hold-status\fR - Set legal hold (ON or OFF)

.PP
Example - upload files with 1 year GOVERNANCE retention:

.EX
zclone copy local:/data remote:bucket \\
    --s3-object-lock-mode GOVERNANCE \\
    --s3-object-lock-retain-until-date 365d
.EE

.SS Copying Object Lock settings from source
When using \fB--metadata\fR, you can use the special value \fBcopy\fR to preserve
the source object's Object Lock settings:

.EX
zclone copy source:bucket dest:bucket \\
    --metadata \\
    --s3-object-lock-mode copy \\
    --s3-object-lock-retain-until-date copy
.EE

.PP
You can also mix copied and explicit values. For example, to change the
mode from COMPLIANCE to GOVERNANCE while preserving the original retention date:

.EX
zclone copy source:bucket dest:bucket \\
    --metadata \\
    --s3-object-lock-mode GOVERNANCE \\
    --s3-object-lock-retain-until-date copy
.EE

.SS Additional Object Lock options
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-bypass-governance-retention\fR - Required to delete or overwrite objects
with GOVERNANCE mode retention before the retention date expires
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-bucket-object-lock-enabled\fR - Enable Object Lock when creating a new
bucket with \fBzclone mkdir\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--s3-object-lock-set-after-upload\fR - Set Object Lock via separate API calls
after upload (for providers that don't support Object Lock headers during PUT)

.PP
 

