.SS --s3-decompress
If set this will decompress gzip encoded objects.

.PP
It is possible to upload objects to S3 with "Content-Encoding: gzip"
set. Normally zclone will download these files as compressed objects.

.PP
If this flag is set then zclone will decompress these files with
"Content-Encoding: gzip" as they are received. This means that zclone
can't check the size and hash but the file contents will be decompressed.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      decompress
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_S3_DECOMPRESS
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false

.SS --s3-might-gzip
Set this if the backend might gzip objects.

.PP
Normally providers will not alter objects when they are downloaded. If
an object was not uploaded with \fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR then it won't
be set on download.

.PP
However some providers may gzip objects even if they weren't uploaded
with \fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR (eg Cloudflare).

.PP
A symptom of this would be receiving errors like

.EX
ERROR corrupted on transfer: sizes differ NNN vs MMM
.EE

.PP
If you set this flag and zclone downloads an object with
Content-Encoding: gzip set and chunked transfer encoding, then zclone
will decompress the object on the fly.

.PP
If this is set to unset (the default) then zclone will choose
according to the provider setting what to apply, but you can override
zclone's choice here.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      might_gzip
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_S3_MIGHT_GZIP
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        Tristate
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     unset

