\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] docs for more info.

.SH Backend commands
Here are the commands specific to the s3 backend.

.PP
Run them with:

.EX
zclone backend COMMAND remote:
.EE

.PP
The help below will explain what arguments each command takes.

.PP
See the backend
\[la]//commands/zclone_backend/\[ra] command for more
info on how to pass options and arguments.

.PP
These can be run on a running backend using the rc command
backend/command
\[la]//rc/#backend\-command\[ra]\&.

.SS restore
Restore objects from GLACIER or INTELLIGENT-TIERING archive tier.

.EX
zclone backend restore remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command can be used to restore one or more objects from GLACIER to normal
storage or from INTELLIGENT-TIERING Archive Access / Deep Archive Access tier
to the Frequent Access tier.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/ --include /object -o priority=PRIORITY -o lifetime=DAYS
zclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/directory -o priority=PRIORITY -o lifetime=DAYS
zclone backend restore s3:bucket -o priority=PRIORITY -o lifetime=DAYS
zclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/directory -o priority=PRIORITY
.EE

.PP
This flag also obeys the filters. Test first with --interactive/-i or --dry-run
flags.

.EX
zclone --interactive backend restore --include "*.txt" s3:bucket/path -o priority=Standard -o lifetime=1
.EE

.PP
All the objects shown will be marked for restore, then:

.EX
zclone backend restore --include "*.txt" s3:bucket/path -o priority=Standard -o lifetime=1
.EE

.PP
It returns a list of status dictionaries with Remote and Status
keys. The Status will be OK if it was successful or an error message
if not.

.EX
[
    {
        "Status": "OK",
        "Remote": "test.txt"
    },
    {
        "Status": "OK",
        "Remote": "test/file4.txt"
    }
]
.EE

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"description": The optional description for the job.
.IP \(bu 2
"lifetime": Lifetime of the active copy in days, ignored for INTELLIGENT-TIERING
storage.
.IP \(bu 2
"priority": Priority of restore: Standard|Expedited|Bulk

.SS restore-status
Show the status for objects being restored from GLACIER or INTELLIGENT-TIERING.

.EX
zclone backend restore-status remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command can be used to show the status for objects being restored from
GLACIER to normal storage or from INTELLIGENT-TIERING Archive Access / Deep
Archive Access tier to the Frequent Access tier.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend restore-status s3:bucket/path/to/object
zclone backend restore-status s3:bucket/path/to/directory
zclone backend restore-status -o all s3:bucket/path/to/directory
.EE

.PP
This command does not obey the filters.

.PP
It returns a list of status dictionaries:

.EX
[
    {
        "Remote": "file.txt",
        "VersionID": null,
        "RestoreStatus": {
            "IsRestoreInProgress": true,
            "RestoreExpiryDate": "2023-09-06T12:29:19+01:00"
        },
        "StorageClass": "GLACIER"
    },
    {
        "Remote": "test.pdf",
        "VersionID": null,
        "RestoreStatus": {
            "IsRestoreInProgress": false,
            "RestoreExpiryDate": "2023-09-06T12:29:19+01:00"
        },
        "StorageClass": "DEEP_ARCHIVE"
    },
    {
        "Remote": "test.gz",
        "VersionID": null,
        "RestoreStatus": {
            "IsRestoreInProgress": true,
            "RestoreExpiryDate": "null"
        },
        "StorageClass": "INTELLIGENT_TIERING"
    }
]
.EE

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"all": If set then show all objects, not just ones with restore status.

.SS list-multipart-uploads
List the unfinished multipart uploads.

.EX
zclone backend list-multipart-uploads remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command lists the unfinished multipart uploads in JSON format.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend list-multipart s3:bucket/path/to/object
.EE

.PP
It returns a dictionary of buckets with values as lists of unfinished
multipart uploads.

.PP
You can call it with no bucket in which case it lists all bucket, with
a bucket or with a bucket and path.

.EX
{
    "zclone": [
        {
            "Initiated": "2020-06-26T14:20:36Z",
            "Initiator": {
                "DisplayName": "XXX",
                "ID": "arn:aws:iam::XXX:user/XXX"
            },
            "Key": "KEY",
            "Owner": {
                "DisplayName": null,
                "ID": "XXX"
            },
            "StorageClass": "STANDARD",
            "UploadId": "XXX"
        }
    ],
    "zclone-1000files": [],
    "zclone-dst": []
}
.EE

.SS cleanup
Remove unfinished multipart uploads.

.EX
zclone backend cleanup remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command removes unfinished multipart uploads of age greater than
max-age which defaults to 24 hours.

.PP
Note that you can use --interactive/-i or --dry-run with this command to see
what it would do.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend cleanup s3:bucket/path/to/object
zclone backend cleanup -o max-age=7w s3:bucket/path/to/object
.EE

.PP
Durations are parsed as per the rest of zclone, 2h, 7d, 7w etc.

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"max-age": Max age of upload to delete.

.SS cleanup-hidden
Remove old versions of files.

.EX
zclone backend cleanup-hidden remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command removes any old hidden versions of files
on a versions enabled bucket.

.PP
Note that you can use --interactive/-i or --dry-run with this command to see
what it would do.

.PP
Usage example:

.EX
zclone backend cleanup-hidden s3:bucket/path/to/dir
.EE

.SS versioning
Set/get versioning support for a bucket.

.EX
zclone backend versioning remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command sets versioning support if a parameter is
passed and then returns the current versioning status for the bucket
supplied.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend versioning s3:bucket # read status only
zclone backend versioning s3:bucket Enabled
zclone backend versioning s3:bucket Suspended
.EE

.PP
It may return "Enabled", "Suspended" or "Unversioned". Note that once
versioning has been enabled the status can't be set back to "Unversioned".

.SS set
Set command for updating the config parameters.

.EX
zclone backend set remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This set command can be used to update the config parameters
for a running s3 backend.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend set s3: [-o opt_name=opt_value] [-o opt_name2=opt_value2]
zclone rc backend/command command=set fs=s3: [-o opt_name=opt_value] [-o opt_name2=opt_value2]
zclone rc backend/command command=set fs=s3: -o session_token=X -o access_key_id=X -o secret_access_key=X
.EE

.PP
The option keys are named as they are in the config file.

.PP
This rebuilds the connection to the s3 backend when it is called with
the new parameters. Only new parameters need be passed as the values
will default to those currently in use.

.PP
It doesn't return anything.

.SS Anonymous access to public buckets {#anonymous-access}
If you want to use zclone to access a public bucket, configure with a
blank \fBaccess_key_id\fR and \fBsecret_access_key\fR\&.  Your config should end
up looking like this:

.EX
[anons3]
type = s3
provider = AWS
.EE

.PP
Then use it as normal with the name of the public bucket, e.g.

.EX
zclone lsd anons3:1000genomes
.EE

.PP
You will be able to list and copy data but not upload it.

.PP
You can also do this entirely on the command line

.EX
zclone lsd :s3,provider=AWS:1000genomes
.EE

.SH Providers
.SS AWS S3
This is the provider used as main example and described in the configuration
\[la]#configuration\[ra]
section above.

.SS AWS Directory Buckets
From zclone v1.69 Directory Buckets
\[la]https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/directory\-buckets\-overview.html\[ra]
are supported.

.PP
You will need to set the \fBdirectory_bucket = true\fR config parameter
or use \fB--s3-directory-bucket\fR\&.

.PP
Note that zclone cannot yet:
.IP \(bu 2
Create directory buckets
.IP \(bu 2
List directory buckets

.PP
See the --s3-directory-bucket flag
\[la]#s3\-directory\-bucket\[ra] for more info

.SS AWS Snowball Edge
AWS Snowball
\[la]https://aws.amazon.com/snowball/\[ra] is a hardware
appliance used for transferring bulk data back to AWS. Its main
software interface is S3 object storage.

.PP
To use zclone with AWS Snowball Edge devices, configure as standard
for an 'S3 Compatible Service'.

.PP
If using zclone pre v1.59 be sure to set \fBupload_cutoff = 0\fR otherwise
you will run into authentication header issues as the snowball device
does not support query parameter based authentication.

.PP
With zclone v1.59 or later setting \fBupload_cutoff\fR should not be necessary.

.PP
eg.

.EX
[snowball]
type = s3
provider = Other
access_key_id = YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key = YOUR_SECRET_KEY
endpoint = http://[IP of Snowball]:8080
upload_cutoff = 0
.EE

