.PP
This will leave your config looking something like:

.EX
[r2]
type = s3
provider = Cloudflare
access_key_id = ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key = SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
region = auto
endpoint = https://ACCOUNT_ID.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
acl = private
.EE

.PP
Now run \fBzclone lsf r2:\fR to see your buckets and \fBzclone lsf
r2:bucket\fR to look within a bucket.

.PP
For R2 tokens with the "Object Read & Write" permission, you may also
need to add \fBno_check_bucket = true\fR for object uploads to work
correctly.

.PP
Note that Cloudflare decompresses files uploaded with
\fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR by default which is a deviation from what AWS
does. If this is causing a problem then upload the files with
\fB--header-upload "Cache-Control: no-transform"\fR

.PP
A consequence of this is that \fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR will never
appear in the metadata on Cloudflare.

.SS Cubbit DS3 {#Cubbit}
Cubbit Object Storage
\[la]https://www.cubbit.io/ds3\-cloud\[ra] is a geo-distributed
cloud object storage platform.

.PP
To connect to Cubbit DS3 you will need an access key and secret key pair. You
can follow this guide
\[la]https://docs.cubbit.io/getting\-started/quickstart#api\-keys\[ra]
to retrieve these keys. They will be needed when prompted by \fBzclone config\fR\&.

.PP
Default region will correspond to \fBeu-west-1\fR and the endpoint has to be specified
as \fBs3.cubbit.eu\fR\&. If you set a custom tenant endpoint, you must use
\fBs3.{tenant_name}.cubbit.eu\fR, because the generic \fBs3.cubbit.eu\fR endpoint will not
work in this case.

.PP
Going through the whole process of creating a new remote by running \fBzclone config\fR,
each prompt should be answered as shown below:

.EX
name> cubbit-ds3 (or any name you like)
Storage> s3
provider> Cubbit
env_auth> false
access_key_id> YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key> YOUR_SECRET_KEY
region> eu-west-1 (or leave empty)
endpoint> s3.cubbit.eu (or s3.{tenant_name}.cubbit.eu)
acl>
.EE

.PP
The resulting configuration file should look like:

.EX
[cubbit-ds3]
type = s3
provider = Cubbit
access_key_id = ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key = SECRET_KEY
region = eu-west-1
endpoint = s3.cubbit.eu
.EE

.PP
You can then start using Cubbit DS3 with zclone. For example, to create a new
bucket and copy files into it, you can run:

.EX
zclone mkdir cubbit-ds3:my-bucket
zclone copy /path/to/files cubbit-ds3:my-bucket
.EE

.SS DigitalOcean Spaces
Spaces
\[la]https://www.digitalocean.com/products/object\-storage/\[ra] is an
S3-interoperable
\[la]https://developers.digitalocean.com/documentation/spaces/\[ra]
object storage service from cloud provider DigitalOcean.

.PP
To connect to DigitalOcean Spaces you will need an access key and secret key.
These can be retrieved on the Applications & API
\[la]https://cloud.digitalocean.com/settings/api/tokens\[ra]
page of the DigitalOcean control panel. They will be needed when prompted by
\fBzclone config\fR for your \fBaccess_key_id\fR and \fBsecret_access_key\fR\&.

.PP
When prompted for a \fBregion\fR or \fBlocation_constraint\fR, press enter to use the
default value. The region must be included in the \fBendpoint\fR setting (e.g.
\fBnyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com\fR). The default values can be used for other settings.

.PP
Going through the whole process of creating a new remote by running \fBzclone config\fR,
each prompt should be answered as shown below:

.EX
Storage> s3
env_auth> 1
access_key_id> YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key> YOUR_SECRET_KEY
region>
endpoint> nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com
location_constraint>
acl>
storage_class>
.EE

.PP
The resulting configuration file should look like:

.EX
[spaces]
type = s3
provider = DigitalOcean
env_auth = false
access_key_id = YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key = YOUR_SECRET_KEY
region =
endpoint = nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com
location_constraint =
acl =
server_side_encryption =
storage_class =
.EE

.PP
Once configured, you can create a new Space and begin copying files. For example:

.EX
zclone mkdir spaces:my-new-space
zclone copy /path/to/files spaces:my-new-space
.EE

.SS Dreamhost
Dreamhost DreamObjects
\[la]https://www.dreamhost.com/cloud/storage/\[ra] is
an object storage system based on CEPH.

.PP
To use zclone with Dreamhost, configure as above but leave the region blank
and set the endpoint.  You should end up with something like this in
your config:

.EX
[dreamobjects]
type = s3
provider = DreamHost
env_auth = false
access_key_id = your_access_key
secret_access_key = your_secret_key
region =
endpoint = objects-us-west-1.dream.io
location_constraint =
acl = private
server_side_encryption =
storage_class =
.EE

.SS Exaba {#exaba}
Exaba
\[la]https://exaba.com/\[ra] is an on-premises, S3-compatible storage
for service providers and large enterprises. It is quick to deploy,
with dynamic node management, tenant accounting, and a built-in key
management system. It delivers secure, high-performance data storage
with flexible, usage-based pricing.

.PP
A container version
exaba/exaba
\[la]https://hub.docker.com/r/exaba/exaba\[ra] is free for
end-users and on that page you can find instructions on how to set it
up. You will need to log into the admin first (on port 9006 by
default) to set up the container, then you can use the service on port
9000.

