.SS Known limitations
These limitations have been observed with HCP version 9.7.x.

.PP
\fBAutomatic directory objects\fP

.PP
HCP automatically creates zero-byte directory objects (keys ending in \fB/\fR) for
every path component when an object is stored. These directory objects are
managed by HCP internally: deleting one via the S3 API does not remove it but
instead creates a delete marker with a special version ID (\fB-3\fR) that cannot
itself be deleted. As a result, zclone cannot fully purge a bucket on HCP —
leftover directory objects will remain after a \fBpurge\fR or \fBdelete\fR operation.

.PP
\fBTransparent gzip decompression\fP

.PP
HCP transparently decompresses
\[la]https://docs.hitachivantara.com/r/en\-us/content\-platform/9.6.x/mk\-95hcph002/using\-the\-hitachi\-api\-for\-amazon\-s3/working\-with\-objects/creating\-an\-object\[ra]
objects uploaded with \fBContent-Encoding: gzip\fR before storing them. This means
the stored object contains the raw (uncompressed) data, while the \fBContent-MD5\fR
sent by zclone was computed over the compressed bytes. HCP will reject such
uploads with a \fBBadDigest\fR error. To avoid this, do not set
\fBcontent-encoding: gzip\fR in object metadata when using HCP.

.SS Huawei OBS {#huawei-obs}
