.SS RackCorp {#RackCorp}
RackCorp Object Storage
\[la]https://www.rackcorp.com/storage/s3storage\[ra] is an S3
compatible object storage platform from your friendly cloud provider RackCorp.
The service is fast, reliable, well priced and located in many strategic
locations unserviced by others, to ensure you can maintain data sovereignty.

.PP
Before you can use RackCorp Object Storage, you'll need to
sign up
\[la]https://www.rackcorp.com/signup\[ra] for an account on our portal
\[la]https://portal.rackcorp.com\[ra]\&.
Next you can create an \fBaccess key\fR, a \fBsecret key\fR and \fBbuckets\fR, in your
location of choice with ease. These details are required for the next steps of
configuration, when \fBzclone config\fR asks for your \fBaccess_key_id\fR and \fBsecret_access_key\fR\&.

.PP
Your config should end up looking a bit like this:

.EX
[RCS3-demo-config]
type = s3
provider = RackCorp
env_auth = true
access_key_id = YOURACCESSKEY
secret_access_key = YOURSECRETACCESSKEY
region = au-nsw
endpoint = s3.rackcorp.com
location_constraint = au-nsw
.EE

.SS Zclone Serve S3 {#zclone}
Zclone can serve any remote over the S3 protocol. For details see the
zclone serve s3
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve_s3/\[ra] documentation.

.PP
For example, to serve \fBremote:path\fR over s3, run the server like this:

.EX
zclone serve s3 --auth-key ACCESS_KEY_ID,SECRET_ACCESS_KEY remote:path
.EE

.PP
This will be compatible with an zclone remote which is defined like this:

.EX
[serves3]
type = s3
provider = Zclone
endpoint = http://127.0.0.1:8080/
access_key_id = ACCESS_KEY_ID
secret_access_key = SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
use_multipart_uploads = false
.EE

.PP
Note that setting \fBuse_multipart_uploads = false\fR is to work around
a bug
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve_s3/#bugs\[ra] which will be fixed in due course.

.SS Scaleway
Scaleway
\[la]https://www.scaleway.com/object\-storage/\[ra] The Object Storage platform
allows you to store anything from backups, logs and web assets to documents and photos.
Files can be dropped from the Scaleway console or transferred through our API and
CLI or using any S3-compatible tool.

.PP
Scaleway provides an S3 interface which can be configured for use with zclone
like this:

.EX
[scaleway]
type = s3
provider = Scaleway
env_auth = false
endpoint = s3.nl-ams.scw.cloud
access_key_id = SCWXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
secret_access_key = 1111111-2222-3333-44444-55555555555555
region = nl-ams
location_constraint = nl-ams
acl = private
upload_cutoff = 5M
chunk_size = 5M
copy_cutoff = 5M
.EE

.PP
Scaleway Glacier
\[la]https://www.scaleway.com/en/glacier\-cold\-storage/\[ra] is the
low-cost S3 Glacier alternative from Scaleway and it works the same way as on
S3 by accepting the "GLACIER" \fBstorage_class\fR\&. So you can configure your remote
with the \fBstorage_class = GLACIER\fR option to upload directly to Scaleway Glacier.
Don't forget that in this state you can't read files back after, you will need
to restore them to "STANDARD" storage_class first before being able to read
them (see "restore" section above)

.SS Scality {#scality}
Scality
\[la]https://www.scality.com/\[ra] RING and ARTESCA both expose an
S3-compatible object storage API that works with zclone using \fBs3\fR as the
type and \fBScality\fR as the provider.

.PP
Both products are S3 clones, so you always need to set the \fBendpoint\fR to the
S3 API of your deployment (the S3 Connector service on RING, or the S3
data-service endpoint on ARTESCA). The \fBScality\fR provider uses path-style
addressing (\fBendpoint/bucket/key\fR) which is supported out of the box and does
not require any wildcard/virtual-host DNS entries.

.PP
Get your access key, secret key and endpoint from your Scality administrator
(on ARTESCA these are created per account under \fIIdentity > Accounts\fP in the
UI; on RING they are created with \fBvaultclient\fR or the S3 Console).

.PP
