.PP
Note that s3 credentials are generated when you create an access
grant
\[la]https://docs.storj.io/dcs/api\-reference/s3\-compatible\-gateway#usage\[ra]\&.

.SS Backend quirks
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--chunk-size\fR is forced to be 64 MiB or greater. This will use more
memory than the default of 5 MiB.
.IP \(bu 2
Server side copy is disabled as it isn't currently supported in the
gateway.
.IP \(bu 2
GetTier and SetTier are not supported.

.SS Backend bugs
Due to issue #39
\[la]https://github.com/storj/gateway\-mt/issues/39\[ra]
uploading multipart files via the S3 gateway causes them to lose their
metadata. For zclone's purpose this means that the modification time
is not stored, nor is any MD5SUM (if one is available from the
source).

.PP
This has the following consequences:
.IP \(bu 2
Using \fBzclone rcat\fR will fail as the metadata doesn't match after upload
.IP \(bu 2
Uploading files with \fBzclone mount\fR will fail for the same reason
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
This can worked around by using \fB--vfs-cache-mode writes\fR or
\fB--vfs-cache-mode full\fR or setting \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR large
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Files uploaded via a multipart upload won't have their modtimes
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
This will mean that \fBzclone sync\fR will likely keep trying to upload
files bigger than \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR
.IP \(bu 2
This can be worked around with \fB--checksum\fR or \fB--size-only\fR or
setting \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR large
.IP \(bu 2
The maximum value for \fB--s3-upload-cutoff\fR is 5GiB though
.RE

.PP
One general purpose workaround is to set \fB--s3-upload-cutoff 5G\fR\&. This
means that zclone will upload files smaller than 5GiB as single parts.
Note that this can be set in the config file with \fBupload_cutoff = 5G\fR
or configured in the advanced settings. If you regularly transfer
files larger than 5G then using \fB--checksum\fR or \fB--size-only\fR in
\fBzclone sync\fR is the recommended workaround.

.SS Comparison with the native protocol
Use the the native protocol
\[la]/storj\[ra] to take advantage of
client-side encryption as well as to achieve the best possible
download performance. Uploads will be erasure-coded locally, thus a
1gb upload will result in 2.68gb of data being uploaded to storage
nodes across the network.

.PP
Use this backend and the S3 compatible Hosted Gateway to increase
upload performance and reduce the load on your systems and network.
Uploads will be encrypted and erasure-coded server-side, thus a 1GB
upload will result in only in 1GB of data being uploaded to storage
nodes across the network.

.PP
For more detailed comparison please check the documentation of the
storj
\[la]/storj\[ra] backend.

