.SH Memory usage {#memory}
The most common cause of zclone using lots of memory is a single
directory with millions of files in. Despite s3 not really having the
concepts of directories, zclone does the sync on a directory by
directory basis to be compatible with normal filing systems.

.PP
Zclone loads each directory into memory as zclone objects. Each zclone
object takes 0.5k-1k of memory, so approximately 1GB per 1,000,000
files, and the sync for that directory does not begin until it is
entirely loaded in memory. So the sync can take a long time to start
for large directories.

.PP
To sync a directory with 100,000,000 files in you would need approximately
100 GB of memory. At some point the amount of memory becomes difficult
to provide so there is
a workaround for this
\[la]/\[ra]
which involves a bit of scripting.

.PP
At some point zclone will gain a sync mode which is effectively this
workaround but built in to zclone.

.SH Limitations
\fBzclone about\fR is not supported by the S3 backend. Backends without
this capability cannot determine free space for an zclone mount or
use policy \fBmfs\fR (most free space) as a member of an zclone union
remote.

.PP
See List of backends that do not support zclone about
\[la]///overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
and zclone about
\[la]///commands/zclone_about/\[ra]\&.


.SH Archive
The Archive backend allows read only access to the content of archive
files on cloud storage without downloading the complete archive. This
means you could mount a large archive file and use only the parts of
it your application requires, rather than having to extract it.

.PP
The archive files are recognised by their extension.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBArchive\fP	\fBExtension\fP
Zip	\fB\&.zip\fR
Squashfs	\fB\&.sqfs\fR
.TE

.PP
The supported archive file types are cloud friendly - a single file
can be found and downloaded without downloading the whole archive.

.PP
If you just want to create, list or extract archives and don't want to
mount them then you may find the \fBzclone archive\fR commands more
convenient.
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive create
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_create/\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive list
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_list/\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
zclone archive extract
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_extract/\[ra]

.PP
These commands supports a wider range of non cloud friendly archives
(but not squashfs) but can't be used for \fBzclone mount\fR or any other
zclone commands (eg \fBzclone check\fR).

.SH Configuration
This backend is best used without configuration.

.PP
Use it by putting the string \fB:archive:\fR in front of another remote,
say \fBremote:dir\fR to make \fB:archive:remote:dir\fR\&.

.PP
Any archives in \fBremote:dir\fR will become directories and any files may
be read out of them individually.

.PP
For example

.EX
$ zclone lsf s3:zclone/dir
100files.sqfs
100files.zip
.EE

.PP
Note that \fB100files.zip\fR and \fB100files.sqfs\fR are now directories:

.EX
$ zclone lsf :archive:s3:zclone/dir
100files.sqfs/
100files.zip/
.EE

.PP
Which we can look inside:

.EX
$ zclone lsf :archive:s3:zclone/dir/100files.zip/
cofofiy5jun
gigi
hevupaz5z
kacak/
kozemof/
lamapaq4
qejahen
quhenen2rey
soboves8
vibat/
wose
xade
zilupot
.EE

.PP
Files not in an archive can be read and written as normal. Files in an archive can only be read.

.PP
The archive backend can also be used in a configuration file. Use the \fBremote\fR variable to point to the destination of the archive.

.EX
[remote]
type = archive
remote = s3:zclone/dir/100files.zip
.EE

.PP
Gives

.EX
$ zclone lsf remote:
cofofiy5jun
gigi
hevupaz5z
kacak/
\&...
.EE

.SH Modification times
Modification times are preserved with an accuracy depending on the archive type.

.EX
$ zclone lsl --max-depth 1 :archive:s3:zclone/dir/100files.zip
       12 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 cofofiy5jun
       81 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 gigi
       58 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 hevupaz5z
        6 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 lamapaq4
       43 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 qejahen
       66 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 quhenen2rey
       95 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 soboves8
       71 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 wose
       76 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 xade
       15 2025-10-27 14:39:20.000000000 zilupot
.EE

.PP
For \fBzip\fR and \fBsquashfs\fR files this is 1s.

.SH Hashes
Which hash is supported depends on the archive type. Zip files use
CRC32, Squashfs don't support any hashes. For example:

.EX
$ zclone hashsum crc32 :archive:s3:zclone/dir/100files.zip/
b2288554  cofofiy5jun
a87e62b6  wose
f90f630b  xade
c7d0ef29  gigi
f1c64740  soboves8
cb7b4a5d  quhenen2rey
5115242b  kozemof/fonaxo
afeabd9a  qejahen
71202402  kozemof/fijubey5di
bd99e512  kozemof/napux
\&...
.EE

.PP
Hashes will be checked when the file is read from the archive and used
as part of syncing if possible.

.EX
$ zclone copy -vv :archive:s3:zclone/dir/100files.zip /tmp/100files
\&...
2025/10/27 14:56:44 DEBUG : kacak/turovat5c/yuyuquk: crc32 = abd05cc8 OK
2025/10/27 14:56:44 DEBUG : kacak/turovat5c/yuyuquk.aeb661dc.partial: renamed to: kacak/turovat5c/yuyuquk
2025/10/27 14:56:44 INFO  : kacak/turovat5c/yuyuquk: Copied (new)
\&...
.EE

.SH Zip
The Zip file format
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZIP_(file_format\[ra])
is a widely used archive format that bundles one or more files and
folders into a single file, primarily for easier storage or
transmission. It typically uses compression (most commonly the DEFLATE
algorithm) to reduce the overall size of the archived content. Zip
files are supported natively by most modern operating systems.

.PP
Zclone does not support the following advanced features of Zip files:
.IP \(bu 2
Splitting large archives into smaller parts
.IP \(bu 2
Password protection
.IP \(bu 2
Zstd compression

.SH Squashfs
Squashfs is a compressed, read-only file system format primarily used
in Linux-based systems. It's designed to compress entire file systems
(including files, directories, and metadata) into a single archive
file, which can then be mounted and read directly, appearing as a
normal directory structure. Because it's read-only and highly
compressed, Squashfs is ideal for live CDs/USBs, embedded devices with
limited storage, and software package distribution, as it saves space
and ensures the integrity of the original files.

.PP
Zclone supports the following squashfs compression formats:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGzip\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLzma\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBXz\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZstd\fR

.PP
These are not yet working:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLzo\fR - Not yet supported
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLz4\fR - Broken with "error decompressing: lz4: bad magic number"

.PP
Zclone works fastest with large squashfs block sizes. For example:

.EX
mksquashfs 100files 100files.sqfs -comp zstd -b 1M
.EE

.SH Limitations
Files in the archive backend are read only. It isn't possible to
create archives with the archive backend yet. However you \fBcan\fP create
archives with zclone archive create
\[la]//commands/zclone_archive_create/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Only \fB\&.zip\fR and \fB\&.sqfs\fR archives are supported as these are the only
common archiving formats which make it easy to read directory listings
from the archive without downloading the whole archive.

.PP
Internally the archive backend uses the VFS to access files. It isn't
possible to configure the internal VFS yet which might be useful.

.SH Archive Formats
Here's a table rating common archive formats on their Cloud
Optimization which is based on their ability to access a single file
without reading the entire archive.

.PP
This capability depends on whether the format has a central \fBindex\fP
(or "table of contents") that a program can read first to find the
exact location of a specific file.

.TS
allbox;
l l l l 
l l l l .
\fBFormat\fP	\fBExtensions\fP	\fBCloud Optimized\fP	\fBExplanation\fP
\fBZIP\fP	\fB\&.zip\fR	\fBExcellent\fP	\fBZip files have an index\fP (the "central directory") stored at the \fIend\fP of the file. A program can seek to the end, read the index to find a file's location and size, and then seek directly to that file's data to extract it.
\fBSquashFS\fP	\fB\&.squashfs\fR, \fB\&.sqfs\fR, \fB\&.sfs\fR	\fBExcellent\fP	T{
This is a compressed read-only \fIfilesystem image\fP, not just an archive. It is \fBspecifically designed for random access\fP\&. It uses metadata and index tables to allow the system to find and decompress individual files or data blocks on demand.
T}
\fBISO Image\fP	\fB\&.iso\fR	\fBExcellent\fP	Like SquashFS, this is a \fIfilesystem image\fP (for optical media). It contains a filesystem (like ISO 9660 or UDF) with a \fBtable of contents at a known location\fP, allowing for direct access to any file without reading the whole disk.
\fBRAR\fP	\fB\&.rar\fR	\fBGood\fP	T{
RAR supports "non-solid" and "solid" modes. In the common \fBnon-solid\fP mode, files are compressed separately, and an index allows for easy single-file extraction (like ZIP). In "solid" mode, this rating would be "Very Poor."
T}
\fB7z\fP	\fB\&.7z\fR	\fBPoor\fP	T{
By default, 7z uses "solid" archives to maximize compression. This compresses files as one continuous stream. To extract a file from the middle, all preceding files must be decompressed first. (If explicitly created as "non-solid," its rating would be "Excellent").
T}
\fBtar\fP	\fB\&.tar\fR	\fBPoor\fP	"Tape Archive" is a \fIstreaming\fP format with \fBno central index\fP\&. To find a file, you must read the archive from the beginning, checking each file header one by one until you find the one you want. This is slow but doesn't require decompressing data.
\fBGzipped Tar\fP	\fB\&.tar.gz\fR, \fB\&.tgz\fR	\fBVery Poor\fP	This is a \fBtar\fR file (already "Poor") compressed with \fBgzip\fR as a \fBsingle, non-seekable stream\fP\&. You cannot seek. To get \fIany\fP file, you must decompress the \fIentire\fP archive from the beginning up to that file.
\fBBzipped/XZ Tar\fP	\fB\&.tar.bz2\fR, \fB\&.tar.xz\fR	\fBVery Poor\fP	This is the same principle as \fBtar.gz\fR\&. The entire archive is one large compressed block, making random access impossible.
.TE

.SH Ideas for improvements
It would be possible to add ISO support fairly easily as the library we use (go-diskfs
\[la]https://github.com/diskfs/go\-diskfs/\[ra]) supports it. We could also add \fBext4\fR and \fBfat32\fR the same way, however in my experience these are not very common as files so probably not worth it. Go-diskfs can also read partitions which we could potentially take advantage of.

.PP
It would be possible to add write support, but this would only be for creating new archives, not for updating existing archives.

.PP
 

.SS Standard options
Here are the Standard options specific to archive (Read archives).

.SS --archive-remote
Remote to wrap to read archives from.

.PP
Normally should contain a ':' and a path, e.g. "myremote:path/to/dir",
"myremote:bucket" or "myremote:".

.PP
If this is left empty, then the archive backend will use the root as
the remote.

.PP
This means that you can use :archive:remote:path and it will be
equivalent to setting remote="remote:path".

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      remote
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_ARCHIVE_REMOTE
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS Advanced options
Here are the Advanced options specific to archive (Read archives).

.SS --archive-description
Description of the remote.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      description
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_ARCHIVE_DESCRIPTION
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS Metadata
Any metadata supported by the underlying remote is read and written.

.PP
See the metadata
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] docs for more info.


.SH Backblaze B2
B2 is Backblaze's cloud storage system
\[la]https://www.backblaze.com/cloud\-storage\[ra]\&.

.PP
Paths are specified as \fBremote:bucket\fR (or \fBremote:\fR for the \fBlsd\fR
command.)  You may put subdirectories in too, e.g. \fBremote:bucket/path/to/dir\fR\&.

.SH Configuration
Here is an example of making a b2 configuration.  First run

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process.  To authenticate
you will either need your Account ID (a short hex number) and Master
Application Key (a long hex number) OR an Application Key, which is the
recommended method. See below for further details on generating and using
an Application Key.

.EX
No remotes found, make a new one\\?
n) New remote
q) Quit config
n/q> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Backblaze B2
   \\ "b2"
[snip]
Storage> b2
Account ID or Application Key ID
account> 123456789abc
Application Key
key> 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789
Endpoint for the service - leave blank normally.
endpoint>
Remote config
Configuration complete.
Options:
- type: b2
- account: 123456789abc
- key: 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789
- endpoint:
Keep this "remote" remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE

.PP
This remote is called \fBremote\fR and can now be used like this

.PP
See all buckets

.EX
zclone lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
Create a new bucket

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:bucket
.EE

.PP
List the contents of a bucket

.EX
zclone ls remote:bucket
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/directory\fR to the remote bucket, deleting any
excess files in the bucket.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:bucket
.EE

.SS Application Keys
B2 supports multiple Application Keys for different access permission
to B2 Buckets
\[la]https://www.backblaze.com/docs/cloud\-storage\-application\-keys\[ra]\&.

.PP
You can use these with zclone too; you will need to use zclone version 1.43
or later.

.PP
Follow Backblaze's docs to create an Application Key with the required
permission and add the \fBapplicationKeyId\fR as the \fBaccount\fR and the
\fBApplication Key\fR itself as the \fBkey\fR\&.

.PP
Note that you must put the \fIapplicationKeyId\fP as the \fBaccount\fR – you
can't use the master Account ID.  If you try then B2 will return 401
errors.

.SS --fast-list
This remote supports \fB--fast-list\fR which allows you to use fewer
transactions in exchange for more memory. See the zclone
docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.SS Modification times
The modification time is stored as metadata on the object as
\fBX-Bz-Info-src_last_modified_millis\fR as milliseconds since 1970-01-01
in the Backblaze standard.  Other tools should be able to use this as
a modified time.

.PP
Modified times are used in syncing and are fully supported. Note that
if a modification time needs to be updated on an object then it will
create a new version of the object.

.SS Restricted filename characters
In addition to the default restricted characters set
\[la]//overview/#restricted\-characters\[ra]
the following characters are also replaced:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
	0x5C	＼
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.PP
Note that in 2020-05 Backblaze started allowing \\ characters in file
names. Zclone hasn't changed its encoding as this could cause syncs to
re-transfer files. If you want zclone not to replace \\ then see the
\fB--b2-encoding\fR flag below and remove the \fBBackSlash\fR from the
string. This can be set in the config.

.SS SHA1 checksums
The SHA1 checksums of the files are checked on upload and download and
will be used in the syncing process.

.PP
Large files (bigger than the limit in \fB--b2-upload-cutoff\fR) which are
uploaded in chunks will store their SHA1 on the object as
\fBX-Bz-Info-large_file_sha1\fR as recommended by Backblaze.

.PP
For a large file to be uploaded with an SHA1 checksum, the source
needs to support SHA1 checksums. The local disk supports SHA1
checksums so large file transfers from local disk will have an SHA1.
See the overview
\[la]//overview/#features\[ra] for exactly which remotes
support SHA1.

.PP
Sources which don't support SHA1, in particular \fBcrypt\fR will upload
large files without SHA1 checksums.  This may be fixed in the future
(see #1767
\[la]/\[ra]).

.PP
Files sizes below \fB--b2-upload-cutoff\fR will always have an SHA1
regardless of the source.

.SS Transfers
Backblaze recommends that you do lots of transfers simultaneously for
maximum speed.  In tests from my SSD equipped laptop the optimum
setting is about \fB--transfers 32\fR though higher numbers may be used
for a slight speed improvement. The optimum number for you may vary
depending on your hardware, how big the files are, how much you want
to load your computer, etc.  The default of \fB--transfers 4\fR is
definitely too low for Backblaze B2 though.

.PP
Note that uploading big files (bigger than 200 MiB by default) will use
a 96 MiB RAM buffer by default.  There can be at most \fB--transfers\fR of
these in use at any moment, so this sets the upper limit on the memory
used.

.SS Versions
The default setting of B2 is to keep old versions of files. This means
when zclone uploads a new version of a file it creates a new version
of it
\[la]https://www.backblaze.com/docs/cloud\-storage\-file\-versions\[ra]\&.
Likewise when you delete a file, the old version will be marked hidden
and still be available.

.PP
Whether B2 keeps old versions of files or not can be adjusted on a per
bucket basis using the "Lifecycle settings" on the B2 control panel or
when creating the bucket using the --b2-lifecycle
\[la]#b2\-lifecycle\[ra]
flag or after creation using the zclone backend lifecycle
\[la]#lifecycle\[ra]
command.

.PP
You may opt in to a "hard delete" of files with the \fB--b2-hard-delete\fR
flag which permanently removes files on deletion instead of hiding
them.

.PP
Old versions of files, where available, are visible using the
\fB--b2-versions\fR flag. These can be deleted as required with \fBdelete\fR\&.

.PP
It is also possible to view a bucket as it was at a certain point in time,
using the \fB--b2-version-at\fR flag. This will show the file versions as they
were at that time, showing files that have been deleted afterwards, and
hiding files that were created since.

.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions, and unfinished large file
uploads, then you can use the \fBzclone cleanup remote:bucket\fR command
which will delete all the old versions of files, leaving the current ones
intact.  You can also supply a path and only old versions under that path
will be deleted, e.g. \fBzclone cleanup remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\fR\&.

.PP
Note that \fBcleanup\fR will remove partially uploaded files from the bucket
if they are more than a day old. If you want more control over the
expiry date then run \fBzclone backend cleanup b2:bucket -o max-age=1h\fR
to remove all unfinished large file uploads older than one hour, leaving
old versions intact.

.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions, leaving current files and
unfinished large files intact, then you can use the

\[la]#cleanup\-hidden\[ra]
command. You can also supply a path and only old versions under that
path will be deleted, e.g.
\fBzclone backend cleanup-hidden remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\fR\&.

.PP
When you \fBpurge\fR a bucket, the current and the old versions will be
deleted then the bucket will be deleted.

.PP
However \fBdelete\fR will cause the current versions of the files to
become hidden old versions.

.PP
Here is a session showing the listing and retrieval of an old
version followed by a \fBcleanup\fR of the old versions.

.PP
Show current version and all the versions with \fB--b2-versions\fR flag.

.EX
$ zclone -q ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt

$ zclone -q --b2-versions ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one-v2016-07-04-141032-000.txt
       16 one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
       15 one-v2016-07-02-155621-000.txt
.EE

.PP
Retrieve an old version

.EX
$ zclone -q --b2-versions copy b2:cleanup-test/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt /tmp

$ ls -l /tmp/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
-rw-rw-r-- 1 ncw ncw 16 Jul  2 17:46 /tmp/one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
.EE

.PP
Clean up all the old versions and show that they've gone.

.EX
$ zclone -q cleanup b2:cleanup-test

$ zclone -q ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt

$ zclone -q --b2-versions ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
.EE

.SS Versions naming caveat
When using \fB--b2-versions\fR flag zclone is relying on the file name
to work out whether the objects are versions or not. Versions' names
are created by inserting timestamp between file name and its extension.

.EX
        9 file.txt
        8 file-v2023-07-17-161032-000.txt
       16 file-v2023-06-15-141003-000.txt
.EE

.PP
If there are real files present with the same names as versions, then
behaviour of \fB--b2-versions\fR can be unpredictable.

.SS Data usage
It is useful to know how many requests are sent to the server in different scenarios.

.PP
All copy commands send the following 4 requests:

.EX
/b2api/v4/b2_authorize_account
/b2api/v1/b2_create_bucket
/b2api/v1/b2_list_buckets
/b2api/v1/b2_list_file_names
.EE

.PP
The \fBb2_list_file_names\fR request will be sent once for every 1k files
in the remote path, providing the checksum and modification time of
the listed files. As of version 1.33 issue
#818
\[la]/\[ra] causes extra requests
to be sent when using B2 with Crypt. When a copy operation does not
require any files to be uploaded, no more requests will be sent.

.PP
Uploading files that do not require chunking, will send 2 requests per
file upload:

.EX
/b2api/v1/b2_get_upload_url
/b2api/v1/b2_upload_file/
.EE

.PP
Uploading files requiring chunking, will send 2 requests (one each to
start and finish the upload) and another 2 requests for each chunk:

.EX
/b2api/v1/b2_start_large_file
/b2api/v1/b2_get_upload_part_url
/b2api/v1/b2_upload_part/
/b2api/v1/b2_finish_large_file
.EE

.SS Viewing versions with --b2-versions
Versions can be viewed with the \fB--b2-versions\fR flag. When it is set
zclone will show and act on older versions of files.  For example

.PP
Listing without \fB--b2-versions\fR

.EX
$ zclone -q ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
.EE

.PP
And with

.EX
$ zclone -q --b2-versions ls b2:cleanup-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one-v2016-07-04-141032-000.txt
       16 one-v2016-07-04-141003-000.txt
       15 one-v2016-07-02-155621-000.txt
.EE

.PP
Showing that the current version is unchanged but older versions can
be seen.  These have the UTC date that they were uploaded to the
server to the nearest millisecond appended to them.

.PP
Note that when using \fB--b2-versions\fR no file write operations are
permitted, so you can't upload files or delete them.

.SS B2 and zclone link
Zclone supports generating file share links for private B2 buckets.
They can either be for a file for example:

.EX
\&./zclone link B2:bucket/path/to/file.txt
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/to/file.txt?Authorization=xxxxxxxx

.EE

.PP
or if run on a directory you will get:

.EX
\&./zclone link B2:bucket/path
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
.EE

.PP
you can then use the authorization token (the part of the url from the
 \fB?Authorization=\fR on) on any file path under that directory. For example:

.EX
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/to/file1?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/file2?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/folder/file3?Authorization=xxxxxxxx

.EE

.PP
 

