Config:      description
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_DRIME_DESCRIPTION
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SH Limitations
Drime only supports filenames up to 255 bytes in length, where filenames are
encoded in UTF8.


.SH Dropbox
Paths are specified as \fBremote:path\fR

.PP
Dropbox paths may be as deep as required, e.g.
\fBremote:directory/subdirectory\fR\&.

.SH Configuration
The initial setup for dropbox involves getting a token from Dropbox
which you need to do in your browser.  \fBzclone config\fR walks you
through it.

.PP
Here is an example of how to make a remote called \fBremote\fR\&.  First run:

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process:

.EX
n) New remote
d) Delete remote
q) Quit config
e/n/d/q> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Dropbox
   \\ "dropbox"
[snip]
Storage> dropbox
Dropbox App Key - leave blank normally.
app_key>
Dropbox App Secret - leave blank normally.
app_secret>
Remote config
Please visit:
https://www.dropbox.com/1/oauth2/authorize?client_id=XXXXXXXXXXXXXXX&response_type=code
Enter the code: XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX_XXXXXXXXXX
Configuration complete.
Options:
- type: dropbox
- app_key:
- app_secret:
- token: XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX_XXXX_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
Keep this "remote" remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE

.PP
See the remote setup docs
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra] for how to set it up on a
machine without an internet-connected web browser available.

.PP
Note that zclone runs a webserver on your local machine to collect the
token as returned from Dropbox. This only
runs from the moment it opens your browser to the moment you get back
the verification code.  This is on \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:53682/\fR and it
may require you to unblock it temporarily if you are running a host
firewall, or use manual mode.

.PP
You can then use it like this,

.PP
List directories in top level of your dropbox

.EX
zclone lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
List all the files in your dropbox

.EX
zclone ls remote:
.EE

.PP
To copy a local directory to a dropbox directory called backup

.EX
zclone copy /home/source remote:backup
.EE

.SS Dropbox for business
Zclone supports Dropbox for business and Team Folders.

.PP
When using Dropbox for business \fBremote:\fR and \fBremote:path/to/file\fR
will refer to your personal folder.

.PP
If you wish to see Team Folders you must use a leading \fB/\fR in the
path, so \fBzclone lsd remote:/\fR will refer to the root and show you all
Team Folders and your User Folder.

.PP
You can then use team folders like this \fBremote:/TeamFolder\fR and
\fBremote:/TeamFolder/path/to/file\fR\&.

.PP
A leading \fB/\fR for a Dropbox personal account will do nothing, but it
will take an extra HTTP transaction so it should be avoided.

.SS Modification times and hashes
Dropbox supports modified times, but the only way to set a
modification time is to re-upload the file.

.PP
This means that if you uploaded your data with an older version of
zclone which didn't support the v2 API and modified times, zclone will
decide to upload all your old data to fix the modification times.  If
you don't want this to happen use \fB--size-only\fR or \fB--checksum\fR flag
to stop it.

.PP
Dropbox supports its own hash
type
\[la]https://www.dropbox.com/developers/reference/content\-hash\[ra] which
is checked for all transfers.

.SS Restricted filename characters
.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
/	0x2F	／
DEL	0x7F	␡
	0x5C	＼
.TE

.PP
File names can also not end with the following characters.
These only get replaced if they are the last character in the name:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
SP	0x20	␠
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.SS Batch mode uploads {#batch-mode}
Using batch mode uploads is very important for performance when using
the Dropbox API. See the dropbox performance guide
\[la]https://developers.dropbox.com/dbx\-performance\-guide\[ra]
for more info.

.PP
There are 3 modes zclone can use for uploads.

.SS --dropbox-batch-mode off
In this mode zclone will not use upload batching. This was the default
before zclone v1.55. It has the disadvantage that it is very likely to
encounter \fBtoo_many_requests\fR errors like this

.EX
NOTICE: too_many_requests/.: Too many requests or write operations. Trying again in 15 seconds.
.EE

.PP
When zclone receives these it has to wait for 15s or sometimes 300s
before continuing which really slows down transfers.

.PP
This will happen especially if \fB--transfers\fR is large, so this mode
isn't recommended except for compatibility or investigating problems.

.SS --dropbox-batch-mode sync
In this mode zclone will batch up uploads to the size specified by
\fB--dropbox-batch-size\fR and commit them together.

.PP
Using this mode means you can use a much higher \fB--transfers\fR
parameter (32 or 64 works fine) without receiving \fBtoo_many_requests\fR
errors.

.PP
This mode ensures full data integrity.

.PP
Note that there may be a pause when quitting zclone while zclone
finishes up the last batch using this mode.

.SS --dropbox-batch-mode async
In this mode zclone will batch up uploads to the size specified by
\fB--dropbox-batch-size\fR and commit them together.

.PP
However it will not wait for the status of the batch to be returned to
the caller. This means zclone can use a much bigger batch size (much
bigger than \fB--transfers\fR), at the cost of not being able to check the
status of the upload.

.PP
This provides the maximum possible upload speed especially with lots
of small files, however zclone can't check the file got uploaded
properly using this mode.

.PP
If you are using this mode then using "zclone check" after the
transfer completes is recommended. Or you could do an initial transfer
with \fB--dropbox-batch-mode async\fR then do a final transfer with
\fB--dropbox-batch-mode sync\fR (the default).

.PP
Note that there may be a pause when quitting zclone while zclone
finishes up the last batch using this mode.

.SS Exporting files
Certain files in Dropbox are "exportable", such as Dropbox Paper
documents. These files need to be converted to another format in
order to be downloaded. Often multiple formats are available for
conversion.

.PP
When zclone downloads a exportable file, it chooses the format to
download based on the \fB--dropbox-export-formats\fR setting. By
default, the export formats are \fBhtml,md\fR, which are sensible
defaults for Dropbox Paper.

.PP
Zclone chooses the first format ID in the export formats list that
Dropbox supports for a given file. If no format in the list is
usable, zclone will choose the default format that Dropbox suggests.

.PP
Zclone will change the extension to correspond to the export format.
Here are some examples of how extensions are mapped:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBFile type\fP	\fBFilename in Dropbox\fP	\fBFilename in zclone\fP
Paper	mydoc.paper	mydoc.html
Paper template	mydoc.papert	mydoc.papert.html
other	mydoc	mydoc.html
.TE

.PP
\fIImporting\fP exportable files is not yet supported by zclone.

.PP
Here are the supported export extensions known by zclone. Note that
zclone does not currently support other formats not on this list,
even if Dropbox supports them. Also, Dropbox could change the list
of supported formats at any time.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBFormat ID\fP	\fBName\fP	\fBDescription\fP
html	HTML	HTML document
md	Markdown	Markdown text format
.TE

.PP
 

