.SH FTP
FTP is the File Transfer Protocol. Zclone FTP support is provided using the
github.com/jlaffaye/ftp
\[la]https://godoc.org/github.com/jlaffaye/ftp\[ra]
package.

.PP
Limitations of Zclone's FTP backend
\[la]#limitations\[ra]

.PP
Paths are specified as \fBremote:path\fR\&. If the path does not begin with
a \fB/\fR it is relative to the home directory of the user.  An empty path
\fBremote:\fR refers to the user's home directory.

.SH Configuration
To create an FTP configuration named \fBremote\fR, run

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
Zclone config guides you through an interactive setup process. A minimal
zclone FTP remote definition only requires host, username and password.
For an anonymous FTP server, see below
\[la]#anonymous\-ftp\[ra]\&.

.EX
No remotes found, make a new one\\?
n) New remote
r) Rename remote
c) Copy remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
n/r/c/s/q> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Enter a string value. Press Enter for the default ("").
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / FTP
   \\ "ftp"
[snip]
Storage> ftp
** See help for ftp backend at: //ftp/ **

FTP host to connect to
Enter a string value. Press Enter for the default ("").
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
 1 / Connect to ftp.example.com
   \\ "ftp.example.com"
host> ftp.example.com
FTP username
Enter a string value. Press Enter for the default ("$USER").
user> 
FTP port number
Enter a signed integer. Press Enter for the default (21).
port> 
FTP password
y) Yes type in my own password
g) Generate random password
y/g> y
Enter the password:
password:
Confirm the password:
password:
Use FTP over TLS (Implicit)
Enter a boolean value (true or false). Press Enter for the default ("false").
tls> 
Use FTP over TLS (Explicit)
Enter a boolean value (true or false). Press Enter for the default ("false").
explicit_tls> 
Remote config
Configuration complete.
Options:
- type: ftp
- host: ftp.example.com
- pass: *** ENCRYPTED ***
Keep this "remote" remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE

.PP
To see all directories in the home directory of \fBremote\fR

.EX
zclone lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
Make a new directory

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:path/to/directory
.EE

.PP
List the contents of a directory

.EX
zclone ls remote:path/to/directory
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/directory\fR to the remote directory, deleting any
excess files in the directory.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:directory
.EE

.SS Anonymous FTP
When connecting to a FTP server that allows anonymous login, you can use the
special "anonymous" username. Traditionally, this user account accepts any
string as a password, although it is common to use either the password
"anonymous" or "guest". Some servers require the use of a valid e-mail
address as password.

.PP
Using on-the-fly
\[la]#backend\-path\-to\-dir\[ra] or
connection string
\[la]//docs/#connection\-strings\[ra] remotes makes it easy to access
such servers, without requiring any configuration in advance. The following
are examples of that:

.EX
zclone lsf :ftp: --ftp-host=speedtest.tele2.net --ftp-user=anonymous --ftp-pass=$(zclone obscure dummy)
zclone lsf :ftp,host=speedtest.tele2.net,user=anonymous,pass=$(zclone obscure dummy):
.EE

.PP
The above examples work in Linux shells and in PowerShell, but not Windows
Command Prompt. They execute the zclone obscure
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]
command to create a password string in the format required by the
pass
\[la]#ftp\-pass\[ra] option. The following examples are exactly the same, except use
an already obscured string representation of the same password "dummy", and
therefore works even in Windows Command Prompt:

.EX
zclone lsf :ftp: --ftp-host=speedtest.tele2.net --ftp-user=anonymous --ftp-pass=IXs2wc8OJOz7SYLBk47Ji1rHTmxM
zclone lsf :ftp,host=speedtest.tele2.net,user=anonymous,pass=IXs2wc8OJOz7SYLBk47Ji1rHTmxM:
.EE

.SS Implicit TLS
Rlone FTP supports implicit FTP over TLS servers (FTPS). This has to
be enabled in the FTP backend config for the remote, or with

\[la]#ftp\-tls\[ra]\&. The default FTPS port is \fB990\fR, not \fB21\fR and
can be set with 
\[la]#ftp\-port\[ra]\&.

.SH TLS Options
TLS options for Implicit and Explicit TLS can be set using the
following flags which are specific to the FTP backend:

.EX
--ftp-no-check-certificate     Do not verify the TLS certificate of the server
--ftp-disable-tls13            Disable TLS 1.3 (workaround for FTP servers with buggy TLS)
--ftp-tls-cache-size int       Size of TLS session cache for all control and data connections (default 32)
.EE

.PP
However any of the global TLS flags can also be used such as:

.EX
--ca-cert stringArray          CA certificate used to verify servers
--client-cert string           Client SSL certificate (PEM) for mutual TLS auth
--client-key string            Client SSL private key (PEM) for mutual TLS auth
--no-check-certificate         Do not verify the server SSL certificate (insecure)
.EE

.PP
If these need to be put in the config file so they apply to just the
FTP backend then use the \fBoverride\fR syntax, eg

.EX
override.ca_cert = XXX
override.client_cert = XXX
override.client_key = XXX
.EE

.SS Restricted filename characters
In addition to the default restricted characters set
\[la]//overview/#restricted\-characters\[ra]
the following characters are also replaced:

.PP
File names cannot end with the following characters. Replacement is
limited to the last character in a file name:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
SP	0x20	␠
.TE

.PP
Not all FTP servers can have all characters in file names, for example:

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBFTP Server\fP	\fBForbidden characters\fP
proftpd	\fB*\fR
pureftpd	\fB\\ [ ]\fR
.TE

.PP
This backend's interactive configuration wizard provides a selection of
sensible encoding settings for major FTP servers: ProFTPd, PureFTPd, VsFTPd.
Just hit a selection number when prompted.

.PP
 

