.PP
See all the buckets in your project

.EX
zclone lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
Make a new bucket

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:bucket
.EE

.PP
List the contents of a bucket

.EX
zclone ls remote:bucket
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/directory\fR to the remote bucket, deleting any excess
files in the bucket.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:bucket
.EE

.SS Service Account support
You can set up zclone with Google Cloud Storage in an unattended mode,
i.e. not tied to a specific end-user Google account. This is useful
when you want to synchronise files onto machines that don't have
actively logged-in users, for example build machines.

.PP
To get credentials for Google Cloud Platform
IAM Service Accounts
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/service\-accounts\[ra],
please head to the
Service Account
\[la]https://console.cloud.google.com/permissions/serviceaccounts\[ra]
section of the Google Developer Console. Service Accounts behave just
like normal \fBUser\fR permissions in
Google Cloud Storage ACLs
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access\-control\[ra],
so you can limit their access (e.g. make them read only). After
creating an account, a JSON file containing the Service Account's
credentials will be downloaded onto your machines. These credentials
are what zclone will use for authentication.

.PP
To use a Service Account instead of OAuth2 token flow, enter the path
to your Service Account credentials at the \fBservice_account_file\fR
prompt and zclone won't use the browser based authentication
flow. If you'd rather stuff the contents of the credentials file into
the zclone config file, you can set \fBservice_account_credentials\fR with
the actual contents of the file instead, or set the equivalent
environment variable.

.SS Service Account Authentication with Access Tokens
Another option for service account authentication is to use access tokens via
\fIgcloud impersonate-service-account\fP\&. Access tokens protect security by avoiding
the use of the JSON key file, which can be breached. They also bypass oauth
login flow, which is simpler on remote VMs that lack a web browser.

.PP
If you already have a working service account, skip to step 3.

.SS 1. Create a service account using
.EX
gcloud iam service-accounts create gcs-read-only
.EE

.PP
You can re-use an existing service account as well (like the one created above)

.SS 2. Attach a Viewer (read-only) or User (read-write) role to the service account
.EX
$ PROJECT_ID=my-project
$ gcloud --verbose iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding \\
   gcs-read-only@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com  \\
   --member=serviceAccount:gcs-read-only@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com \\
   --role=roles/storage.objectViewer
.EE

.PP
Use the Google Cloud console to identify a limited role. Some relevant
pre-defined roles:
.IP \(bu 2
\fIroles/storage.objectUser\fP -- read-write access but no admin privileges
.IP \(bu 2
\fIroles/storage.objectViewer\fP -- read-only access to objects
.IP \(bu 2
\fIroles/storage.admin\fP  -- create buckets & administrative roles

.SS 3. Get a temporary access key for the service account
.EX
$ gcloud auth application-default print-access-token  \\
   --impersonate-service-account \\
      gcs-read-only@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com  

ya29.c.c0ASRK0GbAFEewXD [truncated]
.EE

.SS 4. Update \fBaccess_token\fR setting
hit \fBCTRL-C\fR when you see \fIwaiting for code\fP\&.  This will save the config without
doing oauth flow

.EX
zclone config update ${REMOTE_NAME} access_token ya29.c.c0Axxxx
.EE

.SS 5. Run zclone as usual
.EX
zclone ls dev-gcs:${MY_BUCKET}/
.EE

.SS More Info on Service Accounts
.IP \(bu 2
Official GCS Docs
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/access/service\-accounts\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
Guide on Service Accounts using Key Files (less secure, but similar concepts)
\[la]/\[ra]

.SS Anonymous Access
For downloads of objects that permit public access you can configure zclone
to use anonymous access by setting \fBanonymous\fR to \fBtrue\fR\&.
With unauthorized access you can't write or create files but only read or list
those buckets and objects that have public read access.

.SS Application Default Credentials
If no other source of credentials is provided, zclone will fall back
to
Application Default Credentials
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/video\-intelligence/docs/common/auth#authenticating_with_application_default_credentials\[ra]
this is useful both when you already have configured authentication
for your developer account, or in production when running on a google
compute host. Note that if running in docker, you may need to run
additional commands on your google compute machine -
see this page
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/container\-registry/docs/advanced\-authentication#gcloud_as_a_docker_credential_helper\[ra]\&.

.PP
Note that in the case application default credentials are used, there
is no need to explicitly configure a project number.

.SS --fast-list
This remote supports \fB--fast-list\fR which allows you to use fewer
transactions in exchange for more memory. See the zclone
docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.SS Custom upload headers
You can set custom upload headers with the \fB--header-upload\fR
flag. Google Cloud Storage supports the headers as described in the
working with metadata documentation
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil/addlhelp/WorkingWithObjectMetadata\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
Cache-Control
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Disposition
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Encoding
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Language
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Type
.IP \(bu 2
X-Goog-Storage-Class
.IP \(bu 2
X-Goog-Meta-

.PP
Eg \fB--header-upload "Content-Type text/potato"\fR

.PP
Note that the last of these is for setting custom metadata in the form
\fB--header-upload "x-goog-meta-key: value"\fR

.SS Modification times
Google Cloud Storage stores md5sum natively.
Google's gsutil
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil\[ra] tool stores
modification time with one-second precision as \fBgoog-reserved-file-mtime\fR in
file metadata.

.PP
To ensure compatibility with gsutil, zclone stores modification time in 2
separate metadata entries. \fBmtime\fR uses RFC3339 format with one-nanosecond
precision. \fBgoog-reserved-file-mtime\fR uses Unix timestamp format with one-second
precision. To get modification time from object metadata, zclone reads the
metadata in the following order: \fBmtime\fR, \fBgoog-reserved-file-mtime\fR, object
updated time.

.PP
Note that zclone's default modify window is 1ns.
Files uploaded by gsutil only contain timestamps with one-second precision.
If you use zclone to sync files previously uploaded by gsutil,
zclone will attempt to update modification time for all these files.
To avoid these possibly unnecessary updates, use \fB--modify-window 1s\fR\&.

.SS Restricted filename characters
.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
LF	0x0A	␊
CR	0x0D	␍
/	0x2F	／
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.PP
 

