.PP
See the remote setup docs
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra] for how to set it up on a
machine without an internet-connected web browser available.

.PP
Note that zclone runs a webserver on your local machine to collect the
token as returned from Google if using web browser to automatically
authenticate. This only
runs from the moment it opens your browser to the moment you get back
the verification code.  This is on \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:53682/\fR and it
may require you to unblock it temporarily if you are running a host
firewall, or use manual mode.

.PP
You can then use it like this,

.PP
List directories in top level of your drive

.EX
zclone lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
List all the files in your drive

.EX
zclone ls remote:
.EE

.PP
To copy a local directory to a drive directory called backup

.EX
zclone copy /home/source remote:backup
.EE

.SS Scopes
Zclone allows you to select which scope you would like for zclone to
use.  This changes what type of token is granted to zclone.  The
scopes are defined
here
\[la]https://developers.google.com/drive/v3/web/about\-auth\[ra]\&.

.PP
A comma-separated list is allowed e.g. \fBdrive.readonly,drive.file\fR\&.

.PP
The scope are

.SS drive
This is the default scope and allows full access to all files, except
for the Application Data Folder (see below).

.PP
Choose this one if you aren't sure.

.SS drive.readonly
This allows read only access to all files.  Files may be listed and
downloaded but not uploaded, renamed or deleted.

.SS drive.file
With this scope zclone can read/view/modify only those files and
folders it creates.

.PP
So if you uploaded files to drive via the web interface (or any other
means) they will not be visible to zclone.

.PP
This can be useful if you are using zclone to backup data and you want
to be sure confidential data on your drive is not visible to zclone.

.PP
Files created with this scope are visible in the web interface.

.SS drive.appfolder
This gives zclone its own private area to store files.  Zclone will
not be able to see any other files on your drive and you won't be able
to see zclone's files from the web interface either.

.SS drive.metadata.readonly
This allows read only access to file names only.  It does not allow
zclone to download or upload data, or rename or delete files or
directories.

.SS Root folder ID
This option has been moved to the advanced section. You can set the
\fBroot_folder_id\fR for zclone.  This is the directory (identified by its
\fBFolder ID\fR) that zclone considers to be the root of your drive.

.PP
Normally you will leave this blank and zclone will determine the
correct root to use itself.

.PP
However you can set this to restrict zclone to a specific folder
hierarchy or to access data within the "Computers" tab on the drive
web interface (where files from Google's Backup and Sync desktop
program go). However, this is not the easiest method.
Instead, you can create a shortcut to a folder in "Computers" and
place it in "My Drive." To do so, right-click the folder, select
"Organize", and then choose "Add shortcut" in the Google Drive web interface.
Once you add the shortcut to "My Drive," zclone will display the folder, allowing you to interact with it.
See also Shortcuts
\[la]#shortcuts\[ra]\&.

.PP
If, however, you choose to change your root folder, you will have to find the \fBFolder ID\fR of the
directory you wish zclone to display.  This will be the last segment
of the URL when you open the relevant folder in the drive web
interface.

.PP
So if the folder you want zclone to use has a URL which looks like
\fBhttps://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1XyfxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxKHCh\fR
in the browser, then you use \fB1XyfxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxKHCh\fR as
the \fBroot_folder_id\fR in the config.

.PP
\fBNB\fP folders under the "Computers" tab seem to be read only (drive
gives a 500 error) when using zclone, but they are also read-only in the Google Drive web interface,
likely because this folder is managed by the Google Drive Desktop tool.
Top-level folders in "Computers" are read-only, but you can interact
with folders contained within them using zclone.
These are the folders for which you can create a shortcut as well, as described above.

.PP
There doesn't appear to be an API to discover the folder IDs of the
"Computers" tab - please contact us if you know otherwise!

.PP
Note also that zclone can't access any data under the "Backups" tab on
the google drive web interface yet.

.SS Service Account support
You can set up zclone with Google Drive in an unattended mode,
i.e. not tied to a specific end-user Google account. This is useful
when you want to synchronise files onto machines that don't have
actively logged-in users, for example build machines.

.PP
To use a Service Account instead of OAuth2 token flow, enter the path
to your Service Account credentials at the \fBservice_account_file\fR
prompt during \fBzclone config\fR and zclone won't use the browser based
authentication flow. If you'd rather stuff the contents of the
credentials file into the zclone config file, you can set
\fBservice_account_credentials\fR with the actual contents of the file
instead, or set the equivalent environment variable.

.SS Use case - Google Workspace account and individual Drive
Let's say that you are the administrator of a Google Workspace. The
goal is to read or write data on an individual's Drive account, who IS
a member of the domain. We'll call the domain , and the
user foo@example.com
\[la]mailto:foo@example.com\[ra]\&.

.PP
There's a few steps we need to go through to accomplish this:

.SS 1. Create a service account for example.com
.IP \(bu 2
To create a service account and obtain its credentials, go to the
Google Developer Console
\[la]https://console.developers.google.com\[ra]\&.
.IP \(bu 2
You must have a project - create one if you don't and make sure you are
on the selected project.
.IP \(bu 2
Then go to "IAM & admin" -> "Service Accounts".
.IP \(bu 2
Use the "Create Service Account" button. Fill in "Service account name"
and "Service account ID" with something that identifies your client.
.IP \(bu 2
Select "Create And Continue". Step 2 and 3 are optional.
.IP \(bu 2
Click on the newly created service account
.IP \(bu 2
Click "Keys" and then "Add Key" and then "Create new key"
.IP \(bu 2
Choose type "JSON" and click create
.IP \(bu 2
This will download a small JSON file that zclone will use for authentication.

.PP
If you ever need to remove access, press the "Delete service
account key" button.

.SS 2. Allowing API access to example.com Google Drive
.IP \(bu 2
Go to example.com's Workspace Admin Console
\[la]https://admin.google.com\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
Go into "Security" (or use the search bar)
.IP \(bu 2
Select "Access and data control" and then "API controls"
.IP \(bu 2
Click "Manage domain-wide delegation"
.IP \(bu 2
Click "Add new"
.IP \(bu 2
In the "Client ID" field enter the service account's
"Client ID" - this can be found in the Developer Console under
"IAM & Admin" -> "Service Accounts", then "View Client ID" for
the newly created service account.
It is a ~21 character numerical string.
.IP \(bu 2
In the next field, "OAuth Scopes", enter
\fBhttps://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive\fR
to grant read/write access to Google Drive specifically.
You can also use \fBhttps://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.readonly\fR for read
only access with \fB--drive-scope=drive.readonly\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Click "Authorise"

.SS 3. Configure zclone, assuming a new install
.EX
zclone config

n/s/q> n         # New
name>gdrive      # Gdrive is an example name
Storage>         # Type drive
client_id>       # Can be left blank
client_secret>   # Can be left blank
scope>           # Select the scope use used in step 2
root_folder_id>  # Can be left blank
service_account_file> /home/foo/myJSONfile.json # Path to the JSON file you downloaded in step 1.
y/n>             # Auto config, n

.EE

.SS 4. Verify that it's working
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone -v --drive-impersonate foo@example.com lsf gdrive:backup\fR
.IP \(bu 2
The arguments do:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB-v\fR - verbose logging
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--drive-impersonate foo@example.com\fR - this is what does
the magic, pretending to be user foo.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlsf\fR - list files in a parsing friendly way
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgdrive:backup\fR - use the remote called gdrive, work in
the folder named backup.
.RE

.PP
Note: in case you configured a specific root folder on gdrive and zclone is
unable to access the contents of that folder when using \fB--drive-impersonate\fR,
do this instead:
.IP \(bu 2
in the gdrive web interface, share your root folder with the user/email of the
new Service Account you created/selected at step 1
.IP \(bu 2
use zclone without specifying the \fB--drive-impersonate\fR option, like this:
\fBzclone -v lsf gdrive:backup\fR

.SS Shared drives (team drives)
If you want to configure the remote to point to a Google Shared Drive
(previously known as Team Drives) then answer \fBy\fR to the question
\fBConfigure this as a Shared Drive (Team Drive)?\fR\&.

.PP
This will fetch the list of Shared Drives from google and allow you to
configure which one you want to use. You can also type in a Shared
Drive ID if you prefer.

.PP
For example:

.EX
Configure this as a Shared Drive (Team Drive)?
y) Yes
n) No
y/n> y
Fetching Shared Drive list...
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
 1 / Zclone Test
   \\ "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
 2 / Zclone Test 2
   \\ "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"
 3 / Zclone Test 3
   \\ "zzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz"
Enter a Shared Drive ID> 1
Configuration complete.
Options:
- type: drive
- client_id:
- client_secret:
- token: {"AccessToken":"xxxx.x.xxxxx_xxxxxxxxxxx_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx","RefreshToken":"1/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx","Expiry":"2014-03-16T13:57:58.955387075Z","Extra":null}
- team_drive: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
Keep this "remote" remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE

.SS --fast-list
This remote supports \fB--fast-list\fR which allows you to use fewer
transactions in exchange for more memory. See the zclone
docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.PP
It does this by combining multiple \fBlist\fR calls into a single API request.

.PP
This works by combining many \fB\&'%s' in parents\fR filters into one expression.
To list the contents of directories a, b and c, the following requests will be
send by the regular \fBList\fR function:

.EX
trashed=false and 'a' in parents
trashed=false and 'b' in parents
trashed=false and 'c' in parents
.EE

.PP
These can now be combined into a single request:

.EX
trashed=false and ('a' in parents or 'b' in parents or 'c' in parents)
.EE

.PP
The implementation of \fBListR\fR will put up to 50 \fBparents\fR filters into one request.
It will  use the \fB--checkers\fR value to specify the number of requests to run in parallel.

.PP
In tests, these batch requests were up to 20x faster than the regular method.
Running the following command against different sized folders gives:

.EX
zclone lsjson -vv -R --checkers=6 gdrive:folder
.EE

.PP
small folder (220 directories, 700 files):
.IP \(bu 2
without \fB--fast-list\fR: 38s
.IP \(bu 2
with \fB--fast-list\fR: 10s

.PP
large folder (10600 directories, 39000 files):
.IP \(bu 2
without \fB--fast-list\fR: 22:05 min
.IP \(bu 2
with \fB--fast-list\fR: 58s

.SS Modification times and hashes
Google drive stores modification times accurate to 1 ms.

.PP
Hash algorithms MD5, SHA1 and SHA256 are supported. Note, however,
that a small fraction of files uploaded may not have SHA1 or SHA256
hashes especially if they were uploaded before 2018.

.SS Restricted filename characters
Only Invalid UTF-8 bytes will be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.PP
In contrast to other backends, \fB/\fR can also be used in names and \fB\&.\fR
or \fB\&..\fR are valid names.

.SS Revisions
Google drive stores revisions of files.  When you upload a change to
an existing file to google drive using zclone it will create a new
revision of that file.

.PP
Revisions follow the standard google policy which at time of writing
was
.IP \(bu 2
They are deleted after 30 days or 100 revisions (whatever comes first).
.IP \(bu 2
They do not count towards a user storage quota.

.SS Deleting files
By default zclone will send all files to the trash when deleting
files.  If deleting them permanently is required then use the
\fB--drive-use-trash=false\fR flag, or set the equivalent environment
variable.

.SS Shortcuts
In March 2020 Google introduced a new feature in Google Drive called
drive shortcuts
\[la]https://support.google.com/drive/answer/9700156\[ra]
(API
\[la]https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/shortcuts\[ra]). These
will (by September 2020) replace the ability for files or folders to
be in multiple folders at once
\[la]https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/g\-suite/simplifying\-google\-drives\-folder\-structure\-and\-sharing\-models\[ra]\&.

.PP
Shortcuts are files that link to other files on Google Drive somewhat
like a symlink in unix, except they point to the underlying file data
(e.g. the inode in unix terms) so they don't break if the source is
renamed or moved about.

.PP
By default zclone treats these as follows.

.PP
For shortcuts pointing to files:
.IP \(bu 2
When listing a file shortcut appears as the destination file.
.IP \(bu 2
When downloading the contents of the destination file is downloaded.
.IP \(bu 2
When updating shortcut file with a non shortcut file, the shortcut is removed
then a new file is uploaded in place of the shortcut.
.IP \(bu 2
When server-side moving (renaming) the shortcut is renamed, not the destination
file.
.IP \(bu 2
When server-side copying the shortcut is copied, not the contents of the shortcut.
(unless \fB--drive-copy-shortcut-content\fR is in use in which case the contents of
the shortcut gets copied).
.IP \(bu 2
When deleting the shortcut is deleted not the linked file.
.IP \(bu 2
When setting the modification time, the modification time of the linked file
will be set.

.PP
For shortcuts pointing to folders:
.IP \(bu 2
When listing the shortcut appears as a folder and that folder will contain the
contents of the linked folder appear (including any sub folders)
.IP \(bu 2
When downloading the contents of the linked folder and sub contents are downloaded
.IP \(bu 2
When uploading to a shortcut folder the file will be placed in the linked folder
.IP \(bu 2
When server-side moving (renaming) the shortcut is renamed, not the destination
folder
.IP \(bu 2
When server-side copying the contents of the linked folder is copied, not the shortcut.
.IP \(bu 2
When deleting with \fBzclone rmdir\fR or \fBzclone purge\fR the shortcut is deleted not
the linked folder.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNB\fP When deleting with \fBzclone remove\fR or \fBzclone mount\fR the contents of the
linked folder will be deleted.

.PP
The zclone backend
\[la]///commands/zclone_backend/\[ra] command can be
used to create shortcuts.

.PP
Shortcuts can be completely ignored with the \fB--drive-skip-shortcuts\fR flag
or the corresponding \fBskip_shortcuts\fR configuration setting.

.PP
When \fBzclone lsf --format i\fR lists a file shortcut, the ID field contains the
target file ID followed by the shortcut ID, separated by a tab. This internal
tab is not changed by \fB--separator\fR\&.

.PP
If you have a folder shortcut that points at one of its own parent folders it
would lead to an infinite recursion. Zclone detects this, leaves the offending
shortcut out of the listing and logs an ERROR, so the rest of the drive can
still be copied.

.SS Emptying trash
If you wish to empty your trash you can use the \fBzclone cleanup remote:\fR
command which will permanently delete all your trashed files. This command
does not take any path arguments.

.PP
Note that Google Drive takes some time (minutes to days) to empty the
trash even though the command returns within a few seconds.  No output
is echoed, so there will be no confirmation even using -v or -vv.

.SS Quota information
To view your current quota you can use the \fBzclone about remote:\fR
command which will display your usage limit (quota), the usage in Google
Drive, the size of all files in the Trash and the space used by other
Google services such as Gmail. This command does not take any path
arguments.

.SS Import/Export of google documents
Google documents can be exported from and uploaded to Google Drive.

.PP
When zclone downloads a Google doc it chooses a format to download
depending upon the \fB--drive-export-formats\fR setting.
By default the export formats are \fBdocx,xlsx,pptx,svg\fR which are a
sensible default for an editable document.

.PP
When choosing a format, zclone runs down the list provided in order
and chooses the first file format the doc can be exported as from the
list. If the file can't be exported to a format on the formats list,
then zclone will choose a format from the default list.

.PP
If you prefer an archive copy then you might use \fB--drive-export-formats
pdf\fR, or if you prefer openoffice/libreoffice formats you might use
\fB--drive-export-formats ods,odt,odp\fR\&.

.PP
Note that zclone adds the extension to the google doc, so if it is
called \fBMy Spreadsheet\fR on google docs, it will be exported as \fBMy
Spreadsheet.xlsx\fR or \fBMy Spreadsheet.pdf\fR etc.

.PP
When importing files into Google Drive, zclone will convert all
files with an extension in \fB--drive-import-formats\fR to their
associated document type.
zclone will not convert any files by default, since the conversion
is lossy process.

.PP
The conversion must result in a file with the same extension when
the \fB--drive-export-formats\fR rules are applied to the uploaded document.

.PP
Here are some examples for allowed and prohibited conversions.

.TS
allbox;
l l l l l 
l l l l l .
\fBexport-formats\fP	\fBimport-formats\fP	\fBUpload Ext\fP	\fBDocument Ext\fP	\fBAllowed\fP
odt	odt	odt	odt	Yes
odt	docx,odt	odt	odt	Yes
	docx	docx	docx	Yes
	odt	odt	docx	No
odt,docx	docx,odt	docx	odt	No
docx,odt	docx,odt	docx	docx	Yes
docx,odt	docx,odt	odt	docx	No
.TE

.PP
This limitation can be disabled by specifying \fB--drive-allow-import-name-change\fR\&.
When using this flag, zclone can convert multiple files types resulting
in the same document type at once, e.g. with \fB--drive-import-formats docx,odt,txt\fR,
all files having these extension would result in a document represented as a
docx file.
This brings the additional risk of overwriting a document, if multiple files
have the same stem. Many zclone operations will not handle this name change
in any way. They assume an equal name when copying files and might copy the
file again or delete them when the name changes.

.PP
Here are the possible export extensions with their corresponding mime types.
Most of these can also be used for importing, but there more that are not
listed here. Some of these additional ones might only be available when
the operating system provides the correct MIME type entries.

.PP
This list can be changed by Google Drive at any time and might not
represent the currently available conversions.

.TS
