See the metadata
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] docs for more info.

.SH Backend commands
Here are the commands specific to the drive backend.

.PP
Run them with:

.EX
zclone backend COMMAND remote:
.EE

.PP
The help below will explain what arguments each command takes.

.PP
See the backend
\[la]//commands/zclone_backend/\[ra] command for more
info on how to pass options and arguments.

.PP
These can be run on a running backend using the rc command
backend/command
\[la]//rc/#backend\-command\[ra]\&.

.SS get
Get command for fetching the drive config parameters.

.EX
zclone backend get remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This is a get command which will be used to fetch the various drive config
parameters.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend get drive: [-o service_account_file] [-o chunk_size]
zclone rc backend/command command=get fs=drive: [-o service_account_file] [-o chunk_size]
.EE

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"chunk_size": Show the current upload chunk size.
.IP \(bu 2
"service_account_file": Show the current service account file.

.SS set
Set command for updating the drive config parameters.

.EX
zclone backend set remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This is a set command which will be used to update the various drive config
parameters.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend set drive: [-o service_account_file=sa.json] [-o chunk_size=67108864]
zclone rc backend/command command=set fs=drive: [-o service_account_file=sa.json] [-o chunk_size=67108864]
.EE

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"chunk_size": Update the current upload chunk size.
.IP \(bu 2
"service_account_file": Update the current service account file.

.SS shortcut
Create shortcuts from files or directories.

.EX
zclone backend shortcut remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command creates shortcuts from files or directories.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend shortcut drive: source_item destination_shortcut
zclone backend shortcut drive: source_item -o target=drive2: destination_shortcut
.EE

.PP
In the first example this creates a shortcut from the "source_item"
which can be a file or a directory to the "destination_shortcut". The
"source_item" and the "destination_shortcut" should be relative paths
from "drive:"

.PP
In the second example this creates a shortcut from the "source_item"
relative to "drive:" to the "destination_shortcut" relative to
"drive2:". This may fail with a permission error if the user
authenticated with "drive2:" can't read files from "drive:".

.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
"target": Optional target remote for the shortcut destination.

.SS drives
List the Shared Drives available to this account.

.EX
zclone backend drives remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command lists the Shared Drives (Team Drives) available to this
account.

.PP
Usage example:

.EX
zclone backend [-o config] drives drive:
.EE

.PP
This will return a JSON list of objects like this:

.EX
[
    {
        "id": "0ABCDEF-01234567890",
        "kind": "drive#teamDrive",
        "name": "My Drive"
    },
    {
        "id": "0ABCDEFabcdefghijkl",
        "kind": "drive#teamDrive",
        "name": "Test Drive"
    }
]
.EE

.PP
With the -o config parameter it will output the list in a format
suitable for adding to a config file to make aliases for all the
drives found and a combined drive.

.EX
[My Drive]
type = alias
remote = drive,team_drive=0ABCDEF-01234567890,root_folder_id=:

[Test Drive]
type = alias
remote = drive,team_drive=0ABCDEFabcdefghijkl,root_folder_id=:

[AllDrives]
type = combine
upstreams = "My Drive=My Drive:" "Test Drive=Test Drive:"
.EE

.PP
Adding this to the zclone config file will cause those team drives to
be accessible with the aliases shown. Any illegal characters will be
substituted with "_" and duplicate names will have numbers suffixed.
It will also add a remote called AllDrives which shows all the shared
drives combined into one directory tree.

.SS untrash
Untrash files and directories.

.EX
zclone backend untrash remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command untrashes all the files and directories in the directory
passed in recursively.

.PP
Usage example:

.EX
zclone backend untrash drive:directory
zclone backend --interactive untrash drive:directory subdir
.EE

.PP
This takes an optional directory to trash which make this easier to
use via the API.

.PP
Use the --interactive/-i or --dry-run flag to see what would be restored before
restoring it.

.PP
Result:

.EX
{
    "Untrashed": 17,
    "Errors": 0
}
.EE

.SS copyid
Copy files by ID.

.EX
zclone backend copyid remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command copies files by ID.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend copyid drive: ID path
zclone backend copyid drive: ID1 path1 ID2 path2
.EE

.PP
It copies the drive file with ID given to the path (an zclone path which
will be passed internally to zclone copyto). The ID and path pairs can be
repeated.

.PP
The path should end with a / to indicate copy the file as named to
this directory. If it doesn't end with a / then the last path
component will be used as the file name.

.PP
If the destination is a drive backend then server-side copying will be
attempted if possible.

.PP
Use the --interactive/-i or --dry-run flag to see what would be copied before
copying.

.SS moveid
Move files by ID.

.EX
zclone backend moveid remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command moves files by ID.

.PP
Usage examples:

.EX
zclone backend moveid drive: ID path
zclone backend moveid drive: ID1 path1 ID2 path2
.EE

.PP
It moves the drive file with ID given to the path (an zclone path which
will be passed internally to zclone moveto).

.PP
The path should end with a / to indicate move the file as named to
this directory. If it doesn't end with a / then the last path
component will be used as the file name.

.PP
If the destination is a drive backend then server-side moving will be
attempted if possible.

.PP
Use the --interactive/-i or --dry-run flag to see what would be moved beforehand.

.SS exportformats
Dump the export formats for debug purposes.

.EX
zclone backend exportformats remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.SS importformats
Dump the import formats for debug purposes.

.EX
zclone backend importformats remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.SS query
List files using Google Drive query language.

.EX
zclone backend query remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command lists files based on a query.

.PP
Usage example:

.EX
zclone backend query drive: query
.EE

.PP
The query syntax is documented at Google Drive Search query terms and
operators
\[la]https://developers.google.com/drive/api/guides/ref\-search\-terms\[ra]\&.

.PP
For example:

.EX
zclone backend query drive: "'0ABc9DEFGHIJKLMNop0QRatUVW3X' in parents and name contains 'foo'"
.EE

.PP
If the query contains literal ' or \\ characters, these need to be escaped with
\\ characters. "'" becomes "\\'" and "\\" becomes "\\\\", for example to match a
file named "foo ' \&.txt":

.EX
zclone backend query drive: "name = 'foo \\' \\\\\\.txt'"
.EE

.PP
The result is a JSON array of matches, for example:

.EX
[
    {
        "createdTime": "2017-06-29T19:58:28.537Z",
        "id": "0AxBe_CDEF4zkGHI4d0FjYko2QkD",
        "md5Checksum": "68518d16be0c6fbfab918be61d658032",
        "mimeType": "text/plain",
        "modifiedTime": "2024-02-02T10:40:02.874Z",
        "name": "foo ' \\\\.txt",
        "parents": [
            "0BxAe_BCDE4zkFGZpcWJGek0xbzC"
        ],
        "resourceKey": "0-ABCDEFGHIXJQpIGqBJq3MC",
        "sha1Checksum": "8f284fa768bfb4e45d076a579ab3905ab6bfa893",
        "size": "311",
        "webViewLink": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/0AxBe_CDEF4zkGHI4d0FjYko2QkD/view?usp=drivesdk\\u0026resourcekey=0-ABCDEFGHIXJQpIGqBJq3MC"
    }
]
```console

### rescue

Rescue or delete any orphaned files.

```console
zclone backend rescue remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE

.PP
This command rescues or deletes any orphaned files or directories.

.PP
Sometimes files can get orphaned in Google Drive. This means that they
are no longer in any folder in Google Drive.

.PP
This command finds those files and either rescues them to a directory
you specify or deletes them.

.PP
This can be used in 3 ways.

.PP
First, list all orphaned files:

.EX
zclone backend rescue drive:
.EE

.PP
Second rescue all orphaned files to the directory indicated:

.EX
zclone backend rescue drive: "relative/path/to/rescue/directory"
.EE

.PP
E.g. to rescue all orphans to a directory called "Orphans" in the top level:

.EX
zclone backend rescue drive: Orphans
.EE

.PP
Third delete all orphaned files to the trash:

.EX
zclone backend rescue drive: -o delete
.EE

.SH Limitations
Drive has quite a lot of rate limiting.  This causes zclone to be
limited to transferring about 2 files per second only.  Individual
files may be transferred much faster at 100s of MiB/s but lots of
small files can take a long time.

.PP
Server side copies are also subject to a separate rate limit. If you
see User rate limit exceeded errors, wait at least 24 hours and retry.
You can disable server-side copies with \fB--disable copy\fR to download
and upload the files if you prefer.

.SS Limitations of Google Docs
Google docs will appear as size -1 in \fBzclone ls\fR, \fBzclone ncdu\fR etc,
and as size 0 in anything which uses the VFS layer, e.g. \fBzclone mount\fR
and \fBzclone serve\fR\&. When calculating directory totals, e.g. in
\fBzclone size\fR and \fBzclone ncdu\fR, they will be counted in as empty
files.

.PP
This is because zclone can't find out the size of the Google docs
without downloading them.

.PP
Google docs will transfer correctly with \fBzclone sync\fR, \fBzclone copy\fR
etc as zclone knows to ignore the size when doing the transfer.

.PP
However an unfortunate consequence of this is that you may not be able
to download Google docs using \fBzclone mount\fR\&. If it doesn't work you
will get a 0 sized file.  If you try again the doc may gain its
correct size and be downloadable. Whether it will work on not depends
on the application accessing the mount and the OS you are running -
experiment to find out if it does work for you!

.SS Duplicated files
Sometimes, for no reason I've been able to track down, drive will
duplicate a file that zclone uploads.  Drive unlike all the other
remotes can have duplicated files.

.PP
Duplicated files cause problems with the syncing and you will see
messages in the log about duplicates.

.PP
Use \fBzclone dedupe\fR to fix duplicated files.

.PP
Note that this isn't just a problem with zclone, even Google Photos on
Android duplicates files on drive sometimes.

.SS Zclone appears to be re-copying files it shouldn't
The most likely cause of this is the duplicated file issue above - run
\fBzclone dedupe\fR and check your logs for duplicate object or directory
messages.

.PP
This can also be caused by a delay/caching on google drive's end when
comparing directory listings. Specifically with team drives used in
combination with --fast-list. Files that were uploaded recently may
not appear on the directory list sent to zclone when using --fast-list.

.PP
Waiting a moderate period of time between attempts (estimated to be
approximately 1 hour) and/or not using --fast-list both seem to be
effective in preventing the problem.

.SS SHA1 or SHA256 hashes may be missing
All files have MD5 hashes, but a small fraction of files uploaded may
not have SHA1 or SHA256 hashes especially if they were uploaded before 2018.

.SH Making your own client_id
When you use zclone with Google drive in its default configuration you
are using zclone's client_id.  This is shared between all the zclone
users.

.PP
\fBThis shared client_id is being retired and will stop working during
2026.\fP  To avoid interruption you must create and use your own
client_id, so creating one is now required rather than merely
recommended.  New remotes created with \fBzclone config\fR will warn you if
you leave the client_id blank.

.PP
Using your own client_id has other benefits too. There is a global
rate limit on the number of queries per second that each client_id can
do set by Google. If you have multiple services running, it is
recommended to use an API key for each service. The default Google
quota is 10 transactions per second so it is recommended to stay under
that number as if you use more than that, it will cause zclone to rate
limit and make things slower.

.PP
Here is how to create your own Google Drive client ID for zclone:
.IP "  1." 5
Log into the Google API
Console
\[la]https://console.developers.google.com/\[ra] with your Google
account. It doesn't matter what Google account you use. (It need not
be the same account as the Google Drive you want to access)
.IP "  2." 5
Select a project or create a new project.
.IP "  3." 5
Under "ENABLE APIS AND SERVICES" search for "Drive", and enable the
"Google Drive API".
.IP "  4." 5
Click "Credentials" in the left-side panel (not "Create
credentials", which opens the wizard).
.IP "  5." 5
If you already configured an "Oauth Consent Screen", then skip
to the next step; if not, click on "CONFIGURE CONSENT SCREEN" button
(near the top right corner of the right panel), then click "Get started".
On the next screen, enter an "Application name"
("zclone" is OK); enter "User Support Email" (your own email is OK);
Next, under Audience select "External". Next enter your own contact information,
agree to terms and click "Create". You should now see zclone (or your project name)
in a box in the top left of the screen.

(PS: if you are a GSuite user, you could also select "Internal" instead
of "External" above, but this will restrict API use to Google Workspace
users in your organisation).

You will also have to add some scopes
\[la]https://developers.google.com/drive/api/guides/api\-specific\-auth\[ra],
including
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttps://www.googleapis.com/auth/docs\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttps://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive\fR in order to be able to edit,
create and delete files with Zclone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttps://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.metadata.readonly\fR which you may
also want to add.
.RE
.IP
To do this, click Data Access on the left side panel, click "add or
remove scopes" and select the three above and press update or go to the
"Manually add scopes" text box (scroll down) and enter
"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/docs,https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive,https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.metadata.readonly", press add to table then update.

You should now see the three scopes on your Data access page. Now press save
at the bottom!
.IP "  6." 5
After adding scopes, click Audience.
Scroll down and click "+ Add users". Add yourself as a test user and press save.
.IP "  7." 5
Go to Overview on the left panel, click "Create OAuth client". Choose
an application type of "Desktop app" and click "Create". (the default name is fine)
.IP "  8." 5
It will show you a client ID and client secret. Make a note of these.
(If you selected "External" at Step 5 continue to Step 9.
If you chose "Internal" you don't need to publish and can skip straight to
Step 10 but your destination drive must be part of the same Google Workspace.)
.IP "  9." 5
Go to "Audience" and then click "PUBLISH APP" button and confirm.
Add yourself as a test user if you haven't already.
.IP " 10." 5
Provide the noted client ID and client secret to zclone.
.IP " 11." 5
Run the web-based authorization flow from within \fBzclone config\fR, by answering
"Y" when it asks "Token already configured - replace it?".

.PP
Be aware that, due to the "enhanced security" recently introduced by
Google, you are theoretically expected to "submit your app for verification"
and then wait a few weeks(!) for their response; in practice, you can go right
ahead and use the client ID and client secret with zclone, the only issue will
be a very scary confirmation screen shown when you connect via your browser
for zclone to be able to get its token-id (but as this only happens during
the remote configuration, it's not such a big deal). Keeping the application in
"Testing" will work as well, but the limitation is that any grants will expire
after a week, which can be annoying to refresh constantly. If, for whatever
reason, a short grant time is not a problem, then keeping the application in
testing mode would also be sufficient.

.PP
In most cases verification is not actually required. Google exempts a number of
app categories
\[la]https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/13464323\[ra] from mandatory
verification, including:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPersonal Use apps\fP: if the app is for your personal use (fewer than 100
users), you and your limited number of users can keep using it without
verification - you will just need to click through the "unverified app"
warning screen during sign-in. Verification is only required if you want to
grow your user base beyond 100 users.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDevelopment/Testing/Staging apps\fP: apps in development, testing or staging
mode are not subject to verification (but are limited to the 100-user cap and
the unverified-app warning until verified).

.PP
So for typical personal zclone use you can leave the app unverified, accept the
warning screen, and publish it (rather than leaving it in "Testing") to avoid
the weekly grant expiry described above.

.PP
(Thanks to @balazer on github for these instructions.)

.PP
Sometimes, creation of an OAuth consent in Google API Console fails due to an
error message "The request failed because changes to one of the field of the
resource is not supported". As a convenient workaround, the necessary Google
Drive API key can be created on the
Python Quickstart
\[la]https://developers.google.com/drive/api/v3/quickstart/python\[ra]
page. Just push the Enable the Drive API button to receive the Client ID and Secret.
Note that it will automatically create a new project in the API Console.


.SH Google Photos
The zclone backend for Google Photos
\[la]https://www.google.com/photos/about/\[ra] is
a specialized backend for transferring photos and videos to and from
Google Photos.

.PP
\fBNB\fP The Google Photos API which zclone uses has quite a few
limitations, so please read the limitations section
\[la]#limitations\[ra]
carefully to make sure it is suitable for your use.

.PP
\fBNB\fP From March 31, 2025 zclone can only download photos it
uploaded. This limitation is due to policy changes at Google. You may
need to run \fBzclone config reconnect remote:\fR to make zclone work
again after upgrading to zclone v1.70.

.SH Configuration
The initial setup for google cloud storage involves getting a token from Google Photos
which you need to do in your browser.  \fBzclone config\fR walks you
through it.

.PP
Here is an example of how to make a remote called \fBremote\fR\&.  First run:

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process:

.EX
