.PP
See the remote setup docs
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra] for how to set it up on a
machine without an internet-connected web browser available.

.PP
Note that zclone runs a webserver on your local machine to collect the
token as returned from Google if using web browser to automatically
authenticate. This only
runs from the moment it opens your browser to the moment you get back
the verification code.  This is on \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:53682/\fR and this
may require you to unblock it temporarily if you are running a host
firewall, or use manual mode.

.PP
This remote is called \fBremote\fR and can now be used like this

.PP
See all the albums in your photos

.EX
zclone lsd remote:album
.EE

.PP
Make a new album

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:album/newAlbum
.EE

.PP
List the contents of an album

.EX
zclone ls remote:album/newAlbum
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/images\fR to the Google Photos, removing any excess
files in the album.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/image remote:album/newAlbum
.EE

.SS Layout
As Google Photos is not a general purpose cloud storage system, the
backend is laid out to help you navigate it.

.PP
The directories under \fBmedia\fR show different ways of categorizing the
media.  Each file will appear multiple times.  So if you want to make
a backup of your google photos you might choose to backup
\fBremote:media/by-month\fR\&.  (\fBNB\fP \fBremote:media/by-day\fR is rather slow
at the moment so avoid for syncing.)

.PP
Note that all your photos and videos will appear somewhere under
\fBmedia\fR, but they may not appear under \fBalbum\fR unless you've put them
into albums.

.EX
/
- upload
    - file1.jpg
    - file2.jpg
    - ...
- media
    - all
        - file1.jpg
        - file2.jpg
        - ...
    - by-year
        - 2000
            - file1.jpg
            - ...
        - 2001
            - file2.jpg
            - ...
        - ...
    - by-month
        - 2000
            - 2000-01
                - file1.jpg
                - ...
            - 2000-02
                - file2.jpg
                - ...
        - ...
    - by-day
        - 2000
            - 2000-01-01
                - file1.jpg
                - ...
            - 2000-01-02
                - file2.jpg
                - ...
        - ...
- album
    - album name
    - album name/sub
- shared-album
    - album name
    - album name/sub
- feature
    - favorites
        - file1.jpg
        - file2.jpg
.EE

.PP
There are two writable parts of the tree, the \fBupload\fR directory and
sub directories of the \fBalbum\fR directory.

.PP
The \fBupload\fR directory is for uploading files you don't want to put
into albums. This will be empty to start with and will contain the
files you've uploaded for one zclone session only, becoming empty
again when you restart zclone. The use case for this would be if you
have a load of files you just want to once off dump into Google
Photos. For repeated syncing, uploading to \fBalbum\fR will work better.

.PP
Directories within the \fBalbum\fR directory are also writeable and you
may create new directories (albums) under \fBalbum\fR\&.  If you copy files
with a directory hierarchy in there then zclone will create albums
with the \fB/\fR character in them.  For example if you do

.EX
zclone copy /path/to/images remote:album/images
.EE

.PP
and the images directory contains

.EX
images
    - file1.jpg
    dir
        file2.jpg
    dir2
        dir3
            file3.jpg
.EE

.PP
Then zclone will create the following albums with the following files in
.IP \(bu 2
images
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
file1.jpg
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
images/dir
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
file2.jpg
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
images/dir2/dir3
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
file3.jpg
.RE

.PP
This means that you can use the \fBalbum\fR path pretty much like a normal
filesystem and it is a good target for repeated syncing.

.PP
The \fBshared-album\fR directory shows albums shared with you or by you.
This is similar to the Sharing tab in the Google Photos web interface.

.PP
 

.SS Standard options
Here are the Standard options specific to google photos (Google Photos).

.SS --gphotos-client-id
OAuth Client Id.

.PP
Creating your own is now strongly recommended.
If you leave this blank zclone uses a shared client_id which is being retired and will stop working during 2026.
See //googlephotos/#making-your-own-client-id for how to create your own.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      client_id
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_GPHOTOS_CLIENT_ID
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --gphotos-client-secret
OAuth Client Secret.

.PP
Leave blank to use zclone's shared client_id.
If you created your own client_id then enter its client secret here.

.PP
