Description of the remote.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      description
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_GPHOTOS_DESCRIPTION
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SH Limitations
Only images and videos can be uploaded.  If you attempt to upload non
videos or images or formats that Google Photos doesn't understand,
zclone will upload the file, then Google Photos will give an error
when it is put turned into a media item.

.PP
\fBNB\fP From March 31, 2025 zclone can only download photos it
uploaded. This limitation is due to policy changes at Google. You may
need to run \fBzclone config reconnect remote:\fR to make zclone work
again after upgrading to zclone v1.70.

.PP
Note that all media items uploaded to Google Photos through the API
are stored in full resolution at "original quality" and \fBwill\fP count
towards your storage quota in your Google Account.  The API does
\fBnot\fP offer a way to upload in "high quality" mode..

.PP
\fBzclone about\fR is not supported by the Google Photos backend. Backends without
this capability cannot determine free space for an zclone mount or
use policy \fBmfs\fR (most free space) as a member of an zclone union
remote.

.PP
See List of backends that do not support zclone about
\[la]///overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
See zclone about
\[la]///commands/zclone_about/\[ra]

.SS Downloading Images
When Images are downloaded this strips EXIF location (according to the
docs and my tests).  This is a limitation of the Google Photos API and
is covered by bug #112096115
\[la]https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/112096115\[ra]\&.

.PP
\fBThe current google API does not allow photos to be downloaded at original
resolution. This is very important if you are, for example, relying on
"Google Photos" as a backup of your photos. You will not be able to use
zclone to redownload original images.  You could use 'google takeout'
to recover the original photos as a last resort\fP

.PP
\fBNB\fP you \fBcan\fP use the --gphotos-proxy
\[la]#gphotos\-proxy\[ra] flag to use a
headless browser to download images in full resolution.

.SS Downloading Videos
When videos are downloaded they are downloaded in a really compressed
version of the video compared to downloading it via the Google Photos
web interface. This is covered by bug #113672044
\[la]https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/113672044\[ra]\&.

.PP
\fBNB\fP you \fBcan\fP use the --gphotos-proxy
\[la]#gphotos\-proxy\[ra] flag to use a
headless browser to download images in full resolution.

.SS Duplicates
If a file name is duplicated in a directory then zclone will add the
file ID into its name.  So two files called \fBfile.jpg\fR would then
appear as \fBfile {123456}.jpg\fR and \fBfile {ABCDEF}.jpg\fR (the actual IDs
are a lot longer alas!).

.PP
If you upload the same image (with the same binary data) twice then
Google Photos will deduplicate it.  However it will retain the
filename from the first upload which may confuse zclone.  For example
if you uploaded an image to \fBupload\fR then uploaded the same image to
\fBalbum/my_album\fR the filename of the image in \fBalbum/my_album\fR will be
what it was uploaded with initially, not what you uploaded it with to
\fBalbum\fR\&.  In practise this shouldn't cause too many problems.

.SS Modification times
The date shown of media in Google Photos is the creation date as
determined by the EXIF information, or the upload date if that is not
known.

.PP
This is not changeable by zclone and is not the modification date of
the media on local disk.  This means that zclone cannot use the dates
from Google Photos for syncing purposes.

.SS Size
The Google Photos API does not return the size of media.  This means
that when syncing to Google Photos, zclone can only do a file
existence check.

.PP
It is possible to read the size of the media, but this needs an extra
HTTP HEAD request per media item so is \fBvery slow\fP and uses up a lot of
transactions.  This can be enabled with the \fB--gphotos-read-size\fR
option or the \fBread_size = true\fR config parameter.

.PP
If you want to use the backend with \fBzclone mount\fR you may need to
enable this flag (depending on your OS and application using the
photos) otherwise you may not be able to read media off the mount.
You'll need to experiment to see if it works for you without the flag.

.SS Albums
Zclone can only upload files to albums it created. This is a
limitation of the Google Photos API
\[la]https://developers.google.com/photos/library/guides/manage\-albums\[ra]\&.

.PP
Zclone can remove files it uploaded from albums it created only.

.SS Deleting files
Zclone can remove files from albums it created, but note that the
Google Photos API does not allow media to be deleted permanently so
this media will still remain. See bug #109759781
\[la]https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/109759781\[ra]\&.

.PP
Zclone cannot delete files anywhere except under \fBalbum\fR\&.

.SS Deleting albums
The Google Photos API does not support deleting albums - see bug #135714733
\[la]https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/135714733\[ra]\&.

.SH Making your own client_id
When you use zclone with Google photos in its default configuration you
are using zclone's client_id.  This is shared between all the zclone
users.

.PP
\fBThis shared client_id is being retired and will stop working during
2026.\fP  To avoid interruption you must create and use your own
client_id, so creating one is now required rather than optional.  New
remotes created with \fBzclone config\fR will warn you if you leave the
client_id blank.

.PP
Please follow the steps in the google drive docs
\[la]///drive/#making\-your\-own\-client\-id\[ra]
with the following differences:
.IP \(bu 2
At step 3, instead of enabling the "Google Drive API", search for and
enable the "Photos Library API".
.IP \(bu 2
At step 5, you will need to add different scopes. Use these scopes
instead of the drive ones:

.EX
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary.appendonly
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary.readonly.appcreateddata
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary.edit.appcreateddata
.EE


.SH Hasher
Hasher is a special overlay backend to create remotes which handle
checksums for other remotes. It's main functions include:
.IP \(bu 2
Emulate hash types unimplemented by backends
.IP \(bu 2
Cache checksums to help with slow hashing of large local or (S)FTP files
.IP \(bu 2
Warm up checksum cache from external SUM files

.SH Getting started
To use Hasher, first set up the underlying remote following the configuration
instructions for that remote. You can also use a local pathname instead of
a remote. Check that your base remote is working.

.PP
Let's call the base remote \fBmyRemote:path\fR here. Note that anything inside
\fBmyRemote:path\fR will be handled by hasher and anything outside won't.
This means that if you are using a bucket based remote (S3, B2, Swift)
then you should put the bucket in the remote \fBs3:bucket\fR\&.

.PP
Now proceed to interactive or manual configuration.

.SS Interactive configuration
Run \fBzclone config\fR:

.EX
No remotes found, make a new one\\?
n) New remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
n/s/q> n
name> Hasher1
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Handle checksums for other remotes
   \\ "hasher"
[snip]
Storage> hasher
Remote to cache checksums for, like myremote:mypath.
Enter a string value. Press Enter for the default ("").
remote> myRemote:path
Comma separated list of supported checksum types.
Enter a string value. Press Enter for the default ("md5,sha1").
hashsums> md5
Maximum time to keep checksums in cache. 0 = no cache, off = cache forever.
max_age> off
Edit advanced config? (y/n)
y) Yes
n) No
y/n> n
Remote config
--------------------
[Hasher1]
type = hasher
remote = myRemote:path
hashsums = md5
max_age = off
--------------------
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE

.SS Manual configuration
Run \fBzclone config path\fR to see the path of current active config file,
usually \fBYOURHOME/.config/zclone/zclone.conf\fR\&.
Open it in your favorite text editor, find section for the base remote
and create new section for hasher like in the following examples:

.EX
[Hasher1]
type = hasher
remote = myRemote:path
hashes = md5
max_age = off

[Hasher2]
type = hasher
remote = /local/path
hashes = dropbox,sha1
max_age = 24h
.EE

.PP
Hasher takes basically the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fR is required
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhashes\fR is a comma separated list of supported checksums
(by default \fBmd5,sha1\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax_age\fR - maximum time to keep a checksum value in the cache
\fB0\fR will disable caching completely
\fBoff\fR will cache "forever" (that is until the files get changed)

.PP
Make sure the \fBremote\fR has \fB:\fR (colon) in. If you specify the remote without
a colon then zclone will use a local directory of that name. So if you use
a remote of \fB/local/path\fR then zclone will handle hashes for that directory.
If you use \fBremote = name\fR literally then zclone will put files
\fBin a directory called \fBname\fR located under current directory\fP\&.

.SH Usage
.SS Basic operations
Now you can use it as \fBHasher2:subdir/file\fR instead of base remote.
Hasher will transparently update cache with new checksums when a file
is fully read or overwritten, like:

.EX
zclone copy External:path/file Hasher:dest/path
zclone cat Hasher:path/to/file > /dev/null
.EE

.PP
The way to refresh \fBall\fP cached checksums (even unsupported by the base backend)
for a subtree is to \fBre-download\fP all files in the subtree. For example,
use \fBhashsum --download\fR using \fBany\fP supported hashsum on the command line
(we just care to re-read):

.EX
zclone hashsum MD5 --download Hasher:path/to/subtree > /dev/null
zclone backend dump Hasher:path/to/subtree
.EE

.PP
You can print or drop hashsum cache using custom backend commands:

.EX
zclone backend dump Hasher:dir/subdir
zclone backend drop Hasher:
.EE

.SS Pre-Seed from a SUM File
Hasher supports two backend commands: generic SUM file \fBimport\fR and faster
but less consistent \fBstickyimport\fR\&.

.EX
zclone backend import Hasher:dir/subdir SHA1 /path/to/SHA1SUM [--checkers 4]
.EE

.PP
Instead of SHA1 it can be any hash supported by the remote. The last argument
can point to either a local or an \fBother-remote:path\fR text file in SUM format.
The command will parse the SUM file, then walk down the path given by the
first argument, snapshot current fingerprints and fill in the cache entries
correspondingly.
.IP \(bu 2
Paths in the SUM file are treated as relative to \fBhasher:dir/subdir\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The command will \fBnot\fP check that supplied values are correct.
You \fBmust know\fP what you are doing.
.IP \(bu 2
This is a one-time action. The SUM file will not get "attached" to the
remote. Cache entries can still be overwritten later, should the object's
fingerprint change.
.IP \(bu 2
The tree walk can take long depending on the tree size. You can increase
\fB--checkers\fR to make it faster. Or use \fBstickyimport\fR if you don't care
about fingerprints and consistency.

.EX
zclone backend stickyimport hasher:path/to/data sha1 remote:/path/to/sum.sha1
.EE

.PP
\fBstickyimport\fR is similar to \fBimport\fR but works much faster because it
does not need to stat existing files and skips initial tree walk.
Instead of binding cache entries to file fingerprints it creates \fIsticky\fP
entries bound to the file name alone ignoring size, modification time etc.
Such hash entries can be replaced only by \fBpurge\fR, \fBdelete\fR, \fBbackend drop\fR
or by full re-read/re-write of the files.

