.SH Limitations
.SS Symbolic links
HiDrive is able to store symbolic links (\fIsymlinks\fP) by design,
for example, when unpacked from a zip archive.

.PP
There exists no direct mechanism to manage native symlinks in remotes.
As such this implementation has chosen to ignore any native symlinks present in
the remote. zclone will not be able to access or show any symlinks stored in
the hidrive-remote. This means symlinks cannot be individually removed, copied,
or moved, except when removing, copying, or moving the parent folder.

.PP
\fIThis does not affect the \fB\&.zclonelink\fR-files
that zclone uses to encode and store symbolic links.\fP

.SS Sparse files
It is possible to store sparse files in HiDrive.

.PP
Note that copying a sparse file will expand the holes
into null-byte (0x00) regions that will then consume disk space.
Likewise, when downloading a sparse file,
the resulting file will have null-byte regions in the place of file holes.


.SH HTTP
The HTTP remote is a read only remote for reading files of a
webserver.  The webserver should provide file listings which zclone
will read and turn into a remote.  This has been tested with common
webservers such as Apache/Nginx/Caddy and will likely work with file
listings from most web servers.  (If it doesn't then please file an
issue, or send a pull request!)

.PP
Paths are specified as \fBremote:\fR or \fBremote:path\fR\&.

.PP
The \fBremote:\fR represents the configured url
\[la]#http\-url\[ra], and any path following
it will be resolved relative to this url, according to the URL standard. This
means with remote url \fB/ and path\fRfix\fB, the
resolved URL will be\fR/ while with path
\fB/fix\fR the resolved URL will be `/ as the absolute
path is resolved from the root of the domain.

.PP
If the path following the \fBremote:\fR ends with \fB/\fR it will be assumed to point
to a directory. If the path does not end with \fB/\fR, then a HEAD request is sent
and the response used to decide if it is treated as a file or a directory
(run with \fB-vv\fR to see details). When --http-no-head
\[la]#http\-no\-head\[ra] is
specified, a path without ending \fB/\fR is always assumed to be a file. If zclone
incorrectly assumes the path is a file, the solution is to specify the path with
ending \fB/\fR\&. When you know the path is a directory, ending it with \fB/\fR is always
better as it avoids the initial HEAD request.

.PP
To just download a single file it is easier to use
copyurl
\[la]//commands/zclone_copyurl/\[ra]\&.

