.SH iCloud Drive and Photos
.SH Configuration
The initial setup for an iCloud backend involves getting a trust token/session.
This uses your regular Apple ID password plus 2FA, either from a trusted
device prompt or an SMS code sent to a trusted phone number.

.PP
\fBIMPORTANT\fP: App-specific passwords are not accepted. Only use your
regular Apple ID password and 2FA.

.PP
This backend serves two Apple services:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdrive\fR - iCloud Drive (default)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBphotos\fR - iCloud Photos

.PP
\fBzclone config\fR walks you through the token creation. The trust token is valid
for 30 days. After which you will have to reauthenticate with \fBzclone reconnect\fR
or \fBzclone config\fR\&.

.SH Authentication
zclone authenticates with Apple using SRP (Secure Remote Password)
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secure_Remote_Password_protocol\[ra],
the same protocol used by the iCloud web interface. Your password is never
sent to Apple's servers -- instead, a cryptographic proof is exchanged that
verifies you know the password without revealing it.

.PP
The authentication flow is:
.IP "  1." 5
zclone initiates a session with Apple's identity service
.IP "  2." 5
An SRP key exchange takes place (your password is used locally to derive a key)
.IP "  3." 5
Apple sends a 2FA prompt to your trusted devices, or lets you request an
SMS code
.IP "  4." 5
After you enter the 2FA code, zclone receives a trust token for future sessions

.PP
Here is an example of how to make a Photos remote called \fBicloudphotos\fR\&.
For iCloud Drive, leave \fBservice\fR at its default \fBdrive\fR value. First run:

.EX
zclone config
.EE

