.SH Internet Archive
The Internet Archive backend utilizes Items on archive.org
\[la]https://archive.org/\[ra]

.PP
Refer to IAS3 API documentation
\[la]https://archive.org/services/docs/api/ias3.html\[ra]
for the API this backend uses.

.PP
Paths are specified as \fBremote:bucket\fR (or \fBremote:\fR for the \fBlsd\fR
command.)  You may put subdirectories in too, e.g. \fBremote:item/path/to/dir\fR\&.

.PP
Unlike S3, listing up all items uploaded by you isn't supported.

.PP
Once you have made a remote, you can use it like this:

.PP
Make a new item

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:item
.EE

.PP
List the contents of a item

.EX
zclone ls remote:item
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/directory\fR to the remote item, deleting any excess
files in the item.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:item
.EE

.SH Notes
Because of Internet Archive's architecture, it enqueues write operations (and
extra post-processings) in a per-item queue. You can check item's queue at

\[la]https://catalogd.archive.org/history/item\-name\-here\[ra]\&. Because of that, all
uploads/deletes will not show up immediately and takes some time to be available.
The per-item queue is enqueued to an another queue, Item Deriver Queue.
You can check the status of Item Deriver Queue here.
\[la]https://catalogd.archive.org/catalog.php?whereami=1\[ra]
This queue has a limit, and it may block you from uploading, or even deleting.
You should avoid uploading a lot of small files for better behavior.

.PP
You can optionally wait for the server's processing to finish, by setting
non-zero value to \fBwait_archive\fR key. By making it wait, zclone can do normal
file comparison. Make sure to set a large enough value (e.g. \fB30m0s\fR for smaller
files) as it can take a long time depending on server's queue.

.SH About metadata
This backend supports setting, updating and reading metadata of each file.
The metadata will appear as file metadata on Internet Archive.
However, some fields are reserved by both Internet Archive and zclone.

.PP
The following are reserved by Internet Archive:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsource\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsize\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmd5\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcrc32\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsha1\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBformat\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBold_version\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBviruscheck\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsummation\fR

.PP
Trying to set values to these keys is ignored with a warning.
Only setting \fBmtime\fR is an exception. Doing so make it the identical
behavior as setting ModTime.

.PP
zclone reserves all the keys starting with \fBzclone-\fR\&. Setting value for
these keys will give you warnings, but values are set according to request.

.PP
If there are multiple values for a key, only the first one is returned.
This is a limitation of zclone, that supports one value per one key.
It can be triggered when you did a server-side copy.

.PP
Reading metadata will also provide custom (non-standard nor reserved) ones.

.SH Filtering auto generated files
The Internet Archive automatically creates metadata files after
upload. These can cause problems when doing an \fBzclone sync\fR as zclone
will try, and fail, to delete them. These metadata files are not
changeable, as they are created by the Internet Archive automatically.

.PP
These auto-created files can be excluded from the sync using metadata
filtering
\[la]//filtering/#metadata\[ra]\&.

.EX
zclone sync ... --metadata-exclude "source=metadata" --metadata-exclude "format=Metadata"
.EE

.PP
Which excludes from the sync any files which have the
\fBsource=metadata\fR or \fBformat=Metadata\fR flags which are added to
Internet Archive auto-created files.

