.SS Metadata
User metadata is stored as x-ms-meta- keys. Azure metadata keys are case insensitive and are always returned in lower case.

.PP
Here are the possible system metadata items for the azureblob backend.

.TS
allbox;
l l l l l 
l l l l l .
\fBName\fP	\fBHelp\fP	\fBType\fP	\fBExample\fP	\fBRead Only\fP
cache-control	Cache-Control header	string	no-cache	N
content-disposition	Content-Disposition header	string	inline	N
content-encoding	Content-Encoding header	string	gzip	N
content-language	Content-Language header	string	en-US	N
content-type	Content-Type header	string	text/plain	N
mtime	T{
Time of last modification, read from zclone metadata
T}	RFC 3339	T{
2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00
T}	N
tier	Tier of the object	string	Hot	\fBY\fP
.TE

.PP
See the metadata
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] docs for more info.

.SS Custom upload headers
You can set custom upload headers with the \fB--header-upload\fR flag.
.IP \(bu 2
Cache-Control
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Disposition
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Encoding
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Language
.IP \(bu 2
Content-Type
.IP \(bu 2
X-MS-Tags

.PP
Eg \fB--header-upload "Content-Type: text/potato"\fR or
\fB--header-upload "X-MS-Tags: foo=bar"\fR\&.

.SH Limitations
MD5 sums are only uploaded with chunked files if the source has an MD5
sum.  This will always be the case for a local to azure copy.

.PP
\fBzclone about\fR is not supported by the Microsoft Azure Blob storage backend.
Backends without this capability cannot determine free space for an zclone
mount or use policy \fBmfs\fR (most free space) as a member of an zclone union
remote.

.PP
See List of backends that do not support zclone about
\[la]///overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
and zclone about
\[la]///commands/zclone_about/\[ra]\&.

.SH Azure Storage Emulator Support
You can run zclone with the storage emulator (usually \fIazurite\fP).

.PP
To do this, just set up a new remote with \fBzclone config\fR following
the instructions in the introduction and set \fBuse_emulator\fR in the
advanced settings as \fBtrue\fR\&. You do not need to provide a default
account name nor an account key. But you can override them in the
\fBaccount\fR and \fBkey\fR options. (Prior to v1.61 they were hard coded to
\fIazurite\fP\&'s \fBdevstoreaccount1\fR\&.)

.PP
Also, if you want to access a storage emulator instance running on a
different machine, you can override the \fBendpoint\fR parameter in the
advanced settings, setting it to
\fBhttp(s)://<host>:<port>/devstoreaccount1\fR
(e.g. \fBhttp://10.254.2.5:10000/devstoreaccount1\fR).


.SH Microsoft Azure Files Storage
Paths are specified as \fBremote:\fR You may put subdirectories in too,
e.g. \fBremote:path/to/dir\fR\&.

.SH Configuration
Here is an example of making a Microsoft Azure Files Storage
configuration.  For a remote called \fBremote\fR\&.  First run:

.EX
zclone config
.EE

.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process:

.EX
No remotes found, make a new one?
n) New remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
n/s/q> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Microsoft Azure Files Storage
   \\ "azurefiles"
[snip]

Option account.
Azure Storage Account Name.
Set this to the Azure Storage Account Name in use.
Leave blank to use SAS URL or connection string, otherwise it needs to be set.
If this is blank and if env_auth is set it will be read from the
environment variable `AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME` if possible.
Enter a value. Press Enter to leave empty.
account> account_name

Option share_name.
Azure Files Share Name.
This is required and is the name of the share to access.
Enter a value. Press Enter to leave empty.
share_name> share_name

Option env_auth.
Read credentials from runtime (environment variables, CLI or MSI).
See the [authentication docs](/azurefiles#authentication) for full info.
Enter a boolean value (true or false). Press Enter for the default (false).
env_auth> 

Option key.
Storage Account Shared Key.
Leave blank to use SAS URL or connection string.
Enter a value. Press Enter to leave empty.
key> base64encodedkey==

Option sas_url.
SAS URL.
Leave blank if using account/key or connection string.
Enter a value. Press Enter to leave empty.
sas_url> 

Option connection_string.
Azure Files Connection String.
Enter a value. Press Enter to leave empty.
connection_string> 
[snip]

Configuration complete.
Options:
- type: azurefiles
- account: account_name
- share_name: share_name
- key: base64encodedkey==
Keep this "remote" remote?
y) Yes this is OK (default)
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> 
.EE

.PP
Once configured you can use zclone.

.PP
See all files in the top level:

.EX
zclone lsf remote:
.EE

.PP
Make a new directory in the root:

.EX
zclone mkdir remote:dir
.EE

.PP
Recursively List the contents:

.EX
zclone ls remote:
.EE

.PP
Sync \fB/home/local/directory\fR to the remote directory, deleting any
excess files in the directory.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:dir
.EE

.SS Modified time
The modified time is stored as the SMB \fBLastWriteTime\fR property on
files. It has a precision of 100 ns.

.SS Performance
When uploading large files, increasing the value of
\fB--azurefiles-upload-concurrency\fR will increase performance at the cost
of using more memory. The default of 16 is set quite conservatively to
use less memory. It maybe be necessary raise it to 64 or higher to
fully utilize a 1 GBit/s link with a single file transfer.

.SS Restricted filename characters
In addition to the default restricted characters set
\[la]//overview/#restricted\-characters\[ra]
the following characters are also replaced:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
"	0x22	＂
*	0x2A	＊
:	0x3A	：
<	0x3C	＜
>	0x3E	＞
?	0x3F	？
	0x5C	＼
|	0x7C	｜
.TE

.PP
File names can also not end with the following characters.
These only get replaced if they are the last character in the name:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
\&.	0x2E	．
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.SS Hashes
MD5 hashes are stored with files. Not all files will have MD5 hashes
as these have to be uploaded with the file.

.SS Authentication {#authentication}
There are a number of ways of supplying credentials for Azure Files
Storage. Zclone tries them in the order of the sections below.

.SS Env Auth
If the \fBenv_auth\fR config parameter is \fBtrue\fR then zclone will pull
credentials from the environment or runtime.

.PP
It tries these authentication methods in this order:
.IP "  1." 5
Environment Variables
.IP "  2." 5
Managed Service Identity Credentials
.IP "  3." 5
Azure CLI credentials (as used by the az tool)

.PP
These are described in the following sections

.SS Env Auth: 1. Environment Variables
If \fBenv_auth\fR is set and environment variables are present zclone
authenticates a service principal with a secret or certificate, or a
user with a password, depending on which environment variable are set.
It reads configuration from these variables, in the following order:
.IP "  1." 5
Service principal with client secret
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_TENANT_ID\fR: ID of the service principal's tenant. Also called its
"directory" ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_ID\fR: the service principal's client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_SECRET\fR: one of the service principal's client secrets
.RE
.IP "  2." 5
Service principal with certificate
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_TENANT_ID\fR: ID of the service principal's tenant. Also called its
"directory" ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_ID\fR: the service principal's client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH\fR: path to a PEM or PKCS12 certificate file
including the private key.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD\fR: (optional) password for the
certificate file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_SEND_CERTIFICATE_CHAIN\fR: (optional) Specifies whether an
authentication request will include an x5c header to support subject
name / issuer based authentication. When set to "true" or "1",
authentication requests include the x5c header.
.RE
.IP "  3." 5
User with username and password
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_TENANT_ID\fR: (optional) tenant to authenticate in. Defaults to "organizations".
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_ID\fR: client ID of the application the user will authenticate
to
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_USERNAME\fR: a username (usually an email address)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_PASSWORD\fR: the user's password
.RE
.IP "  4." 5
Workload Identity
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_TENANT_ID\fR: Tenant to authenticate in
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_CLIENT_ID\fR: Client ID of the application the user will authenticate
to
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE\fR: Path to projected service account token file
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAZURE_AUTHORITY_HOST\fR: Authority of an Azure Active Directory endpoint
(default: login.microsoftonline.com).
.RE

.SS Env Auth: 2. Managed Service Identity Credentials
When using Managed Service Identity if the VM(SS) on which this
program is running has a system-assigned identity, it will be used by
default. If the resource has no system-assigned but exactly one
user-assigned identity, the user-assigned identity will be used by
default.

.PP
If the resource has multiple user-assigned identities you will need to
unset \fBenv_auth\fR and set \fBuse_msi\fR instead. See the 
\[la]#use_msi\[ra]\&.

.PP
If you are operating in disconnected clouds, or private clouds such as
Azure Stack you may want to set \fBdisable_instance_discovery = true\fR\&.
This determines whether zclone requests Microsoft Entra instance
metadata from \fBhttps://login.microsoft.com/\fR before authenticating.
Setting this to \fBtrue\fR will skip this request, making you responsible
for ensuring the configured authority is valid and trustworthy.

.SS Env Auth: 3. Azure CLI credentials (as used by the az tool)
Credentials created with the \fBaz\fR tool can be picked up using \fBenv_auth\fR\&.

.PP
For example if you were to login with a service principal like this:

.EX
az login --service-principal -u XXX -p XXX --tenant XXX
.EE

.PP
Then you could access zclone resources like this:

.EX
zclone lsf :azurefiles,env_auth,account=ACCOUNT:
.EE

.PP
Or

.EX
zclone lsf --azurefiles-env-auth --azurefiles-account=ACCOUNT :azurefiles:
.EE

.SS Account and Shared Key
This is the most straight forward and least flexible way.  Just fill
in the \fBaccount\fR and \fBkey\fR lines and leave the rest blank.

.SS SAS URL
To use it leave \fBaccount\fR, \fBkey\fR and \fBconnection_string\fR blank and fill in \fBsas_url\fR\&.

.SS Connection String
To use it leave \fBaccount\fR, \fBkey\fR and "sas_url" blank and fill in \fBconnection_string\fR\&.

.SS Service principal with client secret
If these variables are set, zclone will authenticate with a service principal
with a client secret.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtenant\fR: ID of the service principal's tenant. Also called its "directory" ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_id\fR: the service principal's client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_secret\fR: one of the service principal's client secrets

.PP
The credentials can also be placed in a file using the
\fBservice_principal_file\fR configuration option.

.SS Service principal with certificate
If these variables are set, zclone will authenticate with a service principal
with certificate.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtenant\fR: ID of the service principal's tenant. Also called its "directory" ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_id\fR: the service principal's client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_certificate_path\fR: path to a PEM or PKCS12 certificate file including
the private key.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_certificate_password\fR: (optional) password for the certificate file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_send_certificate_chain\fR: (optional) Specifies whether an authentication
request will include an x5c header to support subject name / issuer based
authentication. When set to "true" or "1", authentication requests include
the x5c header.

.PP
\fBNB\fP \fBclient_certificate_password\fR must be obscured - see zclone obscure
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]\&.

.SS User with username and password
If these variables are set, zclone will authenticate with username and password.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtenant\fR: (optional) tenant to authenticate in. Defaults to "organizations".
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_id\fR: client ID of the application the user will authenticate to
.IP \(bu 2
\fBusername\fR: a username (usually an email address)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpassword\fR: the user's password

.PP
Microsoft doesn't recommend this kind of authentication, because it's
less secure than other authentication flows. This method is not
interactive, so it isn't compatible with any form of multi-factor
authentication, and the application must already have user or admin
consent. This credential can only authenticate work and school
accounts; it can't authenticate Microsoft accounts.

.PP
\fBNB\fP \fBpassword\fR must be obscured - see zclone obscure
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]\&.

.SS Managed Service Identity Credentials {#use_msi}
If \fBuse_msi\fR is set then managed service identity credentials are
used. This authentication only works when running in an Azure service.
\fBenv_auth\fR needs to be unset to use this.

.PP
However if you have multiple user identities to choose from these must
be explicitly specified using exactly one of the \fBmsi_object_id\fR,
\fBmsi_client_id\fR, or \fBmsi_mi_res_id\fR parameters.

.PP
If none of \fBmsi_object_id\fR, \fBmsi_client_id\fR, or \fBmsi_mi_res_id\fR is
set, this is is equivalent to using \fBenv_auth\fR\&.

.SS Federated Identity Credentials
If these variables are set, zclone will authenticate with federated identity.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtenant\fR: tenant ID of of the storage
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient_id\fR: client ID of the application the user will authenticate to storage
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmsi_client_id\fR: managed identity client ID of the application the user will
authenticate to

.PP
By default "api://AzureADTokenExchange" is used as scope for token retrieval
over MSI. This token is then exchanged for actual storage token using
\&'tenant' and 'client_id'.

.SS Azure CLI tool \fBaz\fR {#use_az}
If \fBuse_az\fR is set, use Azure CLI tool 
\[la]https://learn.microsoft.com/en\-us/cli/azure/\[ra]
as the sole means of authentication.

.PP
Setting this can be useful if you wish to use the \fBaz\fR CLI on a host with
a System Managed Identity that you do not want to use.

.PP
Don't set \fBenv_auth\fR at the same time.

.PP
 

