.SS Metadata
OneDrive supports System Metadata (not User Metadata, as of this writing) for
both files and directories. Much of the metadata is read-only, and there are some
differences between OneDrive Personal and Business (see table below for
details).

.PP
Permissions are also supported, if \fB--onedrive-metadata-permissions\fR is set. The
accepted values for \fB--onedrive-metadata-permissions\fR are "\fBread\fR", "\fBwrite\fR",
"\fBread,write\fR", and "\fBoff\fR" (the default). "\fBwrite\fR" supports adding new permissions,
updating the "role" of existing permissions, and removing permissions. Updating
and removing require the Permission ID to be known, so it is recommended to use
"\fBread,write\fR" instead of "\fBwrite\fR" if you wish to update/remove permissions.

.PP
Permissions are read/written in JSON format using the same schema as the
OneDrive API
\[la]https://learn.microsoft.com/en\-us/onedrive/developer/rest\-api/resources/permission?view=odsp\-graph\-online\[ra],
which differs slightly between OneDrive Personal and Business.

.PP
Example for OneDrive Personal:

.EX
[
	{
		"id": "1234567890ABC!123",
		"grantedTo": {
			"user": {
				"id": "ryan@contoso.com"
			},
			"application": {},
			"device": {}
		},
		"invitation": {
			"email": "ryan@contoso.com"
		},
		"link": {
			"webUrl": "https://1drv.ms/t/s!1234567890ABC"
		},
		"roles": [
			"read"
		],
		"shareId": "s!1234567890ABC"
	}
]
.EE

.PP
Example for OneDrive Business:

.EX
[
	{
		"id": "48d31887-5fad-4d73-a9f5-3c356e68a038",
		"grantedToIdentities": [
			{
				"user": {
					"displayName": "ryan@contoso.com"
				},
				"application": {},
				"device": {}
			}
		],
		"link": {
			"type": "view",
			"scope": "users",
			"webUrl": "https://contoso.sharepoint.com/:w:/t/design/a577ghg9hgh737613bmbjf839026561fmzhsr85ng9f3hjck2t5s"
		},
		"roles": [
			"read"
		],
		"shareId": "u!LKj1lkdlals90j1nlkascl"
	},
	{
		"id": "5D33DD65C6932946",
		"grantedTo": {
			"user": {
				"displayName": "John Doe",
				"id": "efee1b77-fb3b-4f65-99d6-274c11914d12"
			},
			"application": {},
			"device": {}
		},
		"roles": [
			"owner"
		],
		"shareId": "FWxc1lasfdbEAGM5fI7B67aB5ZMPDMmQ11U"
	}
]
.EE

.PP
To write permissions, pass in a "permissions" metadata key using this same
format. The 
\[la]///docs/#metadata\-mapper\[ra] tool can
be very helpful for this.

.PP
When adding permissions, an email address can be provided in the \fBUser.ID\fR or
\fBDisplayName\fR properties of \fBgrantedTo\fR or \fBgrantedToIdentities\fR\&. Alternatively,
an ObjectID can be provided in \fBUser.ID\fR\&. At least one valid recipient must be
provided in order to add a permission for a user. Creating a Public Link is also
supported, if \fBLink.Scope\fR is set to \fB"anonymous"\fR\&.

.PP
Example request to add a "read" permission with \fB--metadata-mapper\fR:

.EX
{
    "Metadata": {
        "permissions": "[{\\"grantedToIdentities\\":[{\\"user\\":{\\"id\\":\\"ryan@contoso.com\\"}}],\\"roles\\":[\\"read\\"]}]"
    }
}
.EE

.PP
Note that adding a permission can fail if a conflicting permission already
exists for the file/folder.

.PP
To update an existing permission, include both the Permission ID and the new
\fBroles\fR to be assigned. \fBroles\fR is the only property that can be changed.

.PP
To remove permissions, pass in a blob containing only the permissions you wish
to keep (which can be empty, to remove all.) Note that the \fBowner\fR role will be
ignored, as it cannot be removed.

.PP
Note that both reading and writing permissions requires extra API calls, so if
you don't need to read or write permissions it is recommended to omit
\fB--onedrive-metadata-permissions\fR\&.

.PP
Metadata and permissions are supported for Folders (directories) as well as
Files. Note that setting the \fBmtime\fR or \fBbtime\fR on a Folder requires one extra
API call on OneDrive Business only.

.PP
OneDrive does not currently support User Metadata. When writing metadata, only
writeable system properties will be written -- any read-only or unrecognized keys
passed in will be ignored.

.PP
TIP: to see the metadata and permissions for any file or folder, run:

.EX
zclone lsjson remote:path --stat -M --onedrive-metadata-permissions read
.EE

.PP
Here are the possible system metadata items for the onedrive backend.

.TS
allbox;
l l l l l 
l l l l l .
\fBName\fP	\fBHelp\fP	\fBType\fP	\fBExample\fP	\fBRead Only\fP
btime	T{
Time of file birth (creation) with S accuracy (mS for OneDrive Personal).
T}	RFC 3339	2006-01-02T15:04:05Z	N
content-type	The MIME type of the file.	string	text/plain	\fBY\fP
created-by-display-name	T{
Display name of the user that created the item.
T}	string	John Doe	\fBY\fP
created-by-id	T{
ID of the user that created the item.
T}	string	T{
48d31887-5fad-4d73-a9f5-3c356e68a038
T}	\fBY\fP
description	T{
A short description of the file. Max 1024 characters. No longer supported by Microsoft.
T}	string	Contract for signing	N
id	T{
The unique identifier of the item within OneDrive.
T}	string	T{
01BYE5RZ6QN3ZWBTUFOFD3GSPGOHDJD36K
T}	\fBY\fP
last-modified-by-display-name	T{
Display name of the user that last modified the item.
T}	string	John Doe	\fBY\fP
last-modified-by-id	T{
ID of the user that last modified the item.
T}	string	T{
48d31887-5fad-4d73-a9f5-3c356e68a038
T}	\fBY\fP
malware-detected	T{
Whether OneDrive has detected that the item contains malware.
T}	boolean	true	\fBY\fP
mtime	T{
Time of last modification with S accuracy (mS for OneDrive Personal).
T}	RFC 3339	2006-01-02T15:04:05Z	N
package-type	T{
If present, indicates that this item is a package instead of a folder or file. Packages are treated like files in some contexts and folders in others.
T}	string	oneNote	\fBY\fP
permissions	T{
Permissions in a JSON dump of OneDrive format. Enable with --onedrive-metadata-permissions. Properties: id, grantedTo, grantedToIdentities, invitation, inheritedFrom, link, roles, shareId
T}	JSON	{}	N
shared-by-id	T{
ID of the user that shared the item (if shared).
T}	string	T{
48d31887-5fad-4d73-a9f5-3c356e68a038
T}	\fBY\fP
shared-owner-id	T{
ID of the owner of the shared item (if shared).
T}	string	T{
48d31887-5fad-4d73-a9f5-3c356e68a038
T}	\fBY\fP
shared-scope	T{
If shared, indicates the scope of how the item is shared: anonymous, organization, or users.
T}	string	users	\fBY\fP
shared-time	T{
Time when the item was shared, with S accuracy (mS for OneDrive Personal).
T}	RFC 3339	2006-01-02T15:04:05Z	\fBY\fP
utime	T{
Time of upload with S accuracy (mS for OneDrive Personal).
T}	RFC 3339	2006-01-02T15:04:05Z	\fBY\fP
.TE

.PP
See the metadata
\[la]//docs/#metadata\[ra] docs for more info.

.SS Impersonate other users as Admin
Unlike Google Drive and impersonating any domain user via service accounts,
OneDrive requires you to authenticate as an admin account, and manually setup
a remote per user you wish to impersonate.
.IP "  1." 5
In Microsoft 365 Admin Center
\[la]https://admin.microsoft.com\[ra], open each user
you need to "impersonate" and go to the OneDrive section. There is a heading
called "Get access to files", you need to click to create the link, this
creates the link of the format
\fBhttps://{tenant}-my.sharepoint.com/personal/{user_name_domain_tld}/\fR but also
changes the permissions so you your admin user has access.
.IP "  2." 5
Then in powershell run the following commands:

.EX
   Install-Module Microsoft.Graph -Scope CurrentUser -Repository PSGallery -Force
   Import-Module Microsoft.Graph.Files
   Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "Files.ReadWrite.All"
   # Follow the steps to allow access to your admin user
   # Then run this for each user you want to impersonate to get the Drive ID
   Get-MgUserDefaultDrive -UserId '{emailaddress}'
   # This will give you output of the format:
   # Name     Id                                                                 DriveType CreatedDateTime
   # ----     --                                                                 --------- ---------------
   # OneDrive b!XYZ123                                                           business  14/10/2023    1:00:58 pm
.EE

.IP "  1." 5
Then in zclone add a onedrive remote type, and use the \fBType in driveID\fR
with the DriveID you got in the previous step. One remote per user. It will
then confirm the drive ID, and hopefully give you a message of
\fBFound drive "root" of type "business"\fR and then include the URL of the format
\fBhttps://{tenant}-my.sharepoint.com/personal/{user_name_domain_tld}/Documents\fR

.SH Limitations
If you don't use zclone for 90 days the refresh token will
expire. This will result in authorization problems. This is easy to
fix by running the \fBzclone config reconnect remote:\fR command to get a
new token and refresh token.

.SS Naming
Note that OneDrive is case insensitive so you can't have a
file called "Hello.doc" and one called "hello.doc".

.PP
There are quite a few characters that can't be in OneDrive file
names.  These can't occur on Windows platforms, but on non-Windows
platforms they are common.  Zclone will map these names to and from an
identical looking unicode equivalent.  For example if a file has a \fB?\fR
in it will be mapped to \fB？\fR instead.

.SS File sizes
The largest allowed file size is 250 GiB for both OneDrive Personal and OneDrive
for Business (Updated 13 Jan 2021)
\[la]https://support.microsoft.com/en\-us/office/invalid\-file\-names\-and\-file\-types\-in\-onedrive\-and\-sharepoint\-64883a5d\-228e\-48f5\-b3d2\-eb39e07630fa?ui=en\-us&rs=en\-us&ad=us#individualfilesize\[ra]\&.

.SS Path length
The entire path, including the file name, must contain fewer than 400
characters for OneDrive, OneDrive for Business and SharePoint Online. If you
are encrypting file and folder names with zclone, you may want to pay attention
to this limitation because the encrypted names are typically longer than the
original ones.

.SS Number of files
OneDrive seems to be OK with at least 50,000 files in a folder, but at
100,000 zclone will get errors listing the directory like \fBcouldn’t
list files: UnknownError:\fR\&.  See
#2707
\[la]/\[ra] for more info.

.PP
An official document about the limitations for different types of OneDrive can
be found here
\[la]https://support.office.com/en\-us/article/invalid\-file\-names\-and\-file\-types\-in\-onedrive\-onedrive\-for\-business\-and\-sharepoint\-64883a5d\-228e\-48f5\-b3d2\-eb39e07630fa\[ra]\&.

.SH Versions
Every change in a file OneDrive causes the service to create a new
version of the file.  This counts against a users quota.  For
example changing the modification time of a file creates a second
version, so the file apparently uses twice the space.

.PP
For example the \fBcopy\fR command is affected by this as zclone copies
the file and then afterwards sets the modification time to match the
source file which uses another version.

.PP
You can use the \fBzclone cleanup\fR command (see below) to remove all old
versions.

.PP
Or you can set the \fBno_versions\fR parameter to \fBtrue\fR and zclone will
remove versions after operations which create new versions. This takes
extra transactions so only enable it if you need it.

.PP
\fBNote\fP At the time of writing Onedrive Personal creates versions
(but not for setting the modification time) but the API for removing
them returns "API not found" so cleanup and \fBno_versions\fR should not
be used on Onedrive Personal.

.SS Disabling versioning
Starting October 2018, users will no longer be able to
disable versioning by default. This is because Microsoft has brought
an
update
\[la]https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/Microsoft\-OneDrive\-Blog/New\-Updates\-to\-OneDrive\-and\-SharePoint\-Team\-Site\-Versioning/ba\-p/204390\[ra]
to the mechanism. To change this new default setting, a PowerShell
command is required to be run by a SharePoint admin. If you are an
admin, you can run these commands in PowerShell to change that
setting:
.IP "  1." 5
\fBInstall-Module -Name Microsoft.Online.SharePoint.PowerShell\fR (in case you
haven't installed this already)
.IP "  2." 5
\fBImport-Module Microsoft.Online.SharePoint.PowerShell -DisableNameChecking\fR
.IP "  3." 5
\fBConnect-SPOService -Url https://YOURSITE-admin.sharepoint.com -Credential YOU@YOURSITE.COM\fR
(replacing \fBYOURSITE\fR, \fBYOU\fR, \fBYOURSITE.COM\fR with the actual values; this will
prompt for your credentials)
.IP "  4." 5
\fBSet-SPOTenant -EnableMinimumVersionRequirement $False\fR
.IP "  5." 5
\fBDisconnect-SPOService\fR (to disconnect from the server)

.PP
\fIBelow are the steps for normal users to disable versioning. If you don't see
the "No Versioning" option, make sure the above requirements are met.\fP

.PP
User Weropol
\[la]https://github.com/Weropol\[ra] has found a method to disable
versioning on OneDrive
.IP "  1." 5
Open the settings menu by clicking on the gear symbol at the top of the
OneDrive Business page.
.IP "  2." 5
Click Site settings.
.IP "  3." 5
Once on the Site settings page, navigate to Site Administration > Site libraries
and lists.
.IP "  4." 5
Click Customize "Documents".
.IP "  5." 5
Click General Settings > Versioning Settings.
.IP "  6." 5
Under Document Version History select the option No versioning.
Note: This will disable the creation of new file versions, but will not remove
any previous versions. Your documents are safe.
.IP "  7." 5
Apply the changes by clicking OK.
.IP "  8." 5
Use zclone to upload or modify files. (I also use the --no-update-modtime flag)
.IP "  9." 5
Restore the versioning settings after using zclone. (Optional)

.SH Cleanup
OneDrive supports \fBzclone cleanup\fR which causes zclone to look through
every file under the path supplied and delete all version but the
current version. Because this involves traversing all the files, then
querying each file for versions it can be quite slow. Zclone does
\fB--checkers\fR tests in parallel. The command also supports \fB--interactive\fR/\fBi\fR
or \fB--dry-run\fR which is a great way to see what it would do.

.EX
zclone cleanup --interactive remote:path/subdir # interactively remove all old version for path/subdir
zclone cleanup remote:path/subdir               # unconditionally remove all old version for path/subdir
.EE

.PP
\fBNB\fP Onedrive personal can't currently delete versions

.SH Troubleshooting
.SS Excessive throttling or blocked on SharePoint
If you experience excessive throttling or is being blocked on SharePoint then
it may help to set the user agent explicitly with a flag like this:
\fB--user-agent "ISV|zclone.org|zclone/v1.55.1"\fR

.PP
The specific details can be found in the Microsoft document:
Avoid getting throttled or blocked in SharePoint Online
\[la]https://docs.microsoft.com/en\-us/sharepoint/dev/general\-development/how\-to\-avoid\-getting\-throttled\-or\-blocked\-in\-sharepoint\-online#how\-to\-decorate\-your\-http\-traffic\-to\-avoid\-throttling\[ra]

.SS Unexpected file size/hash differences on Sharepoint
It is a
known
\[la]https://github.com/OneDrive/onedrive\-api\-docs/issues/935#issuecomment\-441741631\[ra]
issue that Sharepoint (not OneDrive or OneDrive for Business) silently modifies
uploaded files, mainly Office files (.docx, .xlsx, etc.), causing file size and
hash checks to fail. There are also other situations that will cause OneDrive to
report inconsistent file sizes. To use zclone with such
affected files on Sharepoint, you
may disable these checks with the following command line arguments:

.EX
--ignore-checksum --ignore-size
.EE

.PP
Alternatively, if you have write access to the OneDrive files, it may be possible
to fix this problem for certain files, by attempting the steps below.
Open the web interface for OneDrive
\[la]https://onedrive.live.com\[ra] and find the
affected files (which will be in the error messages/log for zclone). Simply click
on each of these files, causing OneDrive to open them on the web. This will cause
each file to be converted in place to a format that is functionally equivalent
but which will no longer trigger the size discrepancy. Once all problematic files
are converted you will no longer need the ignore options above.

.SS Replacing/deleting existing files on Sharepoint gets "item not found"
It is a known
\[la]https://github.com/OneDrive/onedrive\-api\-docs/issues/1068\[ra] issue
that Sharepoint (not OneDrive or OneDrive for Business) may return "item not
found" errors when users try to replace or delete uploaded files; this seems to
mainly affect Office files (.docx, .xlsx, etc.) and web files (.html, .aspx, etc.).
As a workaround, you may use
the \fB--backup-dir <BACKUP_DIR>\fR command line argument so zclone moves the
files to be replaced/deleted into a given backup directory (instead of directly
replacing/deleting them). For example, to instruct zclone to move the files into
the directory \fBzclone-backup-dir\fR on backend \fBmysharepoint\fR, you may use:

.EX
--backup-dir mysharepoint:zclone-backup-dir
.EE

.SS access_denied (AADSTS65005)
.EX
Error: access_denied
Code: AADSTS65005
Description: Using application 'zclone' is currently not supported for your organization [YOUR_ORGANIZATION] because it is in an unmanaged state. An administrator needs to claim ownership of the company by DNS validation of [YOUR_ORGANIZATION] before the application zclone can be provisioned.
.EE

.PP
This means that zclone can't use the OneDrive for Business API with your account.
You can't do much about it, maybe write an email to your admins.

.PP
However, there are other ways to interact with your OneDrive account. Have a look
at the WebDAV backend: <//webdav/#sharepoint>

.SS invalid_grant (AADSTS50076)
.EX
Error: invalid_grant
Code: AADSTS50076
Description: Due to a configuration change made by your administrator, or because you moved to a new location, you must use multi-factor authentication to access '...'.
.EE

.PP
If you see the error above after enabling multi-factor authentication for your
account, you can fix it by refreshing your OAuth refresh token. To do that, run
\fBzclone config\fR, and choose to edit your OneDrive backend. Then, you don't need
to actually make any changes until you reach this question:
\fBToken already configured - replace it?\fR\&. For this question, answer \fBy\fR and go through
the process to refresh your token, just like the first time the backend is
configured. After this, zclone should work again for this backend.

.SS Invalid request when making public links
On Sharepoint and OneDrive for Business, \fBzclone link\fR may return an "Invalid
request" error. A possible cause is that the organisation admin didn't allow
public links to be made for the organisation/sharepoint library. To fix the
permissions as an admin, take a look at the docs:
1
\[la]https://docs.microsoft.com/en\-us/sharepoint/turn\-external\-sharing\-on\-or\-off\[ra],
2
\[la]https://support.microsoft.com/en\-us/office/set\-up\-and\-manage\-access\-requests\-94b26e0b\-2822\-49d4\-929a\-8455698654b3\[ra]\&.

.SS Can not access \fBShared\fR with me files
Shared with me files is not supported by zclone
currently
\[la]/\[ra], but there is a workaround:
.IP "  1." 5
Visit https://onedrive.live.com
\[la]https://onedrive.live.com/\[ra]
.IP "  2." 5
Right click a item in \fBShared\fR, then click \fBAdd shortcut to My files\fR in the context
")
.IP "  3." 5
The shortcut will appear in \fBMy files\fR, you can access it with zclone, it
behaves like a normal folder/file.
")
")

.SS Live Photos uploaded from iOS (small video clips in .heic files)
The iOS OneDrive app introduced upload and storage
\[la]https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft\-onedrive\-blog/live\-photos\-come\-to\-onedrive/ba\-p/1953452\[ra]
of Live Photos
\[la]https://support.apple.com/en\-gb/HT207310\[ra] in 2020.
The usage and download of these uploaded Live Photos is unfortunately still
work-in-progress and this introduces several issues when copying, synchronising
and mounting – both in zclone and in the native OneDrive client on Windows.

.PP
The root cause can easily be seen if you locate one of your Live Photos in the
OneDrive web interface. Then download the photo from the web interface. You
will then see that the size of downloaded .heic file is smaller than the size
displayed in the web interface. The downloaded file is smaller because it only
contains a single frame (still photo) extracted from the Live Photo (movie)
stored in OneDrive.

.PP
The different sizes will cause \fBzclone copy/sync\fR to repeatedly recopy
unmodified photos something like this:

.EX
DEBUG : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: Sizes differ (src 4470314 vs dst 1298667)
DEBUG : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: sha1 = fc2edde7863b7a7c93ca6771498ac797f8460750 OK
INFO  : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: Copied (replaced existing)
.EE

.PP
These recopies can be worked around by adding \fB--ignore-size\fR\&. Please note that
this workaround only syncs the still-picture not the movie clip,
and relies on modification dates being correctly updated on all files in all situations.

.PP
The different sizes will also cause \fBzclone check\fR to report size errors something
like this:

.EX
ERROR : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: sizes differ
.EE

.PP
These check errors can be suppressed by adding \fB--ignore-size\fR\&.

.PP
The different sizes will also cause \fBzclone mount\fR to fail downloading with an
error something like this:

.EX
ERROR : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: ReadFileHandle.Read error: low level retry 1/10: unexpected EOF
.EE

.PP
or like this when using \fB--cache-mode=full\fR:

.EX
INFO  : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: vfs cache: downloader: error count now 1: vfs reader: failed to write to cache file: 416 Requested Range Not Satisfiable:
ERROR : 20230203_123826234_iOS.heic: vfs cache: failed to download: vfs reader: failed to write to cache file: 416 Requested Range Not Satisfiable:
.EE


