.SH Authentication Providers
OCI has various authentication methods. To learn more about authentication methods
please refer oci authentication methods
\[la]https://docs.oracle.com/en\-us/iaas/Content/API/Concepts/sdk_authentication_methods.htm\[ra]
These choices can be specified in the zclone config file.

.PP
Zclone supports the following OCI authentication provider.

.EX
User Principal
Instance Principal
Resource Principal
Workload Identity
No authentication
.EE

.SS User Principal
Sample zclone config file for Authentication Provider User Principal:

.EX
[oos]
type = oracleobjectstorage
namespace = id<redacted>34
compartment = ocid1.compartment.oc1..aa<redacted>ba
region = us-ashburn-1
provider = user_principal_auth
config_file = /home/opc/.oci/config
config_profile = Default
.EE

.PP
Advantages:
.IP \(bu 2
One can use this method from any server within OCI or on-premises or from
other cloud provider.

.PP
Considerations:
.IP \(bu 2
you need to configure user’s privileges / policy to allow access to object
storage
.IP \(bu 2
Overhead of managing users and keys.
.IP \(bu 2
If the user is deleted, the config file will no longer work and may cause
automation regressions that use the user's credentials.

.SS Instance Principal
An OCI compute instance can be authorized to use zclone by using it's identity
and certificates as an instance principal. With this approach no credentials
have to be stored and managed.

.PP
Sample zclone configuration file for Authentication Provider Instance Principal:

.EX
[opc@zclone ~]$ cat ~/.config/zclone/zclone.conf
[oos]
type = oracleobjectstorage
namespace = id<redacted>fn
compartment = ocid1.compartment.oc1..aa<redacted>k7a
region = us-ashburn-1
provider = instance_principal_auth
.EE

.PP
Advantages:
.IP \(bu 2
With instance principals, you don't need to configure user credentials and
transfer/ save it to disk in your compute instances or rotate the credentials.
.IP \(bu 2
You don’t need to deal with users and keys.
.IP \(bu 2
Greatly helps in automation as you don't have to manage access keys, user
private keys, storing them in vault, using kms etc.

.PP
Considerations:
.IP \(bu 2
You need to configure a dynamic group having this instance as member and add
policy to read object storage to that dynamic group.
.IP \(bu 2
Everyone who has access to this machine can execute the CLI commands.
.IP \(bu 2
It is applicable for oci compute instances only. It cannot be used on external
instance or resources.

.SS Resource Principal
Resource principal auth is very similar to instance principal auth but used for
resources that are not  compute instances such as
serverless functions
\[la]https://docs.oracle.com/en\-us/iaas/Content/Functions/Concepts/functionsoverview.htm\[ra]\&.
To use resource principal ensure Zclone process is started with these environment
variables set in its process.

.EX
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_VERSION=2.2
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_REGION=us-ashburn-1
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_PRIVATE_PEM=/usr/share/model-server/key.pem
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_RPST=/usr/share/model-server/security_token
.EE

.PP
Sample zclone configuration file for Authentication Provider Resource Principal:

.EX
[oos]
type = oracleobjectstorage
namespace = id<redacted>34
compartment = ocid1.compartment.oc1..aa<redacted>ba
region = us-ashburn-1
provider = resource_principal_auth
.EE

.SS Workload Identity
Workload Identity auth may be used when running Zclone from Kubernetes pod on
a Container Engine for Kubernetes (OKE) cluster. For more details on configuring
Workload Identity, see Granting Workloads Access to OCI Resources
\[la]https://docs.oracle.com/en\-us/iaas/Content/ContEng/Tasks/contenggrantingworkloadaccesstoresources.htm\[ra]\&.
To use workload identity, ensure Zclone is started with these environment
variables set in its process.

.EX
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_VERSION=2.2
export OCI_RESOURCE_PRINCIPAL_REGION=us-ashburn-1
.EE

.SS No authentication
Public buckets do not require any authentication mechanism to read objects.
Sample zclone configuration file for No authentication:

.EX
[oos]
type = oracleobjectstorage
namespace = id<redacted>34
compartment = ocid1.compartment.oc1..aa<redacted>ba
region = us-ashburn-1
provider = no_auth
.EE

.SS Modification times and hashes
The modification time is stored as metadata on the object as
\fBopc-meta-mtime\fR as floating point since the epoch, accurate to 1 ns.

.PP
If the modification time needs to be updated zclone will attempt to perform a server
side copy to update the modification if the object can be copied in a single part.
In the case the object is larger than 5Gb, the object will be uploaded rather than
copied.

.PP
Note that reading this from the object takes an additional \fBHEAD\fR request as the
metadata isn't returned in object listings.

.PP
The MD5 hash algorithm is supported.

.SS Multipart uploads
zclone supports multipart uploads with OOS which means that it can
upload files bigger than 5 GiB.

.PP
Note that files uploaded \fIboth\fP with multipart upload \fIand\fP through
crypt remotes do not have MD5 sums.

.PP
zclone switches from single part uploads to multipart uploads at the
point specified by \fB--oos-upload-cutoff\fR\&.  This can be a maximum of 5 GiB
and a minimum of 0 (ie always upload multipart files).

.PP
The chunk sizes used in the multipart upload are specified by
\fB--oos-chunk-size\fR and the number of chunks uploaded concurrently is
specified by \fB--oos-upload-concurrency\fR\&.

.PP
Multipart uploads will use \fB--transfers\fR * \fB--oos-upload-concurrency\fR *
\fB--oos-chunk-size\fR extra memory.  Single part uploads to not use extra
memory.

.PP
Single part transfers can be faster than multipart transfers or slower
depending on your latency from oos - the more latency, the more likely
single part transfers will be faster.

.PP
Increasing \fB--oos-upload-concurrency\fR will increase throughput (8 would
be a sensible value) and increasing \fB--oos-chunk-size\fR also increases
throughput (16M would be sensible).  Increasing either of these will
use more memory.  The default values are high enough to gain most of
the possible performance without using too much memory.

.PP
 

