excess files in the container.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/local/directory remote:container
.EE

.SS Configuration from an OpenStack credentials file
An OpenStack credentials file typically looks something something
like this (without the comments)

.EX
export OS_AUTH_URL=https://a.provider.net/v2.0
export OS_TENANT_ID=ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff
export OS_TENANT_NAME="1234567890123456"
export OS_USERNAME="123abc567xy"
echo "Please enter your OpenStack Password: "
read -sr OS_PASSWORD_INPUT
export OS_PASSWORD=$OS_PASSWORD_INPUT
export OS_REGION_NAME="SBG1"
if [ -z "$OS_REGION_NAME" ]; then unset OS_REGION_NAME; fi
.EE

.PP
The config file needs to look something like this where \fB$OS_USERNAME\fR
represents the value of the \fBOS_USERNAME\fR variable - \fB123abc567xy\fR in
the example above.

.EX
[remote]
type = swift
user = $OS_USERNAME
key = $OS_PASSWORD
auth = $OS_AUTH_URL
tenant = $OS_TENANT_NAME
.EE

.PP
Note that you may (or may not) need to set \fBregion\fR too - try without first.

.SS Configuration from the environment
If you prefer you can configure zclone to use swift using a standard
set of OpenStack environment variables.

.PP
When you run through the config, make sure you choose \fBtrue\fR for
\fBenv_auth\fR and leave everything else blank.

.PP
zclone will then set any empty config parameters from the environment
using standard OpenStack environment variables.  There is a list of
the
variables
\[la]https://godoc.org/github.com/ncw/swift#Connection.ApplyEnvironment\[ra]
in the docs for the swift library.

.SS Using an alternate authentication method
If your OpenStack installation uses a non-standard authentication method
that might not be yet supported by zclone or the underlying swift library,
you can authenticate externally (e.g. calling manually the \fBopenstack\fR
commands to get a token). Then, you just need to pass the two
configuration variables \fBauth_token\fR and \fBstorage_url\fR\&.
If they are both provided, the other variables are ignored. zclone will
not try to authenticate but instead assume it is already authenticated
and use these two variables to access the OpenStack installation.

.SS Using zclone without a config file
You can use zclone with swift without a config file, if desired, like
this:

.EX
source openstack-credentials-file
export ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYREMOTE_TYPE=swift
export ZCLONE_CONFIG_MYREMOTE_ENV_AUTH=true
zclone lsd myremote:
.EE

.SS --fast-list
This remote supports \fB--fast-list\fR which allows you to use fewer
transactions in exchange for more memory. See the zclone
docs
\[la]//docs/#fast\-list\[ra] for more details.

.SS --update and --use-server-modtime
As noted below, the modified time is stored on metadata on the object. It is
used by default for all operations that require checking the time a file was
last updated. It allows zclone to treat the remote more like a true filesystem,
but it is inefficient because it requires an extra API call to retrieve the
metadata.

.PP
For many operations, the time the object was last uploaded to the remote is
sufficient to determine if it is "dirty". By using \fB--update\fR along with
\fB--use-server-modtime\fR, you can avoid the extra API call and simply upload
files whose local modtime is newer than the time it was last uploaded.

.SS Modification times and hashes
The modified time is stored as metadata on the object as
\fBX-Object-Meta-Mtime\fR as floating point since the epoch accurate to 1
ns.

.PP
This is a de facto standard (used in the official python-swiftclient
amongst others) for storing the modification time for an object.

.PP
The MD5 hash algorithm is supported.

.SS Restricted filename characters
.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
/	0x2F	／
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be used in JSON strings.

.PP
 

