.EE

.SS SSH Authentication
The SFTP remote supports three authentication methods:
.IP \(bu 2
Password
.IP \(bu 2
Key file, including certificate signed keys
.IP \(bu 2
ssh-agent

.PP
Key files should be PEM-encoded private key files. For instance \fB/home/$USER/.ssh/id_rsa\fR\&.
Only unencrypted OpenSSH or PEM encrypted files are supported.

.PP
The key file can be specified in either an external file (key_file) or contained
within the  zclone config file (key_pem).  If using key_pem in the config file,
the entry should be on a single line with new line ('\\n' or '\\r\\n') separating lines.
I.e.

.EX
key_pem = -----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMaMbaIXtE\\n0gAMbMbaSsd\\nMbaass\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
.EE

.PP
This will generate it correctly for key_pem for use in the config:

.EX
awk '{printf "%s\\\\n", $0}' < ~/.ssh/id_rsa
.EE

.PP
If you don't specify \fBpass\fR, \fBkey_file\fR, or \fBkey_pem\fR or \fBask_password\fR then
zclone will attempt to contact an ssh-agent. You can also specify \fBkey_use_agent\fR
to force the usage of an ssh-agent. In this case \fBkey_file\fR or \fBkey_pem\fR can
also be specified to force the usage of a specific key in the ssh-agent.

.PP
Using an ssh-agent is the only way to load encrypted OpenSSH keys at the moment.

.PP
If you set the \fBask_password\fR option, zclone will prompt for a password when
needed and no password has been configured.

.SS Certificate-signed keys
With traditional key-based authentication, you configure your private key only,
and the public key built into it will be used during the authentication process.

.PP
If you have a certificate you may use it to sign your public key, creating a
separate SSH user certificate that should be used instead of the plain public key
extracted from the private key. Then you must provide the path to the
user certificate public key file in \fBpubkey_file\fR or the content of the file in \fBpubkey\fR\&.

.PP
Note: This is not the traditional public key paired with your private key,
typically saved as \fB/home/$USER/.ssh/id_rsa.pub\fR\&. Setting this path in
\fBpubkey_file\fR will not work.

.PP
Example:

.EX
[remote]
type = sftp
host = example.com
user = sftpuser
key_file = ~/id_rsa
pubkey_file = ~/id_rsa-cert.pub
````

If you concatenate a cert with a private key then you can specify the
merged file in both places.

Note: the cert must come first in the file.  e.g.

```console
cat id_rsa-cert.pub id_rsa > merged_key
.EE

.SS Host key validation
By default zclone will not check the server's host key for validation.
This can allow an attacker to replace a server with their own and if
you use password authentication then this can lead to that password
being exposed. Zclone will produce a warning \fBNo host key validation
is being performed\fR each time the backend is started in this mode.

.PP
Host key matching, using standard ssh \fBknown_hosts\fR files can be
turned on by enabling the \fBknown_hosts_file\fR option. This can point to
the file maintained by \fBOpenSSH\fR or can point to a unique file. To
explicitly disable host key checking and silence the warning, the
\fBknown_hosts_file\fR option can be set to \fBnone\fR\&.

.PP
Alternatively zclone can maintain server host keys in a \fBhost_keys\fR
setting in the config file. This can be updated automatically with
\fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR\&.

.PP
These options are described below

.SS Using the OpenSSH known_hosts file
Using the OpenSSH \fBknown_hosts\fR file looks like this:

.EX
[remote]
type = sftp
host = example.com
user = sftpuser
pass = 
known_hosts_file = ~/.ssh/known_hosts
````

Alternatively you can create your own known hosts file like this:

```console
ssh-keyscan -t dsa,rsa,ecdsa,ed25519 example.com >> known_hosts
.EE

.PP
There are some limitations:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzclone\fR will not \fImanage\fP this file for you.  If the key is missing or
wrong then the connection will be refused.
.IP \(bu 2
If the server is set up for a certificate host key then the entry in
the \fBknown_hosts\fR file \fImust\fP be the \fB@cert-authority\fR entry for the CA

.PP
If the host key provided by the server does not match the one in the
file (or is missing) then the connection will be aborted and an error
returned such as

.EX
NewFs: couldn't connect SSH: ssh: handshake failed: knownhosts: key mismatch
.EE

.PP
or

.EX
NewFs: couldn't connect SSH: ssh: handshake failed: knownhosts: key is unknown
.EE

.PP
If you see an error such as

.EX
NewFs: couldn't connect SSH: ssh: handshake failed: ssh: no authorities for hostname: example.com:22
.EE

.PP
then it is likely the server has presented a CA signed host certificate
and you will need to add the appropriate \fB@cert-authority\fR entry.

.PP
The \fBknown_hosts_file\fR setting can be set during \fBzclone config\fR as an
advanced option.

.SS Host key pinning
As an alternative to maintaining a \fBknown_hosts\fR file, zclone supports
Trust On First Use (TOFU) host key pinning via the \fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR
command-line flag.

.PP
The recommended workflow is:
.IP "  1." 5
For the very first connection to a new remote, run zclone once with
\fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR\&. Zclone records the server's host key into the
remote's \fBhost_keys\fR config option and logs the SHA256 fingerprint:

.EX
   $ zclone --sftp-pin-host-key lsd remote:
   2026/01/01 12:00:00 NOTICE: sftp://sftpuser@example.com:22/: Accepted ssh-ed25519 host key SHA256:abc... for example.com:22 on first use
   2026/01/01 12:00:00 NOTICE: sftp://sftpuser@example.com:22/: Pinned ssh-ed25519 host key SHA256:abc... for example.com:22 in config
.EE

.IP "  1." 5
For every subsequent run, omit the flag. Zclone consults the pinned
\fBhost_keys\fR and refuses the connection on any mismatch.

.PP
This is a strict improvement over the default of no host key validation,
but note that the first connection itself is unauthenticated. Zclone
detects later key changes, not a man-in-the-middle who is already on-path
the first time you connect. Ideally do the first connection over a trusted
network or cross-check the fingerprint zclone logs against one provided out
of band by the server operator.

.PP
If \fBknown_hosts_file\fR is also set it takes precedence and
\fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR is ignored.

.PP
After the first successful connection the config will contain a new line:

.EX
[remote]
type = sftp
host = example.com
user = sftpuser
pass = 
host_keys = ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...
.EE

.PP
The \fBhost_keys\fR field is always validated against the offered host key
when non-empty, so you can also pin a known key by hand without ever
running with the flag. Each entry is the complete public key - the
algorithm and base64 fields of a known_hosts line - not its SHA256
fingerprint:

.EX
zclone config update remote host_keys "ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3..."
.EE

.PP
Setting \fBpin_host_key = true\fR persistently in the config file is not
recommended: while it is set, zclone will accept any new host key algorithm
the server later presents, widening the trust surface beyond the initial
pin. Using \fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR as a one-shot flag keeps each
unauthenticated trust event a deliberate decision.

.SS Re-pinning after a legitimate key change
If the server's host key is legitimately rotated, zclone will refuse the
connection with an error containing both the stored and offered SHA256
fingerprints. To accept the new key, clear the stored value and re-run once
with the flag:

.EX
zclone config update remote host_keys ""
zclone --sftp-pin-host-key lsd remote:
.EE

.PP
Alternatively edit \fBhost_keys\fR directly to replace or add the new entry.
Multiple entries (separated by commas) are supported.

.SS Limitations
.IP \(bu 2
If the server uses an \fB@cert-authority\fR-signed host certificate,
host key pinning cannot validate it (the certificate is re-issued
periodically even though the underlying CA is unchanged). Use
\fBknown_hosts_file\fR with an \fB@cert-authority\fR entry instead.
.IP \(bu 2
On-the-fly remotes (\fB:sftp,host=...:\fR) cannot persist the pinned key -
zclone will log a warning and re-accept the server key on every run,
so the flag only provides first-connect fingerprint logging. Named
remotes used with connection string overrides (\fBremote,port=2022:\fR)
are fine: the pinned key is saved to the remote's config section.
.IP \(bu 2
If the \fBssh\fR option is set, the configured ssh program makes the
connection and does its own host key validation, so \fBhost_keys\fR is
not consulted and \fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR pins nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
Load-balanced SFTP endpoints that present a different host key per
backend node will produce a mismatch when a later connection lands on
a different node. Re-run with \fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR after clearing
\fBhost_keys\fR, or repeat the first-connect step until each node's key
has been observed, appending the additional entries to \fBhost_keys\fR by
hand (comma-separated).
.IP \(bu 2
zclone does not yet support OpenSSH's \fBhostkeys@openssh.com\fR extension,
the non-RFC mechanism that helps clients learn additional host keys
during rotation. This is being tracked upstream at
https://github.com/golang/go/issues/37245

.SS ssh-agent on macOS
Note that there seem to be various problems with using an ssh-agent on
macOS due to recent changes in the OS.  The most effective work-around
seems to be to start an ssh-agent in each session, e.g.

.EX
eval `ssh-agent -s` && ssh-add -A
.EE

.PP
And then at the end of the session

.EX
eval `ssh-agent -k`
.EE

.PP
These commands can be used in scripts of course.

.SS Shell access
Some functionality of the SFTP backend relies on remote shell access,
and the possibility to execute commands. This includes checksum
\[la]#checksum\[ra],
and in some cases also about
\[la]#about\-command\[ra]\&. The shell commands that
must be executed may be different on different type of shells, and also
quoting/escaping of file path arguments containing special characters may
be different. Zclone therefore needs to know what type of shell it is,
and if shell access is available at all.

.PP
Most servers run on some version of Unix, and then a basic Unix shell can
be assumed, without further distinction. Windows 10, Server 2019, and later
can also run a SSH server, which is a port of OpenSSH (see official
installation guide
\[la]https://docs.microsoft.com/en\-us/windows\-server/administration/openssh/openssh_install_firstuse\[ra]).
On a Windows server the shell handling is different: Although it can also
be set up to use a Unix type shell, e.g. Cygwin bash, the default is to
use Windows Command Prompt (cmd.exe), and PowerShell is a recommended
alternative. All of these have behave differently, which zclone must handle.

.PP
Zclone tries to auto-detect what type of shell is used on the server,
first time you access the SFTP remote. If a remote shell session is
successfully created, it will look for indications that it is CMD or
PowerShell, with fall-back to Unix if not something else is detected.
If unable to even create a remote shell session, then shell command
execution will be disabled entirely. The result is stored in the SFTP
remote configuration, in option \fBshell_type\fR, so that the auto-detection
only have to be performed once. If you manually set a value for this
option before first run, the auto-detection will be skipped, and if
you set a different value later this will override any existing.
Value \fBnone\fR can be set to avoid any attempts at executing shell
commands, e.g. if this is not allowed on the server.
If you have \fBshell_type = none\fR in the configuration then
the ssh
\[la]#sftp\-ssh\[ra] must not be set.

.PP
When the server is zclone serve sftp
\[la]//commands/zclone_serve_sftp/\[ra],
the zclone SFTP remote will detect this as a Unix type shell - even
if it is running on Windows. This server does not actually have a shell,
but it accepts input commands matching the specific ones that the
SFTP backend relies on for Unix shells, e.g. \fBmd5sum\fR and \fBdf\fR\&. Also
it handles the string escape rules used for Unix shell. Treating it
as a Unix type shell from a SFTP remote will therefore always be
correct, and support all features.

.SS Shell access considerations
The shell type auto-detection logic, described above, means that
by default zclone will try to run a shell command the first time
a new sftp remote is accessed. If you configure a sftp remote
without a config file, e.g. an on the fly
\[la]//docs/#backend\-path\-to\-dir\[ra]
remote, zclone will have nowhere to store the result, and it
will re-run the command on every access. To avoid this you should
explicitly set the \fBshell_type\fR option to the correct value,
or to \fBnone\fR if you want to prevent zclone from executing any
remote shell commands.

.PP
It is also important to note that, since the shell type decides
how quoting and escaping of file paths used as command-line arguments
are performed, configuring the wrong shell type may leave you exposed
to command injection exploits. Make sure to confirm the auto-detected
shell type, or explicitly set the shell type you know is correct,
or disable shell access until you know.

.SS Checksum
SFTP does not natively support checksums (file hash), but zclone
is able to use checksumming if the same login has shell access,
and can execute remote commands. If there is a command that can
calculate compatible checksums on the remote system, Zclone can
then be configured to execute this whenever a checksum is needed,
and read back the results. By default MD5 and SHA-1 are considered,
but also CRC32, SHA-256, BLAKE3, XXH3 and XXH128 are supported,
option \fBhashes\fR can be set to specify which to consider.

.PP
Normally this requires an external utility being available on
the server. E.g. for MD5 checksums, by default zclone will try commands
\fBmd5sum\fR, \fBmd5\fR and \fBzclone md5sum\fR, and the first one found
usable will be picked. These utilities normally need to be in the
remote's PATH to be found.

.PP
In some cases the shell itself is capable of calculating checksums.
PowerShell is an example of such a shell. If zclone detects that the
remote shell is PowerShell, which means it most probably is a
Windows OpenSSH server, zclone will use a predefined script block
to produce the checksums for MD5, SHA-1 and SHA-256 when no external
checksum commands are found (see shell access
\[la]#shell\-access\[ra]). This
assumes PowerShell version 4.0 or newer.

.PP
The options \fBmd5sum_command\fR, \fBsha1_command\fR, etc. can be used to customize
the commands to be executed for calculation of checksums. You can for
example set a specific path to where the md5sum executable are located,
or specify some other tool that print checksums in compatible format.
The value can include command-line arguments, or even shell script blocks
as with PowerShell. Zclone has subcommands hashsum
\[la]//commands/zclone_hashsum/\[ra],
md5sum
\[la]//commands/zclone_md5sum/\[ra] and sha1sum
\[la]//commands/zclone_sha1sum/\[ra]
that use compatible format, which means if you have an zclone executable
on the server it can be used. As mentioned above, they will be automatically
picked up if found in PATH, but if not you can set something like
\fB/path/to/zclone md5sum\fR as the value of option \fBmd5sum_command\fR to
make sure a specific executable is used.

.PP
Remote checksumming is recommended and enabled by default. First time
zclone is using a SFTP remote, if options \fBmd5sum_command\fR or \fBsha1_command\fR
are not set, it will check if any of the default commands for each of them,
as described above, can be used. The result will be saved in the remote
configuration, so next time it will use the same. Value \fBnone\fR
will be set if none of the default commands could be used for a specific
algorithm, and this algorithm will not be supported by the remote.

.PP
Disabling the checksumming completely may be required if you are connecting to
SFTP servers which are not under your control, and to which the execution of
remote shell commands is prohibited. Set the configuration option \fBdisable_hashcheck\fR
to \fBtrue\fR to disable checksumming entirely (you get the same effect by setting
option \fBhashes\fR to \fBnone\fR or options \fBmd5sum_command\fR, \fBsha1_command\fR etc.
to \fBnone\fR). Set option \fBshell_type\fR to \fBnone\fR to not only disable checksumming,
but also disable all other functionality that are based on remote shell command
execution.

.SS Modification times and hashes
Modified times are stored on the server to 1 second precision.

.PP
Modified times are used in syncing and are fully supported.

.PP
Some SFTP servers disable setting/modifying the file modification time after
upload (for example, certain configurations of ProFTPd with mod_sftp). If you
are using one of these servers, you can set the option \fBset_modtime = false\fR in
your Zclone backend configuration to disable this behaviour.

.SS About command
The \fBabout\fR command returns the total space, free space, and used
space on the remote for the disk of the specified path on the remote or,
if not set, the disk of the root on the remote.

.PP
SFTP usually supports the about
\[la]//commands/zclone_about/\[ra] command, but
it depends on the server. If the server implements the vendor-specific
VFS statistics extension, which is normally the case with OpenSSH instances,
it will be used. If not, but the same login has access to a Unix shell,
where the \fBdf\fR command is available (e.g. in the remote's PATH), then
this will be used instead. If the server shell is PowerShell, probably
with a Windows OpenSSH server, zclone will use a built-in shell command
(see shell access
\[la]#shell\-access\[ra]). If none of the above is applicable,
\fBabout\fR will fail.

.PP
 

.SS Standard options
Here are the Standard options specific to sftp (SSH/SFTP).

.SS --sftp-host
SSH host to connect to.

.PP
E.g. "example.com".

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      host
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_HOST
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    true

.SS --sftp-user
SSH username.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      user
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_USER
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     "mizipkin"

.SS --sftp-port
SSH port number.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      port
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_PORT
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        int
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     22

.SS --sftp-pass
SSH password, leave blank to use ssh-agent.

.PP
\fBNB\fP Input to this must be obscured - see zclone obscure
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      pass
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_PASS
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-key-pem
Raw PEM-encoded private key.

.PP
Note that this should be on a single line with line endings replaced with '\\n', eg

.EX
key_pem = -----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMaMbaIXtE\\n0gAMbMbaSsd\\nMbaass\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
.EE

.PP
This will generate the single line correctly:

.EX
awk '{printf "%s\\\\n", $0}' < ~/.ssh/id_rsa
.EE

.PP
If specified, it will override the key_file parameter.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      key_pem
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_KEY_PEM
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-key-file
Path to PEM-encoded private key file.

.PP
Leave blank or set key-use-agent to use ssh-agent.

.PP
Leading \fB~\fR will be expanded in the file name as will environment variables such as \fB${ZCLONE_CONFIG_DIR}\fR\&.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      key_file
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_KEY_FILE
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-key-file-pass
The passphrase to decrypt the PEM-encoded private key file.

.PP
Only PEM encrypted key files (old OpenSSH format) are supported. Encrypted keys
in the new OpenSSH format can't be used.

.PP
\fBNB\fP Input to this must be obscured - see zclone obscure
\[la]//commands/zclone_obscure/\[ra]\&.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      key_file_pass
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_KEY_FILE_PASS
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-pubkey
SSH public certificate for public certificate based authentication.
Set this if you have a signed certificate you want to use for authentication.
If specified will override pubkey_file.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      pubkey
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_PUBKEY
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-pubkey-file
Optional path to public key file.

.PP
Set this if you have a signed certificate you want to use for authentication.

.PP
Leading \fB~\fR will be expanded in the file name as will environment variables such as \fB${ZCLONE_CONFIG_DIR}\fR\&.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      pubkey_file
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_PUBKEY_FILE
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false

.SS --sftp-key-use-agent
When set forces the usage of the ssh-agent.

.PP
When key-file is also set, the ".pub" file of the specified key-file is read and only the associated key is
requested from the ssh-agent. This allows to avoid \fBToo many authentication failures for *username*\fR errors
when the ssh-agent contains many keys.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      key_use_agent
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_KEY_USE_AGENT
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false

.SS --sftp-use-insecure-cipher
Enable the use of insecure ciphers and key exchange methods.

.PP
This enables the use of the following insecure ciphers and key exchange methods:
.IP \(bu 2
aes128-cbc
.IP \(bu 2
aes192-cbc
.IP \(bu 2
aes256-cbc
.IP \(bu 2
3des-cbc
.IP \(bu 2
diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha256
.IP \(bu 2
diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1

.PP
Those algorithms are insecure and may allow plaintext data to be recovered by an attacker.

.PP
This must be false if you use either ciphers or key_exchange advanced options.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      use_insecure_cipher
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_USE_INSECURE_CIPHER
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false
.IP \(bu 2
Examples:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
"false"
.IP \(bu 2
Use default Cipher list.
.IP \(bu 2
"true"
.IP \(bu 2
Enables the use of the aes128-cbc cipher and diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha256, diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1 key exchange.
.RE

.SS --sftp-disable-hashcheck
Disable the execution of SSH commands to determine if remote file hashing is available.

.PP
Leave blank or set to false to enable hashing (recommended), set to true to disable hashing.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      disable_hashcheck
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_DISABLE_HASHCHECK
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false

.SS --sftp-ssh
Path and arguments to external ssh binary.

.PP
Normally zclone will use its internal ssh library to connect to the
SFTP server. However it does not implement all possible ssh options so
it may be desirable to use an external ssh binary.

.PP
Zclone ignores all the internal config if you use this option and
expects you to configure the ssh binary with the user/host/port and
any other options you need.

.PP
\fBImportant\fP The ssh command must log in without asking for a
password so needs to be configured with keys or certificates.

.PP
Zclone will run the command supplied either with the additional
arguments "-s sftp" to access the SFTP subsystem or with commands such
as "md5sum /path/to/file" appended to read checksums.

.PP
Any arguments with spaces in should be surrounded by "double quotes".

.PP
An example setting might be:

.EX
ssh -o ServerAliveInterval=20 user@example.com
.EE

.PP
Note that when using an external ssh binary zclone makes a new ssh
connection for every hash it calculates.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      ssh
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_SSH
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        SpaceSepList
.IP \(bu 2
Default:
.br


.SS Advanced options
Here are the Advanced options specific to sftp (SSH/SFTP).

.SS --sftp-known-hosts-file
Optional path to known_hosts file.

.PP
Set this value to enable server host key validation. Set to \fBnone\fR to silence the "No host key validation" notice.

.PP
Leading \fB~\fR will be expanded in the file name as will environment variables such as \fB${ZCLONE_CONFIG_DIR}\fR\&.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      known_hosts_file
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_KNOWN_HOSTS_FILE
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        string
.IP \(bu 2
Required:    false
.IP \(bu 2
Examples:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
"~/.ssh/known_hosts"
.IP \(bu 2
Use OpenSSH's known_hosts file.
.RE

.SS --sftp-pin-host-key
Pin the server host key on first connection (Trust On First Use).

.PP
Intended for one-time use as the \fB--sftp-pin-host-key\fR command-line
flag. Run zclone once with the flag and the server's host key will be
recorded into the host_keys config option. On subsequent runs (without
the flag) host_keys is consulted and any mismatch is refused.

.PP
Setting this option persistently in the config file is not
recommended. While it is set, zclone will also accept any new
host key algorithm the server later presents, which widens the trust
surface beyond the initial pin. To pin a new key after a legitimate
key change, re-run with the flag.

.PP
The first connection is unauthenticated, so ideally do it over a
trusted network or cross-check the fingerprint zclone logs against
one provided out of band.

.PP
If known_hosts_file is also set, that takes precedence and this option
is ignored.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      pin_host_key
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_PIN_HOST_KEY
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false

.SS --sftp-host-keys
Pinned host keys for this remote, used to verify the server.

.PP
Comma-separated list of "algo base64-key" entries (the same format as
the second and third fields of an OpenSSH known_hosts line). Usually
populated automatically by running once with --sftp-pin-host-key, but
can be set by hand to pin a server's public key obtained out of band.
Note that each entry is the complete public key, not its SHA256
fingerprint.

.PP
When non-empty, the offered host key must match one of the entries or
the connection is refused. To re-pin after a legitimate key change,
clear this option and reconnect with --sftp-pin-host-key, or edit the
value directly.

.PP
At most 16 entries may be pinned.

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      host_keys
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_HOST_KEYS
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        CommaSepList
.IP \(bu 2
Default:
.br


.SS --sftp-ask-password
Allow asking for SFTP password when needed.

.PP
If this is set and no password is supplied then zclone will:
- ask for a password
- not contact the ssh agent

.PP
Properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Config:      ask_password
.IP \(bu 2
Env Var:     ZCLONE_SFTP_ASK_PASSWORD
.IP \(bu 2
Type:        bool
.IP \(bu 2
Default:     false

.SS --sftp-path-override
Override path used by SSH shell commands.

.PP
This allows checksum calculation when SFTP and SSH paths are
different. This issue affects among others Synology NAS boxes.

.PP
E.g. if shared folders can be found in directories representing volumes:

.EX
zclone sync /home/local/directory remote:/directory --sftp-path-override /volume2/directory
.EE

.PP
E.g. if home directory can be found in a shared folder called "home":

.EX
zclone sync /home/local/directory remote:/home/directory --sftp-path-override /volume1/homes/USER/directory
.EE

.PP
To specify only the path to the SFTP remote's root, and allow zclone to add any relative subpaths automatically (including unwrapping/decrypting remotes as necessary), add the '@' character to the beginning of the path.

.PP
E.g. the first example above could be rewritten as:

.EX
zclone sync /home/local/directory remote:/directory --sftp-path-override @/volume2
.EE

.PP
Note that when using this method with Synology "home" folders, the full "/homes/USER" path should be specified instead of "/home".

.PP
E.g. the second example above should be rewritten as:

.EX
zclone sync /home/local/directory remote:/homes/USER/directory --sftp-path-override @/volume1
.EE

.PP
