.SH Limitations
Uloz.to file names can't have the \fB\\\fR character in.  zclone maps this to
and from an identical looking unicode equivalent \fB＼\fR (U+FF3C Fullwidth
Reverse Solidus).

.PP
Uloz.to only supports filenames up to 255 characters in length.

.PP
Uloz.to rate limits access to the API, but exact details are undisclosed.
Practical testing reveals that hitting the rate limit during normal use
is very rare, although not impossible with higher number of concurrently
uploaded files.

.PP
\fBzclone about\fR is not supported by the Uloz.to backend. Although
there's an endpoint to retrieve the information for the UI, it's not
exposed in the API.  Backends without this capability cannot determine
free space for an zclone mount or use policy \fBmfs\fR (most free space)
as a member of an zclone union remote.

.PP
See List of backends that do not support zclone about
\[la]///overview/#optional\-features\[ra]
and zclone about
\[la]///commands/zclone_about/\[ra]\&.


.SH Union
The \fBunion\fR backend joins several remotes together to make a single unified view
of them.

.PP
During the initial setup with \fBzclone config\fR you will specify the upstream
remotes as a space separated list. The upstream remotes can either be a local
paths or other remotes.

.PP
The attributes \fB:ro\fR, \fB:nc\fR and \fB:writeback\fR can be attached to the end of the remote
to tag the remote as \fBread only\fP, \fBno create\fP or \fBwriteback\fP, e.g.
\fBremote:directory/subdirectory:ro\fR or \fBremote:directory/subdirectory:nc\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:ro\fR means files will only be read from here and never written
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:nc\fR means new files or directories won't be created here
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:writeback\fR means files found in different remotes will be written back here.
See the writeback section
\[la]#writeback\[ra] for more info.

.PP
Subfolders can be used in upstream remotes. Assume a union remote named \fBbackup\fR
with the remotes \fBmydrive:private/backup\fR\&. Invoking \fBzclone mkdir backup:desktop\fR
is exactly the same as invoking \fBzclone mkdir mydrive:private/backup/desktop\fR\&.

.PP
There is no special handling of paths containing \fB\&..\fR segments. Invoking \fBzclone
mkdir backup:../desktop\fR is exactly the same as invoking \fBzclone mkdir
mydrive:private/backup/../desktop\fR\&.

