.SS Behavior / Policies
The behavior of union backend is inspired by trapexit/mergerfs
\[la]https://github.com/trapexit/mergerfs\[ra]\&.
All functions are grouped into 3 categories: \fBaction\fP, \fBcreate\fP and \fBsearch\fP\&.
These functions and categories can be assigned a policy which dictates what file
or directory is chosen when performing that behavior. Any policy can be assigned
to a function or category though some may not be very useful in practice. For
instance: \fBrand\fP (random) may be useful for file creation (create) but could
lead to very odd behavior if used for \fBdelete\fR if there were more than one copy
of the file.

.SS Function / Category classifications
.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCategory\fP	\fBDescription\fP	\fBFunctions\fP
action	Writing Existing file	T{
move, rmdir, rmdirs, delete, purge and copy, sync (as destination when file exist)
T}
create	Create non-existing file	T{
copy, sync (as destination when file not exist)
T}
search	Reading and listing file	T{
ls, lsd, lsl, cat, md5sum, sha1sum and copy, sync (as source)
T}
N/A		size, about
.TE

.SS Path Preservation
Policies, as described below, are of two basic types. \fBpath preserving\fR and
\fBnon-path preserving\fR\&.

.PP
All policies which start with \fBep\fR (\fBepff\fP, \fBeplfs\fP, \fBeplus\fP, \fBepmfs\fP, \fBeprand\fP)
are \fBpath preserving\fR\&. \fBep\fR stands for \fBexisting path\fR\&.

.PP
A path preserving policy will only consider upstreams where the relative path
being accessed already exists.

.PP
When using non-path preserving policies paths will be created in target upstreams
as necessary.

.SS Quota Relevant Policies
Some policies rely on quota information. These policies should be used only if
your upstreams support the respective quota fields.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBPolicy\fP	\fBRequired Field\fP
lfs, eplfs	Free
mfs, epmfs	Free
lus, eplus	Used
lno, eplno	Objects
.TE

.PP
To check if your upstream supports the field, run \fBzclone about remote: [flags]\fR
and see if the required field exists.

.SS Filters
Policies basically search upstream remotes and create a list of files / paths for
functions to work on. The policy is responsible for filtering and sorting. The
policy type defines the sorting but filtering is mostly uniform as described below.
.IP \(bu 2
No \fBsearch\fP policies filter.
.IP \(bu 2
All \fBaction\fP policies will filter out remotes which are tagged as \fBread-only\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
All \fBcreate\fP policies will filter out remotes which are tagged \fBread-only\fP
or \fBno-create\fP\&.

.PP
If all remotes are filtered an error will be returned.

.SS Policy descriptions
The policies definition are inspired by trapexit/mergerfs
\[la]https://github.com/trapexit/mergerfs\[ra]
but not exactly the same. Some policy definition could be different due to the
much larger latency of remote file systems.

.TS
allbox;
l l 
l l .
\fBPolicy\fP	\fBDescription\fP
all	Search category: same as \fBepall\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBepall\fP\&. Create category: act on all upstreams.
epall (existing path, all)	T{
Search category: Given this order configured, act on the first one found where the relative path exists. Action category: apply to all found. Create category: act on all upstreams where the relative path exists.
T}
T{
epff (existing path, first found)
T}	T{
Act on the first one found, by the time upstreams reply, where the relative path exists.
T}
T{
eplfs (existing path, least free space)
T}	T{
Of all the upstreams on which the relative path exists choose the one with the least free space.
T}
T{
eplus (existing path, least used space)
T}	T{
Of all the upstreams on which the relative path exists choose the one with the least used space.
T}
T{
eplno (existing path, least number of objects)
T}	T{
Of all the upstreams on which the relative path exists choose the one with the least number of objects.
T}
T{
epmfs (existing path, most free space)
T}	T{
Of all the upstreams on which the relative path exists choose the one with the most free space.
T}
eprand (existing path, random)	Calls \fBepall\fP and then randomizes. Returns only one upstream.
ff (first found)	Search category: same as \fBepff\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBepff\fP\&. Create category: Act on the first one found by the time upstreams reply.
lfs (least free space)	Search category: same as \fBeplfs\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBeplfs\fP\&. Create category: Pick the upstream with the least available free space.
lus (least used space)	Search category: same as \fBeplus\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBeplus\fP\&. Create category: Pick the upstream with the least used space.
lno (least number of objects)	Search category: same as \fBeplno\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBeplno\fP\&. Create category: Pick the upstream with the least number of objects.
mfs (most free space)	Search category: same as \fBepmfs\fP\&. Action category: same as \fBepmfs\fP\&. Create category: Pick the upstream with the most available free space.
newest	T{
Pick the file / directory with the largest mtime.
T}
rand (random)	Calls \fBall\fP and then randomizes. Returns only one upstream.
.TE

.SS Writeback {#writeback}
The tag \fB:writeback\fR on an upstream remote can be used to make a simple cache
system like this:

.EX
[union]
type = union
action_policy = all
create_policy = all
search_policy = ff
upstreams = /local:writeback remote:dir
.EE

.PP
When files are opened for read, if the file is in \fBremote:dir\fR but not \fB/local\fR
then zclone will copy the file entirely into \fB/local\fR before returning a
reference to the file in \fB/local\fR\&. The copy will be done with the equivalent of
\fBzclone copy\fR so will use \fB--multi-thread-streams\fR if configured. Any copies
will be logged with an INFO log.

.PP
When files are written, they will be written to both \fBremote:dir\fR and \fB/local\fR\&.

.PP
As many remotes as desired can be added to \fBupstreams\fR but there should only be
one \fB:writeback\fR tag.

.PP
Zclone does not manage the \fB:writeback\fR remote in any way other than writing
files back to it. So if you need to expire old files or manage the size then you
will have to do this yourself.

.PP
 

