.SH Setting up your own client_id
For Zoho we advise you to set up your own client_id. To do so you have to
complete the following steps.
.IP "  1." 5
Log in to the Zoho API Console
\[la]https://api\-console.zoho.com\[ra]
.IP "  2." 5
Create a new client of type "Server-based Application". The name and website
don't matter, but you must add the redirect URL \fBhttp://localhost:53682/\fR\&.
.IP "  3." 5
Once the client is created, you can go to the settings tab and enable it in
other regions.

.PP
The client id and client secret can now be used with zclone.


.SH Local Filesystem
Local paths are specified as normal filesystem paths, e.g. \fB/path/to/wherever\fR, so

.EX
zclone sync --interactive /home/source /tmp/destination
.EE

.PP
Will sync \fB/home/source\fR to \fB/tmp/destination\fR\&.

.SH Configuration
For consistencies sake one can also configure a remote of type
\fBlocal\fR in the config file, and access the local filesystem using
zclone remote paths, e.g. \fBremote:path/to/wherever\fR, but it is probably
easier not to.

.SS Modification times
Zclone reads and writes the modification times using an accuracy determined
by the OS. Typically this is 1ns on Linux, 10 ns on Windows and 1 Second
on OS X.

.SS Filenames
Filenames should be encoded in UTF-8 on disk. This is the normal case
for Windows and OS X.

.PP
There is a bit more uncertainty in the Linux world, but new
distributions will have UTF-8 encoded files names. If you are using an
old Linux filesystem with non UTF-8 file names (e.g. latin1) then you
can use the \fBconvmv\fR tool to convert the filesystem to UTF-8. This
tool is available in most distributions' package managers.

.PP
If an invalid (non-UTF8) filename is read, the invalid characters will
be replaced with a quoted representation of the invalid bytes. The name
\fBgro\\xdf\fR will be transferred as \fBgro‛DF\fR\&. \fBzclone\fR will emit a debug
message in this case (use \fB-v\fR to see), e.g.

.EX
Local file system at .: Replacing invalid UTF-8 characters in "gro\\xdf"
.EE

.SS Restricted characters
With the local backend, restrictions on the characters that are usable in
file or directory names depend on the operating system. To check what
zclone will replace by default on your system, run \fBzclone help flags local-encoding\fR\&.

.PP
On non Windows platforms the following characters are replaced when
handling file names.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
/	0x2F	／
.TE

.PP
When running on Windows the following characters are replaced. This
list is based on the Windows file naming conventions
\[la]https://docs.microsoft.com/windows/desktop/FileIO/naming\-a\-file#naming\-conventions\[ra]\&.

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
NUL	0x00	␀
SOH	0x01	␁
STX	0x02	␂
ETX	0x03	␃
EOT	0x04	␄
ENQ	0x05	␅
ACK	0x06	␆
BEL	0x07	␇
BS	0x08	␈
HT	0x09	␉
LF	0x0A	␊
VT	0x0B	␋
FF	0x0C	␌
CR	0x0D	␍
SO	0x0E	␎
SI	0x0F	␏
DLE	0x10	␐
DC1	0x11	␑
DC2	0x12	␒
DC3	0x13	␓
DC4	0x14	␔
NAK	0x15	␕
SYN	0x16	␖
ETB	0x17	␗
CAN	0x18	␘
EM	0x19	␙
SUB	0x1A	␚
ESC	0x1B	␛
FS	0x1C	␜
GS	0x1D	␝
RS	0x1E	␞
US	0x1F	␟
/	0x2F	／
"	0x22	＂
*	0x2A	＊
:	0x3A	：
<	0x3C	＜
>	0x3E	＞
?	0x3F	？
	0x5C	＼
|	0x7C	｜
.TE

.PP
File names on Windows can also not end with the following characters.
These only get replaced if they are the last character in the name:

.TS
allbox;
l l l 
l l l .
\fBCharacter\fP	\fBValue\fP	\fBReplacement\fP
SP	0x20	␠
\&.	0x2E	．
.TE

.PP
Invalid UTF-8 bytes will also be replaced
\[la]//overview/#invalid\-utf8\[ra],
as they can't be converted to UTF-16.

.SS Paths on Windows
On Windows there are many ways of specifying a path to a file system resource.
Local paths can be absolute, like \fBC:\\path\\to\\wherever\fR, or relative,
like \fB\&..\\\&wherever\fR\&. Network paths in UNC format, \fB\\\\server\\share\fR, are also supported.
Path separator can be either \fB\\\fR (as in \fBC:\\path\\to\\wherever\fR) or \fB/\fR (as in \fBC:/path/to/wherever\fR).
Length of these paths are limited to 259 characters for files and 247
characters for directories, but there is an alternative extended-length
path format increasing the limit to (approximately) 32,767 characters.
This format requires absolute paths and the use of prefix \fB\\\\?\\\fR,
e.g. \fB\\\\?\\D:\\some\\very\\long\\path\fR\&. For convenience zclone will automatically
convert regular paths into the corresponding extended-length paths,
so in most cases you do not have to worry about this (read more below
\[la]#long\-paths\[ra]).
Using the same prefix \fB\\\\?\\\fR it is also possible to specify path to volumes
identified by their GUID, e.g. \fB\\\\?\\Volume{b75e2c83-0000-0000-0000-602f00000000}\\some\\path\fR\&.

.SS Long paths
Zclone handles long paths automatically, by converting all paths to
extended-length path format
\[la]https://docs.microsoft.com/en\-us/windows/win32/fileio/maximum\-file\-path\-limitation\[ra],
which allows paths up to 32,767 characters.

.PP
This conversion will ensure paths are absolute and prefix them with
the \fB\\\\?\\\fR\&. This is why you will see that your paths, for instance
\fB\&.\\\&files\fR is shown as path \fB\\\\?\\C:\\files\fR in the output, and \fB\\\\server\\share\fR
as \fB\\\\?\\UNC\\server\\share\fR\&.

.PP
However, in rare cases this may cause problems with buggy file
system drivers like EncFS
\[la]/\[ra]\&.
To disable UNC conversion globally, add this to your \fB\&.zclone.conf\fR file:

.EX
[local]
nounc = true
.EE

.PP
If you want to selectively disable UNC, you can add it to a separate entry like this:

.EX
[nounc]
type = local
nounc = true
.EE

.PP
And use zclone like this:

.PP
\fBzclone copy c:\\src nounc:z:\\dst\fR

.PP
This will use UNC paths on \fBc:\\src\fR but not on \fBz:\\dst\fR\&.
Of course this will cause problems if the absolute path length of a
file exceeds 259 characters on z, so only use this option if you have to.

.SS Symlinks / Junction points
Normally zclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows).

.PP
If you supply \fB--copy-links\fR or \fB-L\fR then zclone will follow the
symlink and copy the pointed to file or directory.  Note that this
flag is incompatible with \fB--links\fR / \fB-l\fR\&.

.PP
This flag applies to all commands.

.PP
For example, supposing you have a directory structure like this

.EX
$ tree /tmp/a
/tmp/a
├── b -> ../b
├── expected -> ../expected
├── one
└── two
    └── three
.EE

.PP
Then you can see the difference with and without the flag like this

.EX
$ zclone ls /tmp/a
        6 one
        6 two/three
.EE

.PP
and

.EX
$ zclone -L ls /tmp/a
     4174 expected
        6 one
        6 two/three
        6 b/two
        6 b/one
.EE

.SS --local-links, --links, -l
Normally zclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows).

.PP
If you supply this flag then zclone will copy symbolic links from the local storage,
and store them as text files, with a \fB\&.zclonelink\fR suffix in the remote storage.

.PP
The text file will contain the target of the symbolic link (see example).

.PP
This flag applies to all commands.

.PP
For example, supposing you have a directory structure like this

.EX
$ tree /tmp/a
/tmp/a
├── file1 -> ./file4
└── file2 -> /home/user/file3
.EE

.PP
Copying the entire directory with '-l'

.EX
zclone copy -l /tmp/a/ remote:/tmp/a/
.EE

.PP
The remote files are created with a \fB\&.zclonelink\fR suffix

.EX
$ zclone ls remote:/tmp/a
       5 file1.zclonelink
      14 file2.zclonelink
.EE

.PP
The remote files will contain the target of the symbolic links

.EX
$ zclone cat remote:/tmp/a/file1.zclonelink
\&./file4

$ zclone cat remote:/tmp/a/file2.zclonelink
/home/user/file3
.EE

.PP
Copying them back with '-l'

.EX
$ zclone copy -l remote:/tmp/a/ /tmp/b/

$ tree /tmp/b
/tmp/b
├── file1 -> ./file4
└── file2 -> /home/user/file3
.EE

.PP
However, if copied back without '-l'

.EX
$ zclone copyto remote:/tmp/a/ /tmp/b/

$ tree /tmp/b
/tmp/b
├── file1.zclonelink
└── file2.zclonelink
.EE

.PP
If you want to copy a single file with \fB-l\fR then you must use the \fB\&.zclonelink\fR suffix.

.EX
$ zclone copy -l remote:/tmp/a/file1.zclonelink /tmp/c

$ tree /tmp/c
/tmp/c
└── file1 -> ./file4
.EE

.PP
Note that \fB--local-links\fR just enables this feature for the local
backend. \fB--links\fR and \fB-l\fR enable the feature for all supported
backends and the VFS.

.PP
Note that this flag is incompatible with \fB-copy-links\fR / \fB-L\fR\&.

.SS Symlink targets and the destination
When zclone recreates a \fB\&.zclonelink\fR file as a symlink on local storage,
the symlink can point anywhere - including, with an absolute path or one
using \fB\&../\fR - to a location outside the directory you are copying into. This
is normal - zclone reproduces whatever target the link had, so backups
round-trip faithfully.

.PP
What zclone will \fBnot\fP do is \fIwrite through\fP such a link. If a remote you
are copying from contains both a symlink and a file or directory that would
be placed inside it - for example a \fBdir.zclonelink\fR pointing somewhere
outside the destination, alongside a \fBdir/file.txt\fR - zclone refuses to
follow the symlink when writing \fBdir/file.txt\fR\&. The offending file is
skipped with an error, the rest of the transfer continues, and the skipped
file is counted in the error summary printed at the end of the run.

.PP
This protects you from a malicious or compromised remote using \fB-l\fR /
\fB--links\fR to plant a symlink and then write through it to somewhere outside
your destination. Ordinary symlink round-trips, and symlinks that stay
inside the destination, are unaffected.

.PP
If you have intentionally pre-created a symlinked directory inside your
destination and want zclone to write into the directory it points at, do
not use \fB-l\fR / \fB--links\fR for that copy, or remove the symlink first.

.SS Restricting filesystems with --one-file-system
Normally zclone will recurse through filesystems as mounted.

.PP
However if you set \fB--one-file-system\fR or \fB-x\fR this tells zclone to
stay in the filesystem specified by the root and not to recurse into
different file systems.

.PP
For example if you have a directory hierarchy like this

.EX
root
├── disk1     - disk1 mounted on the root
│   └── file3 - stored on disk1
├── disk2     - disk2 mounted on the root
│   └── file4 - stored on disk12
├── file1     - stored on the root disk
└── file2     - stored on the root disk
.EE

.PP
Using \fBzclone --one-file-system copy root remote:\fR will only copy \fBfile1\fR
and \fBfile2\fR\&. E.g.

.EX
$ zclone -q --one-file-system ls root
        0 file1
        0 file2
.EE

.EX
$ zclone -q ls root
        0 disk1/file3
        0 disk2/file4
        0 file1
        0 file2
.EE

.PP
\fBNB\fP Zclone (like most unix tools such as \fBdu\fR, \fBrsync\fR and \fBtar\fR)
treats a bind mount to the same device as being on the same
filesystem.

.PP
\fBNB\fP This flag is only available on Unix based systems.  On systems
where it isn't supported (e.g. Windows) it will be ignored.

.PP
 

