.SH Bugs and Limitations
.SH Limitations
.SS Directory timestamps aren't preserved on some backends
As of \fBv1.66\fR, zclone supports syncing directory modtimes, if the backend
supports it. Some backends do not support it -- see
overview
\[la]///overview/\[ra] for a complete list. Additionally, note
that empty directories are not synced by default (this can be enabled with
\fB--create-empty-src-dirs\fR\&.)

.SS Zclone struggles with millions of files in a directory/bucket
Currently zclone loads each directory/bucket entirely into memory before
using it.  Since each zclone object takes 0.5k-1k of memory this can take
a very long time and use a large amount of memory.

.PP
Millions of files in a directory tends to occur on bucket-based remotes
(e.g. S3 buckets) since those remotes do not segregate subdirectories within
the bucket.

.SS Bucket-based remotes and folders
Bucket-based remotes (e.g. S3/GCS/Swift/B2) do not have a concept of
directories.  Zclone therefore cannot create directories in them which
means that empty directories on a bucket-based remote will tend to
disappear.

.PP
Some software creates empty keys ending in \fB/\fR as directory markers.
Zclone doesn't do this as it potentially creates more objects and
costs more.  This ability may be added in the future (probably via a
flag/option).

.SH Bugs
Bugs are stored in zclone's GitHub project:
.IP \(bu 2
Reported bugs
\[la]/\[ra]
.IP \(bu 2
Known issues
\[la]/\[ra]


.SH Frequently Asked Questions
.SS Do all cloud storage systems support all zclone commands
Yes they do.  All the zclone commands (e.g. \fBsync\fR, \fBcopy\fR, etc.) will
work on all the remote storage systems.

.SS Can I copy the config from one machine to another
Sure!  Zclone stores all of its config in a single file.  If you want
to find this file, run \fBzclone config file\fR which will tell you where
it is.

.PP
See the remote setup docs
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra] for more info.

.SS How do I configure zclone on a remote / headless box with no browser?
This has now been documented in its own remote setup page
\[la]//remote_setup/\[ra]\&.

.SS How can I get rid of the "Config file not found" notice?
If you see a notice like 'NOTICE: Config file "zclone.conf" not found', this
means you have not configured any remotes.

.PP
If you need to configure a remote, see the config help docs
\[la]//docs/#configure\[ra]\&.

.PP
If you are using zclone entirely with on the fly remotes
\[la]//docs/#backend\-path\-to\-dir\[ra],
you can create an empty config file to get rid of this notice, for example:

.EX
zclone config touch
.EE

.SS Can zclone sync directly from drive to s3
Zclone can sync between two remote cloud storage systems just fine.

.PP
Note that it effectively downloads the file and uploads it again, so
the node running zclone would need to have lots of bandwidth.

.PP
The syncs would be incremental (on a file by file basis).

.PP
e.g.

.EX
zclone sync --interactive drive:Folder s3:bucket
.EE

.SS Using zclone from multiple locations at the same time
You can use zclone from multiple places at the same time if you choose
different subdirectory for the output, e.g.

.EX
Server A> zclone sync --interactive /tmp/whatever remote:ServerA
Server B> zclone sync --interactive /tmp/whatever remote:ServerB
.EE

.PP
If you sync to the same directory then you should use zclone copy
otherwise the two instances of zclone may delete each other's files, e.g.

.EX
Server A> zclone copy /tmp/whatever remote:Backup
Server B> zclone copy /tmp/whatever remote:Backup
.EE

.PP
The file names you upload from Server A and Server B should be
different in this case, otherwise some file systems (e.g. Drive) may
make duplicates.

.SS Why doesn't zclone support partial transfers / binary diffs like rsync?
Zclone stores each file you transfer as a native object on the remote
cloud storage system.  This means that you can see the files you
upload as expected using alternative access methods (e.g. using the
Google Drive web interface).  There is a 1:1 mapping between files on
your hard disk and objects created in the cloud storage system.

.PP
Cloud storage systems (at least none I've come across yet) don't
support partially uploading an object. You can't take an existing
object, and change some bytes in the middle of it.

.PP
It would be possible to make a sync system which stored binary diffs
like rsync does, instead of whole objects, but that would break the
1:1 mapping of files on your hard disk to objects in the remote cloud
storage system.

.PP
All the cloud storage systems support partial downloads of content, so
it would be possible to make partial downloads work.  However to make
this work efficiently this would require storing a significant amount
of metadata, which breaks the desired 1:1 mapping of files to objects.

.SS Can zclone do bi-directional sync?
Yes, since zclone v1.58.0, bidirectional cloud sync
\[la]//bisync/\[ra] is
available.

.SS Can I use zclone with an HTTP proxy?
Yes. zclone will follow the standard environment variables for
proxies, similar to cURL and other programs.

.PP
In general the variables are called \fBhttp_proxy\fR (for services reached
over \fBhttp\fR) and \fBhttps_proxy\fR (for services reached over \fBhttps\fR).  Most
public services will be using \fBhttps\fR, but you may wish to set both.

.PP
The content of the variable is \fBprotocol://server:port\fR\&.  The protocol
value is the one used to talk to the proxy server, itself, and is commonly
either \fBhttp\fR or \fBsocks5\fR\&.

.PP
Slightly annoyingly, there is no \fIstandard\fP for the name; some applications
may use \fBhttp_proxy\fR but another one \fBHTTP_PROXY\fR\&.  The \fBGo\fR libraries
used by \fBzclone\fR will try both variations, but you may wish to set all
possibilities.  So, on Linux, you may end up with code similar to

.EX
export http_proxy=http://proxyserver:12345
export https_proxy=$http_proxy
export HTTP_PROXY=$http_proxy
export HTTPS_PROXY=$http_proxy
.EE

.PP
Note: If the proxy server requires a username and password, then use

.EX
export http_proxy=http://username:password@proxyserver:12345
export https_proxy=$http_proxy
export HTTP_PROXY=$http_proxy
export HTTPS_PROXY=$http_proxy
.EE

.PP
The \fBNO_PROXY\fR allows you to disable the proxy for specific hosts.
Hosts must be comma separated, and can contain domains or parts.
For instance "foo.com" also matches "bar.foo.com".

.PP
e.g.

.EX
export no_proxy=localhost,127.0.0.0/8,my.host.name
export NO_PROXY=$no_proxy
.EE

.PP
Note that the FTP backend does not support \fBftp_proxy\fR yet.

.PP
You can use the command line argument \fB--http-proxy\fR to set the proxy,
and in turn use an override in the config file if you want it set for
a single backend, eg \fBoverride.http_proxy = http://...\fR in the config
file.

.PP
The FTP and SFTP backends have their own \fBhttp_proxy\fR settings to
support an HTTP CONNECT proxy (
--ftp-http-proxy
\[la]///ftp/#ftp\-http\-proxy\[ra] and
--sftp-http-proxy
\[la]///ftp/#sftp\-http\-proxy\[ra] )

.SS Zclone gives x509 SSL root certificates error
\fBx509: failed to load system roots and no roots provided\fR means that \fBzclone\fR can't
find the SSL root certificates.  Likely you are running \fBzclone\fR on a NAS with
a cut-down Linux OS, or possibly on Solaris.

.PP
\fBx509: certificate signed by unknown authority\fR error may occur on outdated systems,
where \fBzclone\fR can't verify the server with the SSL root certificates.

.PP
Zclone (via the Go runtime) tries to load the root certificates from
these places on Linux.

.EX
"/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt", // Debian/Ubuntu/Gentoo etc.
"/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt",   // Fedora/RHEL
"/etc/ssl/ca-bundle.pem",             // OpenSUSE
"/etc/pki/tls/cacert.pem",            // OpenELEC
.EE

.PP
Install a reviewed CA bundle through your operating system's approved local
package source, or copy an approved bundle from controlled removable media or
an internal artifact store. Also ensure the system clock is correct.

.PP
The two environment variables \fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR and \fBSSL_CERT_DIR\fR, mentioned in
the x509 package
\[la]https://godoc.org/crypto/x509\[ra], provide an additional way to
provide the SSL root certificates on Unix systems other than macOS.

.PP
Do not use an unverified HTTPS download or \fB--insecure\fR to obtain trust roots.
That defeats certificate validation and makes a compromised bundle difficult to
detect.

.PP
On macOS, install a reviewed CA bundle through your approved local software
distribution process, then specify it with the --ca-cert
\[la]//docs/#ca\-cert\-stringarray\[ra]
flag when required.

.SS Zclone gives Failed to load config file: function not implemented error
Likely this means that you are running zclone on Linux version not
supported by the go runtime, ie earlier than version 2.6.23.

.PP
See the system requirements section in the go install
docs
\[la]https://golang.org/doc/install\[ra] for full details.

.SS All my uploaded docx/xlsx/pptx files appear as archive/zip
This is caused by uploading these files from a Windows computer which
hasn't got the Microsoft Office suite installed.  The easiest way to
fix is to install the Word viewer and the Microsoft Office
Compatibility Pack for Word, Excel, and PowerPoint 2007 and later
versions' file formats

.SS tcp lookup some.domain.com no such host
This happens when zclone cannot resolve a domain. Please check that
your DNS setup is generally working, e.g.

.EX
# both should print a long list of possible IP addresses
dig www.googleapis.com          # resolve using your default DNS
dig www.googleapis.com @8.8.8.8 # resolve with Google's DNS server
.EE

.PP
If you are using \fBsystemd-resolved\fR (default on Arch Linux), ensure it
is at version 233 or higher. Previous releases contain a bug which
causes not all domains to be resolved properly.

.PP
The Go resolver decision can be influenced with the \fBGODEBUG=netdns=...\fR
environment variable. This also allows to resolve certain issues with
DNS resolution. On Windows or MacOS systems, try forcing use of the
internal Go resolver by setting \fBGODEBUG=netdns=go\fR at runtime. On
other systems (Linux, *BSD, etc) try forcing use of the system
name resolver by setting \fBGODEBUG=netdns=cgo\fR (and recompile zclone
from source with CGO enabled if necessary). See the
name resolution section in the go docs
\[la]https://golang.org/pkg/net/#hdr\-Name_Resolution\[ra]\&.

.SS Failed to start auth webserver on Windows
.EX
Error: config failed to refresh token: failed to start auth webserver: listen tcp 127.0.0.1:53682: bind: An attempt was made to access a socket in a way forbidden by its access permissions.
\&...
yyyy/mm/dd hh:mm:ss Fatal error: config failed to refresh token: failed to start auth webserver: listen tcp 127.0.0.1:53682: bind: An attempt was made to access a socket in a way forbidden by its access permissions.
.EE

.PP
This is sometimes caused by the Host Network Service causing issues with opening
the port on the host.

.PP
A simple solution may be restarting the Host Network Service with eg. Powershell

.EX
Restart-Service hns
.EE

.SS The total size reported in the stats for a sync is wrong and keeps changing
It is likely you have more than 10,000 files that need to be
synced. By default, zclone only gets 10,000 files ahead in a sync so as
not to use up too much memory. You can change this default with the
--max-backlog
\[la]//docs/#max\-backlog\-int\[ra] flag.

.SS Zclone is using too much memory or appears to have a memory leak
Zclone is written in Go which uses a garbage collector.  The default
settings for the garbage collector mean that it runs when the heap
size has doubled.

.PP
However it is possible to tune the garbage collector to use less
memory by setting GOGC
\[la]https://dave.cheney.net/tag/gogc\[ra] to a lower
value, say \fBexport GOGC=20\fR\&.  This will make the garbage collector
work harder, reducing memory size at the expense of CPU usage.

.PP
The most common cause of zclone using lots of memory is a single
directory with millions of files in.

.PP
Before zclone v1.70 has to load this entirely into memory as zclone
objects. Each zclone object takes 0.5k-1k of memory. There is
a workaround for this
\[la]/\[ra]
which involves a bit of scripting.

.PP
However with zclone v1.70 and later zclone will automatically save
directory entries to disk when a directory with more than

\[la]//docs/#list\-cutoff\[ra] (1,000,000 by default) entries
is detected.

.PP
From v1.70 zclone also has the --max-buffer-memory
\[la]//docs/#max\-buffer\-memory\[ra]
flag which helps particularly when multi-thread transfers are using
too much memory.

.SS Zclone changes fullwidth Unicode punctuation marks in file names
For example: On a Windows system, you have a file with name \fBTest：1.jpg\fR,
where \fB：\fR is the Unicode fullwidth colon symbol. When using zclone
to copy this to your Google Drive, you will notice that the file
gets renamed to \fBTest:1.jpg\fR, where \fB:\fR is the regular (halfwidth) colon.

.PP
The reason for such renames is the way zclone handles different
restricted filenames
\[la]//overview/#restricted\-filenames\[ra] on different
cloud storage systems. It tries to avoid ambiguous file names as
much and allow moving files between many cloud storage systems
transparently, by replacing invalid characters with similar looking
Unicode characters when transferring to one storage system, and replacing
back again when transferring to a different storage system where the
original characters are supported. When the same Unicode characters
are intentionally used in file names, this replacement strategy leads
to unwanted renames. Read more under section caveats
\[la]//overview/#restricted\-filenames\-caveats\[ra]\&.

.SS Why does zclone fail to connect over TLS but another client works?
If you see TLS handshake failures (or packet captures show the server
rejecting all offered ciphers), the server/proxy may only support
legacy TLS cipher suites (for example RSA key-exchange ciphers
such as \fBRSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA256\fR, or old 3DES ciphers). Recent Go
versions (which zclone is built with) have \fBremoved insecure ciphers
from the default list\fP, so zclone may refuse to negotiate them even
if other tools still do.

.PP
If you can't update/reconfigure the server/proxy to support modern TLS
(TLS 1.2/1.3) and ECDHE-based cipher suites you can re-enable legacy
ciphers via \fBGODEBUG\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
Windows (cmd.exe):

.EX
  set GODEBUG=tlsrsakex=1
  zclone copy ...
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
Windows (PowerShell):

.EX
  $env:GODEBUG="tlsrsakex=1"
  zclone copy ...
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
Linux/macOS:

.EX
  GODEBUG=tlsrsakex=1 zclone copy ...
.EE

.PP
If the server only supports 3DES, try:

.EX
GODEBUG=tls3des=1 zclone ...
.EE

.PP
This applies to \fBany zclone feature using TLS\fP (HTTPS, FTPS, WebDAV
over TLS, proxies with TLS interception, etc.). Use these workarounds
only long enough to get the server/proxy updated.


